HT_DATA.load("detail", {"RjD1aLm4Thg":{"wy":"HN regularly calls it mind-blowing and recommends it as an introduction to Levin's work on bioelectricity and regeneration.","q":{"x":"I highly recommend this video from Levin. It was my introduction to his work.","i":27064434,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":37885238,"author":"Wherecombinator","month":"2023-10","story":"","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Mind-blowing talk that started following Levin","quote":"First saw this talk from Michael Levin on HN a few years back and it blew my mind.","text":"First saw this talk from Michael Levin on HN a few years back and it blew my mind. Ive been following him ever since. [VIDEO]"},{"id":28420509,"author":"7373737373","month":"2021-09","story":"Fish eyes grown in a petri dish from embryonic stem cells","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Levin's talk on bioelectric computation","quote":"I highly recommend this talk","text":"I highly recommend this talk \"What Bodies Think About: Bioelectric Computation Outside the Nervous System - NeurIPS 2018\" by Prof. Michael Levin: [VIDEO]"},{"id":27833226,"author":"7373737373","month":"2021-07","story":"Mike Levin on using bioelectricity to study how cells form (2019)","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Mind-blowing Levin talk","quote":"This talk of his blew my mind","text":"This talk of his blew my mind: \"What Bodies Think About: Bioelectric Computation Outside the Nervous System - NeurIPS 2018\" [VIDEO]"},{"id":27064434,"author":"k2enemy","month":"2021-05","story":"Persuading the Body to Regenerate Its Limbs","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Good introduction to Levin's work","quote":"I highly recommend this video from Levin. It was my introduction to his work.","text":"I highly recommend this video from Levin. It was my introduction to his work. What Bodies Think About: Bioelectric Computation Outside the Nervous System - NeurIPS 2018 [VIDEO]"},{"id":24796197,"author":"suby","month":"2020-10","story":"The second decade of synthetic biology: 2010–2020","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Bioelectric patterns store information separate from genome","quote":"one of the best talks I've ever seen was this 2018 talk on the subject.","text":"I'm very excited to see where we'll be in another 10 years. The possibilities in terms of medical and industrial applications seems hard to overstate. Related, but one of the best talks I've ever seen was this 2018 talk on the subject. It gets into how organisms seem to have bioelectrical patterns that store information separately from genomic, anatomical states. These bioelectrical patterns play a huge role in developmental processes, as they appear to control how the organism is structured at a high level. As a layman and a programmer, to me it sounds like the genome is the actual implementation of a function and the bioelectrical pattern is the sequence of functions you want to execute. They're able to manipulate these bioelectrical patterns to do things like replace a worms tail with another head. It's absolutely crazy to think about what could be possible here. [VIDEO]"},{"id":18706339,"author":"leesec","month":"2018-12","story":"On Having No Head: Cognition Throughout Biological Systems (2016)","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Levin's talk on bioelectric computation in the body","quote":"Possibly the most amazing talk I have ever seen.","text":"Was recently floored by Michael Levin's (one of this papers authors) talk at NIPS. Possibly the most amazing talk I have ever seen. [VIDEO]"},{"id":18741385,"author":"gt5050","month":"2018-12","story":"Ask HN: Best talks of 2018?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Piqued interest despite no biology background","quote":"One of the most interesting talks of 2018.","text":"What bodies think about - by Michael Levin at NeurIPS 2018. [VIDEO] I dont really know anything about biology, but this piqued my interest.One of the most interesting talks of 2018."},{"id":18742131,"author":"aeleos","month":"2018-12","story":"Ask HN: Best talks of 2018?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"How cell electrical networks shape organism growth","quote":"He talks about how long term low energy electrical networks between all cells in living organisms shape how the organism grows.","text":"Recent but great talk posted here, What Bodies Think About: Bioelectric Computation Outside the Nervous System [1] by Prof. Michael Levin. He talks about how long term low energy electrical networks between all cells in living organisms shape how the organism grows. I think what he talks about will be the future of medicine as it allows for an amazing degree of high level control over how animals grow. 1. [VIDEO]"},{"id":42457708,"author":"jstrieb","month":"2024-12","story":"Classical sorting algorithms as a model of morphogenesis (2023)","stance":"rec","why":"Biology results and biologically-inspired AI musings","quote":"includes fascinating biology research results, as well as musings on the future of biologically-inspired artificial intelligence.","text":"The last coauthor listed on this preprint is Michael Levin, who has a lot of other cool work. In particular, this talk of his from NeurIPS 2018 includes fascinating biology research results, as well as musings on the future of biologically-inspired artificial intelligence. [VIDEO] HN discussion about the talk: [LINK]"},{"id":26674382,"author":"flobosg","month":"2021-04","story":"Frog skin cells turned themselves into living machines","stance":"rec","why":"Fascinating Levin lecture","quote":"I find this lecture of his fascinating","text":"Speaking of Michael Levin, I find this lecture of his fascinating: What Bodies Think About: Bioelectric Computation Outside the Nervous System - [VIDEO]"},{"id":25324330,"author":"tent3n3","month":"2020-12","story":"Antioxidants prevent health-promoting effects of physical exercise (2009) [pdf]","stance":"rec","why":"Levin's team manipulating electric fields in animals","quote":"Michael Levin and team show us that manipulation of electric fields in animals","text":"I feel like there’s some underlying relationship here with Godel and infinitely large numbers. We cannot know all states of all particles that ever were or will be, how can we ever concretely analyze human existence when space-time is so infinite? Michael Levin and team show us that manipulation of electric fields in animals ...well see for yourself: [VIDEO] All that physical structure we nurture as concrete truth? Not so sure about that."},{"id":21978164,"author":"tyscorp","month":"2020-01","story":"Technology Will Soon Give Us Precise Control Over Our Brains and Genes?","stance":"rec","why":"Programming bodies at a higher level than genes","quote":"a talk [1] I saw about programming our bodies at a higher level than genes.","text":"There is a talk [1] I saw about programming our bodies at a higher level than genes. Analogous to editing HDL over physically altering an ASIC. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":18837758,"author":"navinsylvester","month":"2019-01","story":"Ask HN: What tech talk does everyone need to see?","stance":"rec","why":"Nominated as a tech talk everyone should see","quote":"What Bodies Think About: Bioelectric Computation Outside the Nervous System talk by Prof. Michael Levin","text":"What Bodies Think About: Bioelectric Computation Outside the Nervous System talk by Prof. Michael Levin - [VIDEO]"},{"id":31002898,"author":"carapace","month":"2022-04","story":"How do people learn to cook a poisonous plant safely? (2019)","stance":"reference","why":"Cells all think, per Levin's lab work","quote":"Check out the recent scientific advances from Michael Levin's lab's work","text":"> As described, the individual plant is not the friend, but rather the spirit of its species. Aye. > I have no way to properly apprehend any of this. Sure you do! You're a living being too, eh? :) You've got all the hardware, you just need to learn the protocols. It's not that hard to learn, the hardest part is \"letting your hair down\" so to speak. Check out the recent scientific advances from Michael Levin's lab's work, e.g. \"What Bodies Think About: Bioelectric Computation Outside the Nervous System\" - NeurIPS 2018 [VIDEO] [LINK] [LINK] In a nutshell, whatever \"thought\" is, the biomolecular machinery that supports it is common to all cells (not just neurons.) In other words, all cellular life \"thinks\". Combine that insight with the \"Law Of Regulatory Models\" from Cybernetics, to wit: \"every good regulator of a system must be (contain) a model of that system.\" and it seems to me that we have a solid basis to talk about \"nature spirits\", eh?"},{"id":30124731,"author":"flobosg","month":"2022-01","story":"Frogs Regrow Missing Limbs in Lab Study","stance":"reference","why":"Listed among further Levin material","quote":"More about Michael Levin","text":"More about Michael Levin: What Bodies Think About: Bioelectric Computation Outside the Nervous System – [VIDEO] Why don’t (today’s) Robots get Cancer? – [LINK] The electrical blueprints that orchestrate life – [LINK] And a tweet about the article – [LINK]"},{"id":27316017,"author":"meh99","month":"2021-05","story":"Humans Could Live up to 150 Years, New Research Suggests","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Transplant medicine is going to seem like archaic nonsense in the future. We’ll just consume the chemical cocktails that normalize us: [VIDEO]"},{"id":23799038,"author":"yehbut99","month":"2020-07","story":"Why general artificial intelligence will not be realized","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Computers can’t heal themselves. Our bodies do that on their own without conscious prompting. Humans evolve the complexity of a computers electron states, not the computers own inherent properties. My use doesn’t force transistors to evolve into better transistors. It has no self regeneration. You don’t see the issue in principle but here you have an article by an expert pointing them out. Perhaps be a better listener? A computer has observable, literal limitations relative to a humans mechanical functionality. You can’t scrape away literal reality to arrive at some reductionist idea of what a consciousness is. Our only known good model for a machine that can create our consciousness is us. It took billions of years of the universe churning at random to accidentally generate us. We have no clue how to replicate that scale. A computer literally lacks a whole lot of literal information that’s embedded in the hardware and software of a person. Watch this and tell me the last time your computer reconfigured it’s literal shape when you altered its electric field properties: [VIDEO] There’s something to “life” we’ll never be able to jam into silicon."},{"id":21537680,"author":"carapace","month":"2019-11","story":"The importance of decentralisation","stance":"reference","why":"Planarian regeneration as example of biological intelligence","quote":"","text":"Yes, exactly. Is the human body centralized or decentralized? The ego could be said to be the central \"pivot\" of the system, but then you have the phenomenon of \"multiple personalities\", and so on. Once you start to think of biology as the study of billion-year-old self-optimizing nanotechnology it makes sense to look to life for models for designing the structure of our own artificial systems. There's the question of what do we want to be like? A starfish? A meta-human? A decentralized system that depends on each or most of its elements to function is a single \"point\" of failure for the whole. (E.g., a relatively small wound can cause death by exsanguination.) On the other hand, consider the radical indignities that the lowly planarian will endure only to placidly regrow a tail or a head or whatever. (See \"What Bodies Think About: Bioelectric Computation Outside the Nervous System\" [VIDEO] [LINK] Which, incidentally, adds a whole layer of complexity and nuance to the concept of intelligent design , since the whole of Nature has now been scientifically shown to think ...) It remains to be seen whether Humans + Internet = Self-Organization or along what lines. (What's the news out of Hong Kong this week? Are the distributed, self-organizing people of HK prevailing against the centralized imperialistic communist state?) Governments everywhere are waking up and smelling the data coffee. The major players already have databases on the bulk of world population and are collecting ("},{"id":20186934,"author":"sinkpoint","month":"2019-06","story":"Woman Grows a Nose on Her Spine After Stem Cell Experiment","stance":"reference","why":"Evidence of extracellular electric gradients guiding tissue","quote":"there's tantalising evidence that it has to do with macro extracellular electric gradient in tissue","text":"Short answer is no one knows, but there's tantalising evidence that it has to do with macro extracellular electric gradient in tissue: [VIDEO]"},{"id":18726302,"author":"talkingtab","month":"2018-12","story":"On Having No Head: Cognition Throughout Biological Systems (2016)","stance":"reference","why":"Linked video on ion channels offering neuron-like functionality","quote":"","text":"If a \"debate\" is a kind of AI/neural network activity this makes sense. The smaller groups could correspond to layers, and one would expect that the level of interchange would be higher in groups of small groups compared to single large group. In this model, a debate is \"thinking\" where the participants act as neurons. A recent thread on HN ( [LINK] ) linked to a video [VIDEO] about ion channels providing similar functionality to neurons, so maybe we humans can create \"brains\" as well."}],"r":[["2yDCox-qKbk",1],["5RKbSi87J5Q",1],["586Zn1ct-QA",1],["dCSIXLIzhzk",1],["vtIzMaLkCaM",1],["CiHN0ZWE5bk",1],["1rCrfQUcXDI",1],["wfm2xCKOCNk",1],["f9EbD6iY9zI",1],["FzRP1qdwPKw",1],["uNjxe8ShM-8",1],["iphcyNWFD10",1],["yhGjCzxJV3E",1],["Y9FZ4igNxNA",1],["rnmcRTnTNC8",1]]},"EZ05e7EMOLM":{"wy":"HN repeatedly calls it the best talk on testing, and many say it changed how they approach tests, mocks and the meaning of a unit.","q":{"x":"This excellent talk completely changed the way I approached testing. Every developer who writes tests needs to watch this now!","i":26531969,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":40012575,"author":"globular-toast","month":"2024-04","story":"Unit Tests Considered Harmful","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Argues the unit is a module and against mocking","quote":"This the best talk I've ever seen about testing (Ian Cooper)","text":"This the best talk I've ever seen about testing (Ian Cooper): [VIDEO] I see he's done a much more recent follow up, which I intend to watch: [LINK] Not sure if it contains new ideas. He says the unit is not \"a class\" but a module. It's up to you to decide what the module does (ie. what level of abstraction it's at). More importantly, he argues against mocking and essentially \"unit testing\" in most cases."},{"id":29945191,"author":"globular-toast","month":"2022-01","story":"Favor real dependencies for unit testing","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Talk that changes how you approach testing","quote":"Well worth listening to this talk. It completely changed the way I did testing.","text":"Ian Cooper talked about this (and more) 5 years ago: [VIDEO] Well worth listening to this talk. It completely changed the way I did testing."},{"id":26531969,"author":"globular-toast","month":"2021-03","story":"CUPID – the back story","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Changed how commenter approaches testing; mocking everything blocks refactoring.","quote":"This excellent talk completely changed the way I approached testing. Every developer who writes tests needs to watch this now!","text":"> Firstly if the dependency isn't doing any io, you can test your code as a whole along with its dependency. No need to mock. This the thing. People have a tendency to overuse mocks. The point of automated testing (whether it's unit tests or something else), is to enable refactoring. That's really the only reason. Code that you don't touch doesn't suddenly change its behaviour one day. In a previous jobs the developers started to go crazy with mocking and it reached a kind of singularity where essentially if a function called anything, that thing was mocked. It definitely tests each function in complete isolation, but what's the point? It makes refactoring impossible, which is the entire point of the tests in the first place! This excellent talk completely changed the way I approached testing. Every developer who writes tests needs to watch this now! [VIDEO]"},{"id":24281805,"author":"VHRanger","month":"2020-08","story":"Is TDD Dead? (2014)","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Best talk on TDD, restoring original principles.","quote":"The best talk on this topic, IMO is Ian Cooper: \"TDD, Where did it all go wrong?","text":"The best talk on this topic, IMO is Ian Cooper: \"TDD, Where did it all go wrong?\" [VIDEO] Couple of notes: - TDD, much like scrum, got corrupted by the \"Agile Consulting Industry\". Sticking to the original principles as laid out by Kent Beck results in fairly sane practices. - When people talk about \"unit tests\", a unit doesn't refer to the common pattern of \"a single class\". A unit is a piece of the software with a clear boundary. It might be a whole microservice, or a chunk of a monolith that is internally consistent. - What triggers writing a test is what matters. Overzealous testers test for each new public method in a class. This leads to testing on implementation details of the actual unit, because most classes are only consumed within a single unit. - Behavior driven testing makes most sense to decide what needs tests. If it's required behavior to the people across the boundary, it needs a test. Otherwise, tests may be extraneous or even harmful. - As such, a good trigger rule is \"one test per desired external behavior of the unit, plus one test per bug fixed\". The test for each bugfix comes from experience -- they delineate tricky parts of your unit and enforce workign code around them."},{"id":18400308,"author":"macca321","month":"2018-11","story":"Signs you are doing continuous testing wrong","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Explains why testing public interfaces beats testing internals.","quote":"please watch Ian Cooper's \"TDD, Where Did It All Go Wrong","text":"'Test Pyramid' considered harmful IMO. It discourages people from writing tests against the public interface of their system , and instead encourages testing against internals. This leads to a test pack that prevents refactoring, and can't be trusted to catch regression errors. This is partly down to the widely confused definition of a 'unit test' (which a lot of people interpret as 'one test per class'). If you haven't seen it, please watch Ian Cooper's \"TDD, Where Did It All Go Wrong\" [VIDEO] Also, I thought continuous testing was having your test pack run all the time while coding, but hey ho."},{"id":40709635,"author":"bunderbunder","month":"2024-06","story":"How to test without mocking","stance":"rec","why":"Covers why throwaway test code is fine if you actually throw it away","quote":"I'm curious if you've seen Ian Cooper's excellent talk","text":"I'm curious if you've seen Ian Cooper's excellent talk, \"TDD, Where Did It All Go Wrong?\" [VIDEO] One of his key points is that throwaway test code is only a problem if you neglect to throw it away."},{"id":35635246,"author":"imran-iq","month":"2023-04","story":"Horrible Code, Clean Performance","stance":"rec","why":"Clarifies what TDD actually is: testing requirements, not implementations","quote":"The problem is most people dont know what TDD is","text":"That's why TDD is about testing requirements and not implementations. The problem is most people dont know what TDD is: [VIDEO]"},{"id":34296131,"author":"elboru","month":"2023-01","story":"Testing Without Mocks: A Pattern Language","stance":"rec","why":"Explains broad in-memory tests instead of mocking every dependency","quote":"I think Ian Cooper does a great work explaining it in this talk","text":"There's a middle tier, broad tests in the sense of not mocking local dependencies but only external (slow) ones. Many developers mock every dependency in a SUT, even if they own the dependency. I like the idea of broad tests that run-in memory (no I/O interaction). I think Ian Cooper does a great work explaining it in this talk (he talks about TDD, but I think the main idea applies even if you write tests after code): [VIDEO]"},{"id":32511377,"author":"imran-iq","month":"2022-08","story":"I have complicated feelings about TDD","stance":"rec","why":"Explains behavior-focused TDD that allows fearless refactoring","quote":"That's the right way to do TDD, see this talk","text":"That's the right way to do TDD, see this talk: [VIDEO] One of the above comments mentions BDD as a close cousin to TDD, but that is wrong as TDD is actually BDD as you should only be testing behaviours, which allow you to \"fearlessly refactor\""},{"id":32514079,"author":"ttctciyf","month":"2022-08","story":"I have complicated feelings about TDD","stance":"rec","why":"Pragmatic advice on applying TDD principles judiciously","quote":"a lot of sage advice about TDD, well informed by years of practice, is in two Ian Cooper NDC talks","text":"IMO, a lot of sage advice about TDD, well informed by years of practice, is in two Ian Cooper NDC talks, his controversial-at-the-time \"TDD, Where Did It All Go Wrong?\"[1] and, seven years later, \"TDD Revisited\"[2]. The blurb from the latter: > In this talk we will look at the key Fallacies of Test-Driven Development, such as 'Developers write Unit Tests', or 'Test After is as effective as Test First' and explore a set of Principles that let us write good unit tests instead. Attendees should be able to take away a clear set of guidelines as to how they should be approaching TDD to be successful. The session is intended to be pragmatic advice on how to follow the ideas outlined in my 2013 talk \"TDD Where Did it All Go Wrong\" The talks focus on reasons to avoid slavish TDD and advocate for the benefits of judiciously applying TDD's originating principles. 1: [VIDEO] 2: [LINK]"},{"id":26532190,"author":"snidane","month":"2021-03","story":"CUPID – the back story","stance":"rec","why":"Explains unit as module, not function, and overfitted tests.","quote":"TDD: where did it all go wrong.","text":"You are doing interface/function level testing and calling it unit testing. That's what industry converged on, that a function/method = a unit, but it apparently used to be that a module meant to be a unit. It can be that both interfaces and modules can be considered a \"bag of functions\". Seems like a lot of confusion about unit testing, mocking and DI stems from this historical shift. I believe that interface/method level testing is too granular and mostly results in overfitted tests. Testing implementation, not behaviour. Which can be useful for some algorithm package for example, but probably not so much applicable to typical business logic code. TDD: where did it all go wrong. [VIDEO]"},{"id":26566737,"author":"globular-toast","month":"2021-03","story":"My Python testing style guide (2017)","stance":"rec","why":"Talk arguing to test real things, not mocks.","quote":"I linked this excellent talk in another thread recently.","text":"Just don't use them unless you have to. The two main reasons to mock are for network requests and because something is too slow. Other than that, test things for real. Do not isolate parts of your code from other parts of your code by using mocks. If your code does side effects on your own machine, like writes to the file system, let it write to a temporary directory. I linked this excellent talk in another thread recently. I'll put it here again: [VIDEO]"},{"id":24349956,"author":"beders","month":"2020-09","story":"Best Practices for Testing in Java (2019)","stance":"rec","why":"Clarifies testing points; warns against overusing mocks.","quote":"Mandatory link to","text":"Mandatory link to [VIDEO] It clarifies a few of the points made in the article. Personally I would add: Don't overuse mocks, only use dependency injection on the module level (Ludicrous use of singleton DI will hinder your design, not help it)"},{"id":19939857,"author":"chriswarbo","month":"2019-05","story":"Ask HN: What overlooked class of tools should a self-taught programmer look into","stance":"rec","why":"Matches commenter's experience with automated testing; pragmatic, empirical testing advice.","quote":"describes my own experiences with automated testing quite well.","text":"I find [VIDEO] describes my own experiences with automated testing quite well. tl;dr: - Focus on \"automated testing\", don't get obsessed with philosophising about \"the true nature of a 'unit'\", or other such dogma. - Be empirical: base your rules on what works; don't base your work on \"the rules\". - The goal of testing is to expose problems in our program: \"test failure\" is a success, because we've found a problem (even if that problem is with the test!). Anything else is secondary (e.g. isolating the location of failures, documenting our API, etc.). Avoiding this goal defeats the point (e.g. choosing to ignore edge cases). - Focus on functionality rather than implementation details, e.g. 'changing a user's email address' rather than 'the setEmail method of the User class'. This improves reliability and makes failures more useful/meaningful (i.e. \"this feature broke\" vs \"this calling convention has changed\"). - Mocking is a crutch: it works-around problems that can usually be avoided entirely during design; it can still be very useful when a design can't be changed (e.g. adding tests to a legacy system). - Testing a real thing is objectively better than testing a fake thing; we should only mock if testing the real thing is unacceptable. - If two components always exist together, pretending that they're independent is a waste of time and complexity. - Having some poor tests is better than having no tests. Tests can be added, removed and improved over time, just like anything e"},{"id":20050675,"author":"benjiweber","month":"2019-05","story":"Is High Quality Software Worth the Cost?","stance":"rec","why":"Shows tests that enable refactoring rather than resisting it.","quote":"Recommend","text":"I see this opinion a lot from people who haven't seen tests and code written by people experienced with TDD. The tests should not end up that coupled to the code. The implementation structure and the test structure end up somewhat different when refactoring every time the tests are green. When listening to the feedback from the tests & code. With the skills to spot the refactoring opportunities. Oftentimes people seem to equate unit testing with a 1:1 correspondence of test and implementation with high coupling between the two. These sort of tests resist refactoring, rather than enabling it. With good tests you can pivot the implementation and tests independently. Recommend [VIDEO] and [LINK] on TDD"},{"id":18450518,"author":"macca321","month":"2018-11","story":"Ask HN: What's the largest amount of bad code you have ever seen work?","stance":"rec","why":"Explains the approach to TDD discussed.","quote":"For those who want to know more about this approach: see Ian Cooper - TDD, Where Did It All Go Wrong","text":"For those who want to know more about this approach: see Ian Cooper - TDD, Where Did It All Go Wrong [VIDEO] I post this all the time, it's like free upvotes :)"},{"id":40942050,"author":"jen20","month":"2024-07","story":"Binance built a 100PB log service with Quickwit","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as a good summary of what a unit test really is","quote":"that's not what a unit test is - see [1] for a good summary of why","text":"It is quite unlikely that a regulator will ask you for proof you have a unit test for anything (also, that's not what a unit test is - see [1] for a good summary of why). It _is_ likely a regulator will ask you to prove that you are developing within the quality assurance framework you have claimed you are, though. Finally though, logs are not an audit trail, and almost no-one can prove their logs are correct with respect to the state of the system at any given time. [1]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":37998044,"author":"VHRanger","month":"2023-10","story":"SumatraPDF Reader","stance":"reference","why":"Describes the rule of testing top-level behaviors","quote":"There's a good rule of testing top level behaviors described in this talk","text":"The problem with tests is the TDD dogma, which wastes time and makes code harder to change (because even reasonable changes break a bunch of tests). There's a good rule of testing top level behaviors described in this talk [1] For code reviews, it's about knowledge handoff. No one disputes you can write great code alone. The problem is that singular geniuses writing functional but unmaintainable code only they understand and then getting hit by a bus or changing jobs is a real issue. 1. [VIDEO]"},{"id":36092369,"author":"still_grokking","month":"2023-05","story":"Lessons from Creating a VSCode Extension with GPT-4","stance":"reference","why":"Cited for the idea that implementation-detail tests have negative value","quote":"If AI can write your tests, you're testing likely implementation details.","text":"If AI can write your tests, you're testing likely implementation details. This has \"negative value\"! [VIDEO] But an AI can't write higher level tests as it would need to understand large chunks of code (sometimes whole systems), which it can't."},{"id":33742571,"author":"sph","month":"2022-11","story":"Ask HN: Why the Linux Kernel doesn't have unit tests?","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as backing for skepticism of unit-test dogma","quote":"TDD has some good ideas but for a while it had turned in a religion.","text":"To add to all the excellent answers in this thread: unit tests are massively overrated. It is good to know that your base64 encoding function is tested for all corner cases, but integration and behaviour tests for the external interface/API are more important than exercising an internal implementation detail. What is the external interface of the kernel? What is its surface area? A kernel is so central and massive the only way to test its complete contract with the user (space) is... just to run stuff and see if it breaks. TDD has some good ideas but for a while it had turned in a religion. While tests are great to have, a good and underrated integration testing system is just for someone to run your software. If no one complains, either no one is using it, or the software is doing its work. Do you really need tests for the read(2) syscall when Linux is running on a billion devices, and that syscall is called some 10^12 times per second globally? [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["qS34oV-jv_A",1],["PbmJkMhmrVI",1],["otAcmD6XEEE",1],["Tb823aqgX_0",1],["SxdOUGdseq4",1],["gSKTfG1GXYQ",1],["O61WihMRdjM",1],["pkCLMl0e_0k",1],["8Ab3ArE8W3s",1],["sj8Sg8qnjOg",1],["MBnnXbOM5S4",1],["RijGNytjbOI",1]]},"LKtk3HCgTa8":{"wy":"It is one of HN's most frequently recommended programming talks, cited whenever people debate complexity, simplicity and abstraction, and valued well beyond Clojure.","q":{"x":"I highly recommend Rich Hickey's \"Simple Made Easy\" talk as a way to think about software complexity.","i":31683328,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":46416354,"author":"yooogurt","month":"2025-12","story":"Rich Hickey: Thanks AI","stance":"strong_rec","why":"","quote":"you're fortunate to have the opportunity to watch \"Simple Made Easy\" for the first time","text":"If you haven't heard of Rich Hickey, then you're fortunate to have the opportunity to watch \"Simple Made Easy\" for the first time: [VIDEO]"},{"id":38288916,"author":"funcDropShadow","month":"2023-11","story":"Push ifs up and fors down","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Explains simple versus easy, applicable beyond Clojure.","quote":"there is a great talk of Rich Hickey [0], that explains that distinction","text":"This doesn't sound like a simple solution from your fellow devs. It appears to have been an easy solution. If the distinction isn't familiar to you, there is a great talk of Rich Hickey [0], that explains that distinction and some more. The talk is definitely not only applicable to Clojure. YAGNI is a great slogan, but must not be a dogma. If you already know, that you are going to need custom rules. Then prepare for it. But, if --- for example --- the current rules are almost trivial, and you don't know yet, which rule engine will be the right fit later on. Then it might be a good idea to postpone the decision about the rule engine, until you know more. In the meantime a hard-coded solution could be enough. [0]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":34972258,"author":"xiphias2","month":"2023-02","story":"Do the Simplest Thing That Can Possibly Work (2004)","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Explains complexity as entwining, relevant to layering and abstraction choices.","quote":"Actually Rick Hickey's talk ( ) is amazing","text":"Actually Rick Hickey's talk ( [VIDEO] ) is amazing because he talks about your case by going back to the original meaining of words: ,,Complex comes from the Latin complecti, which means “to entwine around, to embrace'' Simplicity requires layering, so in your examples the main requirement for simplicity is about how intertwined your hobby project is with the transciever code or ethernet code. As long as the abstraction layer works well for you without getting too much into the details of the implementation, it's a simple solution. Of course it's not a clear answer whether you should do things yourself or use a third-party, but if the third-party works perfectly for use case without significant tradeoff in your system, of course it's better to use it."},{"id":31683328,"author":"loudmax","month":"2022-06","story":"The collapse of complex software","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Useful framework for understanding the nature of software complexity.","quote":"I highly recommend Rich Hickey's \"Simple Made Easy\" talk as a way to think about software complexity.","text":"I highly recommend Rich Hickey's \"Simple Made Easy\" talk as a way to think about software complexity. We all know that complex systems are hard to work with, but I've found his presentation very useful in understanding the nature of complexity. Understanding complexity helps being able to avoid or minimize it. For example, easy is not the same as simple, and simple changes to one part of a system can introduce great complexity at the macro level. [VIDEO]"},{"id":29035199,"author":"elamje","month":"2021-10","story":"Ask HN: Programming talks that made something click for you?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Hickey's talk distinguishing simple from easy in software.","quote":"strongest programming speaker I've found to date","text":"Simple Made Easy ( [VIDEO] ) The Language of the System ( [LINK] ) The Value of Values ( [LINK] ) All by Rich Hickey (Clojure creator) - strongest programming speaker I've found to date."},{"id":48160234,"author":"ale","month":"2026-05","story":"My Thoughts on Bun's Rust Rewrite","stance":"rec","why":"Hickey classic on bugs passing all tests.","quote":"reminds me of a Rick Hickey classic","text":"Great point about testing. The knee jerk reaction to defend this enormous rewrite because it passes a test suite reminds me of a Rick Hickey classic: what’s true of every bug found in the field? It passed all the tests. [VIDEO]"},{"id":47080169,"author":"delaminator","month":"2026-02","story":"Ask HN: In the Age of AI, How Do I Grow as a Software Engineer?","stance":"rec","why":"Master advice on architecture and explicit rules.","quote":"Study the craft and listen to the masters","text":"> Claude can make decisions about architecture but they are often not very good decisions, and sometimes absolutely terrible I have to kick it back onto the right track, but as you sort of point out, I got that experience from making my own terrible decisions and finding out the hard way the blind alley I was walking up. Second system, inner platform, too general, planning for scenarios that never happen, stuff that doesn't scale - I've made them all. I think I learned as much from reading as doing though, I could later put names on the mistakes I made. So, for what it's worth - study the meta. Read about architectural theory, system design Study the craft and listen to the masters - [VIDEO] And when you talk to Claude say \"follow rich hickeys advice about explicit rules\" and it knows what you mean more than you do !"},{"id":37861977,"author":"liveoneggs","month":"2023-10","story":"Can't Be Fucked: Underrated Cause of Tech Debt","stance":"rec","why":"","quote":"If you've never watched Simple Made Easy I really recommend it","text":"take it up with Rich Hickey I suppose :) If you've never watched Simple Made Easy I really recommend it: [VIDEO]"},{"id":31882498,"author":"frogulis","month":"2022-06","story":"Revisiting the principles of data-oriented programming","stance":"rec","why":"Better explanation of the definition of simple.","quote":"provide a better explanation of these ideas IMO","text":"Rich Hickey's talks \"Simple Made Easy\" [1] and \"Effective Programs\" [2] provide a better explanation of these ideas IMO. The specific definition of \"simple\" is pretty crucial. [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK]"},{"id":41675291,"author":"gavinhoward","month":"2024-09","story":"Rewriting Rust: A Response","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"> I'm sadly not very well versed with this alternative to OOP. But something tells me that a more \"basic\" and less dynamic scheme like that is probably best for performance reasons, since you'd either need a Julia-like JIT or a granular \"dynamism knob\" like [LINK] to truly amortize the cost of CLOS-like dispatch and polymorphism; though magic can be done, as the \"Fast generic dispatch for Common Lisp\" paper shows. You know, for someone who isn't \"well versed\" with it, you certainly nailed exactly why I chose it. Also, simplicity. The \"as easy as Python\" is only possible if I remove complecting [1] things like inheritance. > Huh! If this includes types deduced by compiler inference and not just user declarations (since Yao seems gradually typed, to my eyes), that'd be pretty impressive. It does, actually. But Yao is not gradually typed, unfortunately. Too complecting. [1]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":37246138,"author":"Pet_Ant","month":"2023-08","story":"No One Wants Simplicity","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"I believe GP is using it in the literal etymologically correct way in the vein of Rich Hickey: [VIDEO] Pedantically, Common Lisp being a Lisp-2 vs a Lisp-1 arguably makes it a duplex vs a simplex…"},{"id":33097689,"author":"zackmorris","month":"2022-10","story":"Show HN: Async UI: A Rust UI Library Where Everything is a Future","stance":"reference","why":"Cited for easy-versus-simple argument against async.","quote":"","text":"I second this, as personally I believe that async is an anti-pattern. It's an unfortunate result of the programming world choosing easy over simple so it could recruit vast armies of inexperienced programmers for profit: \"Simple Made Easy\" by Rich Hickey: [VIDEO] I'm old school, I want the runtime to do as much work for me as possible so I don't have to. Basically that looks like the runtime providing things like process isolation and concurrency, even if the underlying hardware can't do that. Especially if we're using a high-level scripting language like Javascript anyway. Rust I could maybe see at least providing access to async functionality, but I'd vote specifically against that footgun and go with lightweight threads and message passing (how Go does it) or scatter-gather arrays (there may be a better term for this) with the compiler detecting side effects and auto-parallizing everything else like loops. The simplest way to facilitate that is to use immutable data as much as possible, passed via copy-on-write (the Unix way). The idea of async being scattered around operating systems and kernels and such is anathema to my psyche. Code smells setting off my spidey sense everywhere I look. To the point where if the world goes that route, I just don't think we'll have determinism anymore. That makes me want to get out of programming. Note that I feel the same dismay about stuff like the DSP approach used by video cards, where the developer has to manually manage vertex buffe"},{"id":31504417,"author":"almostdeadguy","month":"2022-05","story":"Minimalism in programming language design","stance":"reference","why":"Often-cited talk attempting an objective notion of simplicity.","quote":"The oft-cited Rich Hickey talk [0] I think gets _somewhat_ closer to vindicating the concept of simplicity","text":"This conversation almost always strikes me as a pointless exercise. We can't even agree on what it means to be \"simple\" or precisely define what it means to have \"less features\" in a programming language (or perhaps we can by saying complexity is anything more than what is provided by machine language, but we get no closer to an understanding of how much of this is necessary or when this is desirable). A programming language is an abstraction over machines and inevitably all languages introduce features to unburden the programmer from extraneous detail, toil, or precarity involved in orchestrating the operation of that machine. The author is correct that many \"simple\" languages like Brainfuck are actually very complex to use, but this strikes at the inherent feebleness of the premise that we can clearly define what we mean by a \"simple\" language (or at least any clarity on when the complexity tax is worthwhile or not worthwhile). Many programming language features are conditioned on a trade-off that having to think about X less in exchange for requiring knowledge of Y is a net benefit to the programmer. If we take the idea that simplicity means \"less features\" at face value, we throw out the notion of any high level language having the potential to be simple. The oft-cited Rich Hickey talk [0] I think gets _somewhat_ closer to vindicating the concept of simplicity being something we can talk about more objectively, but I'm still not convinced the concept is worth redeeming. I"},{"id":31551614,"author":"dgb23","month":"2022-05","story":"Why does this code execute more slowly after strength-reducing multiplications?","stance":"reference","why":"Source of definitions of simple versus complex (complected).","quote":"I'm taking the definition of simple and complex (or complected) from this talk[0].","text":"If we ignore the details, then this is a perfect example of how simpler code should be preferred _by default_ over complex code. Both for performance and reasoning. In this case and often elsewhere, these are related things. I'm taking the definition of simple and complex (or complected) from this talk[0]. In short: Simple means individual pieces are standing on their own, not necessarily easy or minimal. Complex means individual pieces are braided together into a whole. The first code is simpler than the second. Just have a look at the two solutions and you see what I mean: The individual instructions in the first stand on their own and clearly declare what they mean as well. The second example is more complex, because each line/subexpression cannot be understood in isolation, there is an implicit coupling that requires the programmer _and_ the machine code interpreter to understand the whole thing for it to make sense. The CPU apparently fails at that and cannot optimize the machine instructions into more efficient microcode. The example illustrates performance benefits of simpler code, but there are others too: For example a type checker might be able to infer things from simpler code, but not from complex code. Again, complexity is about coupling, which can for example arise from making assumptions about things that are out of bounds or logic that happens in some other part of your program, which _this_ part relies on. Things like these can either be outright rejected by "},{"id":29738165,"author":"zackmorris","month":"2021-12","story":"Features of a dream programming language","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Oh I'm looking at the development side, being able to iterate quickly and having full visibility of the code. This fits in with stuff like aspect-oriented-programming (AOP) so that things like execution traces can be generated at runtime without modifying code. I mostly work with the shell and scripting languages. I'm racing as fast as I can all day to get anything at all to work, then building on that to get to a solution. Most of the time, I can't really edit the code I'm working with and still maintain velocity, so can't do any kind of manual type annotation. Also I question if there is really any merit to hiding the type of a variable at runtime. It feels like a power trip by the language, a form of security theater. I also view the \"private\" and \"final\" keywords with similar skepticism. If I can call typeof on an element in my own struct/class, then I get frustrated when I can't do that with anonymous data from a framework or in the REPL. It also frustrates me when I can't access the call stack or get information about the calling function. If there's a way to do it in C++ (no matter how ugly), then I must be able to do it in whatever language I'm using or my psyche views it as stifling and I lose motivation. I guess I find the obsession with types today to be a form of pedantry. So much concern for only accepting a narrow range of shapes, but then holding that info close to the chest. It should be the opposite. That's also the reason why I've gotten away from object-ori"},{"id":29256448,"author":"tacotacotaco","month":"2021-11","story":"Etsy’s Journey to TypeScript","stance":"reference","why":"Cited for easy not equalling quality.","quote":"","text":"No, my argument is that TS makes writing code easier and that easier does not equal to quality [0]. I have been writing code for more than 20 years. Personally, TS doesn't impress me. This is not meant as a criticism. Personally, I have found functional programming to be revelatory. I am shocked that is as been around as long as it has and that our schools don't teach it (or just mention it) and the majority of people in our industry as resistant to it. \"No matter what language you work in, programming in a functional style provides benefits. You should do it whenever it is convenient, and you should think hard about the decision when it isn’t convenient.\" -- John Carmack [1] [0] \"Simple Made Easy\" Rich Hickey [VIDEO] [1] \"John Carmack on Functional Programming in C++\" [LINK]"},{"id":29331742,"author":"zackmorris","month":"2021-11","story":"Overengineering can kill a product","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Engineering within the wrong paradigm can kill your product. I see far more problems on a day-to-day basis with patterns and anti-patterns taken too far. For example, I never use the factory pattern, because it leads one down the Java road where everything ends up an object with mutable state. Which isn't scalable over some metric (like a million lines of code) because a human brain can't trace execution, even with a debugger. A far better pattern generally is to take a functional approach of accepting data, swizzling it, and returning the resulting data without mutability or side effects. Another really bad pattern is when execution suspends and resumes somewhere else (goto hell). Any project which uses queuing, eventual consistency, promises, nonblocking streams, even basic notifications or things as complex as monads will encounter this nondeterminism and inability to statically analyze code. Note that pretty much all web development suffers from some aspect of this due to its async and multi-signaling nature. So what I do now, which I don't see much these days, is solve problems abstractly in a spreadsheet (pure functional programming), in the shell (the Actor model) or as a flowchart (declarative and data-driven design), and then translate that to whatever crappy language/framework I have to use for the project. I find that today, roughly 90% of developer effort goes to discovery, refactoring and testing of ill-conceived code. Only 10% is \"actual work\" and that's probabl"}],"r":[["tD5NrevFtbU",1],["skos4B5x7qE",1],["lJ3NC-R3gSI",1],["7EmboKQH8lM",1],["d0vY0CKYhPY",1],["SWEts0rlezA",1],["-6BsiVyC1kM",1],["ROor6_NGIWU",1],["2V1FtfBDsLU",1],["Uj6skZIxPuI",1]]},"vTMF6xEiAaY":{"wy":"Cited heavily in Roblox threads as a damning investigation into child labor, exploitation, and weak moderation, and suggested viewing for parents.","q":{"x":"Watch both People Make Games video about Roblox and try to argue that the company should not deserve all the public controversy it is getting.","i":32774393,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":32774393,"author":"philistine","month":"2022-09","story":"It seems that the good times for online Minecraft play are over","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Shows Roblox stalling moderation to protect profit.","quote":"Watch both People Make Games video about Roblox and try to argue that the company should not deserve all the public controversy it is getting.","text":"Watch both People Make Games video about Roblox and try to argue that the company should not deserve all the public controversy it is getting. They are actively stalling any moderation and protection because their profit margin would be severely diminished. Children's safety versus money, the choice is oh so simple. [LINK] [VIDEO]"},{"id":39038417,"author":"follower","month":"2024-01","story":"Ask HN: 9-yo son wants to build a game, I'm lost. What can I do?","stance":"rec","why":"Reports parents should watch before letting kids into Roblox development.","quote":"has a couple of reports that I'd suggest parents consider watching first before going down that road","text":"The YouTube channel \"People Make Games\" has a couple of reports that I'd suggest parents consider watching first before going down that road: * \"Investigation: How Roblox Is Exploiting Young Game Developers\": [LINK] * \"Roblox Pressured Us to Delete Our Video. So We Dug Deeper.\": [VIDEO]"},{"id":35311028,"author":"shagie","month":"2023-03","story":"Utah is first US state to limit teen social media access","stance":"rec","why":"Investigations by a respected channel.","quote":"People Make Games is one of the YouTube channels that I respect in this domain...","text":"People Make Games is one of the YouTube channels that I respect in this domain... Investigation: How Roblox Is Exploiting Young Game Developers - [LINK] Roblox Pressured Us to Delete Our Video. So We Dug Deeper. - [VIDEO]"},{"id":33117561,"author":"pull_my_finger","month":"2022-10","story":"Roblox sued for allegedly enabling young girl’s sexual, financial exploitation","stance":"rec","why":"Related investigation into Roblox exploitation.","quote":"Some interesting related investigation (I think they mention this girl in the second video)","text":"Some interesting related investigation (I think they mention this girl in the second video) related to roblox is anyone is interested. I had never used it, but after seeing these I don't think I would ever use it or encourage anyone to use it. [LINK] [VIDEO]"},{"id":32016410,"author":"fabianhjr","month":"2022-07","story":"Problems at Roblox","stance":"rec","why":"Investigation into Roblox child labor and monopolistic aims.","quote":"There are two video investigations from People Make Games into economic aspects of Roblox","text":"There are two video investigations from People Make Games into economic aspects of Roblox in particular child labor/exploitation and monopolistic aspirations via platform capitalism. 1. Investigation: How Roblox Is Exploiting Young Game Developers ( [LINK] ) 2. Roblox Pressured Us to Delete Our Video. So We Dug Deeper. ( [VIDEO] )"},{"id":30856915,"author":"AlexandrB","month":"2022-03","story":"Facebook paid Republican strategy firm to malign TikTok","stance":"rec","why":"Damning look at Roblox moderation and user exploitation.","quote":"a pretty damning video about Roblox's lack of moderation and exploitation of its users","text":"Like most adults, most of what I know about Roblox comes secondhand from the media. Recently I saw[1] a pretty damning video about Roblox's lack of moderation and exploitation of its users. My basic perception at this point is that TikTok, Facebook, and Roblox all deserve each other. Hopefully they waste their money attacking each other until they mutually self-destruct. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":30015783,"author":"DerArzt","month":"2022-01","story":"Roblox October Outage Postmortem","stance":"rec","why":"Followup showing more of Roblox's problems.","quote":"which also has a followup[2] that show even more of the issue at hand","text":"To add, there is a nice documentary here[1] which also has a followup[2] that show even more of the issue at hand. Kids making games and only getting 24.5% of the profit is one thing, but everything else that Roblox does is much worse. [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO]"},{"id":30086917,"author":"gorjusborg","month":"2022-01","story":"The trouble with Roblox, the video game empire built on child labour","stance":"rec","why":"Roblox's responsibility gap around exploitation of child developers.","quote":"Essentially, Roblox has made the decision that they are only responsible for interactions that happen on their platform","text":"The 'People Make Games' videos discuss this: [VIDEO] Essentially, Roblox has made the decision that they are only responsible for interactions that happen on their platform. Parents may be letting their children develop games thinking that it's a cute hobby. Roblox knows that there are game companies wooing child devs and doing shady stuff that can be potentially harmful to them. They are not doing anything to stop the practices because they aren't technically on their platform . But the whole interaction is around making games for their platform. So they may potentially not be legally be at fault, but it does seem that they are making a lot of money, albeit indirectly, from turning a blind eye to companies extracting labor from young people, and in some cases abusing them. As a parent who is a developer and has kids who love Roblox, I think it's important that the situation be discussed and not swept under the rug. I like the idea of being able to develop and extend and open world like Roblox. I also think that when you market to kids, you have to meet a higher bar of responsibility in protecting them."},{"id":29669631,"author":"josephg","month":"2021-12","story":"Twitter’s founder admits that shutting down the API was “worst thing we did”","stance":"rec","why":"Critique of Roblox and its response to questions.","quote":"I saw an excellent critique of Roblox the other day","text":"> Tech sufferers greatly from a \"God Complex\" syndrome, and it's exactly what will prevent it from getting better. I saw an excellent critique of Roblox the other day, which covered many aspects of their business and different kinds of abuse which are happening in and around their game. The most bizarre part was that they sent some questions to the company - and the company responded by saying they “clearly weren’t being objective” and essentially gaslighting the people making the video. I think the people in charge of Roblox seriously don’t see themselves as holding any sort of responsibility to the broader society that pays their bills. It’s like someone watched The Corporation and thought - “oh companies can get away with sociopathic behaviour? I want in!”. Doing harm to the world in the name of your bottom line doesn’t make you clever. It makes you the villain. (Video in question: [VIDEO] )"},{"id":36044151,"author":"jerf","month":"2023-05","story":"Nintendo sued for “immoral” Mario Kart lootboxes","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"the People Make Games investigations into them, for instance.","text":"I don't like that Nintendo does this. I don't care who does it, it's wrong. That said, of all the people doing this, it seems to me Nintendo is among the worst targets to go after. They were, to my eye, dragged into this almost against their will, and if anything they've been backing off from it. It certainly hasn't wormed its way in to their mainline games the way it is well into worming its way in to almost every other major publisher out there's games. Again, I don't say this to defend them. It's wrong for everyone. What I am saying though is that this is not a very target rich environment, and there are target-rich environments elsewhere. There are companies that seem to be entirely based not only on exploitative mechanics, but exploitative mechanics specifically targeted at children, like Roblox. [LINK] and [VIDEO] , the People Make Games investigations into them, for instance. Although there is an interesting strategy for Nintendo to pursue here where they get sued, and then basically deliberately construct a precedent-setting loss to knee-cap their competition on this front. There's a lot of competition for Nintendo out there surviving on the basis of these cheap drug hits of monetary injection whose ability to develop good games on their own merits are clearly well into the process of atrophy, and Nintendo would probably stand to benefit even if they have to pay some millions of dollars in settlement payouts to impose those restrictions on them. If those mechanics wer"},{"id":31975399,"author":"AndrewOMartin","month":"2022-07","story":"Roblox tried to sue me for $1.6M [video]","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"Here's more than you wanted to know","text":"Here's more than you wanted to know: - People Make Games: Investigation: How Roblox Is Exploiting Young Game Developers. [LINK] - People Make Games: Roblox Pressured Us to Delete Our Video. So We Dug Deeper. [VIDEO] I think you're right that it's something that can be used in good or bad ways, and the thrust of the argument is that there aren't the kind of safeguards you'd hope for to stop kids falling to one of the bad ways. Like how we keep kids out of Casinos (or used to at least, now we just ship casinos to kids in their FIFA games)."},{"id":31777617,"author":"aloisdg","month":"2022-06","story":"Game Design Mimetics (Or, What Happened to Game Design?)","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Investigation: How Roblox Is Exploiting Young Game Developers [LINK] Roblox Pressured Us to Delete Our Video. So We Dug Deeper. [VIDEO]"},{"id":30024536,"author":"tytrdev","month":"2022-01","story":"Microsoft to Acquire Activision Blizzard","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"Tangentially relevant due to Tencent involvement","text":"I was just getting at the illusion of choice. At the end of the day it's all owned by the same handful of small groups. Found out yesterday that Blizzard apparently owns King. Tencent owns a cut of Activision Blizzard, as well as a slice of basically every gaming company. [LINK] Note that I'm not even criticizing or otherwise knocking these business practices, I'm simply making some observations. My use of the term bullshit was particularly to describe the illusion of choice. Not that it's anything new. [LINK] I think the world would be just fine without fortnite, but I will say unreal engine is pretty nice to have. Probably just a matter of time until Microsoft owns unreal engine as well. Tangentially relevant due to Tencent involvement: [LINK] [VIDEO]"},{"id":29680617,"author":"cupcake-unicorn","month":"2021-12","story":"Be curious, not judgmental","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"I recently saw this video on YouTube about some of the shadiness going on with Roblox","text":"It's a tough issue, for sure, because the playing field is just so unequal. To even be able to approach these issues in a more equal way you'd probably have to start a lawsuit and go through a process of discovery because the corporation is bound by all these bizarre legal, financial, and political motives that don't apply when people are interacting with each other. This leads to corporations responding to such requests in a way that's inherently against the interests of the public answering these questions. Without a system in place that further aligns and incentivizes companies interests with their users the only way you can really approach this in a more equal manner is something like a lawsuit. Many times the problem is incredibly complex and while some people, even C level people may be all for providing this transparency it breaks down at scale and the company is at the end of the day beholden to its own interests and perpetuating itself. I recently saw this video on YouTube about some of the shadiness going on with Roblox: [VIDEO] The official answers they are giving is basically meaningless legalese and that's becoming more and more common. As I said before since Roblox isn't entering into this conversation in good faith the only way to force that conversation would be legal actions. Any kinds of laws further aligning the needs of clients/the general populace against the needs of big corporations/political elite have a really hard time passing because the system is s"},{"id":47329276,"author":"amiga386","month":"2026-03","story":"Roblox Is Minting Teen Millionaires","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"Roblox turns a blind eye to child exploitation","text":"Roblox turns a blind eye to child exploitation (whether being creeped on by adults, or being exploited by teens/adults to make games) and makes a fortune out of it. If it weren't online, it'd be illegal and people would be in jail. [LINK] [VIDEO]"},{"id":45987242,"author":"dbg31415","month":"2025-11","story":"Roblox Requires Age Checks for Communication, Ushering in New Safety Standard","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"Remember, Roblox is toxic and exploits young people.","text":"Remember, Roblox is toxic and exploits young people. - Roblox Isn't a Game | Psychology Today // [LINK] - Investigation: How Roblox Is Exploiting Young Game Developers - YouTube // [LINK] - Roblox Pressured Us to Delete Our Video. So We Dug Deeper. - YouTube // [VIDEO] Roblox is Darker Than You Think (ft. Ruben Sim) - YouTube // [LINK]"},{"id":46013961,"author":"sitkack","month":"2025-11","story":"Roblox CEO interview about child safety didn't go well","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"dystopian hellscape that is the whole game publishing platform","text":"The article doesn't even directly mention Schlep [LINK] whom Roblox kicked off the platform for catching pedophiles. Roblox Is Threatening to Sue Me For Protecting Kids (Schlep) [LINK] Roblox, Take a Seat (ft: Chris Hansen) [LINK] #freeschlep It was awesome seeing the kids on Roblox revolt against Baszucki for doubling down on supporting pedophiles. The leadership team at Roblox is so entirely tone deaf, if I were the the board, I'd remove them all. I watched Baszucki and This is in addition to the money laundering and dystopian hellscape that is the whole game publishing platform that has been documented here on hn. Investigation: How Roblox Is Exploiting Young Game Developers [LINK] Roblox Pressured Us to Delete Our Video. So We Dug Deeper. [VIDEO] My own child says that no parent should allow their child on Roblox. Smart kid."},{"id":40229369,"author":"ethanholt1","month":"2024-05","story":"Roblox players to start seeing video ads in its virtual realms","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"lackluster moderation allowing kids to be harassed or worse","text":"Roblox has been on a constant downward trend. They’ve been doing some pretty awful stuff, including exploitation of children developers [0], unregulated market which encourages kids to gamble, lackluster moderation allowing kids to be harassed or worse, [both 1] and allowing manipulative and predatory games on their site [2]. [0] [LINK] [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK]"},{"id":35807041,"author":"felixg3","month":"2023-05","story":"Unity to lay off 8% of its workforce","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"most of the game development is done by sub-subcontracted child labour [2]","text":"Cool, sell your soul to a corporation that literally exploits minors [1][3]. Of course they can afford to pay Silicon Valley salaries, given that most of the game development is done by sub-subcontracted child labour [2]. [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO] [3] [LINK]"},{"id":30945452,"author":"intsunny","month":"2022-04","story":"Meta plans ‘Zuck bucks’ virtual coins for Facebook and Instagram users","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"And let us not forget how incredibly manipulative and insidious Roblox is","text":"According to this The Verge [0] article: “Instead, Meta is leaning towards introducing in-app tokens that would be centrally controlled by the company, similar to those used in gaming apps such as the Robux currency in popular children’s game Roblox,” according to the FT. Roblox has built a huge business selling Robux, and Meta could try to emulate some of that success on its own platforms.\" And let us not forget how incredibly manipulative and insidious Roblox is: \"Roblox Pressured Us to Delete Our Video. So We Dug Deeper.\" [VIDEO] [0] [LINK] edit: typo"}],"r":[["_gXlauRB1EQ",4],["4PaWFYm0kEw",1],["CbIZU8cQWXc",1],["yiw6_JakZFc",1],["9RUHJhskW00",1],["jPhJbKBuNnA",1],["klaJqofCsu4",1],["LxgMdjyw8uw",1],["rvskMHn0sqQ",1],["TwRjO3kHxU4",1],["XVmsfL5LG90",1],["HeQX2HjkcNo",1],["wO2lWmgEK1Y",1],["hyCIpKAIFyo",1],["MFEHFsO-XSI",1],["tafGL02EUOA",1],["OHPkdMGI6D4",1],["fD-R35DSSZY",1],["1YFrISfN7jo",1],["bLk1cjZ4ll0",1]]},"wlR5gYd6um0":{"wy":"Repeatedly called the best video introduction to Vim's underlying model.","q":{"x":"In video form, the best introduction I have seen is Chris Toomey’s","i":13044549,"y":"2016"},"m":[{"id":48313220,"author":"dredmorbius","month":"2026-05","story":"It's time to talk about my writerdeck","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Excellent video that taught much after decades of use.","quote":"Mastering the Vim Language\" is an excellent video","text":"My first experience, on a BSD machine at about the same time, was similarly traumatic. I've since learnt the tool. Its ubiquity (particularly over pretty much all of the other ones I'd listed, at least on the machines I had ready access to) cemented that. I did like EVE when I'd used it, but have had little access to VAXen since. And was proficient for a time on Emacs (until a subsequent gig's director insisted that if it didn't come installed software wouldn't be added to our Unix server). My WP-fu fell with the end of DOS, though I did hang on for quite some time, up to the late 1990s / early aughts. CUA has its benefits, but it is only one of multiple competing keyboard standards. Everything is convention. Oddly: I'm vi/vim in the editor, but readline on the shell. No, I don't set vimkeys bindings in Bash. But I do drop to bash as my shell editor. Also: for those stumbling across this thread later, \"Mastering the Vim Language\" is an excellent video I'd come across in my third decade of using the editor, and learned much from. It's now 11 years old itself: < [VIDEO] > Vim may have a long learning curve, but it also has a steep payoff function."},{"id":19416757,"author":"dredmorbius","month":"2019-03","story":"Vim Anti-Patterns (2012)","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Explains why Vim idioms exist and the Vim language.","quote":"Understanding vim's model, what Chris Toomey of Thoughtbot calls \"the Vim Language\", helps incredibly","text":"Disclaimer: My vim knowledge has many gaps. Understanding vim's model, what Chris Toomey of Thoughtbot calls \"the Vim Language\", helps incredibly: [VIDEO] I've picked up numerous tips watching this and other of Thoughtbot's excellent video series. More importantly, though, is that I've learned why specific vim idioms and motifs exist. They've lead me to rethink my own usage patterns, sometimes changing them, sometimes holding them just a bit more tightly. (Say, my practice of doing global regex pattern-replacement edits rather than repeated vim edits or macros, generally.) But for a tool I use for coding, data cleaning, systems administration, email, writing, document preparation, and more, it pays to be on the lookout for more effective, faster, reliable, and/or repeatable methods. And no, vim's not the only editor I have, or do, use. It's not even the first Unix editor I'd used. There's been DOS EDIT, EDLIN, MacWrite, WP51, WordStar (me and GRR!), EDT, EVE, the ISPF editor, emacs, pico, ae, MS Word, ... you get the idea. But back in the 1980s, I first encountered (and balked noisily at, for a few weeks), BSD vi. And the basic muscle memory acquired, after a brief period of pain, has almost certainly been my best technical investment after learning to touch-type itself. Most importantly becaue both have been remarkably durable skills. No, vim is not vi, but it builds on its core . And over 30 years later, I find it useful both to see what new capabilities have been added, an"},{"id":13044549,"author":"begemotz","month":"2016-11","story":"How to start using VIM?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Best video introduction to Vim.","quote":"In video form, the best introduction I have seen is Chris Toomey’s","text":"In video form, the best introduction I have seen is Chris Toomey’s ‘Mastering the Vim Language’: [VIDEO] But here is something I wrote while I was learning how to migrate to Vim.. [LINK]"},{"id":44125333,"author":"jsilence","month":"2025-05","story":"Edamagit: Magit for VSCode","stance":"rec","why":"Shows vim keybindings as an editing language.","quote":"finally found joy in vim [0]","text":"Have been using Spacemacs and Doom Emacs some time ago, but turned off evil mode, bc I was not used to vim style editing. Lately I understood that vim key bindings is actually an editing language and finally found joy in vim [0]. Surely going to return to Doom-Emacs with evil mode on. Maybe switching to evil god mode later. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":33211794,"author":"tantony","month":"2022-10","story":"Ask HN: How to become good at Emacs/Vim?","stance":"rec","why":"Listed among resources that helped learn Vim.","quote":"Some resources that helped","text":"I dabbled with neovim on and off for a while - kind of following the same logic as you, wanting to be like many people I saw on Youtube. It never really worked since I was really comfortable with my existing workflow - vscode + iterm and \"swiping\" between screens on macOS. I was super-fast with the Macbook trackpad with its sensitivity turned up to the max. It finally clicked when I started a job where I had to use a windows laptop with a linux VM as my primary computer. I found that using vscode either within the VM or over \"Remote-SSH\" from windows was severely cramping my productivity. So I switched to NeoVim+Tmux cold turkey (SSHing into VM using \"Windows Terminal\"). I set up some shortcuts that made sense for me somewhat from vscode (e.g. Alt+<number> for switching \"tabs\", alt+hjkl or alt+arrow keys to switch between tmux panes etc.). Within a couple of weeks I was back to my old level of productivity. My usual setup has neovim on the left and a terminal in the right pane. Adding more panes vertically or horizontally is super easy as well. This may not be the most optimal setup, and there are still a few pain points from time to time - particularly with the LSP/\"intellisense\" stuff. But it works for me. Oh and when I am coding on my MacBook Pro at home, I still prefer to use vscode + iTerm. Some resources that helped: \"Practical Vim\" by Drew Neil. I didn't read it cover to cover but I have scanned through it enough to pick up the important stuff. [LINK] [LINK] [VIDEO]"},{"id":23703348,"author":"urmish","month":"2020-07","story":"How Did Vim Become So Popular?","stance":"rec","why":"Shows text objects and the power of mastering Vim.","quote":"Even without plugins mastering vim makes writing code quite natural.","text":"The parent described only a fraction of what vim can do. The notion of text objects and combining them with plugins makes vim much more powerful. Even without plugins mastering vim makes writing code quite natural. [VIDEO]"},{"id":19795498,"author":"dredmorbius","month":"2019-05","story":"Intermediate Vim","stance":"rec","why":"Listed among recommended Vim resources.","quote":"Recommended","text":"Recommended: Learn vim For the Last Time: A Tutorial and Primer [LINK] Mastering the Vim Language [VIDEO] An Incremental Approach to Vim [LINK]"}],"r":[["RrHnLXMTOWM",1],["fXW-QjBsruE",1],["HXAW2GseICs",1],["sfXn_ecH5Rw",1],["AItTqnTsVjA",1],["6h3RJhoqgK8",1],["hlrv7DIHllE",1],["F-QA2rkpBSY",1],["VnvGwFegbC8",1],["jh8supIUj6c",1],["hFSPHfZQpIQ",1],["ISAKc9gpGjw",1],["8JrqH2oOTK4",1]]},"nYg6jzotiAc":{"wy":"HN commenters point to it as an equation-free, charming way to hear Feynman explain physics to laypeople, including his famous refusal to explain magnetism with simple analogies.","q":{"x":"It's an hour of fun science, and especially accessible for anyone who is curious about physics but doesn't enjoy all the equations.","i":30794816,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":42941328,"author":"Stratoscope","month":"2025-02","story":"Ask HN: What is interviewing like now with everyone using AI?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Feynman explaining jiggling atoms and how trains stay on tracks","quote":"(Highly recommended video.)","text":"Somehow this reminds me of my old physics professor, who often had simplistic, almost boring explanations of things, like jiggling atoms making friends with each other, or how a train stays on the track. His name was Richard Feynman. [VIDEO] (Highly recommended video.) I should also note that \"teach me something\" was intended to give the candidate a chance to be the expert at the beginning the interview. It wasn't something they would be judged on, it was an ice-breaker. And a fun one, based on the feedback I got from candidates."},{"id":32784769,"author":"morphle","month":"2022-09","story":"How Transistors Work [video]","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Feynman explaining electricity, heat, magnetism and mirrors for laymen","quote":"I would say that this the most important video to see for any human being on the planet.","text":"Richard Feynman is the best teacher we know (explaining electricity): [VIDEO] He also explains seeing, heat, electro magnetism, elasticity and mirrors among other things. His academic lectures are just as good but too long and hard for laymen to follow. - Every time I switch on an electrical device I hear Feynman say 'Zzzzinggg' and I see the copper bars jiggling across town. - Every time I see a cup of hot liquid I hear Feynman say \"jiggling atoms\" - Watch his hands and fingers telling the more accurate science story, simulating the electrons and atoms. - I would say that this the most important video to see for any human being on the planet. De second most important thing would be half of Alan Kay's lectures [LINK] Great video's to watch with your kids! (from 3-4 years and older)."},{"id":30794816,"author":"Stratoscope","month":"2022-03","story":"The Feynman Lectures on Physics (1964)","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Hour of accessible, equation-free physics talk from Feynman","quote":"It's an hour of fun science, and especially accessible for anyone who is curious about physics but doesn't enjoy all the equations.","text":"If you enjoy Feynman's lectures as much as I do (he taught freshman physics when I went to Caltech!) I highly recommend his armchair series, \"Fun to Imagine\": [VIDEO] He's sitting at home in Pasadena, talking about stuff that comes to mind. • Jiggling atoms who like to make friends with each other. • Where do trees come from? (It's not the dirt.) • Why don't your arms sink through the armrests of your chair? • How do trains stay on the tracks? It's an hour of fun science, and especially accessible for anyone who is curious about physics but doesn't enjoy all the equations."},{"id":39966654,"author":"lloeki","month":"2024-04","story":"When a black hole and a neutron star merge","stance":"rec","why":"Feynman on the limits of analogies when explaining magnetism","quote":"but the whole chapter is worth a watch","text":"My understanding of it is somewhat similar, although I'd argue that's where our mental imagery breaks down: the \"weight on fabric\" metaphor works to a point then stops conveying what's really happening and becomes misleading. I think of it like \"magnets as rubber bands\", and what I call \"Feynman lies\": > I can't explain [magnetic] attraction in terms of anything else that's familiar to you. For example, if we’d said the magnets attract like as if they were connected by rubber bands, I would be cheating you. Because they're not connected by rubber bands, I shouldn’t be in trouble, you’d soon ask me about the nature of the bands… and secondly if you were curious enough, you'd ask me why rubber bands tend to pull back together again, and I would end up explaining that in terms of electrical forces, which are the very things that I'm trying to use the rubber bands to explain, so I have cheated very badly, you see. > [...] > But I really can't do a good job, any job, of explaining magnetic force in terms of something else that you're more familiar with, because I don’t understand them in terms of anything else that you are more familiar with. direct link: [VIDEO] but the whole chapter is worth a watch: [VIDEO]"},{"id":39677897,"author":"lloeki","month":"2024-03","story":"Lie-to-children","stance":"rec","why":"Feynman on why he cannot explain magnetism via familiar analogies","quote":"but the whole chapter is worth a watch","text":"In more general terms - not just applying to children but to anyone - I call these \"Feynman lies\": the kind of lies you have to tell so as not to drown the receiver into very deep technical details. In essence this means presenting a model of the world that is sort of fundamentally incorrect, but one that is useful in allowing one's to wrap their head around the ideas. \"All models are wrong, but some are useful\", yadda yadda. \"meet where they are\" is a really nice way to put it, although it can be tricky to not paint ourselves in a \"like rubber bands\" corner! > I can't explain [magnetic] attraction in terms of anything else that's familiar to you. For example, if we’d said the magnets attract like as if they were connected by rubber bands, I would be cheating you. Because they're not connected by rubber bands, I shouldn’t be in trouble, you’d soon ask me about the nature of the bands… and secondly if you were curious enough, you'd ask me why rubber bands tend to pull back together again, and I would end up explaining that in terms of electrical forces, which are the very things that I'm trying to use the rubber bands to explain, so I have cheated very badly, you see. > [...] > But I really can't do a good job, any job, of explaining magnetic force in terms of something else that you're more familiar with, because I don’t understand them in terms of anything else that you are more familiar with. direct link: [VIDEO] but the whole chapter is worth a watch: [VIDEO]"},{"id":28350059,"author":"modeless","month":"2021-08","story":"Train Wheels Are Cones","stance":"rec","why":"Full Feynman interview in better quality with subtitles","quote":"Here it is in better quality, the whole thing, and with subtitles","text":"Here it is in better quality, the whole thing, and with subtitles: [VIDEO] Particularly good parts are the explanation of fire and trees (\"trees come out of the air\"): [VIDEO] and the explanation of the mirror problem, i.e. how does a mirror know to reverse left and right but not up and down: [VIDEO]"},{"id":38956924,"author":"amiga386","month":"2024-01","story":"Feynman: I am burned out and I'll never accomplish anything (1985)","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"I can just imagine this in his voice (e.g. [VIDEO] ) Feynman is my favourite physicist. Of course you could pick someone else, you could argue about the relative importance of each person's contribution to scientific knowledge, but it was his aimiability, playfulness and curiousity, combined with the fact that he _did_ break new frontiers in quantum physics that make him so inspiring for me. His famed series of lectures at Caltech were a great introduction to physics. I was hoping to see more of his time at Los Alamos in the Oppenheimer biopic, but he doesn't even seem to be in it. There's supposedly someone playing him, but I don't see it; I assume the two-second silent shot of someone from behind playing the bongos was meant to be him."}],"r":[["ZfuierUvx1A",1],["oAVjF_7ensg",1],["AD5aAd8Oy84",1],["3GVO-MfIl1Q",1],["msN87y-iEx0",1],["awADEuv5vWY",1],["1e8VZlPBx_0",1],["id1WShzzMCQ",1],["jVkLVRt6c1U",1],["P1ww1IXRfTA",1],["FvmTSpJU-Xc",1],["QTJRwKOFddc",1],["N9c7_8Gp7gI",1],["TLm7Q92xMjQ",1],["ywWBy6J5gz8",1],["YNTARSM-Fjc",1],["kAG39jKi0lI",1],["8S0FDjFBj8o",1],["rRi8LptvFZY",1],["GJVSDjRXVoo",1]]},"MZvmYaFkNJI":{"wy":"HN points people to it as the demo that shows why LiveView and Elixir are worth a look.","q":{"x":"the demo completely blew my mind","i":25540421,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":25538244,"author":"realusername","month":"2020-12","story":"Ask HN: Best Talks of 2020?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Impressive live demo of real-time app with live reload","quote":"Never before I've been so bluffed by a new technology","text":"My favorite talk is the Live View twitter demo on my case: [VIDEO] . Never before I've been so bluffed by a new technology, he's building a small twitter clone in 20min with live reload, browser sync and everything, this would take days with any other traditional tech I know."},{"id":25540421,"author":"staticelf","month":"2020-12","story":"Ask HN: Best Talks of 2020?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Demo that blew the commenter's mind","quote":"the demo completely blew my mind","text":"I think the \"talk\" by Chris McCord should apply here \"Build a real-time Twitter clone in 15 minutes with LiveView and Phoenix 1.5\", while not really a talk this year was special and the demo completely blew my mind. it's available here: [VIDEO]"},{"id":25575003,"author":"realusername","month":"2020-12","story":"Ruby 3.0 and the new FiberScheduler interface","stance":"strong_rec","why":"LiveView demo that shows why Elixir is worth a look","quote":"Phoenix LiveView really is a game changer, you should have a look at Elixir just for that.","text":"> But I'm wondering if it brings enough other advantages that I shouldn't view it that simplistically. So to start with, Phoenix LiveView really is a game changer, you should have a look at Elixir just for that. This is the talk which really bluffed me when I first saw it: [VIDEO] . For the other upsides, I like a little bit better the way everything is architectured in Phoenix, there's much less magic and it's easier to follow the data flow and what is going on."},{"id":35022255,"author":"dmitriid","month":"2023-03","story":"Phoenix Dev Blog – Streams","stance":"rec","why":"Good overview of building dynamic apps with LiveView","quote":"will give a good overview of this","text":"Liveview solves the \"write a fully dynamic and interactive application with next to zero Javascript that you need to write by hand\" or, if you will, \"server-driven UI apps\". And it's hard to overstate, but to also properly explain, how important this is and how it feels like magic when you do it. Because it lets you tap into everything Elixr (and Erlang VM) have to offer on the backend without ever having to think of \"how the hell do I bring all that to the client\". My use-cases are: a big chunk of data is being processed, and while its being processed regular updates are sent over PubSub. Most actions a user initiates are long-ish-running and their updates are also sent over PubSub. Things that other users are doing are sent over PubSub and possibly need to be reflected in the UI. In \"traditional\" JS-on-the-client-whatever-on-the-server model you have a plethora of questions of how to get that to UI: which endpoints to query, which updates to send over the wire, the formats, the frequence, authorisation, auth... With LiveView your \"client\" is an Elixir view living on the server which just updates the view based on those PubSubs (or timers, or Kafka streams, or user-initiated actions, or...) and LiveView takes care of upating the DOM/HTML in the browser. \"Build a real-time Twitter clone in 15 minutes with LiveView and Phoenix 1.5\" will give a good overview of this: [VIDEO] [1] Built-in in Phoenix, [LINK]"},{"id":28375002,"author":"frellus","month":"2021-08","story":"Why Erlang?","stance":"rec","why":"Neat demo of interactive app with no JavaScript","quote":"Here's a neat demo. No JavaScript required.","text":"Yes and no. The real answer (with an Erlang bias) is to look at Elixir + Phoenix + LiveView. Here's a neat demo. No JavaScript required. [VIDEO]"},{"id":25791639,"author":"sodapopcan","month":"2021-01","story":"StimulusReflex, or LiveView for Rails","stance":"rec","why":"Explains LiveView's tiny websocket payloads while demoing it","quote":"This video explains it well (though it's also a demo of LiveView)","text":"Even with tooling getting better, you're still faced with creating an API to the backend that has a high chance of your frontend being the only ever consumer of it. You're also faced with duplicating logic on both front and backends in many scenarios (though of course this can be mitigated with a node backend? I don't know... I've never used node). As for latency, this is over websockets. It becomes a problem if you are connecting to a server across the ocean but otherwise, it's incredibly fast and feels just as snappy as JS. I actually also haven't used Stimulus Reflex, just Phoenix LiveView so I'm not sure if they are exactly the same, but the TL;DR is that they are sending VERY tiny payloads over websockets. This video explains it well (though it's also a demo of LiveView): [VIDEO]"},{"id":24430073,"author":"mercer","month":"2020-09","story":"Vue.js Creator Evan You Interview","stance":"rec","why":"Live demo building a Twitter clone with LiveView","quote":"This one's also pretty good","text":"This one's also pretty good: [VIDEO] (Build a real-time Twitter clone in 15 minutes with LiveView and Phoenix 1.5)"},{"id":23499400,"author":"bigbassroller","month":"2020-06","story":"Ask HN: Go vs. Ruby/Rails for first time indie dev?","stance":"rec","why":"Builds a Twitter timeline clone quickly with LiveView","quote":"you can build a Twitter timeline clone in under 18 minutes using Phoenix LiveView","text":"> I don't like the idea of needing to learn both Elixir and Erlang You dont need to learn Erlang to use Elixir, rather you would need to know Elixir to use Phoenix; like you need to know Ruby to use Rails. Also you can build a Twitter timeline clone in under 18 minutes using Phoenix LiveView: [VIDEO]"},{"id":42990714,"author":"vmchale","month":"2025-02","story":"Ask HN: Which functional language for full stack web development?","stance":"reference","why":"Phoenix framework demo that impressed the commenter","quote":"I've been impressed but what I've seen of the Phoenix web framework.","text":"I've been impressed but what I've seen of the Phoenix web framework. [VIDEO] On the F# side: \"Where are the F# jobs? I've checked the various FP-centric job-sites - they're as barren as everywhere else. It almost seems as if Haskell jobs are more plentiful! Haskell!\" [LINK]"},{"id":36957964,"author":"himangshuj","month":"2023-08","story":"Ask HN: Who is hiring? (August 2023)","stance":"reference","why":"Tutorial for the interview assignment","quote":"","text":"HamiltonPractice, REMOTE, INTERNS, ~1000$ pm, 3 months HamiltonPractice is an AI Practice Manager built on the cutting-edge stack. We optimize and encrypt every HTTP call to automate complex healthcare and financial data operations. Our web stack is built on top of Elixir Phoenix MQTT protocol, and our backend processing works on distributed Postgres pub-sub to build efficient and fault-tolerant platforms. We are looking for full-stack functional programmer interns who could join us full-time. Prior experience in open-source projects and functional programming is plus but optional. Assignment (Waived for anyone with meaningful open-source web experience including but not limited to GSOC) Build an authenticated website on elixir live-view ( [LINK] , tutorial [VIDEO] ). After logging, the user name should be displayed on the logged-in page. There should be an icon on the password button to change the password element to text. Bonus points on deploying the code to fly dot io. Interest candidates please email to hh AT hamiltonpractice.com"},{"id":36128081,"author":"sph","month":"2023-05","story":"Dynamic Forms with LiveView Streams","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"The Live View demo or the Rails one? :-) Rails, it was this one 17 years ago: [LINK] Live View, I'm not sure which is the first demo. There's \"Build a real-time Twitter clone in 15 minutes with LiveView and Phoenix 1.5\" ( [VIDEO] )"},{"id":29602758,"author":"dmitriid","month":"2021-12","story":"Remix: On Rails","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as the one stack that replicates the Rails blog demo","quote":"there's no tech stack that can replicate this demo (except Elixir LiveView [1])","text":"> 16 years ago, David Heinemeier Hansson (DHH) took the stage and delivered one of the most impressive code demos the world has ever seen. 16 years later there's no tech stack that can replicate this demo (except Elixir LiveView [1]). And yet we're told tech has progressed. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":25914522,"author":"brobdingnagians","month":"2021-01","story":"Birdwatch Overview","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Good place to post the \"Build a real-time Twitter clone in 15 minutes with LiveView and Phoenix 1.5\" ;) [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["TTM_b7EJg5E",1],["5IUj1EZwpJY",1],["2u6HTG8LuXQ",1],["agZ0xISi40E",1],["5cWck_xcH64",1],["KqFNbCcyFkk",1],["qSfdtmcZ4d0",1],["hxM8QmyZXtg",1],["r9rGX91rq5I",1],["Z1PCtIaM_GQ",1]]},"aSEQfqNYNAc":{"wy":"Cited as the classic rant on maps versus objects in discussions of data modeling and OOP.","q":{"x":"A classic rant by Rich Hickey","i":38360086,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":38360086,"author":"travisjungroth","month":"2023-11","story":"","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Classic Hickey rant on maps versus objects","quote":"A classic rant by Rich Hickey","text":"A classic rant by Rich Hickey: [VIDEO] I say this even though I don’t usually code this way: I think the big benefit for business code is sort of a constrained flexibility. Objects tend to get really big (the alternative is you have a ton of classes). Think about all the data that makes up one Uber ride. So if I want to do anything with a Ride, I need everything. An even the many classes thing doesn’t really solve that because maybe what I want is cross-cutting. So either way, I just end up having to supply a bunch of stuff I don’t need. The maps-first approach is more incremental. Every function takes what it needs and gives what it can."},{"id":26788213,"author":"daly","month":"2021-04","story":"Languages you must know #10 (Lisp)","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Shows how Hickey thinks in lisp and uses maps","quote":"Watch anything Rich Hickey talks about. He really \"gets it\".","text":"Rich Hickey \"thinks in lisp\". For example, he talks about his \"maps\" data structure (aka association lists). See [VIDEO] Since data and programs are the same thing in lisp you can fluidly shift from one to the other, like I did with \"knowledge\" vs \"rules\". They both have the same representation (defstructs) but they are also code and have execution semantics. Clojure allows you to do this with map objects. The fact that you can use the whole language on everything at any time and that everything at any time is also \"the language\" is how you \"think in lisp\". Nobody thinks of Python programs as \"data\" or data as Python programs. Watch anything Rich Hickey talks about. He really \"gets it\"."},{"id":26364381,"author":"TacticalCoder","month":"2021-03","story":"Clojure from a Schemer's Perspective","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Hickey rant on maps and Java getters","quote":"it's golden","text":"> Do people actually build large codebases with the 'hash map first' approach? There are a few large projects written in Clojure. I'd say it's a \"data first\" approach, not uniquely \"map first\". There's this famous 4 minutes rant by Rich Hickey (the creator of Clojure) where he talks about maps and about how Java does OOP (and how maps are served through getters but you can't see easily that they're maps), it's golden: [VIDEO]"},{"id":48378665,"author":"drob518","month":"2026-06","story":"My thoughts after using Clojure for about a month","stance":"rec","why":"Argues for maps and data-transforming functions over OOP","quote":"This is with your time","text":"> I personally find OOP to be the most intuitive for large scale systems design, but that's just me. At one point, I was the same. But after going functional in Clojure, I can’t imagine going back. Using maps nd just having common functions that transform data into different data is definitely the way to go. This is with your time: [VIDEO]"},{"id":43655327,"author":"filoeleven","month":"2025-04","story":"Why I Program in Lisp","stance":"rec","why":"Excerpt illustrating why data beats functions in Clojure design","quote":"which is a good illustration of why data is better than functions, a key driver of Clojure's design.","text":"I like \"Clojure, Made Simple\" even more. [LINK] Someone helpfully pulled out this chunk, which is a good illustration of why data is better than functions, a key driver of Clojure's design. [VIDEO]"},{"id":45782033,"author":"codemonkey-zeta","month":"2025-11","story":"On Having a Data Object","stance":"reference","why":"Cited in a comment on data objects","quote":"","text":"Author is on the verge of having a Clojure epiphany. > 1. You should often be using different objects in different contexts. This is because \"data\" are just \"facts\" that your application has observed. Different facts are relevant in different circumstances. The User class in my application may be very similar to the User class in your application, they may even have identical \"login\" implementations, but neither captures the \"essence\" of a \"User\", because the set of facts one could observe about Users is unbounded, and combinatorially explosive. This holds for subsets of facts as well. Maybe our login method only cares about a User's email address and password, but to support all the other stuff in our app, we have to either: 1. Pass along every piece of data and behavior the entire app specifies 2. Create another data object that captures only the facts that login cares about (e.g. a LoginPayload object, or a LoginUser object, Credential object, etc.) Option 1 is a nightmare because refactoring requires taking into consideration ALL usages of the object, regardless of whether or not the changes are relevant to the caller. Option 2 sucks because your Object hierarchy is combinatorial on the number of distinct _callers_. That's why it is so hard to refactor large systems programmed in this style. > 3. The classes get huge and painful. The author observed the combinatorial explosion of facts! If you have a rich information landscape that is relevant to your application, you are"},{"id":41783523,"author":"hcarvalhoalves","month":"2024-10","story":"Don't let dicts spoil your code","stance":"reference","why":"Counterpoint to the article on dicts","quote":"Here's a counter-point","text":"Debatable. Here's a counter-point: [VIDEO] But ok, it's less bad in Python since objects are dicts anyway and you don't need getters."},{"id":41785060,"author":"lispisok","month":"2024-10","story":"Don't let dicts spoil your code","stance":"reference","why":"Extreme counterexample demonstrating against getters and setters","quote":"Here is Rich Hickey with an extreme counter example","text":"Maps arent nearly as problematic in clojure because data is immutable by default on top of the functional paradigm where your program is basically a big composition of functions and the language is built around using maps. In Python I largely agree with the author. In clojure I love my maps. Here is Rich Hickey with an extreme counter example although I would argue he's really demonstrating against getters and setters. [VIDEO]"},{"id":37233183,"author":"stefcoetzee","month":"2023-08","story":"A History of Clojure (2020) [pdf]","stance":"reference","why":"Hickey on just using a map","quote":"Just use a map\" [0]","text":"Where generally the proverbial \"one data structure\" is a hash map. \"Just use a map\" [0] [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":31434073,"author":"ducktective","month":"2022-05","story":"Rust: A Critical Retrospective","stance":"reference","why":"Related section of a Hickey talk on density","quote":"Reminds me of this section of Rich Hickey talk","text":"> I found Rust syntax to be dense, heavy, and difficult to read Reminds me of this section of Rich Hickey talk: [VIDEO]"},{"id":30428363,"author":"simongray","month":"2022-02","story":"Try Clojure – An interactive tutorial in the browser","stance":"reference","why":"Rebuttal to claims about lack of tooling","quote":"","text":"> there was no type-checking (...) intellisense/autocomplete popups for the methods. First of all, yes there is. Maybe your editor didn't support it, but e.g. IntelliJ IDEA with the Cursive plugin does. Secondly: [VIDEO]"},{"id":28041219,"author":"uDontKnowMe","month":"2021-08","story":"Six Years of Professional Clojure","stance":"reference","why":"Linked in a long static typing discussion","quote":"","text":"Not who you are responding to, but the common idea that static types are all win and no cost has become very popular these days, but isn't true, it's just that the benefits of static typing are immediately apparent and obvious, but their costs are more diffuse and less obvious. I thought this was a pretty good write up on the subject that gets at a few of the benefits [LINK] Just to name some of the costs of static types briefly: * they are very blunt -- they will forbid many perfectly valid programs just on the basis that you haven't fit your program into the type system's view of how to encode invariants. So in a static typing language you are always to greater or lesser extent modifying your code away from how you could have naturally expressed the functionality towards helping the compiler understand it. * Sometimes this is not such a big change from how you'd otherwise write, but other times the challenge of writing some code could be virtually completely in the problem of how to express your invariants within the type system, and it becomes an obsession/game. I've seen this run rampant in the Scala world where the complexity of code reaches the level of satire. * Everything you encode via static types is something that you would actually have to change your code to allow it to change. Maybe this seems obvious, but it has big implications against how coupled and fragile your code is. Consider in Scala you're parsing a document into a static type like. case class Record( "},{"id":23197455,"author":"simongray","month":"2020-05","story":"Pretending OOP Never Happened","stance":"reference","why":"Hickey rant as counterpoint to object-method discovery","quote":"As a counterpoint (relevant Rich Hickey rant)","text":"> I have an object... what do I do with it? So I hit the '.' on my keyboard and read the list of things that the object can do. As a counterpoint (relevant Rich Hickey rant): [VIDEO]"},{"id":25383429,"author":"simongray","month":"2020-12","story":"The Future of Clojure","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Some people need something to happen when they press dot, though","text":"The same amount of static analysis as Python, Ruby, or any other language that doesn't have static type checking. Some people need something to happen when they press dot, though: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["zAFcV7zuUDA",1],["OoJsPvmFixU",1],["AGUwcs9qJXY",1],["XziuEdpVUe0",1],["SxdOUGdseq4",1],["PQmSUHhP3ug",1],["sWblpsLZ-O8",1],["_YWMGuh15nE",1],["fwcl17Q0bpk",1],["qBpiXcyB7wU",1],["DJtef410XaM",1],["ROor6_NGIWU",1],["UnURElCzGc0",1],["loCBvaj4eSg",1]]},"bpQFCcSI0pU":{"wy":"Frequently posted in early COVID origin and lab-leak threads as a compelling early case, though other commenters cite rebuttals and note it is speculative.","q":{"x":"I wish more people would watch this [0], might not be true but damn does it seem convincing.","i":25587616,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":27312180,"author":"phyalow","month":"2021-05","story":"The case for a leak of a virus engineered in a Wuhan lab","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Details a missing researcher and her scrubbed internet presence","quote":"I highly recommend this video from early 2020","text":"Exactly. I am not into conspiracies, but I highly recommend this video from early 2020 which details how one of the Chinese researchers at WIV, Huang Yan Ling disappeared in late 2019 and how subsequently her internet presence was memory holed [1] by Chinese censors (all verifiable with the links in the video description and wayback). [VIDEO] This to me is one of the smoking guns. [1] - [LINK]"},{"id":25587616,"author":"june11","month":"2020-12","story":"Israeli startup claims Covid-19 likely originated in a lab, willing to bet on it","stance":"strong_rec","why":"A convincing case for lab origin","quote":"I wish more people would watch this [0], might not be true but damn does it seem convincing.","text":"I wish more people would watch this [0], might not be true but damn does it seem convincing. Seems pretty obvious that it was lab originated, even if it isn't manufactured. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":34951030,"author":"Izkata","month":"2023-02","story":"Lab Leak Most Likely Origin of Covid-19 Pandemic, U.S. Agency Now Says","stance":"rec","why":"A 10-minute summary on a virologist censored off the Chinese internet","quote":"10-minute video that includes that plus a few other things","text":"> Patient zero was a virologist that seems to have been censored off the Chinese internet afterwards, and I can't find the links anymore :-/ 10-minute video that includes that plus a few other things: [VIDEO]"},{"id":23071081,"author":"Tarlox","month":"2020-05","story":"Wuhan lab did research on bat viruses, but no evidence of accidental release","stance":"rec","why":"Sources in the description seem reasonably reliable","quote":"The sources listed in the description of this video seem somewhat reliable.","text":"[VIDEO] The sources listed in the description of this video seem somewhat reliable. While I'm not completely sold on the lab being the source I think the odds that it was are still pretty high for me."},{"id":22851856,"author":"light_cone","month":"2020-04","story":"China clamping down on coronavirus research, deleted pages suggest","stance":"rec","why":"Presents what the commenter calls the most credible evidence on the origin","quote":"The most credible evidence has been gathered and presented in a youtube video","text":"The most credible evidence has been gathered and presented in a youtube video [VIDEO] . The youtuber was a long time china immigrant before being forced to move abroad."},{"id":22851935,"author":"abc-xyz","month":"2020-04","story":"China clamping down on coronavirus research, deleted pages suggest","stance":"rec","why":"Presents compelling evidence about the possible patient zero","quote":"The evidence is very compelling and this video does a good job at presenting it","text":"Her name was Huang Yan Ling (黄燕玲) for anyone who might be interested in learning more about the potential patient 0. The evidence is very compelling and this video does a good job at presenting it: [VIDEO]"},{"id":32252305,"author":"drak0n1c","month":"2022-07","story":"Huanan Seafood Market in Wuhan was early epicenter of the Covid-19 pandemic","stance":"reference","why":"Cited for retroactive censorship of institute materials","quote":"","text":"The reason for the doubt is that it wasn't transparent nor obvious. The Chinese government retroactively censored the institute's publications, Fall 2019 job postings, staff listings, and internal discussion of new symptoms amongst certain researchers [1]. There is a possibility that there were a few earlier cases than the market ones which were covered up to conceal negligence at the institute. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":27719742,"author":"Izkata","month":"2021-07","story":"The risky bat-virus engineering that links America to Wuhan","stance":"reference","why":"Cited for a brief mention of sick lab workers, at 6:30","quote":"they're mentioned from 6:30 to 6:50","text":"> While you're pondering all that keep in mind that the person who \"broke\" the story in the WSJ about the sick workers at the Wuhan lab was Michael R. Gordon. He's also notable for having co-authored with Judith Miller in 2002 the NYT story on Hussein's nuclear program and WMDs that arguably helped propel us into the Iraq War. Uh, are you referring to this [0]? It's the only obvious thing I can find on Google linked to his name. It was also posted over a year after when we first heard of those sick workers. Here's [1] one example (they're mentioned from 6:30 to 6:50). [0] [LINK] [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":27390360,"author":"Izkata","month":"2021-06","story":"The lab-leak theory: inside the fight to uncover Covid-19’s origins","stance":"reference","why":"Early example of the lab leak theory being followed","quote":"this video was posted in April 2020","text":"> Looking back in hindsight, I wonder how we could have picked out the lab leak theory as being worthy of consideration, given the context. There were a lot of threads to pull, and some of us were following it. For example, this video was posted in April 2020: [VIDEO]"},{"id":27174522,"author":"leaveyou","month":"2021-05","story":"The Covid lab-leak theory goes mainstream","stance":"reference","why":"Early April 2020 video pointing to signs of a cover-up","quote":"there were clear signs of a coverup at that institute very early in 2020","text":"Exactly. I remember seeing this video on youtube in April 2020 and being kind of confused about it's [VIDEO] what that guy is saying there, there were clear signs of a coverup at that institute very early in 2020."},{"id":27191340,"author":"tkinom","month":"2021-05","story":"How I learned to stop worrying and love the lab-leak theory","stance":"reference","why":"Background on the missing Wuhan lab researcher","quote":"","text":"[VIDEO] Early last year (2020), whole Chinese internet social media was looking for \"黄燕玲\" - a missing researcher in Wuhan lab. She disappeared around Oct/Nov 2019. Chinese social media suspected she was patient-0. The messages showed up multiple times in my wechat feeds for couple days. They all got taken down a few days later. Government officials still claimed she was alive but was not able to produce any proof. If WHO really want to investigate, they just need ask : If \"黄燕玲\" worked for Wuhan lab? Where is she now? Where is her families, friends, co-workers? Can we talk to any of them?"},{"id":26758592,"author":"phyalow","month":"2021-04","story":"Scientists who say the lab-leak hypothesis for SARS-CoV-2 shouldn't be ruled out","stance":"reference","why":"Evidence about a Wuhan lab researcher as patient zero, with archive links","quote":"(links are at the bottom of the video description)","text":"Probably only one. There is strong tertiary evidence that patient zero was a Wuhan Institute of Virology researcher named Huang YanLing 黃燕玲, this has all been scrubbed from public memory. The wayback archive links still hold up. [VIDEO] (links are at the bottom of the video description)."},{"id":22765308,"author":"gnusty_gnurc","month":"2020-04","story":"The coronavirus wasn’t made in a lab. A genetic analysis shows it’s from nature","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"at first glance, it didn't seem all that unreasonable to think the institute somehow originated it","text":"I think this video got posted yesterday, but at first glance, it didn't seem all that unreasonable to think the institute somehow originated it: [VIDEO]"},{"id":22849530,"author":"throw3727","month":"2020-04","story":"Detection of novel coronaviruses in bats in Myanmar","stance":"reference","why":"Offered as the source of the virus, with no elaboration","quote":"","text":"Climate change is not new, just three hundreds years ago there was little ice age. Global travel is new. As for source of the virus: [VIDEO]"},{"id":22851693,"author":"MrBuddyCasino","month":"2020-04","story":"China clamping down on coronavirus research, deleted pages suggest","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"> I don't buy the escaped/released from a lab theories espoused here and on Facebook and the like. Apparently the mutations within the SARS-COV-2 found in humans compared to the bat virus are purely random and hence the thing is not or very unlikely genetically engineered. Only the conspiracy theorists claim it was an \"engineered bio-weapon\". The most likely explanation is that it just escaped involuntarily from the lab. Why from the lab, and not the wet market? - that lab was specifically researching coronavirii in bats - there are reports researchers there got splashed with blood and fecies of bats [0] - the researcher in question (Huang Yan Lin), the likely patient zero, disappeared without a trace, both physically and online [0] - the horseshoe bats that most likely hosted the virus weren't even sold on the wuhan wet market, according to several eye witnesses - there are no known horseshoe bat colonies in a 900km diameter around wuhan - the lab is just 280 meters away from the wet market, so the market was a very convenient scape goat And why was COVID-19 not engineered? Simple, it is not necessary to explain the pandemic potential. All of this is not new and there were warnings about exactly this happening: - it was long known that horseshoe bats were a dangerous reservoir of SARS-CoV like virii [1] - there were US simulations held on how to respond to a respiratory virus with chinese origin in 2019 [2] - there were WHO warnings on how respiratory RNA virii are prime can"},{"id":22851981,"author":"surge","month":"2020-04","story":"China clamping down on coronavirus research, deleted pages suggest","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"No one is saying its a bioweapon, merely shoddy containment in a facility that did viral research, the research could have been entirely altruistic, its still their fault for letting it out of the lab and then hiding the fact, arresting doctors and journalists and letting it spread. When they could have been letting other countries take precautions to prepare for it and shut down travel out of the country sooner instead of telling the world there is no real danger, no human to human transmission, and everything is fine. On the other hand, prefaced with the fact that it isn't one, or was still under research if that, bio-weapons are just as effective when they disrupt an economy and infrastructure and a virus targets the elders or those at an age of those most likely in leadership positions. So it doesn't have to kill military aged people to be effective. Again, this is likely not one, but the disruption its caused and economic hit its taken is in line with how modern wars are fought. They only need to overwhelm medical facilities, its like the case with mines, they don't kill so much as maim, because maiming soldiers puts a greater strain on an army to keep them alive, transport them and treat them (even though they'll never return to the battle field), then if they were simply dead. You kill a soldier, they're down a man, you maim/disable a soldier, they're down a man, two other soldiers needed to evacuate him, they're down transport needed to get them back to a medical cent"},{"id":22895537,"author":"asdf21","month":"2020-04","story":"Coronavirus clue? Most cases aboard U.S. aircraft carrier are symptom-free","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as support for the claim the virus came from a bat lab","quote":"","text":"It wasn't from food, they were messing around with bats in a lab seeing if people could be infected, surprise, they could be. [VIDEO]"},{"id":22914444,"author":"Izkata","month":"2020-04","story":"Rt: Effective Reproduction Number of Covid-19","stance":"reference","why":"Documents much of the story, with sources linked in the description","quote":"much of the rest is documented here","text":"The part about the undertaker seems made-up, but much of the rest is documented here: [VIDEO] (Sources used in the video are linked at the bottom of the description)"},{"id":29659497,"author":"wh313","month":"2021-12","story":"Apache Log4j bug: China’s industry ministry pulls support from Alibaba Cloud","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"And here's a pretty thorough analysis of the flaws with this theory","text":"Here's a two of his videos where he claims that COVID-19 emerged from a lab: [VIDEO] [LINK] And here's a pretty thorough analysis of the flaws with this theory: [LINK]"},{"id":29664198,"author":"wh313","month":"2021-12","story":"Apache Log4j bug: China’s industry ministry pulls support from Alibaba Cloud","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"This preprint has been shown to be false by future scholarship","text":"I believe you misunderstood his analysis. At timestamp 6:36 of his video [1], laowhy86 analyzes a copy of the draft \"The possible origins of 2019-nCoV coronavirus\" to conclude that the virus must have come from the lab. This preprint has been shown to be false by future scholarship (see the citations of the draft at [2] for some examples). Potholer then analyzes the veracity of the claims within that preprint in his video [3], starting with an excerpt of [1] starting at 2:02. So the timeline should be: Feb. 2020 - Preprint published -> Apr. 2020 - laowhy's video -> May 2020 - potholer's rebuttal [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK] [3] [LINK]"}],"r":[["8S0FDjFBj8o",1],["-VsmF9m_Nt8",1],["IESEcsjDcmM",1]]},"pXOK-ZVJMaU":{"wy":"HN recommends it in hair-loss threads as a funny, inspiring example of accepting baldness.","q":{"x":"Patrick Stewart has this awesome interview about his baldness.","i":20532684,"y":"2019"},"m":[{"id":20532684,"author":"SenHeng","month":"2019-07","story":"A \"cure\" for baldness could be around the corner","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Inspiring interview on Stewart's baldness.","quote":"Patrick Stewart has this awesome interview about his baldness.","text":"Patrick Stewart has this awesome interview about his baldness. I'm not balding yet but it has inspired me enough that I plan to shave it all off when I eventually start. [VIDEO] Transcribe for those who can't watch videos, from [LINK] Even though Stewart lost his hair at an early age, he didn’t shave it bald for a number of years. He relates the hilarious story of the first time he went fully bald, “There was a man who was at drama school with me, Hungarian, he was older than the rest of us, he ran his own theater in Budapest. He was a man of strong personality and a good loyal friend to me. He and his wife invited me for lunch one day, the two of them got to the end of their lunch and I thought they were going to make a coffee or something. Then all of a sudden, I was grabbed from behind by George, this big powerful man, and I thought he was playing some kind of game, so I was laughing and joking but then his wife appeared in front of me with a pair of scissors and I knew in an instant what she was going to do, and I began to scream and shout, and now it was serious. I was fighting to keep my appearance, then she lifted my hair and cut it all off. Then George came around, knelt down in front of me and said, now you be yourself – no more hiding! And he was right because I used to walk constantly embarrassed, I couldn’t stand-up properly. It was not only inhibiting as a person, but it was hopeless if you were an actor, of course.”"},{"id":4608052,"author":"ddellacosta","month":"2012-10","story":"Men with shaved heads are perceived to be more masculine","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Patrick Stewart on acclimating to baldness.","quote":"There's a great interview with Patrick Stewart where he talks about his experience acclimating to baldness, I highly recommend it","text":"There's a great interview with Patrick Stewart where he talks about his experience acclimating to baldness, I highly recommend it: [VIDEO]"},{"id":32277184,"author":"wsc981","month":"2022-07","story":"Scientists have finally found the potential “cure” for baldness","stance":"rec","why":"Inspiration on accepting baldness.","quote":"Patrick Stewart can be an inspiration in this regard","text":"I also felt better after accepting my baldness. Patrick Stewart can be an inspiration in this regard: [VIDEO]"},{"id":26252211,"author":"EdwardCoffin","month":"2021-02","story":"Researchers find stem cells for hair regeneration","stance":"rec","why":"Funny interview excerpt on going bald.","quote":"It's pretty funny.","text":"Patrick Stewart touched on the topic of going bald in an excerpt from an interview on the BBC (Parkinson) [1]. It's pretty funny. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":6808924,"author":"w00tnes","month":"2013-11","story":"My genetics are making me miserable","stance":"rec","why":"Might help with coping with hair loss.","quote":"Maybe watch the patrick stewart interview, maybe it'll help a bit","text":"Just turned 26, been loosing hair since middle school (16-17), a few more years and I'll be rockin' a Balmer look :) How I cope with it? I've accepted the situation a long time ago and I don't let it get to me anymore (it did when I was younger tho, had huge self-confidence problems because of it). Hair isn't all that important, just be a good person... :) Maybe watch the patrick stewart interview, maybe it'll help a bit: [VIDEO] ;)"},{"id":4608326,"author":"michael_michael","month":"2012-10","story":"Men with shaved heads are perceived to be more masculine","stance":"reference","why":"Source for Roddenberry bald captain anecdote.","quote":"a reporter once told Gene Roddenberry that having a bald captain wasn't believable.","text":"According to Patrick Stewart ( [VIDEO] ), a reporter once told Gene Roddenberry that having a bald captain wasn't believable. By the 24th century they will have cured baldness. Roddenberry's responded that, by the 24th century, no one will care."}],"r":[["hV41QEKiMlM",1],["D9H9qTdserM",1],["agw-wlHGi0E",1],["-NL76uQOpI0",1],["bmSAYlu0NcY",1],["_L3gNaAVjQ4",1],["RCCigccBzIU",1],["AxFzf6yIfcc",1],["QeLu_yyz3tc",1],["GnO7D5UaDig",1],["FssULNGSZIA",1],["re96UgMk6GQ",1],["HnOix9TFy1A",1],["FF-tKLISfPE",1]]},"q5SRrsr-Iug":{"wy":"HN commenters shared it in 2020 and 2021 as an early, in-depth presentation of the lab-leak case, though it is a contested viewpoint.","q":{"x":"he had a very interesting conversation with a russian virologist who also thinks this is the case","i":25588328,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":26620483,"author":"loceng","month":"2021-03","story":"The WHO-China search for the origins of the coronavirus","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Argues Wuhan lab origin is clear from video.","quote":"Watch this though - - and it's very clear that it likely originated from the Wuhan lab","text":"There's tons of propaganda trying to diminish the likelihood of a lab leak (purposeful or a mistake). Watch this though - [VIDEO] - and it's very clear that it likely originated from the Wuhan lab (they were studying those bats originating from 200 miles away + multiple markers in the genes showing human intervention/editing) - and that what's being promoted by these orgs into mainstream is gaslighting."},{"id":23468441,"author":"GillBates666","month":"2020-06","story":"Ignore the conspiracy theories: scientists know Covid-19 wasn't created in a lab","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Presents lab leak case against media narrative.","quote":"","text":"[VIDEO] How does mainstream media keep their credibility? A considerable portion of the population is just ok with being lied too I guess. Shrug. At the very least we should be investigating all possibilities in regards to the origin of the virus. China has a terrible track record with viruses. They leaked SARS1 twice. The first largely publicized cluster is right next to the coronavirus lab there. Now we have a ton of genetic evidence that can't be hidden. To what length will the media go to hide this and redirect the conversation?"},{"id":27390533,"author":"qrybam","month":"2021-06","story":"The lab-leak theory: inside the fight to uncover Covid-19’s origins","stance":"rec","why":"Early podcast episode covering lab leak in depth.","quote":"Here’s an episode of Bret’s podcast from June 2020 covering this in depth","text":"For anyone who’s listened to Bret Weinstein’s podcast from early on in the pandemic, “gain of function research” will be a familiar term. Great to see this hypothesis getting traction in mainstream media. What’s still crazy to me is how quickly things were explained away and how strongly the directly related scientific community said the hypothesis was “fringe”. Here’s an episode of Bret’s podcast from June 2020 covering this in depth: [VIDEO] Edit: a word"},{"id":27610846,"author":"0-_-0","month":"2021-06","story":"Scientist finds early virus sequences that had been mysteriously deleted","stance":"rec","why":"Podcast discussed deleted sequences and lab leak indicators.","quote":"He's been the canary in the coal mine on numerous issues surrounding COVID.","text":"If I remember correctly, the disappearance of these sequences was discussed more than a year ago in Bret Weinstein's podcast, among many other indicators of a lab leak: [VIDEO] He's been the canary in the coal mine on numerous issues surrounding COVID. edit: listen from 30:43, also from 25:11: [VIDEO]"},{"id":25656050,"author":"not_a_moth","month":"2021-01","story":"An Uncommon Cold","stance":"rec","why":"Lab leak hypothesis unrefuted, presented by Weinstein.","quote":"there has of yet been no refutation of the lab leak hypothesis","text":"> The covid-19 virus isn't the first coronavirus to jump from animals to humans Sigh, while controversial to state, there has of yet been no refutation of the lab leak hypothesis presented by scientists like Brett Weinstein [1] [1] [VIDEO] Wanting to discuss virus origins should not be political or knee jerk censored. At the very surface, natural origins next to a P4 lab doing gain of function coronavirus research is already asking a lot from us."},{"id":25587335,"author":"loceng","month":"2020-12","story":"Israeli startup claims Covid-19 likely originated in a lab, willing to bet on it","stance":"rec","why":"Deigin discusses markers of manipulation in the virus.","quote":"there are multiple markers that it was manipulated","text":"It would seem if you have a deep understanding and look at the actual structure of COVID-19 - then there are multiple markers that it was manipulated, if you believe Yuri Deigin knows what he's talking about: [VIDEO]"},{"id":25588328,"author":"nyolfen","month":"2020-12","story":"Israeli startup claims Covid-19 likely originated in a lab, willing to bet on it","stance":"rec","why":"Conversation with a Russian virologist on lab origin.","quote":"he had a very interesting conversation with a russian virologist who also thinks this is the case","text":"specifically he is a specialist in bat biology, but perhaps more relevantly he had a very interesting conversation with a russian virologist who also thinks this is the case: [VIDEO] here is an essay written by his guest: [LINK]"},{"id":23506412,"author":"nradov","month":"2020-06","story":"Officer of China’s People’s Liberation Army Arrested at LAX","stance":"rec","why":"Lab leak argued plausible, with Deigin's essay.","quote":"Yuri Deigin wrote a good argument for that possibility.","text":"There is no proof that SARS-CoV-2 escaped from a lab, however it remains a plausible hypothesis. Yuri Deigin wrote a good argument for that possibility. [LINK] [VIDEO]"},{"id":27482211,"author":"lodi","month":"2021-06","story":"Linus Torvalds on mRNA Vaccines","stance":"reference","why":"Podcast outlining the lab leak allegation.","quote":"For example, see this podcast from a year ago","text":"> \"Lab-leak\" is non-sensical in all forms which don't begin with \"lab evolved/bioengineered\"... That's exactly the allegation circa March 2020 though: scientists collect various bat-borne coronaviruses (e.g. RaTG13) in Yunnan province around 2013, evolve one of them at the Wuhan Institute of Virology though gain-of-function research, and then due to lax safety protocols end up accidentally leaking an evolved virus in late 2019. For example, see this podcast from a year ago: [VIDEO]"},{"id":27308360,"author":"fsckboy","month":"2021-05","story":"The media's lab leak fiasco","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Not just the media, folks, also the rest of our \"sense making apparatus\". Wikipedia has been suppressing lab leak discussion, and still to this moment will not allow the old Lab Leak Hypothesis page back. It still redirects to a declared \"Misinformation!\" page [LINK] And guess where else lab leak was suppressed? Here on HN. Yes, dang, you suppressed discussion of a perfectly reasonable hypothesis many times. So, while I have your attention, the real problem vis a vis Covid19 is not the lab leak. The real problem is \"gain of function\" research, using gene splicing to make more virulent-to-humans pathogens. And don't read into anything I'm saying, please pay attention to literally what I say here; if you have to reword what I say to cast doubt, you're not playing fair. This virus was likely man-made because gain of function man made viruses are quite common in research; and this is not a coverup conspiracy caused by the Chinese tendency for face-saving, swept under the carpet, \"truths\", though there is plenty of that. This scandal was caused by western experts: our best virologists are themselves engaging in gain of function research right here in our very own cities. If you ask them, as the media did, \"what likely happened here\", their answer is not going to point the finger at themselves, too much funding is at stake. Good background and in-depth research on the gain of function and lab leak hypotheses was carried out and published on the web over a year ago by Yuri Deigin [L"}],"r":[["nphxoUxSvgY",1],["rOzrJjdZDRQ",1],["tIuj-oMN-Fk",1],["_b4qKv1Ctv8",1],["9jMONZMuS2U",1],["pwaWilO_Pig",1],["Z7rd04KzLcg",1],["DfznnKUwywQ",1]]},"kU3BceoMuaA":{"wy":"The standard recommendation for MiniDisc history, especially as a non-American counterpoint to the failed-format story.","q":{"x":"Wonderful video about the format","i":19594918,"y":"2019"},"m":[{"id":19594918,"author":"LeoPanthera","month":"2019-04","story":"Ask HN: Which great products didn't succeed?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Appreciation of the MiniDisc format and its success outside the USA.","quote":"Wonderful video about the format","text":"It was a massive success everywhere except the USA. But because the USA defines history, it was a \"failure\". Wonderful video about the format: [VIDEO]"},{"id":35737354,"author":"NoZebra120vClip","month":"2023-04","story":"Minidisc FAQ: MDLP (“Long-Play”) Mode Topics","stance":"rec","why":"Techmoan's take on the MiniDisc craze.","quote":"Reference to Techmoan is de rigeur","text":"Reference to Techmoan is de rigeur : [VIDEO] Especially any of you kids who weren't around when the MiniDisc craze swept the world like a wildfire gone berzerk."},{"id":27482461,"author":"zinekeller","month":"2021-06","story":"The MiniDisc: the failure of a forgotten format (2020)","stance":"rec","why":"British viewpoint pushing back on the American-centric MiniDisc failure narrative.","quote":"British viewpoint by Techmoan","text":"Ah yes, another American-centric viewpoint (which is correct, no qualms about that part) but in other countries (probably with the exclusion of inner reaches of Africa and, uhm, Canada) MiniDisc (at the late 1990's and early 2000's)... is just there: a reliable way to have portable copies of your music. P.S. British viewpoint by Techmoan (which pointed out the American-centric \"MiniDisc is a failure\"): [VIDEO] P.P.S. A direct rebuttal (to the point it was titled \"The (Not) Forgotten Audio Format That (Never) Failed\"): [LINK]"},{"id":24858337,"author":"mikepurvis","month":"2020-10","story":"Building replacement proprietary battery packs","stance":"rec","why":"Mini-documentary with a lengthy history of MiniDisc devices.","quote":"I recently enjoyed this mini-documentary","text":"As a younger person who missed out, I recently enjoyed this mini-documentary— especially the surprisingly lengthy history of the many devices for a supposedly \"failed\" format: [VIDEO]"},{"id":22701873,"author":"sorenjan","month":"2020-03","story":"Dumping MiniDisc Media","stance":"rec","why":"Techmoan videos about the MiniDisc format.","quote":"TechMoan on Youtube have a few videos about the MiniDisc format for anyone interested.","text":"> There is still something so cool about MiniDisc though Agree. I really wanted one for years, I even had some blank discs to fidget with, but when I could finally afford one the iPod had come out and I bought that instead. Part of me still wants to buy a MiniDisc player and some discs, but I try to keep the amount of stuff I own down to the essentials, and vintage electronic doesn't really make the cut. TechMoan on Youtube have a few videos about the MiniDisc format for anyone interested. [VIDEO] [LINK]"},{"id":24397089,"author":"vertis","month":"2020-09","story":"A floppy-disk Walkman using a Raspberry Pi","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"Tangent. I had a Minidisc player for a while circa 1997 (bit hazy). It was so awesome. I spent a lot of time putting tracks on the minidiscs. I always wanted to be able to use it with a computer to store files as well, alas that required a special drive. While it existed never really took off (at least in Australia). Memory chips might be the future, but minidiscs still look cooler. Cool enough for Neo to sell one in the Matrix :D [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["s4QOHe4bOZc",1],["3GJUM6pCpew",1],["lwhMThePdIo",1],["9xxK5yyecRo",1],["L-x8DYTOv7w",1],["SYeeTvitvFU",1],["_-mBeYC2KGc",1],["KaOC9danxNo",1],["CJlrbMHLBd4",1],["esUb7Zy5Oio",1]]},"4nigRT2KmCE":{"wy":"HN shares it whenever Zork or text adventures come up, as a fun tribute for geeks.","q":{"x":"Now enjoy this immortal tribute","i":38215242,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":42771859,"author":"DonHopkins","month":"2025-01","story":"Zork: The Great Inner Workings (2020)","stance":"rec","why":"Zork rap video made for Get Lamp documentary","quote":"The song and the video are in service of Jason's upcoming documentary about text adventures","text":"MC Frontalot - It Is Pitch Dark: [VIDEO] >Front geeked around for an evening in a basement in Massachusetts. It got filmed in HD. Then Jason Scott made a whole video out of it. The song and the video are in service of Jason's upcoming documentary about text adventures, Get Lamp, but you get to enjoy it now. Peek the cameo by Steve Meretzky. \"Get Lamp\" documentary: [LINK] Trailers: [LINK] Full archive: [LINK] Interviews: [LINK]"},{"id":38215242,"author":"musicale","month":"2023-11","story":"Play the first ever text adventure game in your Linux terminal","stance":"rec","why":"An immortal tribute to Zork","quote":"Now enjoy this immortal tribute","text":"Wrong game, but I expect that's the joke! Now enjoy this immortal tribute: MC Frontalot - It is Pitch Dark [VIDEO]"},{"id":18524378,"author":"krapp","month":"2018-11","story":"A funny rap song about the pains of pair programming","stance":"rec","why":"Nerd rap HN might enjoy","quote":"If you want some more \"nerd\" or parody rap here's some MC Frontalot[0]","text":"Rap can be as diverse in terms of subject matter and style as any other musical genre. If you want some more \"nerd\" or parody rap here's some MC Frontalot[0] and Optimus Rhyme[1] that HN might enjoy. [0] [VIDEO] [1] [LINK]"},{"id":10641042,"author":"Intermernet","month":"2015-11","story":"Get Lamp","stance":"rec","why":"Zork rap featuring Steve Meretzky","quote":"Also starring Steve Meretzky.","text":"I feel that this post also needs a link to MC Frontalot's It Is Pitch Dark[1]. Also starring Steve Meretzky. [1]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":3464886,"author":"jmcnevin","month":"2012-01","story":"Indie artist trying to claim his domain name","stance":"rec","why":"Rap about Zork","quote":"might I suggest you check out \"It is Pitch Dark,\" his rap about Zork.","text":"For those who aren't familiar with MC Frontalot, might I suggest you check out \"It is Pitch Dark,\" his rap about Zork. [VIDEO]"},{"id":46744594,"author":"DonHopkins","month":"2026-01","story":"Gas Town's Agent Patterns, Design Bottlenecks, and Vibecoding at Scale","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"I take it you never played The Sims? The simulation madness ship sailed 26 years ago, if not in 1986 with SimCity! ;) Palm is a fictional character in a text adventure — same tradition as Zork, LambdaMOO, and every MUD since 1978. The emojis are deliberate (navigation aids for LLMs, actually, and ethical simulated person flagging). The whimsy is intentional. I've been building simulated characters, world and city building tools since before The Sims shipped. This is just the next iteration. You may recognize tributes to many classic simulated characters in MOOLLM. MC Frontalot - It Is Pitch Dark: [VIDEO] The Grue monster carries his own game mechanics with him, eating you if you go for long enough in the maze without your lamp lit: grue: [LINK] The Wumpus has prototypes for his game playing pieces in his character directory: [LINK] wumpus-snorax: [LINK] BOTTOMLESS-PIT.yml: [LINK] SUPERBATS.yml: [LINK] And yes, it works great, and is fun and easy to author, for much less money than Steve's costly \"Infinite Number of Typewriters Communicating Via Carrier Pigeon\" approach."},{"id":46787370,"author":"DonHopkins","month":"2026-01","story":"Show HN: I Wrapped the Zorks with an LLM","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"In MOOLLM Adventures, you can have NPC characters with entire game mechanics, rules, and playing piece prototypes embedded in them, that can run cooperatively in parallel in the same game! The Grue — The Darkness That IS The Game: [LINK] Hint: GET LAMP [LINK] MC Frontalot - It Is Pitch Dark: [VIDEO] Snorax the Patient — The Wumpus Who IS The Game [LINK] Bottomless Pit: [LINK] Superbats: [LINK] Hunt the Wumpus BASIC source code from 1973 to resolve any rule ambiguities: [LINK] With a classic Zork mailbox integrated with the postal system, including junk mail, stamp collecting, chain mail, and offers you can't refuse: [LINK] Postal System: [LINK] Not to be confused with the Postel System: [LINK]"},{"id":45998957,"author":"DonHopkins","month":"2025-11","story":"Microsoft makes Zork open-source","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"If this predicament seems particularly cruel, consider whose fault it could be","text":"If this predicament seems particularly cruel, consider whose fault it could be: not a torch or a match in your inventory. MC Frontalot - It Is Pitch Dark [VIDEO] Featuring Steve Meretzky! [LINK]"},{"id":23116887,"author":"sandymcmurray","month":"2020-05","story":"Zork source code, 1977","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"You are likely to be eaten by a Grue.","text":"\"You are likely to be eaten by a Grue. If this predicament seems particularly cruel, consider whose fault it might be; not a torch or a match in your inventory.\" [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["v4Wy7gRGgeA",1],["RxD6H3ri8RI",1],["helScS3coAE",1],["KVN_0qvuhhw",1],["yMjPqr1s-cg",1],["z_dt7NG38V4",1],["whPWKecazgM",1],["pqQSwQLDIK8",1],["AdNJ3fydeao",1],["8Gv0H-vPoDc",1],["wpOxbesRNBc",1],["7hwO8Q_TyCA",1],["mZyvIHYn2zk",1],["I4flQ8XdvJM",1],["8QiPFmIMxFc",1],["1iAJPoc23-M",1],["Jd3-eiid-Uw",1],["Kr3quGh7pJA",1],["4JWwOzEDGss",1],["qybUFnY7Y8w",1]]},"099cHWSbAL8":{"wy":"Strongly recommended as a gripping case study whose lessons carry over to engineering management and safety.","q":{"x":"I watched a talk[1] by Nickolas Means recently that goes deeper into the events of that day and the lessons learned.","i":20545260,"y":"2019"},"m":[{"id":39174669,"author":"bertil","month":"2024-01","story":"Why you've never been in a plane crash","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Nail-biting in-depth analysis ending with management lessons.","quote":"Strongly recommend.","text":"When the conference LeadDev happens in London, they often have Nickolas Means give a talk about an aeronautic topic. It’s usually 45 minutes of nail-biting in-depth analysis of a very complicated problem, and ends with a 5 minute rotation to how the lessons from that incident apply to engineering management in general. (You also feel like you could engineer an airplane, but no you can’t: that’s just Nickolas’ talent for explanation that is fooling you. Airplanes are very hard to make.) This is all done in such a smart, seamless, obvious way to deliver a lesson that would make the Brothers Grimm feel cheated. Strongly recommend. [LINK] [VIDEO] [LINK]"},{"id":33988222,"author":"latchkey","month":"2022-12","story":"Waymo's collision avoidance testing","stance":"rec","why":"Applicable lessons from air crash analysis for safety engineering.","quote":"I watched this video yesterday which seems really applicable...","text":"I watched this video yesterday which seems really applicable... How to crash an airplane – Nickolas Means [VIDEO] Reflecting on the video, they don't need to save everyone, but it certainly should be the first time when not everyone dies."},{"id":24376454,"author":"wp381640","month":"2020-09","story":"List of YouTube channels for improving web development and programming skills","stance":"rec","why":"Good conference talk on aviation incidents.","quote":"Nickolas Means - not a channel but two presentations that are good","text":"This list with no context is bordering on useless. Here are some channels i've watched and enjoyed: 1. interviewing.io - they do tech interviews with big5/6 tech co candidates and step through them: [LINK] 2. CS Dojo - ex-googler who steps through CS topics (data structures, algorithms) at the intermediate level and applications in python + javascript: [LINK] 3. Academind - Heard of tech x and have no idea what it is? Chances are there is a 20-30 min video on this channel explaining it clearly. He also does longer form \"build x app\" videos: [LINK] 4. comma.ai / George Hotz - ML + AI livestreams from geohot: [LINK] 5. Lex Fridman - ML, AI, python and tons of awesome interviews with luminaries: [LINK] 6. Joma Tech - data science, big companies / startups as a dev and some funny vids: [LINK] 7. Wes Bos - decent css explainer vids for whenever you're _how do i align the fucking boxes_ + react / js [LINK] 8. Rect conf - Sophie and Dan's presentations are great: [LINK] 9. Nickolas Means - not a channel but two presentations that are good: 9.1 How to crash a plane : [VIDEO] 9.2 The original skunk works: [LINK] There are tons of shitty tutorial videos and channels to avoid if you just rely on search (the comments are full of what appear to be dev contractors out of their depth begging for solutions)"},{"id":20545260,"author":"nrb","month":"2019-07","story":"In a Life-or-Death Crisis, Humility Is Everything","stance":"rec","why":"Deeper look at United 232 and lessons learned.","quote":"I watched a talk[1] by Nickolas Means recently that goes deeper into the events of that day and the lessons learned.","text":"The article does a poor job of explaining how truly incredibly the flight crew of United Airlines Flight 232 performed under crisis; I watched a talk[1] by Nickolas Means recently that goes deeper into the events of that day and the lessons learned. Wikipedia also has a good summary of the NTSB accident report[3]. 1: [VIDEO] 2: [LINK] 3: [LINK]"},{"id":28582593,"author":"lmilcin","month":"2021-09","story":"How to Land a ‘Completely Uncontrollable’ Passenger Jet (2018)","stance":"reference","why":"Covers United 232 losing all flight control.","quote":"here is United Airlines Flight 232 where they lost all control","text":"If you are into that sort of thing here is United Airlines Flight 232 where they lost all control except for being able to throttle one remaining egine (and consequently steer but only to the right) [VIDEO]"},{"id":24333985,"author":"hnarn","month":"2020-08","story":"FedEx feeder plane takes flight without pilot in test for Reliable Robotics","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"That depends on your definition of \"tricky\". If you mean something that requires human intelligence, I would say it's the complete opposite. More or less the first thing pilots start doing during an emergency is to follow lists, it's all very procedural and most failures have been predicted in advance, as well as what would need to be done to resolve them, or at least eliminate the immediate threat -- and if these procedures do not help, you are in a very bad place anyway. There are exceptions, of course.[1] I would say that the \"tricky parts\", as always, are very generally the situations that we did not predict and did not program for. [1]: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["ncPyMUfNyVM",1],["dNs_kNilSjk",1],["2Vrhk5OjBP8",1],["pL3Yzjk5R4M",1]]},"Imquk_3oFf4":{"wy":"HN people share it as a mind-blowing example of demoscene craft and the reigning champion of 256-byte demos.","q":{"x":"producing stuff that is just as mindblowing as it was when it all began.","i":34201390,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":34201390,"author":"jacquesm","month":"2022-12","story":"Ask HN: What does artisanal software look like?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Mindblowing 256 byte demo showing demoscene craft","quote":"producing stuff that is just as mindblowing as it was when it all began.","text":"Check out the demo scene, they are still going strong after 4 decades and producing stuff that is just as mindblowing as it was when it all began. [VIDEO] That's 256 bytes ."},{"id":33295966,"author":"throwawayben","month":"2022-10","story":"The Commodordion","stance":"rec","why":"Reigning champion of 256 byte demos","quote":"this is probably the reigning champion of the 256 byte demo","text":"[VIDEO] I don't think it uses any fancy modern CPU features but this is probably the reigning champion of the 256 byte demo, and its write up[0] [0] [LINK]"},{"id":32794889,"author":"oddly","month":"2022-09","story":"New Linux malware combines unusual stealth with a full suite of capabilities","stance":"rec","why":"Revision competition 256 byte demo","quote":"","text":"I suggest you check out the demoscene if you haven’t. This is “Memories” from the Revision competition: [VIDEO] 256 bytes"},{"id":22928153,"author":"stallmanite","month":"2020-04","story":"Memories – a multipart demoscene demo in only 256 bytes","stance":"rec","why":"256 byte demo producing minutes of video and sound","quote":"The 256 byte category of demos is inspirational.","text":"The 256 byte category of demos is inspirational. The idea that a relative handful of symbols that you could write on a sheet of notebook paper and view in its entirety could produce minutes of video and sound is hard to wrap my mind around. To save anyone else the clicking here’s the video [VIDEO]"},{"id":23002906,"author":"guiambros","month":"2020-04","story":"Memories – 256 bytes demo winner of Revision 2020","stance":"reference","why":"Final product of the 256 byte demo","quote":"The entire video is here [2], if you just want to watch the final product.","text":"The source code really looks like black magic. It's incredible they were able to cram the tunnel effect into 64 bytes [1]. The entire video is here [2], if you just want to watch the final product. EDIT: link fixed. [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO]"},{"id":23004638,"author":"clan","month":"2020-04","story":"Memories – 256 bytes demo winner of Revision 2020","stance":"reference","why":"Demo in action; commenter prefers running the source themselves","quote":"I am always very impressed when I see these demos and how much can be done with so little.","text":"I am always very impressed when I see these demos and how much can be done with so little. If you are like me you just jumped to Youtube[1] to see it in action. When trying to make my significant other to understand what was happening I wanted to run it myself. I was amazed how simple that was! - Install the assembler[2] - Install dosbox[3] - Get the source[4] and put it into c:\\temp\\demo\\memories.asm - Start nasm and enter: cd c:\\temp\\demo nasm.exe memories.asm -fbin -o memories.com - Start dosbox and enter: mount d c:\\temp\\demo d: dir memories - Press [ALT][ENTER] for fullscreen The dosbox config is not optimized but it runs with sound with the default settings! For me this is somehow much more impressive than simply watching the video. [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK] [3] [LINK] [4] [LINK]"}],"r":[["0fKBhvDjuy0",1],["NGFhc8R_uO4",1],["nnR8fDW3Ilo",1],["HCUct4NlxE0",1],["muCPjK4nGY4",1],["QEJ9HrZq7Ro",1]]},"Qam5BkXIEhQ":{"wy":"HN points to it for black hole energy and colonization ideas and recommends the whole channel.","q":{"x":"I highly recommend the whole channel.","i":31377659,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":31377659,"author":"ajuc","month":"2022-05","story":"Can we make a black hole? And if we could, what could we do with it?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Black holes for colonization and energy; recommends the whole channel.","quote":"I highly recommend the whole channel.","text":"Some other uses of black holes: - space propulsion [LINK] - colonization and energy source [VIDEO] - weapons [LINK] I highly recommend the whole channel."},{"id":28235345,"author":"lvncelot","month":"2021-08","story":"A Dyson sphere around a black hole","stance":"rec","why":"Video likely matching the concept discussed, recommended to the curious.","quote":"for everyone else who's intrigued by this comment's concepts - I can recommend watching it.","text":"I'm pretty sure you're referencing this video: [VIDEO] But on the off-chance you're not - and for everyone else who's intrigued by this comment's concepts - I can recommend watching it."},{"id":23212988,"author":"cletus","month":"2020-05","story":"Timeline of the Far Future","stance":"rec","why":"Part of a list of must-see Isaac Arthur videos.","quote":"Mandatory Isaar Arthur links","text":"Mandatory Isaar Arthur links: - Civilizations at the End of Time: Iron Stars [1] - Civilizations at the End of Time: Black Hole Farming [2] - Boltzman Brains and the Anthropic Principle [3] [4] [1]: [LINK] [2]: [VIDEO] [3]: [LINK] [4]: [LINK]"},{"id":21182040,"author":"yourapostasy","month":"2019-10","story":"Not long ago, the center of the Milky Way exploded","stance":"rec","why":"Isaac Arthur as entry point, includes farming black holes.","quote":"My go-to entry point for exploring those kinds of questions was Isaac Arthur.","text":"My go-to entry point for exploring those kinds of questions was Isaac Arthur. He goes into the Kardashev scale [1], dying Earth [2], dying stars [3], farming black holes [4], and going past iron stars [5]. A Boltzmann Brain's description bears remarkable similarity to the Judeo-Christian concept of God before time begins, and has been fertile ground for ruminations [6], though none with any verifiable claims backing them in our current knowledge of physics [7]. Now if we could only restructure our implementation of capitalism to be pinned to advancements towards resolving physics questions to survive those kinds of timescales and challenges instead of locked down to a single planet's worth of baryonic matter and cognitive capacity... [1] [LINK] [2] [LINK] [3] [LINK] [4] [VIDEO] [5] [LINK] [6] [LINK] [7] [LINK]"},{"id":34371794,"author":"cletus","month":"2023-01","story":"The Halo Drive: Fuel-Free Relativistic Propulsion (2019)","stance":"reference","why":"Listed among far-future uses of black holes.","quote":"Colonizing black holes [3][4]","text":"A lot of thought has gone into far-future uses of black holes: - Black hole starships [1] - Weaponizing black holes [2] - Colonizing black holes [3][4] - Black hole computers [5] Black holes may be the most efficient forms of batteries and power generators yet conceived. If our model of the Universe is mostly correct then the Univese will have an era of stars that will ultimately burn through all the fuel and we'll just be left in the dark with black holes that will evaporate over an extraordinarily long period of time, many orders of magnitude than the stellar era. For propulsion, this ultimately comes down to photons have momentum. We can use them to move things. This is the idea behind the solar sail. Black hole propulsion revolves around how you use the momentum from an evaporating black hole into motion for a ship. There are a bunch of factors to balance out here. Too small and the black holes dies too quick (and is too energetic). Too large and it's too hard to move and has a lower output and so on. Turns out there's a sweet spot for all this of about a million tons, which is in the realm of what we call Kugelblitz black holes. I personally think using stellar output to accelerate and decelerate ships it he most likely outcome for a spacefaring species. If fusion ever becomes viable this may be an alternative for when you're not near a star. Ultimately black holes may replace that but there are significant theoretical and engineering challenges to that like how you'd ev"},{"id":31643965,"author":"cletus","month":"2022-06","story":"Amino acids found in asteroid samples collected by Japan's Hayabusa2 probe","stance":"reference","why":"Cited for digital existence in the black hole era.","quote":"A digital existence looks highly likely in the black hole era of the universe [2].","text":"> Consciousness is an emergent property of computation It's interesting to see the negative comments about this here because I agree with you. \"Consciousness\" or even \"sentience\" is notoriously difficult to define. Even defining what an organism is isn't entirely obvious (eg some might consider a colony of ants or bees as being organism-like). Our views tend to be extremely human-centric because we can relate. I imagine once we actually invent an AGI many will debate if it's truly sentient or conscious for a long, long time afterwards. > Computation is more efficient in smaller, less massy media There are physical limits on computation. A smaller package ultimately has a lower limit. That alone will (IMHO) keep a space for very large computational units. At the top end of this is Matrioshka Brains [1]. A digital existence looks highly likely in the black hole era of the universe [2]. This is the era after all the stars have faded out and will be much longer (based on current understanding) than the stellar era, so much so that the 13-14B year lifetime of the universe thus far will be like the blink of an eye. As for detecting communication, that's too narrow a focus. Whether we're miniscule cyborgs that communicate with qubits or living as virtual beings in a world-sized computer, the ultimate limits of what any such civilization can do is is the amount of energy they can consume. More energy means more is possible. So even if we end up living as a K2 civilization in a billio"},{"id":27753210,"author":"cletus","month":"2021-07","story":"Kepler telescope glimpses population of free-floating planets","stance":"reference","why":"Cited for staggering timeframes where a trillion years is a blink.","quote":"To be clear we're talking on truly staggering time frames here where a trillion years is a mere blink of an eye [1][2].","text":"> What's the point of interstellar colonization? Ultimately it's to claim matter, which is the ultimate limiting factor in how long a civilization lives. For example, you talk about engrossing digital worlds. Well, those worlds require energy and energy means matter. To be clear we're talking on truly staggering time frames here where a trillion years is a mere blink of an eye [1][2]. [1]: [VIDEO] [2]: [LINK]"},{"id":26756783,"author":"cletus","month":"2021-04","story":"The Expansion of the Universe","stance":"reference","why":"Frames the visible-light era as the Golden Age of the universe.","quote":"Another perspective is that this will be the Golden Age of the Universe [1].","text":"The visible light era of the Universe is relatively short compared to what comes after. People think of this as a long dark night. To some extent that's true. Another perspective is that this will be the Golden Age of the Universe [1]. [1]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":17033060,"author":"stcredzero","month":"2018-05","story":"No Thanks, Google. I'll Speak for Myself","stance":"reference","why":"Example of detailed, information-dense video content.","quote":"I see a flourishing of detailed video content, much of which is interesting and fairly information dense.","text":"The ship has really already sailed on longform communication, perhaps out of laziness or cost/benefit. Really? I see a flourishing of detailed video content, much of which is interesting and fairly information dense. Examples: Civilizations at the End of Time: Black Hole Farming [VIDEO] CGP Grey. Rules for Rulers [LINK] Granted, that these are really pointers to other sources, in many cases text sources. Cost/Benefit is a big factor! Remember: The street finds its own uses for things."}],"r":[["Pld8wTa16Jk",2],["LMbI6sk-62E",1],["zmCTmgavkrQ",1],["O5BJVO3PDeQ",1],["F-QA2rkpBSY",1],["rDPj5zI66LA",1],["E3URhJx0NSw",1],["o4-YnLpLgtk",1],["OUNXFHpUhu8",1]]},"Z2DU0qLfPIY":{"wy":"HN recommends it as the original LiveView announcement and demo, a compelling argument for server-side rendering as an alternative to JavaScript front ends.","q":{"x":"you can't ignore it, the coolest thing happening right now about server-side rendering","i":19466580,"y":"2019"},"m":[{"id":19466580,"author":"lelf","month":"2019-03","story":"Ask HN: What happened to server-side rendering?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Demo of Phoenix LiveView for server-side rendering.","quote":"you can't ignore it, the coolest thing happening right now about server-side rendering","text":"Phoenix.LiveView — you can't ignore it, the coolest thing happening right now about server-side rendering. Demo: [VIDEO] Links: [LINK] [LINK] PS please stop calling it isomorphic — this is disgrace for the mathematics."},{"id":20853103,"author":"jeremy_k","month":"2019-09","story":"Lumen: An alternative BEAM implementation, designed for WebAssembly","stance":"rec","why":"LiveView announcement keynote and its fit for CRUD-style apps.","quote":"I would encourage you to watch the keynotes on LiveView","text":"I can't give a deep comparison since I haven't done anything too deep with LiveView (yet!) but I would encourage you to watch the keynotes on LiveView[1][2] if you're really interested. I have spent the last few years working with React / Redux on a big SPA and I believe the entire thing could easily be supported by LiveView; we don't do anything fancy, its basically just a bunch of CRUD based pages. Another shallow comparison is that LiveView isn't meant to compete with modern progressive SPAs. While it can update the DOM with data from the server and is very good at doing that, it has limitations such as no offline support. Chris (the speaker in the videos) also notes that if your clients require very good latency or a very complex UI (think Google Sheets) then a JS framework will probably better suit your needs. However, if you're building out a majority of websites (CRUD type pages) that a lot of us web developers do, my personal opinion is that LiveView will be able to everything needed. [1] Announcing LiveView @ ElixirConf 2018 - [VIDEO] [2] LiveView Update @ ElixirConf 2019 - [LINK]"},{"id":19406975,"author":"sfusato","month":"2019-03","story":"Phoenix: LiveView","stance":"rec","why":"LiveView keynote with a demo showing latency is not a bottleneck.","quote":"the video starts at 41:43 showcasing a cool demo where you would expect the latency to be a bottleneck, yet it's not","text":"Check the demos here (look at the snake game regarding latency concerns): [LINK] Or look at the ElixirConf keynote: [VIDEO] (the video starts at 41:43 showcasing a cool demo where you would expect the latency to be a bottleneck, yet it's not)"},{"id":19173356,"author":"karthikksv","month":"2019-02","story":"Show HN: Purview – A server-side component framework","stance":"rec","why":"Chris McCord's ElixirConf talk that inspired Purview.","quote":"Chris gave a great talk about it at ElixirConf","text":"Yes, that was my inspiration! Chris gave a great talk about it at ElixirConf is anyone is interested in watching: [VIDEO] Some differences compared to LiveView: - Type-checking: there are extensive JSX typings ( [LINK] ) that ensure you're attaching event handlers with the correct signatures, specifying supported props, using valid HTML tags and attributes, etc. Static-typing guarantees are one of my big priorities. - I'm not sure if LiveView intends to support nested components like React does. Having the ability to split up complex pages into components that you can nest and reuse (with mostly one-way data flow) is a key part of maintainability. I wanted to maintain a very familiar React interface, so you can pick up Purview quickly if you're comfortable with React."},{"id":18831447,"author":"neya","month":"2019-01","story":"Ask HN: Go-to web stack today?","stance":"rec","why":"Keynote demo of LiveView removing the need for JS frontends.","quote":"Watch they keynote presentation for the liveview demo","text":"If you didn't provide constraints (node/react), I would use the following. Please note, this is highly opinionated, so nothing to get offended or upset about. I like to keep my stack simple: A. Simple static sites: - Jekyll B. Medium complexity, CRUD applications: - Phoenix/Elixir - Coffeescript Note: With the latest version of Phoenix, you absolutely don't any JS frontends at all. Watch they keynote presentation for the liveview demo: [VIDEO] C. Complex Web applications: - Phoenix/Elixir - GraphQL/Absinthe - VueJs + Vuex + Coffeescript Developing with Phoenix/Elixir is so much better than any of the node frameworks in the ecosystem - this has consistently been my experience. So, give it a shot if you can."},{"id":18905394,"author":"snake117","month":"2019-01","story":"Ask HN: Hardware guy needs to learn web application development – what language?","stance":"rec","why":"Chris McCord's 2018 keynote introducing LiveView.","quote":"definitely check out Chris McCord's 2018 keynote","text":"If you are feeling a bit adventurous and open-minded as you begin this venture, I would highly suggest looking into Elixir and Phoenix, especially since you are asking for \"...best bang for the buck\". In fact, the 1.8.0 release for Elixir was just announced earlier today here: [LINK] ( [LINK] ) What makes Phoenix great is that, not only is it blazing fast, but the framework serves as more of a web interface to your application as oppose to \"taking over\" your application, like with other major frameworks. Moreover, you get the full power of Erlang and OTP as well. I will have to say that some things are a little rough still, like deploying your application. This process is not as smooth as what you would experience with a Rails or Django app, but it will definitely get better over time. One of the features of Phoenix that is also really exciting is LiveView. If you have some time, definitely check out Chris McCord's 2018 keynote: [VIDEO] There are a lot of great books and resources to explore. I listed a few below: [LINK] [LINK] [LINK] (just released) [LINK] [LINK] With regards to Python, I cannot give too much recommendation as I have experience in Ruby/Rails and Elixir/Phoenix primarily. However, if you really want to begin building, stick with Python, since you already have experience with that language, and look into a popular framework, like Django or Flask."},{"id":18239870,"author":"mercer","month":"2018-10","story":"Evergreen: a React UI Framework built by Segment","stance":"reference","why":"Phoenix keynote mentioned in a discussion of React maintenance.","quote":"","text":"> If I had to guess, I would guess that React will still be around in 5 years, but I'm not sure web components will. I agree, and this is why I use React (or rather Preact) for both apps and interactive elements on the page. The big caveat is that in practice it's really easy to end up with a React project that is much more difficult to maintain compared to a monolithic framework (Ember, Angular, Vue, even), because of all the other crap that might be used to take care of all the stuff React doesn't do. For example, I recently took on a React project that used a whole bunch of other packages for state management, routing, etc., and a number of those packages were no longer supported (or outright compromised). While it's nice that React is still React, a lot of these other packages are tightly woven into the whole 'fabric' (react-swipeable-redux-router type stuff). For my part, when I start a React project I try to minimize these problems by generally avoiding react-specific packages. So instead of react-router (which had its share of non-backwards-compatible updates), I'll just use page.js for routing. In some cases I might use the reactified version (Redux comes to mind, but I avoid that most of the time for smaller stuff). This still isn't a panacea, and as a result I've been moving more and more stuff to a more 'vanilla' Railsy backend (Elixir/Phoenix, specifically). While the ecosystem does seem to be stabilizing a bit, it's still insane, and thankfully there are or will "},{"id":17963915,"author":"mrdoops","month":"2018-09","story":"The “Developer Experience” Bait-And-Switch","stance":"reference","why":"Keynote showing server-rendered HTML over websockets narrowing the SPA gap.","quote":"You can render on the fly, and deliver new HTML over websockets.","text":"Don't even need Ajax anymore. You can render on the fly, and deliver new HTML over websockets. The gap between SPA and classic server-side rendering is getting smaller fast. [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["xrIjfIjssLE",1],["rRbY3TMUcgQ",1],["7y1xJAVZxXg",1]]},"gV5obrYaogU":{"wy":"HN cites it as a startling example of how advanced Lisp machine interactive graphics were, often in Lisp and REPL-driven development discussions.","q":{"x":"I really enjoyed the Symbolics graphics and animations demo[0] linked in the article.","i":18226522,"y":"2018"},"m":[{"id":18226522,"author":"loosetypes","month":"2018-10","story":"How Lisp Became God's Own Programming Language","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Surprisingly advanced 1980s Lisp machine graphics and animation","quote":"I really enjoyed the Symbolics graphics and animations demo[0] linked in the article.","text":"For whatever reason I really enjoyed the Symbolics graphics and animations demo[0] linked in the article. I was born in the 90s and have constantly heard the narrative that technological progress is getting faster and faster, and that we're currently at the forefront. And then I see something like this. They had basically photoshop before I was even alive!? [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":41767000,"author":"hcarvalhoalves","month":"2024-10","story":"The Rise of Worse Is Better (1991)","stance":"rec","why":"Interactive Lisp systems that outclass modern editors","quote":"It makes working on VSCode today look like banging rocks together, let alone 30 years ago.","text":"See these demos for the kinds of interactive systems LISP enabled back in the day: [LINK] [VIDEO] It makes working on VSCode today look like banging rocks together, let alone 30 years ago."},{"id":30948200,"author":"mepian","month":"2022-04","story":"The computers used to do 3D animation for Final Fantasy VII in 1996","stance":"rec","why":"Symbolics 3D animation system in action","quote":"If you want to see it in action, watch this","text":"I'm glad the middle screen is explained, Symbolics always gets overshadowed by SGI. If you want to see it in action, watch this: [VIDEO]"},{"id":25622823,"author":"pjmlp","month":"2021-01","story":"On Repl-Driven Programming","stance":"rec","why":"One of several classic demos of early interactive environments","quote":"Be prepared to be amazed with what Xerox PARC and others were doing","text":"Be prepared to be amazed with what Xerox PARC and others were doing, while Bell Labs was busy pushing for UNIX. \"Eric Bier Demonstrates Cedar\" [LINK] \"Emulating a Xerox Star (8010) Information System Running the Xerox Development Environment (XDE) 5.0\" [LINK] \"Documents as User Interfaces Video Demo\" [LINK] \"SYMBOLICS S-PACKAGES 3D GRAPHICS AND ANIMATION DEMO\" [VIDEO] \"Alto System Project: Dan Ingalls demonstrates Smalltalk\" [LINK] \"Action!, the worlds first dynamic interface builder - 1988\" (Interface Builder percursor, written in Lisp) [LINK] \"The Interlisp programming environment\" [LINK] And the cherry, how Lucid used Lisp ideas for their Energize C++ IDE, including an image based format for AST storage [LINK] [LINK]"},{"id":48096177,"author":"pjmlp","month":"2026-05","story":"Ratty – A terminal emulator with inline 3D graphics","stance":"reference","why":"S-Package used to develop for Nintendo 64","quote":"this time with S-PACKAGE used to develop Nintendo 64.","text":"The example on the linked video it isn't, correct. Here is another video, this time with S-PACKAGE used to develop Nintendo 64. [VIDEO] Which given the REPL capabilities, you can easily embedd them on it, just like the other video."},{"id":48309077,"author":"pjmlp","month":"2026-05","story":"Why Ctrl+V won't paste images in Claude Code on WSL, with a fix","stance":"reference","why":"S-Package used to develop for Nintendo 64","quote":"Or using S-PACKAGE used to develop Nintendo 64.","text":"SBCL REPL cannot do this on xterm, it needs a proper hosting REPL environment like SLIME, which is no wonder, given how Emacs came to be and the interaction with genera. Inline graphics from 1981, [LINK] Or using S-PACKAGE used to develop Nintendo 64. [VIDEO] You are missing integration with live debugging, calling anything on the OS during a REPL session, e.g. OS APIs, calling into automation points of the OS, Can try out directly on the browser, courtesy of WebAssembly and recovery of Xerox PARC software, [LINK] . Or get either Squeak or Pharo, and see how using the Transcript integrates with the whole platform Windows, with either PowerShell ISE, or its new VSCode integration, are the closest to these kind of experiences. To finalise with Xerox PARC view on UNIX, from 1989 \"UNIX Needs A True Integrated Environment: CASE Closed\" [LINK]"},{"id":33991573,"author":"33985868","month":"2022-12","story":"A trip through the Graphics Pipeline","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"You're very welcome. Computer graphics has a very interesting history, and like many things, there's a lot to learn from studying it. Surprisingly, many techniques and principles are still relevant to this day. Thankfully, the Internet is full of documentation, post-mortem accounts and reference implementations to learn more about all this. Here's a bunch of random noteworthy things and references I forgot to link (sorry for potentially digging rabbit holes): The best information tends to come from official documentation detailing almost everything you want to know about the inner workings of systems. Additionally, unofficial community documentation can also be of great quality and complement official sources. The architecture overview posts from Rodrigo Copetti ( [LINK] ) pack a lot of accurate information at a glance, and are a great starting point if a specific topic piques your interest. The Resident Evil 2 microcode post-mortem: [LINK] Improved lighting, reverberation, Dolby surround and MP3 microcodes from Rare: [LINK] H.264 decoding microcode: [LINK] The Reality Signal Processor's programming guide: [LINK] Emulators' progress reports can reveal a lot about the details and intricacies required for accurately replicating these systems' features: [LINK] is one such amazing source of information, but all other major emulation efforts have equally interesting content. Transparency sorting of surfaces ( [LINK] ) is a hard problem to solve for traditional scanline renderers ("},{"id":27639081,"author":"ur-whale","month":"2021-06","story":"Gloss3D – an open source 3D Modeler for Linux and Windows","stance":"reference","why":"Symbolics system inspired Wings3D workflow","quote":"it was actually inspired by the Symbolics 3D animation system [1]","text":"Wings3D's UI workflow is really nice for 3D mesh modeling (it was actually inspired by the Symbolics 3D animation system [1]). Blender feels very cumbersome when you learned 3D modeling with Wings. It's really unfortunate that they chose to implement it in a language (ERLANG) that severely limits the number of people that can contribute to the software, and as a result, the development of Wings sort of stagnates. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":26530527,"author":"pjmlp","month":"2021-03","story":"Emacs is the 2D Command-line Interface","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"Here are some videos that try to convey it, from my playlists,","text":"On Oberon, Mesa/Cedar and Lisp Machines, the experience extends to the whole OS and any application, or REPL, not just a plain text editor. Althought I haven't mentioned, Smalltalk also provides a similar experience. Here are some videos that try to convey it, from my playlists, \"Eric Bier Demonstrates Cedar\" [LINK] \"Emulating a Xerox Star (8010) Information System Running the Xerox Development Environment (XDE) 5.0\" [LINK] \"SymbolicsTalk28June2012\" \"SYMBOLICS S-PACKAGES 3D GRAPHICS AND ANIMATION DEMO\" [VIDEO] \"Symbolics Lisp Machine demo Jan 2013\" [LINK] The Native Oberon demos available at [LINK]"},{"id":23212974,"author":"pavlov","month":"2020-05","story":"Ronin – A Lisp-based image processing tool","stance":"reference","why":"1989 Symbolics 3D graphics suite demo","quote":"Symbolics had a Lisp-programmable 3D graphics suite that seems to have been fairly successful at the end of the decade.","text":"Lisp has a long history in computer graphics. It was particularly popular in the 1980s. There was Symbolics, a manufacturer of Lisp workstations (sometimes also remembered as the first company to register a .com domain). Symbolics had a Lisp-programmable 3D graphics suite that seems to have been fairly successful at the end of the decade. Here’s a video from 1989: [VIDEO] The video doesn’t seem to show any Lisp though. On the 2D side, the immensely popular AutoCAD adopted Lisp as its expansion language in 1985. Autodesk founder John Walker explains the decision in this contemporary memo “Why Lisp”: [LINK]"},{"id":23218518,"author":"dmix","month":"2020-05","story":"Ronin – A Lisp-based image processing tool","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"Reminds me of Symbolics S-PACKAGES from the 1980s... (also LISP)","text":"Reminds me of Symbolics S-PACKAGES from the 1980s... (also LISP) [VIDEO] Edit: looks like someone beat me to it below"},{"id":22502459,"author":"hhdave","month":"2020-03","story":"CLUI: Building a Graphical Command Line","stance":"reference","why":"Symbolics S-packages built on command and presentation concepts","quote":"The Symbolics S-packages used these concepts to make (I presume) powerful 3d modelling programs back in the day","text":"Genera on the Lisp Machine is very much an embodiment of this graphical command line (or rather just command) user interface. There's quite a lot to it. CLIM (and the open source McCLIM) is the current realisation of this. There's some documentation about 'Commands' in CLIM II here for example: [LINK] Commands are first class objects which live in command tables and which CLIM can look at to generate bits of UI and allow parameters to be specified. Central to this in CLIM is the idea of presentations - where domain objects are 'presented' onto the screen and the link to the domain object is maintained. That way you can invoke a command on that object in various ways, for example you can type a command name and then click a presented object which is acceptable as a parameter. The Symbolics S-packages used these concepts to make (I presume) powerful 3d modelling programs back in the day (I haven't used them): [VIDEO] - sorry for the poor video quality. [LINK] (lispm on here) sometimes posts interesting things about Genera"},{"id":21240075,"author":"lispm","month":"2019-10","story":"Show HN: Bel","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"> i use word count as the benchmark value Words are called 'symbols' in Lisp. Making extremely expressive domain level constructs is reducing the code size a lot in larger Lisp programs. > Lisp is not a particularly declarative type of language Just the opposite. Lisp is one of the major tools to write declarative code. The declarations are a part of the running system and can be queried&changed while the program is running. > Lisp ... all win contests for fewest characters, Most Lisp since the end 70s don't care about character count. Lisp usually uses very descriptive symbols as operator names. Lisp users invented machines with larger memory sizes in the late 70s, to get rid of limitations in code and data size. Paul Graham is favoring low-character count code, but this is not representative for general Lisp code, which favors low-symbol count code through expressive constructs. > understand this domain specific language without executing the code the languages will be documented and will be operators in the language. Other programming languages also create large vocabularities, but in different ways. Lisp makes it easy to integrate syntactic abstractions in the software, without the need to write external languages, which many other systems need to do. For example one can see the Common Lisp Object System as a domain-specific extension to write object-oriented code in Lisp. It's constructs are well documented and widely use in Lisp. > it/is firmly routed in the terminal/co"},{"id":13785988,"author":"lispm","month":"2017-03","story":"The Unix-Haters Handbook (1994) [pdf]","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"There was never a really cheap Lispm. The 'cheap' ones were a Mac II + TI microexplorer or the Mac II + MacIvory. But developer workstations were always expensive - Lispm or not - or underpowered. What was also expensive was the software. I got a KEE license on a used machine I was given. I think it cost $50k. Similar Symbolics extensions for their OS could be extremely expensive. The graphics suite did cost several 10k per module (paint, model, render, tools, ...). > a system that was so much designed for programmers was probably never going to command a very big market Well, they could not live from developers alone. They were also looking for end users. Some of the machines were thought as delivery machines, cheaper and with less resources. There were also tools like 'firewall', which were supposed to keep users from getting in contact with the development environment (like debugger, ...). Actually Symbolics also targeted high-end endusers, both with hardware and with software. They had to go beyond government developers - to be able to sell machines/software. There were applications that were kind of development centric, like the iCAD cad system - where one could program 3d models via Flavors extensions. But one huge area for Symbolics was the graphics market. 2d, 2.5d animation, 3d animation, paint/model/render. Much of that was used without programming. The graphics suite was mostly targeted at graphics professionals, often doing 3d visualizations for TV, cinema and gam"},{"id":7303882,"author":"mjn","month":"2014-02","story":"Why Did Symbolics Fail? (2009)","stance":"reference","why":"Symbolics S-Package 3D modeling and animation system demo","quote":"Some bits from that era: the S-Package 3d modeling/animation system","text":"Good article I hadn't run across before; thanks for linking it. One interesting side story is the odd Symbolics foray into animation and video through the Symbolics Graphics Division, which afaict was reasonably successful for a few years. It seemed like the sort of application-oriented business that might let the Symbolics machines break out of single-purpose \"AI machines\" into being seen as general workstations, or at least dual-purpose AI/graphics workstations. But that business seems to have sort of evaporated along with the rest of their business, despite not being AI and there being no \"graphics winter\". Perhaps steamrollered by Silicon Graphics, and the same trends towards Unix and commodity CPUs? Or not marketed well enough, by a company whose public materials focused too exclusively on Lisp+AI? Some bits from that era: the S-Package 3d modeling/animation system ( [VIDEO] ), the PaintAmation package ( [LINK] ), and the first HDTV processing on a workstation ( [LINK] )."}],"r":[["NH1Tta7purM",1],["xnqTKD8uD64",1],["YnWhqhNdYyk",1],["DRq2NgeFcO0",1],["z_dt7NG38V4",1],["JfmTagWcqoE",1],["D7Sd8A6_fYU",1],["cj02_UeUnGQ",1],["YX3iRjKj7C0",1],["XkDEzfpdcSg",1],["hEx5DNLWGgA",1],["NqKyHEJe9_w",1],["uknEhXyZgsg",1],["AnrlSqtpOkw",1],["lgivCGdmFrw",1],["ds77e3aO9nA",1],["CnbVCNIh1NA",1],["lyu7v7nWzfo",1],["S0qjK3TWZE8",1],["7PoajCqNKpg",1]]},"816VLQNdPMM":{"wy":"HN points to it as the standard explanation of why perfect pitch is a childhood window, in threads about absolute pitch training.","q":{"x":"This video convinced me that adults cannot develop or train perfect pitch.","i":35760692,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":35760692,"author":"rendall","month":"2023-04","story":"Children aged 2-6 successfully trained to acquire absolute pitch","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Convinced the commenter adults cannot train perfect pitch.","quote":"This video convinced me that adults cannot develop or train perfect pitch.","text":"This video convinced me that adults cannot develop or train perfect pitch. \"Why Adults Can't Develop Perfect Pitch\" by Rick Beato [VIDEO]"},{"id":15780929,"author":"lj3","month":"2017-11","story":"How I learned to write music in real time","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Best video on perfect pitch; digs into the science after four minutes.","quote":"Here's the best video[0] on Perfect Pitch I've seen.","text":"Perfect Pitch is 30% more likely in societies with tonal language. Kids who are exposed to complicated music at an early age also have a higher chance of having perfect pitch. Here's the best video[0] on Perfect Pitch I've seen. It starts at 4:25 because he spends the first four minutes responding to indignant youtube commenters. Then he digs into the science. [0]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":36425336,"author":"kerblang","month":"2023-06","story":"How My Children (N=2) Acquired Absolute Pitch","stance":"rec","why":"One of the more interesting Beato videos on perfect pitch.","quote":"Here's one of the more interesting videos","text":"Rick Beato made several videos about perfect pitch, also featuring his kid who has PP and can identify entire chords. Apparently his opinion is that the key was listening to atonal music at an early age, like you say. Here's one of the more interesting videos: [VIDEO]"},{"id":35758483,"author":"hashmap","month":"2023-04","story":"Children aged 2-6 successfully trained to acquire absolute pitch","stance":"rec","why":"Covers why trained pitch memory is not absolute pitch.","quote":"Rick Beato covers the subject rather well in his video","text":"This isn't quite right; the University of Chicago study replicates what has already been known for a long time - that musicians can develop their pitch memory around the timbral context clues of a particular instrument. However, this learned pitch does not generalize to other instruments and/or common everyday sounds (train horns, washing machine vibrations) in the way that people with absolute pitch can instantly identify. An analogy to this might be that a colorblind person if shown enough fabric and told what the colors were can eventually associate the texture of the fabric with a given color and can learn to do this translating in their head, but they will not be able to train their eyes to see color no matter how much practice takes place. Rick Beato covers the subject rather well in his video \"Why Adults Can't Develop Perfect Pitch\" [VIDEO] If it were the case that such a substantial number of amateur test subjects could develop absolute pitch from doing a few exercises, we would expect to see commensurate numbers of musicians who spend thousands of hours playing music to also develop this skill, but this is not and has never been the case."},{"id":43907808,"author":"lylejantzi3rd","month":"2025-05","story":"Infants and Babies can become pitch-perfect with early exposure to certain music","stance":"reference","why":"Listed among Beato's perfect pitch videos.","quote":"Rick Beato has done a lot of videos on perfect pitch.","text":"Rick Beato has done a lot of videos on perfect pitch. His son has perfect pitch, but he does not. How Children Develop Perfect Pitch - [LINK] Perfect Pitch vs Relative Pitch: Which Is More Important? - [LINK] Why Adults Can't Develop Perfect Pitch - [VIDEO]"},{"id":35757503,"author":"avodonosov","month":"2023-04","story":"Children aged 2-6 successfully trained to acquire absolute pitch","stance":"reference","why":"Beato's hypothesis that young children can acquire perfect pitch.","quote":"In another video Rick Beato said he facilitated development of his son's perfect pitch","text":"In another video Rick Beato said he facilitated development of his son's perfect pitch by exposing him to a lot of complex music in early age. [VIDEO] His hypothesis, based on some studies, is that children in ealy age have ability for perfect pitch but later loose it, if it's not utilized. The same way as young children are able to percieve any phoneme of any language, but later loose this ability and only recognise phonemes of the language spoken in the family (that's a known scientific fact). [LINK]"},{"id":24873650,"author":"grizmaldi","month":"2020-10","story":"Show HN: Perfect Pitch Ear Training","stance":"reference","why":"Beato on why adults cannot develop perfect pitch.","quote":"Rick Beato's take on adults not being able to develop perfect pitch","text":"Rick Beato's take on adults not being able to develop perfect pitch - [VIDEO] I must say though, watching videos of his kid demonstrate perfect pitch is quite astounding!"},{"id":24874698,"author":"Dowwie","month":"2020-10","story":"Show HN: Perfect Pitch Ear Training","stance":"reference","why":"Beato says perfect pitch must be trained before age ten.","quote":"a child can learn pitch perfection but it must be trained before 10 years of age","text":"Rick Beato explains on his YouTube that a child can learn pitch perfection but it must be trained before 10 years of age [VIDEO]"},{"id":20698316,"author":"swframe2","month":"2019-08","story":"Rachmaninoff – Little Red Riding Hood [video]","stance":"reference","why":"Related video on perfect pitch.","quote":"Check out Rick Beato's video","text":"Somewhat related. Maybe you can use these videos to teach your new baby prefect pitch. Check out Rick Beato's video: [VIDEO]"},{"id":18242594,"author":"mistersquid","month":"2018-10","story":"Acquiring absolute pitch in adulthood is difficult but possible","stance":"reference","why":"Beato explains why adults cannot develop perfect pitch, after lengthy context.","quote":"Rick Beato provides a lot of (too much) context before explaining","text":"(Disclosure/Disclaimer: I don't have perfect pitch nor do I aspire to acquire it. I am, however, fascinated that some people do have perfect pitch.) Rick Beato provides a lot of (too much) context before explaining \"Why Adults Can't Develop Perfect Pitch\". [0] (Timecode link to Beato's point.) Beato's video was featured on MetaFilter a couple of months ago. [1] [0] [VIDEO] [1] [LINK]"}],"r":[["hpgK51w6uhk",1],["uxykI30fS54",1],["V6VP-2aIcSc",1],["cfyWvJdsDRI",1]]},"amJ-ev8Ial8":{"wy":"HN commenters use it as a source of study references suggesting standard low-salt advice may be too restrictive.","q":{"x":"This is a nice video discussing some studies that show that high levels of salt is significantly better if it comes with high levels of potassium","i":18460108,"y":"2018"},"m":[{"id":42665835,"author":"lylejantzi3rd","month":"2025-01","story":"Kirin demos \"electric salt spoon\" at CES","stance":"rec","why":"Part of a series arguing you probably need more salt.","quote":"He has 3 more salt videos, if you're intrigued.","text":"> Is salt really that bad? Here's a brief video that makes the claim that you probably need more salt in your diet. He has 3 more salt videos, if you're intrigued. You probably need more salt (short): [LINK] Salt: Are you getting Enough?: [VIDEO] WHY Low Salt Stresses the Body: [LINK] Low Sodium's link to Fat Gain & Insulin Resistance: [LINK]"},{"id":34195674,"author":"balaji1","month":"2022-12","story":"Ask HN: Does sweet sensitivity go down with age?","stance":"rec","why":"Suggests recommended salt amounts might be too low.","quote":"This what I’ve learned video discusses salt and recommended standard sale amount might be too low","text":"Salt could be misunderstood. This what I’ve learned video discusses salt and recommended standard sale amount might be too low: [VIDEO] But if the GP comment is listening to his body, it’s fine. I personally prefer more salty food(not snacks) than others around me. And the only opposition seems to be that less salt is better. And less salt means more mental stability."},{"id":18460108,"author":"mtgx","month":"2018-11","story":"Western lifestyle may cause blood pressure to rise with age","stance":"rec","why":"Nice discussion of studies on salt with high potassium.","quote":"This is a nice video discussing some studies that show that high levels of salt is significantly better if it comes with high levels of potassium","text":"I really don't think high amounts of salt is a big deal, unless you almost never eat foods with potassium, too. But even then high levels of salt is probably a bigger issue for people who already have border-line high blood pressure due to atherosclerosis, and then eating a lot of salt pushes them over the limit. It's also much easier for doctors to say \"eat less salt\" than to say \"eat more potassium-rich foods\", even if they know that the two need to be in balanced. When doing a 5-day fasting recently I purposefully tried to consume significant amounts of salt because I felt like I was lacking in electrolytes. I could barely maintain my blood pressure around 10.5/6. Normally, it's around 11.5-12/7. This is a nice video discussing some studies that show that high levels of salt is significantly better if it comes with high levels of potassium compared to low levels of salt but even lower levels of potassium (by ratio): [VIDEO]"},{"id":18112822,"author":"mtgx","month":"2018-10","story":"A bone-marrow transplant treated a patient’s leukemia – and his schizophrenia","stance":"rec","why":"Gives studies backing the salt-to-potassium proportion.","quote":"This video has some references to studies backing this","text":"It seems that high amounts of salt are \"bad for you\" only in the context of low potassium . But most doctors either aren't aware of the fact that there needs to be a certain proportion of salt and potassium, or they don't bother telling patients because the doctors know most people won't eat anymore potassium-rich foods, so they just tell them to \"eat less salt.\" This video has some references to studies backing this: [VIDEO] There's also this book that argues for the importance of salt in our food, of which I heard good things but haven't read: [LINK]"},{"id":15963855,"author":"biaachmonkie","month":"2017-12","story":"In Raising the World’s I.Q., the Secret’s in the Salt (2006)","stance":"reference","why":"Cited on salt intake having halved over a century.","quote":"In the US at least on average people are getting about 1/2 the salt they where 100 years ago.","text":"In the US at least on average people are getting about 1/2 the salt they where 100 years ago. We no longer preserve food with salt primarily we use refrigeration now. [Salt: Are you getting Enough?] [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["TgFdics3uKo",1],["Bea4FS-zDzc",1],["WTYet-qf1jo",1],["tk862BbjWx4",1],["rStL7niR7gs",1],["LMbI6sk-62E",1],["lr4MmmWQtZM",1],["qp0HIF3SfI4",1],["nJPERZDfyWc",1],["mw2z9lV3W1g",1],["v3pK0dccQ38",1],["dBnniua6-oM",1],["1gEz__sMVaY",1]]},"snz7JJlSZvw":{"wy":"Recommended to anyone interested in telescope making as a guide from Dobson himself.","q":{"x":"Here's a 90 minute youtube video on how to build one, with John Dobson himself","i":26256267,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":43353139,"author":"nvalis","month":"2025-03","story":"Amateur Telescope Making Main Page","stance":"rec","why":"Detailed documentation of telescope building techniques with Dobson's insights.","quote":"There is a very detailed video documentation [0] about telescope building techniques","text":"There is a very detailed video documentation [0] about telescope building techniques, featuring insights from John Dobson, the inventor of the Dobsonian telescope mentioned on the page. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":26256267,"author":"Prey4Jesus","month":"2021-02","story":"Ask HN: What entry-level telescope should I choose?","stance":"rec","why":"90 minute guide to building a Dobsonian with Dobson himself.","quote":"Here's a 90 minute youtube video on how to build one, with John Dobson himself","text":"The most important thing to look for is the diameter of the aperture. Your resolution is proportional to the diameter of the telescope (absent atmospheric conditions). I would recommend a minimum of 6\" in diameter. 8-12\" is preferred. With this in mind, the best telescope you can get for the price is a Dobsonian. Dobsonians are extremely mechanically and optically simple. This makes them long, and bulky. It also makes them cheap. It also makes them rather easy to point. They have a lot of mass and inertia, and a large moment of inertia. This makes them easy to move and point slowly and carefully. Dobsonians are ideal beginner telescopes. Easy to point, cheap, good resolution. This is the model I have (8\" aperture, $450): [LINK] They're also rather easy to construct, if you are of a DIY mind. Here's a 90 minute youtube video on how to build one, with John Dobson himself: [VIDEO]"},{"id":22897343,"author":"DanBC","month":"2020-04","story":"Hand Scraping a Truly Flat Plane (1908) [pdf]","stance":"rec","why":"Dobson builds a complete telescope including hand grinding the mirrors.","quote":"I like this video from John Dobson about handmade telescopes","text":"I like this video from John Dobson about handmade telescopes, where he builds a complete telescope including hand grinding the mirrors. [VIDEO]"},{"id":13840144,"author":"danbruc","month":"2017-03","story":"Mirror grinding","stance":"rec","why":"90 minute film of Dobson building a 16 inch telescope from scratch.","quote":"There is also a 90 minutes film","text":"There is also a 90 minutes film »Telescope building with John Dobson« [1], inventor of the Dobsonian telescope [2], going through the entire process of building a 16\" telescope from scratch. [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK]"},{"id":34943747,"author":"joshvm","month":"2023-02","story":"Online Lens/Optics Simulator","stance":"reference","why":"Dobson is known for cheap home-made telescope mirrors.","quote":"This is what John Dobson (of the Dobsonian) is famous for.","text":"I'm not sure if it will necessarily be cheap, maybe cheaper than an off the shelf mirror of equivalent size. This is what John Dobson (of the Dobsonian) is famous for. However you still need to do stuff like coat the primary mirror once it's polished and you can't easily do that at home. See [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["-6rWqJhDv7M",1],["pBTvmrRGlbE",1],["w_znRopGtbE",1],["-Qph8BNrnLY",1]]},"17oCQakzIl8":{"wy":"HN points to it as an accessible, well-made mix of education and entertainment for anyone wanting to understand the Xinjiang situation.","q":{"x":"John Oliver did an excellent piece on the situation of the uighur last week","i":24057879,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":24057879,"author":"cycomanic","month":"2020-08","story":"China Uighurs: A model's video gives a rare glimpse inside internment","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Excellent mix of education and entertainment on Uighur situation","quote":"John Oliver did an excellent piece on the situation of the uighur last week","text":"John Oliver did an excellent piece on the situation of the uighur last week you can watch it here: [VIDEO] I have to say he has really developed his show into an excellent mix of education and entertainment. I used to not like him very much (didn't like his type of humor), but I learn something every time I watch one of his shows, even if it covers a topic I'm already quite familiar with."},{"id":43719023,"author":"blagie","month":"2025-04","story":"TikTok is harming children at an industrial scale","stance":"rec","why":"Learn about persecution of Uyghurs","quote":"you can learn more here","text":"I see. That question makes sense. I don't hate China. I'm very glad we have a competing model of governance, and I'd like to see a diverse range of governments in the world. China is experimenting with a very different model, and for all the faults with that model, I'm grateful for that. If the goal is to understand the anger and hatred, a good place to start is the persecution of the Uyghurs. That one is pretty over-the-top evil, and you can learn more here: [VIDEO] There are many other such things happening. In general, hate is based on cherrypicking the bad things about \"them\" and the good things about \"us.\" That's as true across political lines, as across countries, as across some religious divides. To your original question: \"What makes the Chinese government evil in a way that the US Government is not,\" the US government does NOT do things like this. It does a completely different set of evil things, and a completely different set of good things. Part of the reason cherrypicking works is there is no equivalence between e.g. Muslims in Gitmo and cultural oppression in Tibet. They're different. The Chinese government is evil in a way the US government is not, good in a way the US government is not (and vice-versa). Which is worse depends on your set of values, and consequently, it's very easy to make one side seem like pure evil and the other side to seem like pure good. All that said, I do think TikTok (and a lot of other social media) has about the same level of negativ"},{"id":26605900,"author":"mkolodny","month":"2021-03","story":"Nike Statement on Xinjiang","stance":"rec","why":"Great episode explaining what is happening in Xinjiang","quote":"this is a great Last Week Tonight episode about it","text":"In case anyone's not aware of what's happening in Xinjiang, this is a great Last Week Tonight episode about it: [VIDEO]"},{"id":37968156,"author":"cableshaft","month":"2023-10","story":"The Problem with Jon Stewart cancellation highlights a problem for Apple content","stance":"reference","why":"Example of a show covering Uighur treatment without being cancelled","quote":"John Oliver also had a whole show about China and their treatment of Uighurs","text":"Well AT&T and Warner/Discovery own the show, so one significant difference is it's a joke about just their company that they can determine if they're okay with or not, and don't have to worry about losing anything due to external third party retaliation. Whereas with a topic about China, that's a whole government that has a history of not being able to take a joke, and will likely retaliate and cost Apple a significant amount of money. Not saying it's good or right, though. John Oliver also had a whole show about China and their treatment of Uighurs, though, so that seems to be a more direct comparison. That aired back in 2020 and his show hasn't been cancelled yet. [VIDEO]"},{"id":24298050,"author":"netsharc","month":"2020-08","story":"Blanked-out spots on China's maps helped us uncover Xinjiang's camps","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"They could put cartoon images of \"land\", like this TV show's overlay on top of a lady wearing Muslim dress: [VIDEO] Interesting how the IMO Chinese \"Eh, imma just half-ass it!\" mentality (hopefully having visited the country and experienced half-assery, I'm allowed to comment) basically lead to this research work."},{"id":24304077,"author":"nedsma","month":"2020-08","story":"China Built a Vast New Infrastructure to Imprison Uighurs","stance":"reference","why":"Oliver episode on Uighurs in China","quote":"John Oliver recently made a episode on the subject","text":"John Oliver recently made a episode on the subject (China & Uighurs: Last Week Tonight with John Oliver (HBO)) [VIDEO] The Communist Party of China (CPC) are running sinicization and antireligious campaigns of ethnic and religious minorities. The totalitarian and authoritarian countries don't make good neighbors. China will be making inroads into Taiwan soon if the world does not act."}],"r":[["If61baWF4GE",1],["5OtKEetGy2Y",1],["5pidokakU4I",1]]},"24OXzIRIiMQ":{"wy":"HN fans share it as a way to hear the game's philosophical, sci-fi quotes in sequence.","q":{"x":"I find them to be timeless, and quite heady when listened to in sequence.","i":21904360,"y":"2019"},"m":[{"id":22753667,"author":"kyuudou","month":"2020-04","story":"The Collective Body: Russian experiments in life after death","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Special project videos are some of the best sci-fi creative work","quote":"The special projects videos in that game are some of the best science fiction creative work ever made, IMO.","text":"The special projects videos in that game are some of the best science fiction creative work ever made, IMO. Also, something I still think is predictive and sends chills up my neck, \"as the Americans painfully learned in Earth's final century\" (presumably the 21st): [LINK] Text of the whole spoken: \"As the Americans learned so painfully in Earth's final century, free flow of information is the only safeguard against tyranny. The once-chained people whose leaders at last lose their grip on information flow will soon burst with freedom and vitality, but the free nation gradually constricting its grip on public discourse has begun its rapid slide into despotism. Beware of he who would deny you access to information, for in his heart he dreams himself your master.\" -- Commissioner Pravin Lal, \"U.N. Declaration of Rights\"\" All special projects video text, starting with 0-24: [VIDEO] Cutscene vids start here: [LINK] Although a bit dated, I still don't see this kind of intellectual quality in most games. Paradox Interactive's Stellaris is about the closest thing to a spiritual successor to SMAC, but it's also its own beast and next-gen 4X."},{"id":24736480,"author":"duxup","month":"2020-10","story":"Sid Meier's Memoir","stance":"rec","why":"Clips and quotes give one of gaming's most interesting atmospheres","quote":"one of the most interesting stories / atmospheres in video game history","text":"Some how Sid Meier's Alpha Centauri was a 4x game that had one of the most interesting stories / atmospheres in video game history, even if it was just from a few clips and quotes. [VIDEO]"},{"id":21904360,"author":"alchemism","month":"2019-12","story":"Paean to Sid Meier's Alpha Centauri (2016)","stance":"rec","why":"Timeless, heady game quotes best heard in sequence","quote":"I find them to be timeless, and quite heady when listened to in sequence.","text":"Some thoughtful sorts put all of them into a series of YouTube segments. I find them to be timeless, and quite heady when listened to in sequence. [VIDEO]"},{"id":11679417,"author":"civilian","month":"2016-05","story":"Civilization 6 is coming in October, with big changes","stance":"reference","why":"Tech quotes from the game available on YouTube","quote":"You can find the technology quotes on youtube","text":"Yeah it did. You can find the technology quotes on youtube: [VIDEO] > “He held his arm too stiffly, and so was thrown back repeatedly, until at last I seized his forearm and snapped it back against itself. His training suffered while the arm healed, of course, but I felt this was a lesson he must learn early, and well.” I know that my brother had difficulty with this quote. The mental image of someone's arm being jui-jitsu'd off is pretty powerful. My brother would click \"OKAY\" as fast as possible to avoid listening to the quote."}],"r":[["VPjODKUxV5g",1],["UWvRorX0KhQ",1],["hSrGfElyfVE",1],["OINAk2xl8Bc",1],["iY57ErBkFFE",1],["p_iZr2-Coqc",1]]},"AxZWxxZ2JGE":{"wy":"Used in hiring and management debates as an analysis of fast evaluation, quick dismissal without stigma, and the cost of keeping underperformers.","q":{"x":"Possibly the best analysis of this I have yet seen is the video","i":29168746,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":29168746,"author":"m0llusk","month":"2021-11","story":"Coding Interviews Are BS (largely)","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Hire quickly, evaluate within weeks, dismiss fast without stigma","quote":"Possibly the best analysis of this I have yet seen is the video","text":"Possibly the best analysis of this I have yet seen is the video Why our generals were most successful in World War II that in Korea, Vietnam or Iraq/Afghanistan: [VIDEO] Essentially, the answer is the hiring methodology. If someone seemed like a best fit for a job then they were hired. They then had two weeks to demonstrate basic functional capacity and six weeks to make significant progress. If at any point things did not work out then they were immediately dismissed and replaced with someone else. The high rate of this process meant that there were always open opportunities even for those who had been dismissed from other positions, so things worked out for both employers and employees."},{"id":39851998,"author":"sofrimiento","month":"2024-03","story":"Peter principle","stance":"rec","why":"Ricks on culture of firing officers during WWII versus later wars","quote":"This is a interesting talk held on UC Berkeley about the culture of firing officers during WWII","text":"This is a interesting talk held on UC Berkeley about the culture of firing officers during WWII: [VIDEO] Thomas E. Ricks main hypothesis is that the US failures in Vietnam, Afghanistan and Iraq can be attributed to the culture of firing under-performing disappearing. It mirrors what I've seen in a lot of organizations as well, under-performing executives keeping their jobs when they shouldn't."},{"id":23478532,"author":"AdrianB1","month":"2020-06","story":"Advice to new managers: don't joke about firing people","stance":"rec","why":"Lengthy explanation of why firing underperformers matters","quote":"There is a lengthy explanation in this","text":"Never joke about firing people, but don't hesitate to do it. That is the complete advice. Joking about firing people never brought anything good; firing people when needed, that is essential for organizations. There is a lengthy explanation in this: \"Why our generals were more successful in World War II than in Korea, Vietnam or Iraq/Afghanistan\" ( [VIDEO] )"},{"id":34091838,"author":"m0llusk","month":"2022-12","story":"Against All Applications","stance":"reference","why":"WWII hiring approach: fast evaluation, quick firing, no stigma","quote":"A potentially interesting contrast","text":"A potentially interesting contrast: Why our generals were more successful in WWII than in Korea, Vietnam, or Iraq/Afghanistan [VIDEO] Briefly, minimize the application process and aggressively review work. Any mistake or failure and you're fired, two weeks to prove competence or you're fired, six weeks until a significant accomplishment or you're fired. And no stigma against being fired, just keep trying until you find a fit."},{"id":28362601,"author":"m0llusk","month":"2021-08","story":"If software engineering is in demand, why is it so hard to get a job?","stance":"reference","why":"Historian's theory of WWII generals' dynamic assignment and reassignment","quote":"WWII generals were unusually successful because of the dynamic way they were assigned and potentially reassigned within weeks","text":"This reminds me of this historian's theory that WWII generals were unusually successful because of the dynamic way they were assigned and potentially reassigned within weeks: (US Berkeley Events 2011) [VIDEO]"},{"id":18350666,"author":"Nomentatus","month":"2018-11","story":"Musk shakes up SpaceX in race to make satellite launch window: sources","stance":"reference","why":"Importance of relieving many generals in wartime","quote":"how important firing (actually, relieving) high numbers of generals is in war","text":"Interesting comparison - how important firing (actually, relieving) high numbers of generals is in war, when the rubber has to hit the road. [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["hk-0qJXyido",1],["8hAw1z8GdE8",1],["d0yGdNEWdn0",1]]},"EgiMu8A3pi0":{"wy":"HN commenters recommend the series for its in-depth, hands-on explanation of how V2 turbopumps and peroxide decomposition work.","q":{"x":"this series of videos is definitely worth watching","i":29924214,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":29924214,"author":"VBprogrammer","month":"2022-01","story":"Engine cooling – why rocket engines don’t melt","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Series detailing V2 parts, this one on the turbopump","quote":"this series of videos is definitely worth watching","text":"If that interests you then this series of videos is definitely worth watching. It details several parts of the German V2 missile, the grand father of all modern liquid propellant rockets. This one is about the turbopump which is one of the most interesting parts. [VIDEO]"},{"id":24376131,"author":"liability","month":"2020-09","story":"Rocket Helicopter (2010)","stance":"rec","why":"Long documentary on V2 turbopumps and peroxide catalysis","quote":"Here's a pretty good 2.5 hour long documentary about the V2 turbopumps specifically","text":"The German V2 rockets also used hydrogen peroxide and a catalyst (sodium or possibly potassium permanganate) to drive their turbopumps (which pumped LOx and alcohol into the engine.) Here's a pretty good 2.5 hour long documentary about the V2 turbopumps specifically: [VIDEO] (The H2O2/catalyst system is discussed/demonstrated around the 45 minute mark.)"},{"id":20818330,"author":"magicalhippo","month":"2019-08","story":"Chemists discover water microdroplets spontaneously produce hydrogen peroxide","stance":"rec","why":"In-depth V2 turbopump explanation with peroxide permanganate demo","quote":"For an in-depth and interesting explanation of how that works, I enjoyed this video","text":"For an in-depth and interesting explanation of how that works, I enjoyed this video: [VIDEO] Includes a demonstration of the potassium permanganate and hydrogen peroxide at around the 52 minute mark."}],"r":[["bAUVCn_jw5I",1],["tiKCORN-6m8",1],["HUMGc8hEkpc",1],["mceaM2_zQd8",1],["jhicDUgXyNg",1],["L0KuAx1COEk",1],["TfYf_rPWUdY",1],["gNRnrn5DE58",1],["36GT2zI8lVA",1],["fRMMSyCcTDI",1],["ghUs-84NAAU",1],["zBkNBP00wJE",1],["85ZCTuekjGA",1],["rnmcRTnTNC8",1],["r-A78RgMhZU",1],["4F72VULWFvc",1],["uUmIdq0D6-A",1]]},"OQQ-W_63UgQ":{"wy":"Recommended as one of the best free courses for learning modern deep learning for NLP.","q":{"x":"I will suggest two brilliant courses from Stanford","i":16168374,"y":"2018"},"m":[{"id":16168374,"author":"neel8986","month":"2018-01","story":"Ask HN: How to Seriously Start with Machine Learning and AI","stance":"strong_rec","why":"One of two brilliant Stanford courses on recent deep learning advances.","quote":"I will suggest two brilliant courses from Stanford","text":"This is kind of a masters degree course i created for myself to get knowledge of Machine Learning from bottoms up First, you need a strong mathematical base. Otherwise, you can copy paste an algorithm or use an API but you will not get any idea of what is happening inside Following concepts are very essential 1) Linear Algebra (MIT [LINK] ) 2) Probability (Harvard [LINK] ) Get some basic grasp of machine learning. Get a good intuition of basic concepts 1) Andrew Ng coursera course ( [LINK] ) 2) Tom Mitchell book ( [LINK] ) Both the above course and book are super easy to follow. You will get a good idea of basic concepts but they lack in depth. Now you should move to more intense books and courses You can get more in-depth knowledge of Machine learning from following sources 1)Nando machine learning course ( [LINK] ) 2)Bishops book ( [LINK] ) Especially Bishops book is really deep and covers almost all basic concepts. Now for recent advances in Deep learning. I will suggest two brilliant courses from Stanford 1) Vision ( [LINK] ) 2) NLP ( [VIDEO] ) The Vision course by Karparthy can be a very good introduction to Deep learning. Also, the mother book for deep learning ( [LINK] )is good"},{"id":16583619,"author":"morugin","month":"2018-03","story":"Amazon Is Hiring More Developers for Alexa Than Google Is Hiring for Everything","stance":"rec","why":"Good Stanford NLP class that takes time to absorb.","quote":"That class is good. it should take a long time to grok.","text":"I have read like 1/3 of the comments but its interesting that the most interesting problem of everything we are talking about was only noticed by a non-programmer (so far). Everything that was talked about in the comments all hinge on the state of the art (SOTA) of ai in natural language processing (nlp). John Carmack recommended [VIDEO] stanford's nlp class (i didnt know who he was until i saw that post on hn). That class is good. it should take a long time to grok. you'll be solid up to LSTMs a general current standard."},{"id":15843714,"author":"ahussain","month":"2017-12","story":"Theoretical Machine Learning Lecture Series: Deep Learning and Cognition","stance":"rec","why":"Stanford NLP course lectures by Prof. Manning.","quote":"For those who like Prof Manning's style and want to get more in depth, here are the lecture videos for the NLP course he teaches at Stanford","text":"For those who like Prof Manning's style and want to get more in depth, here are the lecture videos for the NLP course he teaches at Stanford: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["NfnWJUyUJYU",1],["KbB0FjPg0mw",1],["dEaecUuEqfc",1],["bBC-nXj3Ng4",1]]},"byjhpzEoXFs":{"wy":"HN commenters recommend it to see how a top mathematician approaches an unfamiliar problem through experiments and curiosity.","q":{"x":"Gowers has one of my favourite video series about how he approaches a problem he is unfamiliar with","i":48215033,"y":"2026"},"m":[{"id":31134858,"author":"mungal","month":"2022-04","story":"I stopped working on black hole information loss","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Gowers explores a strange determinant fact with numerical experiments.","quote":"His yt channel is an excellent resource of theoretical understanding through numerical methods.","text":"I’ll add to this to give an example of someone who I feel is doing a great job of satisfying this very request in the field of mathematics: Timothy Gowers. His yt channel is an excellent resource of theoretical understanding through numerical methods. Here’s my favorite series where he confirms/elucidates a “strange” theoretical fact with an explorative numerical style: [VIDEO] Simply superb content."},{"id":48215033,"author":"missyougowers","month":"2026-05","story":"An OpenAI model has disproved a central conjecture in discrete geometry","stance":"rec","why":"Gowers shows how he approaches an unfamiliar problem.","quote":"Gowers has one of my favourite video series about how he approaches a problem he is unfamiliar with","text":"Unfortunately Gowers has taken Tao's lead on this one. Gowers has one of my favourite video series about how he approaches a problem he is unfamiliar with: [VIDEO] It is disheartening to see him jump into this GenAI puffery. I hope these GenAI labs are paying Tao handsomely for legitimizing their slop, but more likely he's feeling pressure from his University to promote and work with these labs. My guess is Gowers wants in on that action, or his University does. Either way, it makes me sad. If its self motivated... even sadder."},{"id":40101854,"author":"jrhvmt","month":"2024-04","story":"Programming vs. Mathematical Curiosity","stance":"rec","why":"Series exploring a strange determinant, suggested as a fun rabbit hole.","quote":"series from Timothy Gowers","text":"But I do appreciate the comedy! Well while I have you, the message I feel I failed to adhere to in my comment was my interest in letting you, or others who follow their passion in math and programming, know that what you are doing is in fact “real” math! Keep it up! Your post made me click through a bunch of links in it. Thank you, but give me back that hour! ;P Better yet, I’ll trade you a different rabbit hole. Based on your post I think you’d really enjoy this “strange determinant” series from Timothy Gowers: [VIDEO] [LINK]"},{"id":39471384,"author":"juniperror","month":"2024-02","story":"An 'education legend' has created an AI that will change your mind about AI","stance":"reference","why":"Example of exploring problems instead of memorizing answers.","quote":"","text":"> How would you solve a problem that no one has ever touched before, and that no one knows the answer to? Where are you going to be provided the answer? This is very near and dear to my heart. We are talking about “teaching” and I doubt anyone who supports the current education status quo is advocating to teach using unsolved problems. That said, I do feel, personally, and have long advocated for, teaching using unsolved problems as a better technique, because you learn strategies for approaching a problem much better than: here’s theorem 13.4 heres an exercise problem that uses the theorem, what’s the answer? It reads like you see my opinions are black and white and yours as spectra. Using your own reading on your own comment the logical conclusion is “a system that teaches to an answer will be unable to answer unanswered questions because the students were only taught to answer questions that already have answers.” A great example of the answer being the most useless part of one’s own personal knowledge is Timothy Gowers YouTube content: [VIDEO] But who’s cares what some YouTuber is up to? He’s just out here promoting an inherently incorrect education method wrought from a dysfunctional mind."}]},"dFs9WO2B8uI":{"wy":"Recommended as an accessible animated introduction to McGilchrist's thesis on hemispheres and narrow versus diffuse attention.","q":{"x":"This entire video is really excellent","i":29332605,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":29332605,"author":"gerbilly","month":"2021-11","story":"Your Brain Can Only Take So Much Focus","stance":"strong_rec","why":"McGilchrist describes narrow versus diffuse attention","quote":"This entire video is really excellent","text":"I think you're right. There seems to be two sorts of attention in the mind, narrowly focused attention and broad diffuse attention. I see the goal of meditation not so much as alternating between the two modes but rather like balancing them and running them both in parallel. This entire video is really excellent, but see particularly this bit here, where Iain McGilchrist describes the concept. [VIDEO]"},{"id":17896715,"author":"AltruisticGap","month":"2018-09","story":"Naked Europe covers up","stance":"rec","why":"Animated summary of McGilchrist's work on the right hemisphere","quote":"There is a good animated summary of his work","text":"Hey, thanks for the appreciation. Indeed, Ian Mc Gilchrist mentions how empathy is the realm of the right brain hemisphere. There is a good animated summary of his work: RSA ANIMATE: The Divided Brain [VIDEO]"},{"id":17135439,"author":"no_identd","month":"2018-05","story":"Is Information in the Brain Represented in Continuous or Discrete Form?","stance":"rec","why":"Animated introduction to McGilchrist's divided brain thesis","quote":"You might want to watch the RSA Animated talk by McGilchrist","text":"That question has a very complex and complicated answer, hence why McGilchrist devoted the entire first half of the book to it, I can hardly summarize it here. You might want to watch the RSA Animated talk by McGilchrist: [VIDEO] If this reminds you of Julian Jaynes, then yes, there exists some similarity, but, to quote Wikipedia: \"McGilchrist, while accepting Jayne's intention, felt that Jayne's hypothesis was \"the precise inverse of what happened\" and that rather than a shift from bicameralism there evolved a separation of the hemispheres.\" In my opinion, it seems like a better question to ask then consists of asking if what McGilchrist describes holds 'all the way down', and I lack an answer to that."},{"id":24494472,"author":"anon73044","month":"2020-09","story":"DMT drug study investigates the ‘entities’ people meet while tripping","stance":"reference","why":"Nuanced view of left/right brain lateralization","quote":"As usual with biology, it's a bit more complicated than the '60s explanation ever revealed to the public.","text":">(The old: left hemisphere math, right art or vice versa has been debunked if I remember correctly) As usual with biology, it's a bit more complicated than the '60s explanation ever revealed to the public. [VIDEO]"},{"id":14064787,"author":"themgt","month":"2017-04","story":"A girl was found living among monkeys in an Indian forest","stance":"reference","why":"McGilchrist's hemisphere theory cited alongside the Genie case","quote":"the left/right hemisphere concepts Iain McGilchrist has been best known for","text":"I want to quote at length because it's fascinating in the context of the left/right hemisphere concepts Iain McGilchrist has been best known for (especially the idea that left-hemisphere dominance is a relatively recent human adaptation): [VIDEO] [LINK] [LINK] [LINK] Linguists also administered several brain exams specifically geared towards measuring Genie's language comprehension. On one such test she had no difficulty giving the correct meaning of sentences containing familiar homophones, demonstrating that her receptive comprehension was significantly better than her expressive language. Genie also did very well at identifying rhymes, both tasks that adult split-brain and left hemispherectomy patients had previously been recorded performing well on. During these tests an EEG consistently picked up more activity from the two electrodes over the right hemisphere of her brain than from those over the normal locations of the Broca's area and Wernicke's area, and found especially high involvement from her right anterior cerebral cortex, lending further support to the researchers' conclusion that Genie was using her right hemisphere to acquire language. As early as 1972 Genie scored between the level an 8-year-old and an adult on all right-hemisphere tasks the scientists tested her on, and showed extraordinarily rapid improvement on them. Her ability to piece together objects solely from tactile information was exceptionally good, and on spatial awareness tests her scores were "}],"r":[["fwcl17Q0bpk",1]]},"1sl5KJ69qiA":{"wy":"Recommended as a worthwhile real-world experiment whenever trolley problems or moral dilemmas come up.","q":{"x":"The video is well worth a watch","i":26264404,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":47751883,"author":"Imustaskforhelp","month":"2026-04","story":"Sam Altman's home targeted in second attack","stance":"rec","why":"Vsauce on a real-life trolley problem","quote":"Vsauce did a video about it worth watching[0]","text":"> Perhaps something to think about in a scenario like this. Personally I think it's interesting that some people are so quick to condone aggressive attacks on powerful people, yet have no comment on those powerful people committing lower levels of violence against the masses. It's all social context. Can I just say that out of all of this discourse happening, this might be the most insightful yet succint position to explain my stance on all of this especially the \"its all social context.\" line. I feel like many of us here might share an answer publicly but I have always believed that if I am in the shoes of someone else, I might act the way they do so in a sense I understand the human part of it. A human did the violence and why. I understand that. Now we can call this violence inhuman, sure, but this action is still done by human and for many reasons. And I also understand why people condemn these actions, we wish to live in a clean and structural world and then we see the messiness of the world. I just feel like just condemning an action would do nothing unless we change the ground conditions but that isn't in the hands of even many of us Hackernews users and this is basically a class aspect to it. I personally feel like there are some similarities to this incident to the Trolley problem actually. Vsauce did a video about it worth watching[0] Thank you for writing this comment. [0]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":32023894,"author":"Sceptique","month":"2022-07","story":"Absurd Trolley Problems","stance":"rec","why":"Real-life trolley problem experiment","quote":"It's not 100% perfect but it is still an amazing test.","text":"They did this experiment in real life [VIDEO] . It's not 100% perfect but it is still an amazing test."},{"id":26264404,"author":"wongarsu","month":"2021-02","story":"Hypothetical Judgment versus Real-Life Behavior in Trolley-Style Moral Dilemmas","stance":"rec","why":"Vsauce stages a real trolley switch scenario with unsuspecting subjects","quote":"The video is well worth a watch","text":"The youtube channel vsauce tried putting people in this situation by putting them in a fake railroad switching outpost and having the operator step out for a phonecall. They took care to educate the subject exactly on how the system works without them suspecting anything, trying to eliminate many of the factors you mention. The video is well worth a watch [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["XZ3w_jec1v8",1],["k1BneeJTDcU",1],["yftBiNu0ZNU",1],["5eo8nz_niiM",1]]},"PbC4gqZGPSY":{"wy":"Shared as encouragement for beginners learning design or craft, on persisting through the taste-skill gap.","q":{"x":"You learn how to differentiate between good and bad much faster than you learn how to create good.","i":4855724,"y":"2012"},"m":[{"id":8902316,"author":"tswartz","month":"2015-01","story":"What Doesn't Seem Like Work?","stance":"rec","why":"Advice to keep practicing until skill matches your taste.","quote":"Push through and keep practicing until your skill matches your taste.","text":"Ira Glass has some advice on the issue of when you start something you enjoy, but your skill doesn't match your taste. Push through and keep practicing until your skill matches your taste. [VIDEO]"},{"id":5768248,"author":"cooop","month":"2013-05","story":"Ask HN: I'm an engineer, how do I learn design?","stance":"rec","why":"Ira Glass articulates how taste develops through repetition and practice.","quote":"Ira Glass articulates this well describing the creative process","text":"A large aspect of design that separates great professional designers from amateurs is very hard to teach. It can be described as training your eye and developing your taste. This simply comes from repetition and practise. Ira Glass articulates this well describing the creative process — [VIDEO] Another aspect of design is the mindset and mentality required when looking to solve a problem. Something I feel often gets overlooked in the software world, where culture is generally engineer focused. IDEO pitch this in as 'design thinking'. While I'm not so keen on the term they do a good job of communicating the importance of certain mental traits that designers possess that are key to the design process. Sketch. Considering a user flow/feature or user story? Sketch out 50 ideas, explore and exhaust all possible scenarios, no matter how achievable, obvious or silly. Get it down on paper. I often think that getting the idea down on paper allows my mind to forget on it and move on to another potential solution. Try to not let your technical expertise constrain this exploration, that'll come later as you whittle your ideas down. Personally, if I have the time I quite like to produce hi-fidelity sketches. It may seem frivolous when a quick sharpie sketch will do, but as I spend time sketching I find the thinking time valuable and often find myself with another piece of paper jotting down notes/ideas etc. Learn to draw — I believe designers should sketch and draw. There are so many less"},{"id":4855724,"author":"joeld42","month":"2012-11","story":"2D Game Art for Programmers","stance":"rec","why":"Ira Glass on the gap between taste and skill when starting out.","quote":"You learn how to differentiate between good and bad much faster than you learn how to create good.","text":"This is actually a very common stumbling block. See the Ira Glass video.. [VIDEO] You learn how to differentiate between good and bad much faster than you learn how to create good. Which can be super discouraging because it means that at first all it feels like is that you just keep realizing how bad you are and how much further you have to go. Just keep practicing."}]},"XH0F9r0siTI":{"wy":"HN commenters call his x86 internals talks spectacular and mention them whenever low-level processor surprises come up.","q":{"x":"I remember watching his Defcon talks on x86 shenanigans[^1][^2] and being amazed at what he's been able to discover","i":49286937,"y":"2026"},"m":[{"id":49286937,"author":"aecsocket","month":"2026-08","story":"Spaghettifying DRAM","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Amazing x86 discoveries by Christopher Domas.","quote":"I remember watching his Defcon talks on x86 shenanigans[^1][^2] and being amazed at what he's been able to discover","text":"Holy shit, Christopher Domas is back. I remember watching his Defcon talks on x86 shenanigans[^1][^2] and being amazed at what he's been able to discover. Then he got whisked away by Intel and now drops this. I'm excited. [^1]: [VIDEO] [^2]: [LINK]"},{"id":28182090,"author":"daniel-thompson","month":"2021-08","story":"Compiling C Programs Into “Mov” Instructions (And Only “Mov” Instructions)","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Spectacular talks on x86 processor internals.","quote":"And yes, they are spectacular.","text":"These links don't work for me, but the videos are on YT: - [LINK] - [VIDEO] And yes, they are spectacular."},{"id":20234384,"author":"kbenson","month":"2019-06","story":"Everybody Be Cool, This Is a Robbery","stance":"reference","why":"Related talk on vulnerabilities via additional onboard processors.","quote":"two Defcon talks I watched in the last day or two","text":"While somewhat interesting, I'm not sure there's much to discuss unless the presentation or details are already available. Interestingly, this reminds me of two Defcon talks I watched in the last day or two, which also cover vulnerability through additional processors onboard.[1][2] 1: [LINK] 2: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["jmTwlEh8L7g",2]]},"aIx2N-viNwY":{"wy":"Cited in threads about perceived time as a good, detailed explanation of the effect.","q":{"x":"Veritasium explores this and IMO the alternative arguments are more compelling","i":29109565,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":41205164,"author":"activatedgeek","month":"2024-08","story":"Perceived Age","stance":"rec","why":"Covers the effect for the interested","quote":"Veritasium covered this effect in a video [1] for the interested.","text":"This effect is very interesting. Veritasium covered this effect in a video [1] for the interested. [1]: [VIDEO] (2016)"},{"id":29109534,"author":"shmde","month":"2021-11","story":"Why time “speeds up” as we get older (2019)","stance":"rec","why":"Good video on why life seems to speed up","quote":"There is a good video by Veritasium on this topic","text":"There is a good video by Veritasium on this topic titled - \"Why life seems to speed up as we age\" [VIDEO]"},{"id":29109565,"author":"cornstalks","month":"2021-11","story":"Why time “speeds up” as we get older (2019)","stance":"rec","why":"Offers more compelling alternative explanations than the common argument","quote":"Veritasium explores this and IMO the alternative arguments are more compelling","text":"That's a common argument. Veritasium explores this and IMO the alternative arguments are more compelling: [VIDEO]"},{"id":24856523,"author":"hudixt","month":"2020-10","story":"Ask HN: As I get older, I see seconds tick physically faster. Am I alone?","stance":"rec","why":"Explains in detail why time seems to speed up with age","quote":"Video explains it in detail about why this happens.","text":"Here's the video from Veritasium around same topic [VIDEO] Video explains it in detail about why this happens."},{"id":35081238,"author":"mortehu","month":"2023-03","story":"Letter to Pixar President Ed Catmull [pdf]","stance":"reference","why":"Time passes faster when older even at minute scale","quote":"It turns out that when we're older time passes more quickly even on the scale of minutes.","text":"Veritasium has a video about this subject: [VIDEO] It turns out that when we're older time passes more quickly even on the scale of minutes."}],"r":[["uqH_Y1TupoQ",1],["AaZ_RSt0KP8",1]]},"kb-m2fasdDY":{"wy":"Often brought up as an honest account of microservice sprawl, including the fact that nobody at Uber knew exactly how many services existed.","q":{"x":"I enjoyed that video","i":12626814,"y":"2016"},"m":[{"id":17567419,"author":"jppope","month":"2018-07","story":"Ask HN: What are the things keep in mind while giving/preparing for a tech talk?","stance":"rec","why":"Listed among fantastic technical speeches","quote":"Here's a collection of some fantastic technical speeches","text":"1) Do not attempt to live code. change your code via version control, build small videos, copy and paste... whatever you do... do not try and live code 2) Read \"The presentation secrets of Steve Jobs\" ... its not really about Steve Jobs so much as its about how to be a great presenter. 3) building or writing a talk is significantly less important than the delivery... which means that more of your time should be spent on how to deliver the speech (recording I find is the best way to practice) 4) Pick a single thing and do it well, do not attempt to put too much into a talk or tackle something massively complex. 5) Make sure that everyone is walking away with something they can actually use. This doesn't require it be a hard skill... \"Soft\" or philosophical utilities are just as good 6) Have Fun and be funny... Speakers have a tendency to want to show off the size of their intellect, and the level their competency- having a speech be entertaining is way way more important. 7) DO NOT READ FROM YOUR SLIDES. know your shit. slides are supplementary Extra Credit. Here's a collection of some fantastic technical speeches: * [LINK] * [LINK] * [LINK] * [LINK] * [LINK] * [VIDEO]"},{"id":15159748,"author":"kartD","month":"2017-09","story":"Ask HN: Recommended (recent) videos for a Lunch and Learn","stance":"rec","why":"One of several suggested talks for a lunch and learn","quote":"","text":"Little drunk so bear with me :) Rich Hickey: Clojure concurrency[ [LINK] ] Keynote - Rust: confident, productive systems programming by Aaron Turon [ [LINK] ] RustConf 2016 - A Modern Editor Built in Rust by Raph Levien [ [LINK] ] Microservices at Netflix Scale: Principles, Tradeoffs & Lessons Learned[ [LINK] ] What I Wish I Had Known Before Scaling Uber to 1000 Services[ [VIDEO] ] Dynamic Code Optimization and the NVIDIA Denver Processor [ [LINK] ] * if you can get anything on Apple's Ax series that's even better"},{"id":12626814,"author":"smcl","month":"2016-10","story":"Why's that company so big? I could do that in a weekend","stance":"rec","why":"Honest, open talk on Uber's service sprawl","quote":"I enjoyed that video","text":"Joking aside I enjoyed that video ( [VIDEO] - for those who didn't see it), the presenter was pretty honest and way more open than I expected. I'm just so curious how those services break down - if it's genuinely on the \"lstrip as a service\" level or whatever, whether that's \"1,000 services\" figure was actually a relatively simple API with 10 versions at various levels of deprecation, whether he meant it was 1,000 different service endpoints (250 services each with POST, GET, PUT, DELETE) or what. I want to know more basically :)"},{"id":23449910,"author":"djsumdog","month":"2020-06","story":"Microservices Considered Harmful (2019)","stance":"reference","why":"Uber engineer said they could not count their microservices","quote":"an Uber engineer said they had so many microservices it was impossible to really count how many they had","text":"At a goto; talk, an Uber engineer said they had so many microservices it was impossible to really count how many they had: [VIDEO]"},{"id":17606796,"author":"tzhenghao","month":"2018-07","story":"Why Google Stores Billions of Lines of Code in a Single Repository (2016)","stance":"reference","why":"Cited on duplicate wheels across many repos","quote":"","text":"Having worked at different companies adopting both monorepo and the multiple repos approach, I find monorepo a better normalizer at scale in consolidating all \"software\" that runs the company. Just like what many commenters here have mentioned, the monorepo approach is a forcing function on keeping compatibility issues at bay. What you don't want is to end up in a situation where teams reinvent their own wheels instead of building on top of existing code, and at scale, I think the multiple repo approach tends to breed such codebase smell. [1] I'm sure 8000 repos is living hell for most organizations. [1] - [VIDEO]"},{"id":15987624,"author":"itsmenotyou","month":"2017-12","story":"On the Moral Economy of Tech (2016)","stance":"reference","why":"Microservices let people avoid difficult conversations","quote":"one of the downsides of moving to microservices being that people can avoid difficult conversations","text":"I think as technologists we have a natural preference for clean technological solutions that don't involve politics or messy interpersonal relationship issues. Failing to confront that whole human sphere of life leads to negative consequences everywhere, and the talk does a great job of pointing out some of the macro social effects. On a similar note but a more localized scale I'm reminded of this talk on one of the downsides of moving to microservices being that people can avoid difficult conversations [VIDEO]"},{"id":13684252,"author":"securingsincity","month":"2017-02","story":"Uber launches 'urgent investigation' into sexual harassment claims","stance":"reference","why":"Cites Uber not knowing its service count","quote":"We have so many services in production we don't even know how many there are.","text":"Total armchair observer viewpoint - In reading Susan's blog, there are a lot of sexual harassment claims but there are many claims of cloak and dagger behavior. This points to a dysfunctional organization that lacks trust. And in that, I couldn't help but think of Conway's law [1] and the video from the goto; conference [2] about Uber's infrastructure. \"We have so many services in production we don't even know how many there are.\" This may reflect a few different trust issues. One of which includes \"Not invented here\" thus building services that are duplicative. The goal of which is to show value and get promoted. Given the blog, the communication and reporting structure is also broken. Which, results in >1000 services in production and it being unclear how many there are and what they all do. Most likely, this is how the teams are structured as well. [1] - [LINK] [2] - [VIDEO]"},{"id":37975591,"author":"belter","month":"2023-10","story":"Uber migrates microservices to multi-cloud platform running Kubernetes and Mesos","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Apparently they started at 1000 and went from there...","text":"Apparently they started at 1000 and went from there... \"What I Wish I Had Known Before Scaling Uber to 1000 Services\" - [VIDEO]"},{"id":12632904,"author":"developer2","month":"2016-10","story":"Why's that company so big? I could do that in a weekend","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"He talks about every regret regarding their infrastructure.","text":"You're going to talk about Uber? Watch this video: [1]. He talks about every regret regarding their infrastructure. At first you kind of nod your head along with \"yeah, it's not that easy\", but as he keeps talking you can't help but wonder why the company has absolutely no internal technical direction whatsoever . They employ over 2000 engineers (just devs, excluding support staff, etc.). They use micro-services designed to the point where teams can't reuse pre-existing services. They are rewriting the same functionality over and over, and over and over again, as separate teams don't even have a clue what has already been written. Pick a piece of functionality you need. There are straight up 20 different APIs that do the exact same thing , but good luck figuring out which one you should be using - oh wait, why bother when you can just write the 21st version of the same thing. They are at the point where they have no clue what their repositories contain. In the video, he can't even provide an accurate number of deployed micro-services. THEY CANNOT EVEN ASSESS WHAT THEY ARE RUNNING IN PRODUCTION . They're literally drowning, and cannot recover. The point of the article is wrong. It's not about what can be done in one weekend, it's what can be done in 12-24 months by a competent team. Yes, Uber operates internationally which comes with a very crazy set of challenges. 2000 engineers worth of challenge? Not even remotely close. You should need fewer than 100 people who really know"}],"r":[["SKtQgFBRUvQ",1],["csyL9EC0S0c",1],["hyYCn_kAngI",1],["a-BOSpxYJ9M",1],["34rdxDgpaaA",1],["I8LbkfSSR58",1],["2gFsOoKAHZg",1]]},"w70Xc9CStoE":{"wy":"HN points to it as the original statement of the Stop Killing Games campaign.","q":{"x":"Here's the original intro to Stop Killing Games by Ross","i":41666720,"y":"2024"},"m":[{"id":41666720,"author":"boltzmann-brain","month":"2024-09","story":"Stop Killing Games Interview with Ross/AccursedFarms and Damian/SKG Organizer [video]","stance":"rec","why":"Original introduction to Stop Killing Games by Ross.","quote":"Here's the original intro to Stop Killing Games by Ross","text":"The [recent CA legislation]( [LINK] ) brought back the topic of Stop Killing Games which is an ongoing campaign to pass laws similar to the CA ban and much more. If you want a very short exposition of what Stop Killing Games is, here's a ~1 minute video: [LINK] Ross Scott is best known for his youtube series \"Freeman's Mind\" where he plays Half-Life and narrates what Gordon Freeman must be thinking, with a lot of deeply philosophical considerations. It's a staple of YouTube. He's also been running a series called \"Dead Game News\" and that's how Stop Killing Games was born. The other organizer, Damian, is a real-deal neckbeard dev and has pretty much done it all from BASIC on 8-bit micros to theorem provers and from video games to cryptography audits. Here's the original intro to Stop Killing Games by Ross: [VIDEO] And here's a subsequent FAQ: [LINK] [Related discussion]( [LINK] ) P.S. had to edit the title a tiny bit as it was a few chars over limit."},{"id":41127591,"author":"worble","month":"2024-08","story":"Stop Killing Games – European Citizens' Initiative","stance":"rec","why":"Original video laying out the campaign's avenues.","quote":"For extra context, you can watch Ross Scott's videos about his campaign to stop games being killed.","text":"For extra context, you can watch Ross Scott's videos about his campaign to stop games being killed. Original video with all the different avenues he's trying: [VIDEO] The one specifically about this initiative: [LINK] He's done smaller update videos on how it's been progressing on his channel if you want additional info."},{"id":39909576,"author":"sph","month":"2024-04","story":"Stop Killing Games","stance":"rec","why":"Ross Scott's companion video explaining the campaign.","quote":"Companion video by the author, Ross Scott","text":"This is a campaign to stop game publishers from turning off and rendering their games-as-a-service which people might have paid for completely inaccessible. The goal is to contact as many consumer agencies worldwide until we have some solid legislation that forces game companies to patch or release their backend, so that paying users can keep playing the game indefinitely after the publishers decide to pull the plug. Games-as-a-service are an increasingly common practice that puts an expiration date to games that are sold at full price, and many users are totally unaware that after a few years the game they have paid for is basically worthless. There is no technological reason this is the case, but pure greed by publishers. Many of today's GaaS are single-player games that have no business at all being online-only. A studio might choose this publishing strategy to extract as much money from consumers, to milk the playerbase with a monthly subscription and killing games when a sequel is announced. There is no law today stopping companies from doing so. We can play games from the 1970s, we can play multiplayer games from the 1990s but many others not even a decade old now are lost forevermore. This is unacceptable. --- Companion video by the author, Ross Scott: \"The largest campaign ever to stop publishers destroying games\" - [VIDEO] --- EDIT: it is quite disheartening to see software engineers in this forum defending the practice and saying it is impossible to patch an online-"},{"id":41666381,"author":"boltzmann-brain","month":"2024-09","story":"Sony, Ubisoft scandals lead to California ban on deceptive digital goods sales","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"SKG organizer here. Something like this CA legislation was our \"worst case scenario, everything else failed, at least we could do this much, we've compromised on everything\" goal. That is to say, now opponents can't push us to compromise to that level and our worst case scenario in case we pass anything at all is looking better. We're really happy this is happening because it changes the Overton window for us and makes our case stronger and easier to argue for, as you say. A lot of change has been happening in the past few months and even weeks with regards to the market and legislative situation around the problematic of SKG and while you can't ever fully attribute something, we hope that it's thanks to our actions. Ubisoft promising end of life offline modes for The Crew 2 and the third game in the series called Motorfest. Capcom bringing back Windows 7 era games that were lost to G4WL. The \"Ubisoft scandal\" mentioned in the headline - specifically the unforced shutdown and resulting removal of functioning state from The Crew - is something that SKG have no doubt popularized. Now that we're at 350 000 signatures of a goal of 1 000 000 in our direct democracy initiative, companies and lawmakers are starting to take things seriously. And this is with a $0 budget. We're still in need of more signatures over the next 10 months to reach the goal, so if you're an EU citizen, go click the link Sniffnoy posted above and sign. Worth doing even if you're not a gamer, just to claw bac"},{"id":39931914,"author":"hn_acker","month":"2024-04","story":"Stop Killing Games","stance":"reference","why":"Pointing to a specific timestamp in the video.","quote":"","text":"Maybe it was the one called \"The largest campaign ever to stop publishers destroying games\". Check timestamp 24:55 (&t=1495s) [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["xUezfuy8Qpc",1],["rxOKEsBx4NU",1],["0PclgBd6_Zs",1],["ia8Q51ouA_s",1],["zBgCRJluWTc",1],["jpNU72dny2s",1],["mb3uK-_QkOo",1],["RTB5XhjbgZA",1],["9-IWMbJXoLM",1],["gp4U5aH_T6A",1],["8k2AbqTBxao",1],["i6Fr65PFqfk",1],["SqayDnQ2wmw",1],["JvBT4XBdoUE",1],["BFld4EBO2RE",1],["aMcJ1Jvtef0",1]]},"4YTfxresvS8":{"wy":"HN people point to it as an excellent, inspirational talk on Rust GUI architecture, and it led many to the Druid project.","q":{"x":"I found this excellent talk to be complementary to my talk[1] on data-oriented GUI in Rust","i":17980155,"y":"2018"},"m":[{"id":17980155,"author":"raphlinus","month":"2018-09","story":"Using Rust for Game Development [video]","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Excellent talk on data-oriented GUI in Rust","quote":"I found this excellent talk to be complementary to my talk[1] on data-oriented GUI in Rust","text":"I found this excellent talk to be complementary to my talk[1] on data-oriented GUI in Rust, not to plug my own work too much. I found a lot of common ground: * Trying to write object-oriented code in Rust doesn't work well. Writing data-oriented code does. * \"Fighting the borrow checker\" is a sign you might be doing it wrong. Try data-oriented approaches instead. * Use a structure-of-arrays rather than an array-of-structures. * Use what I call \"state splitting\" to borrow mutable references to just the state you need; this works well with the structure-of-arrays approach and is a powerful motivator to use it. * To build graph-like structures, reach for a Vec of components, and indexes into the Vec for relationships, as your first choice. Self-references, arena allocators, and Rc are viable alternatives, but you should have a good reason to use them instead of Vec. * Some form of dynamic typing is useful to keep your system loosely coupled (though we ended up with very different forms of dynamic typing, see below). * Data-oriented approaches have taken root in the C++ gaming community, mostly motivated by performance, but adapt well to Rust, and some of the ideas may be useful in domains beyond gaming. There were some other points that went beyond what I talked about, but could fit in well: * A \"generational index\" is a good way to avoid use-after-free style errors that result from the use of a stale index. The slotmap crate can help. And now for the things that are different. "},{"id":24987890,"author":"exDM69","month":"2020-11","story":"Dear ImGui – Bloat-free graphical user interface library for C++","stance":"rec","why":"Inspirational work on Rust GUIs","quote":"I have been following Raph Levien's fantastic work on Druid and Rust GUIs which has been very inspirational","text":"As much as I like Dear ImGui, the immediate mode paradigm doesn't work everywhere. The problem of \"page tearing\" (not to be confused with vsync) means you either need to render frames continuously, or at least 2 frames for every time you get input or need to implement some kind of \"retained mode state tracking\" on top of Imgui. It's good for games, but for applications where you're not rendering new frames constantly, the paradigm doesn't seem a very good fit. I wish there was a retained mode GUI library that would be as easy to integrate in your game and graphics apps as ImGui (or nuklear) is. On my spare time, I've been working on a retained mode GUI layout and rendering framework (not a complete GUI toolkit) that uses a DOM-like tree, CSS style flexbox layouts and produces a vertex buffer for rendering like ImGui does. Unfortunately life gets in the way, and all I have to show for my project is a small prototype in C and another one in Rust (can't decide which I like more) that draws some text and rectangles with a fancy layout. Code is not public at the moment but I'm willing to share it with anyone (on GitHub) who responds with a thoughtful comment or a good question. I have been following Raph Levien's fantastic work on Druid and Rust GUIs which has been very inspirational, this video in particular [0]. Where Raph is focused on native GUI rendering and high fidelity text output, I've only focused on rendering in a GPU application using shaders and only very basic text l"},{"id":22977095,"author":"exDM69","month":"2020-04","story":"Puny GUI – A tiny cross platform GUI Kit in Janet","stance":"reference","why":"Inspired commenter's efficient layout prototype","quote":"I got inspired by Raph Levien's talk on Rust and GUI layout [0]","text":"I haven't shared the code in public but if you give your GitHub username, I can share it to you (and others who are interested) and you can reach out via email (address in commit log). I got inspired by Raph Levien's talk on Rust and GUI layout [0] and realized that a simple flexbox layout algorithm can be implemented really efficiently. So I started making a prototype in C (also a small proto in Rust), using a densely packed array to implement a DOM-like tree. The tree nodes have short strings, boxes (with border, padding, etc), images (texture coordinates in an atlas), layout constraints, containers, etc. Flexbox layout is applied to the tree in linear time (and constant memory!) and then the tree is traversed to output an ordered list of axis aligned rectangles. The output list can be fed to the GPU, where a special (but simple) shader draws clipped triangles that get applied with textures, msdf font, etc. The whole GUI gets rendered with a single draw call. This all can be done with very fast performance (sub-millisecond for layout and rendering, excluding GPU latency). There are other implementation details that make it efficient for cache (sizeof(node) == cache line), no recursion, no memory allocation, no unbounded loops, no system calls, etc. I'm fairly convinced that this approach should be enough to cover 80% of modern GUI paradigms. However I don't have the time or the interest to build a complete widget set GUI library. Right now I'm sporadically working on the pr"},{"id":19398243,"author":"vanderZwan","month":"2019-03","story":"Fast, Bump-Allocated Virtual Doms with Rust and Wasm","stance":"reference","why":"Video about Druid to watch later","quote":"going to watch the video about Druid linked in the README.MD later[0]","text":"Thanks! Looks very interesting, going to watch the video about Druid linked in the README.MD later[0]. And no worries, it's very easy to forget not everyone is introduced! Anyway, to actually engage with your point: I can see the appeal of an all-Rust framework: good typing system + high performance (both in speed and low memory use, ideally) sounds fantastic! So out of curiosity: how do you think hot swapping would be handled? I'm asking this who has not dived into Rust at all and only observed it with interest from a distance. For starters, I understand Rust has fairly slow compilation times, no? Or is that only true for optimized code and do we have fast debug options? Similarly, hot swapping requires maintaining state. With JS that's not too difficult because you don't worry about memory layout: as long as the high-level structure and names are the same the code works fine (it just kills the JIT optimizations). With WASM, that goes out the window: add or remove a field to your struct and all offsets change. Rust may guarantee no memory leaks, but that is not the same as guaranteeing that a snapshot of the memory state of one Rust program works on a different one. I guess some kind of \"export to/import from JavaScript based representation\" glue code that runs every hot reload could work, but that sounds like it could really freeze the browser when hot-reloading the big frameworks. [0] [VIDEO] would be funny if I forgot to include links myself at this point :p"}],"r":[["B5NULPSiOGw",1],["kLiwvnr4L80",1],["Ll6-eGDpimU",1],["dEaecUuEqfc",1],["Tml94je2edk",1]]},"LmpuerlbJu0":{"wy":"HN users call it a mind-blowing and good explainer on how the immune system works, and cite it in vaccine and disease threads.","q":{"x":"this Kurzgeasgt video blew my mind on this topic. Our immune system really is amazing.","i":29941030,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":29941030,"author":"hallway_monitor","month":"2022-01","story":"Multiple Sclerosis Causality","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Mind-blowing look at how amazing the immune system is.","quote":"this Kurzgeasgt video blew my mind on this topic. Our immune system really is amazing.","text":"I don't see many YouTube links posted here, but this Kurzgeasgt video blew my mind on this topic. Our immune system really is amazing. [VIDEO]"},{"id":29524076,"author":"Wowfunhappy","month":"2021-12","story":"BNT162b2 Vaccine Booster and Mortality Due to Covid-19","stance":"rec","why":"Explains how the immune system handles variations of invaders.","quote":"Kurzgesagt made a good explainer video on this recently","text":"Vaccines are different from antibiotics in that they are designed to use your own immune system. Whereas an antibiotic targets a bacterial weakness which the bacteria can fix, the immune system watches for both specific invaders and possible variations of those invaders . Kurzgesagt made a good explainer video on this recently: [VIDEO]"},{"id":39938147,"author":"chasil","month":"2024-04","story":"Rejuvenating the blood cell population","stance":"reference","why":"Immune cell types related to thymus shutdown.","quote":"","text":"Does this relate to the shutdown of the thymus (the \"murder university\")? The thymus goes dormant at the age of 85,as I've read. The lymphoid strain can specialize into t-cells, which is the speciality of the thymus. [VIDEO] The myeloid does provide macrophages, but they are of little value compared to an activated b-cell on a mission."},{"id":36036916,"author":"mcny","month":"2023-05","story":"What if you did the exact opposite?","stance":"reference","why":"Animated video on cancer cells that prompted the commenter's musings.","quote":"Edit: I think it is this one","text":"> Some fraction may not go through the normal cycle, but it can't be many. Maybe it is because I just saw an animated video on cancer (I’ll find the link when I get home) on YouTube but then so are we thinking of a bee hive as one individual? Like in the video, they talk about how cancer cells refuse to die when something bad happens like they are supposed to… So this is going to sound cheesy but now I have to wonder, could you zoom out on humans as well? On earth? Are what we call “living beings” what we would call “malignant cells” or at best mutations? Now that’s a scary thought. Edit: I think it is this one [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["ajGX7odA87k",1],["Jzfpyo-q-RM",1]]},"MVYrJJNdrEg":{"wy":"HN points to it as a demonstration that VR presence can feel close to talking in person.","q":{"x":"The Fridman - Zuckerberg VR interview really convinced me of this, if you haven't seen it, check it out, it's incredible","i":42643928,"y":"2025"},"m":[{"id":42643928,"author":"lambdaba","month":"2025-01","story":"Four causes for 'Zoom fatigue' (2021)","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Convincing VR interview that remote presence can feel real.","quote":"The Fridman - Zuckerberg VR interview really convinced me of this, if you haven't seen it, check it out, it's incredible","text":"The Fridman - Zuckerberg VR interview really convinced me of this, if you haven't seen it, check it out, it's incredible [VIDEO]"},{"id":37745142,"author":"somsak2","month":"2023-10","story":"Return to Office Is Bullshit and Everyone Knows It","stance":"rec","why":"Shows VR close to in-person conversation quality.","quote":"I think it's getting close to the point where it's just as good as talking to someone in person","text":"Have you seen some of the virtual reality from FB? I think it's getting close to the point where it's just as good as talking to someone in person, with the obvious benefit that not everyone has to be in the same physical office. [VIDEO]"},{"id":38025057,"author":"minton","month":"2023-10","story":"Reality Labs has lost $11.4B during 2023 (there's still one quarter left)","stance":"rec","why":"Podcast conducted remotely in the metaverse.","quote":"Check out this podcast[0] between Lex Fridman and Mark Zuckerberg where they meet remotely via the metaverse.","text":"I think VR can enable human connection where it otherwise isn’t possible due to distance. Of course, it isn’t a full substitute for in-person interaction, but it doesn’t have to be either or. We can have a great remote connection via VR and still exist in the real world. Check out this podcast[0] between Lex Fridman and Mark Zuckerberg where they meet remotely via the metaverse. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":39051741,"author":"abrichr","month":"2024-01","story":"Mark Zuckerberg’s new goal is creating artificial general intelligence","stance":"reference","why":"Offered as a relevant example.","quote":"","text":"Perhaps this qualifies: Mark Zuckerberg: First Interview in the Metaverse | Lex Fridman Podcast #398 [VIDEO]"},{"id":38572579,"author":"muglug","month":"2023-12","story":"Gaussian Head Avatar: Ultra High-Fidelity Head Avatar via Dynamic Gaussians","stance":"reference","why":"Podcast demoed the avatar tech; capture remains the roadblock.","quote":"I think the tech in that paper was demoed for this podcast","text":"I think the tech in that paper was demoed for this podcast: [VIDEO] The big roadblock to commercialisation for the moment is the original capture — for the paper they used a 110-camera capture rig under ideal lighting conditions. In the above podcast Zuckerberg mentions that in the future people will be able to use their phones to do that same capture, but I don’t think that tech is coming next year. I wonder if there’ll be an interim period where people who want high-quality avatars will have to book an appointment."},{"id":37903385,"author":"indeedmug","month":"2023-10","story":"The real engine behind \"AI\" is the bad tech economy","stance":"reference","why":"Example of VR's vision being more real than expected.","quote":"","text":"The biggest lesson I took away from all of these hype cycles is how unpredictable they are in their arc. Some trends like AV seem like they were going to take over the world, but they died down as quickly as they rose when we realize how hard safety is. VR seem like it was dead with Google Glasses and Meta. But recent releases show that the [vision of VR is more real than I thought]( [VIDEO] ). Apple releasing their VR definitely provides more legitimacy to the idea. Technology is always hard to actually implement in the real world. I think have expectations that the world would flip over with new innovations in a matter of months. But it actually takes years of hard work to get people to adopt it. The internet and mobile phones are good examples where the promise was clear but the journey being universally adopted was not easy. * * For the internet, you have the massive struggle of web standards, getting implementations web browser to align, and achieving acceptable performance. Internet Explorer still haunts the dreams of many web devs. It's remarkable how many fully featured web apps there are like Onshape and Google Maps. * For mobile phones, they require a massive amount of technology investment and unit economics to get the price cheap enough for normal people. It's amazing how wireless technology gotten to the point where we can stream videos on phones."},{"id":37720099,"author":"clnq","month":"2023-09","story":"'Counterfeit people': The danger posed by Meta’s AI celebrity lookalike chatbots","stance":"reference","why":"Zuckerberg discusses AI avatars with Lex Fridman.","quote":"Lex Fridman talked to Mark Zuckerberg about this just a few days ago [0].","text":"Lex Fridman talked to Mark Zuckerberg about this just a few days ago [0]. It didn't sound like Meta is has good answers for how this should be done. So it seems unlikely that they'd be significantly more than 6 months into research like this (as the tweet chain says). It seems like Meta is exploring AI avatars, but for much more practical, menial tasks. [0] [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["-f-CFQxaUY4",1]]},"T6JFTmQCFHg":{"wy":"HN points to it as the best filmed version of the story whenever aliens or the nature of intelligence come up.","q":{"x":"O'Reagan's short is the best take on it.","i":35770861,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":35770861,"author":"Borrible","month":"2023-05","story":"Alien Supercivilizations Absent from 100k Nearby Galaxies (2015)","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Short film adaptation of the aliens-meet-meat story.","quote":"O'Reagan's short is the best take on it.","text":"I always liked the idea and think O'Reagan's short is the best take on it. A black and white version set in Les Deux Magots, Sartre and Beauvoir at a table in the background, would be nice. [LINK] [VIDEO]"},{"id":47689878,"author":"api","month":"2026-04","story":"They're Made Out of Meat (1991)","stance":"rec","why":"Short film version of the story.","quote":"Great short film version","text":"Great short film version: [VIDEO] I do wonder sometimes if someone out there is waiting for something actually intelligent to emerge down here."},{"id":47691760,"author":"amiga386","month":"2026-04","story":"They're Made Out of Meat (1991)","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"I like that the bearded one can't help cracking up when he says","text":"I like that the bearded one can't help cracking up when he says \"the ones you probed\": [VIDEO]"},{"id":46893484,"author":"rbanffy","month":"2026-02","story":"Kevin Kelly – The Singularity Is Always Near","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"> and many of them have no particular reason to be visible at astronomical distances. Or to want to talk to meat. [VIDEO]"}]},"dPK6t7echuA":{"wy":"Recommended as a fantastic talk, valued both for its parallelism ideas and for the punch-card story.","q":{"x":"It's a fantastic talk in its own right","i":38166248,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":38166248,"author":"svieira","month":"2023-11","story":"In the bad old days we had Punchcards. How did people deal with that?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Fitting programs onto punch cards, eye opening","quote":"It's a fantastic talk in its own right","text":"By thinking about things that were not applicable to the problem domain says Guy Steele in \"How to Think about Parallel Programing: Not!\": [VIDEO] It's a fantastic talk in its own right, but the first bit about fitting a program onto a punch card by self-modifying the code involved (and just how much thought was about fitting-the-program, and how little relatively is involved in the-purpose-of-the-program) is quite eye opening."},{"id":36784047,"author":"MathMonkeyMan","month":"2023-07","story":"Coroutines for Go","stance":"rec","why":"Guy Steele talk on parallelism","quote":"There are a few good talks that he gives","text":"Guy Steele Jr. worked on a language called \"Fortress.\" There are a few good talks that he gives: - (toy problem) [LINK] - (parallelism) [VIDEO] - (languages) [LINK]"},{"id":40899898,"author":"sitkack","month":"2024-07","story":"Synchronization Is Bad for Scale","stance":"reference","why":"Steele on avoiding synchronization and ordering","quote":"Synchronization and ordering and a lack of idempotency need to be heavily justified.","text":"I would use Aerospike, it has strong consistency or session consistency and will soon get multirecord transactions. [LINK] And also see the second bullet point from this Guy Steele talk from 2010 Strange Loop. Synchronization and ordering and a lack of idempotency need to be heavily justified. \"How to Think about Parallel Programming: Not!\" - Guy L. Steele Jr. (Strange Loop 2010) [VIDEO] transcript [LINK] thanks matthiasn! Previous discussion [LINK]"},{"id":38049091,"author":"yakubin","month":"2023-10","story":"Algebra","stance":"reference","why":"Commutativity matters for parallelism","quote":"Commutativity is important for e.g. parallel programming.","text":"Commutativity is important for e.g. parallel programming. See e.g. < [VIDEO] >. In the context of linear algebra, it would also be good for folks to know that matrix multiplication (/linear transformation composition) is not commutative."},{"id":33697207,"author":"EdwardCoffin","month":"2022-11","story":"Ask HN: What story from the PDP-8 era would make a good script?","stance":"reference","why":"Steele recounts packing a utility onto one punch card","quote":"He describes all the gymnastics he went through to try to pack it into a single punch card","text":"Guy Steele has a talk [1] in which he recounts making some sort of utility program for the contemporary of the PDP-8, the IBM 1130. He describes all the gymnastics he went through to try to pack it into a single punch card, and also describes the benefits of having such a program on a single punch card rather than a series of them. [1] \"How to Think about Parallel Programming: Not!\" - Guy L. Steele Jr. (Strange Loop 2010) [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["oyLBGkS5ICk",1],["p33CVV29OG8",1],["R2Aa4PivG0g",1],["dCuZkaaou0Q",1],["5TFDG-y-EHs",1],["ftcIcn8AmSY",1]]},"EH6vE97qIP4":{"wy":"Recommended as a solid, level-headed academic introduction to blockchain, often noted because Gensler later led the SEC.","q":{"x":"I've been enjoying a playlist of blockchain lectures by Gary Gensler","i":27177384,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":33775767,"author":"CharlieDigital","month":"2022-11","story":"Crypto firm BlockFi files for bankruptcy as FTX fallout spreads","stance":"rec","why":"Gensler's MIT blockchain talks show he is well equipped for regulation.","quote":"his series of talks at MIT on blockchain[0] technologies leads me to believe that there are few people better equipped","text":"> Regulators have been slow and ineffective when it comes to crypto, which has lent itself to the crypto v SEC narrative, but all the adults in the room known there is a need for some regulation It's a \"damned if you do, damned if you don't\" situation. Mainly because of the outsized lobbying power of the wealthy folks backing VC's backing crypto. Clamp down on crypto with regulations and many, many wealthy political contributors are going to be pissed off by stifling growth. Don't clamp down and you get the situation we're in now where everyone is losing their shirts. What about the middle ground? I think we can only now talk about it because enough wealthy people also lost big chunks of their investment in these last few months. BTW, despite whatever implied connections to FTX Gary Gensler has, his series of talks at MIT on blockchain[0] technologies leads me to believe that there are few people better equipped to navigate this from a political perspective. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":27177384,"author":"jazzyjackson","month":"2021-05","story":"Missing line in a smart contract leads to $10M hack","stance":"rec","why":"Blockchain lecture series by Gary Gensler.","quote":"I've been enjoying a playlist of blockchain lectures by Gary Gensler","text":"Well at least take a look around and see if it's interesting to you. I've been enjoying a playlist of blockchain lectures by Gary Gensler, current chair of the SEC. Lecture 6 is smart contracts with Lawrence Lessig guest speaking. [0]/[1] I've been avoiding smart contracts since hearing about hacks like these (similar to the Multisig Parity Bug years ago, neglected to initialize, let someone else become the owner and kill the contract) - but I've been educating myself the past week and find that there are really cool things that can be done, maybe cooler a year or 15 in the future when ETH finally gets its fees under control. As a programmer, you'd probably be interested to see the ethereum virtual machine's \"assembly\" language [2], I'm pretty impressed with how little code underlies all these ERC20 tokens. [0] [LINK] [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK]"},{"id":33574860,"author":"bogomipz","month":"2022-11","story":"Alameda, FTX Executives Are Said to Have Known FTX Was Using Customer Funds","stance":"reference","why":"Gensler taught blockchain course at MIT before leading the SEC.","quote":"Professor Gary Gensler taught a course at MIT called \"Blockchain And Money.","text":"Yes and before becoming Chair at the SEC, Professor Gary Gensler taught a course at MIT called \"Blockchain And Money.\"[1][2] Given that both SBF's parents are Professors at Stanford, this couple were very much ivory tower children. It would really be something if Gary Gensler was part of the SEC probe looking into the FTX collapse.[3] It's practically a Hollywood screenplay that almost writes itself. 1. [LINK] 2. [VIDEO] 3. [LINK]"},{"id":27930643,"author":"toomuchtodo","month":"2021-07","story":"Central Bank Digital Currency – US Senate Testimony of Dr. Neha Narula [pdf]","stance":"reference","why":"Gensler's MIT blockchain course cited in regulation argument.","quote":"","text":"Because there's no benefit over a central system. If you trust the central bank (which a vast majority of participants in the financial system do), you don't need distributed anything. Blockchains are for trustless systems. The US financial and capital systems are built on legal and regulatory trust. Your smart contract will never override a judge or a regulatory body. The federal government need not deploy a crypto token in order to kill Tether. They will simply regulate it out of existence. It is clear this is the path we're on from Yellen's statements and the nomination of Gary Gensler to the SEC (who previously taught about cryptocurrency systems at MIT) [1]. Globally, China is cracking down hard, and Europe seems to be preparing to do so. None of this should be unexpected to an educated scholar of history and nation state mechanics. > It is important to note that a CBDC might not be the only way to address some of these problems; for example, in the US we might improve financial inclusion by requiring commercial banks to provide free, no-minimum accounts to users [2] [3], or by limiting or eliminating fees, as these were some of the reasons listed when the US unbanked were asked why they don’t have bank accounts. So, instant payments are coming in the next 18 months [3] (it is actively in testing currently with a handful of participants), and free accounts for everyone is a stroke of legislative pen. What's blockchain solving? [1] [VIDEO] (MIT 15.S12 Blockchain and Money"}],"r":[["_DTpQ4Kk2wA",1],["B1J2RMorJXM",1],["OhnjMdzGusc",1],["sj8Sg8qnjOg",1],["Og6cTlwBTrk",1],["g2tMcMQqSbA",1],["jEbRVNxL44c",1]]},"Gksxu-yeWcU":{"wy":"HN uses it to illustrate how simply reordering scenes can transform a story.","q":{"x":"NerdWriter on youtube has a good video on how the change would look","i":27694881,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":27694881,"author":"rtkwe","month":"2021-06","story":"“The Martian” + Starship","stance":"rec","why":"Shows how the rearranged Passengers would play","quote":"NerdWriter on youtube has a good video on how the change would look","text":"Yeah but it wouldn't have been tempered by growing attached to Pratt's character and we would get to experience the same feeling of confusion as JLaw's character. NerdWriter on youtube has a good video on how the change would look, does a better job of selling it than I ever could: [VIDEO]"},{"id":16564231,"author":"aequitas","month":"2018-03","story":"Pixar's Rules of Storytelling (2013)","stance":"rec","why":"Shows how reordering scenes changes a movie","quote":"Just a minor change in order of the same scenes completely changed a movie.","text":"A good example of the rearranging is from Nerdwriters YouTube [VIDEO] Just a minor change in order of the same scenes completely changed a movie."},{"id":34137615,"author":"clarkdale","month":"2022-12","story":"Glass Onion: A Knives Out Mystery – Bad Writing","stance":"reference","why":"Example of re-ordering a movie's scenes to change its genre","quote":"A similar response was made to Passengers[1]","text":"It would be interesting to watch an edit played in chronological order. A similar response was made to Passengers[1], where if it is NOT made chronologically, the director could have an opportunity to push the theme entirely close to a horror genre. 1. [VIDEO]"},{"id":27693679,"author":"joshuahedlund","month":"2021-06","story":"“The Martian” + Starship","stance":"reference","why":"Passengers rearranged so the audience shares the character's confusion","quote":"The movie could have been great if it was oriented around the female passenger waking up","text":"The movie could have been great if it was oriented around the female passenger waking up and not knowing what had already happened before instead of the audience knowing everything linearly [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["NcOb3Dilzjc",1],["OMi6xgdSbMA",1],["AdKNCBrkZQ4",1],["iIpPuJ_r8Xg",1],["g3Ud6mHdhlQ",1]]},"LMxate9gegg":{"wy":"HN recommends the series as awesome and notes it is now legally on YouTube; commenters cite it for radio history.","q":{"x":"awesome series, now freely/legally posted on YT","i":35345436,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":35345436,"author":"throw0101b","month":"2023-03","story":"Apple Music Classical","stance":"rec","why":"Series now legally posted on YouTube","quote":"awesome series, now freely/legally posted on YT","text":"This is the \"Take Five\" variant by Val Bennett, \"The Russians Are Coming\", used in the show Secret Life of Machines (awesome series, now freely/legally posted on YT): * [VIDEO] * [LINK] * [LINK]"},{"id":29603391,"author":"y04nn","month":"2021-12","story":"I bought 1000 meters of wire to settle a physics debate [video]","stance":"rec","why":"Demonstrates AM radio picking up a switch's interference","quote":"it worth watching the whole video if you are interested","text":"Absolutely, if you turn on and off a light switch while listening an AM radio (even static) you would hear it through your speaker, as in this demonstration [1] (it worth watching the whole video if you are interested). [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":39826050,"author":"helsinkiandrew","month":"2024-03","story":"Radios, how do they work?","stance":"reference","why":"Remastered program recreating Hertz and Marconi experiments","quote":"which recreates some of Hertz and Marconi","text":"Tim Hunkin has posted a remastered version of his \"The Secret Life of the Radio\" TV program (from 1987) which recreates some of Hertz and Marconi's experiments with spark gaps and coherers. [VIDEO]"},{"id":37373598,"author":"djmips","month":"2023-09","story":"Coherer Effect [video]","stance":"reference","why":"Covers the coherer in context of radio development","quote":"also nicely covered in the Secret Life of Radio in context of the development of radio communication.","text":"also nicely covered in the Secret Life of Radio in context of the development of radio communication. [VIDEO]"},{"id":33727355,"author":"LeoPanthera","month":"2022-11","story":"The tragic birth of FM radio (2006)","stance":"reference","why":"Animated look at Armstrong's invention of FM radio","quote":"A slightly comical animated look at Edwin Armstrong","text":"A slightly comical animated look at Edwin Armstrong's invention of FM radio can be seen as part of Tim Hunkin's \"The Secret Life of the Radio\": [VIDEO] It's split into two parts, with the second part later in the same video, here: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["fXW-QjBsruE",1],["QWWgr2rN45o",1],["qE7dYhpI_bI",1],["HNkLcD3PKyk",1],["YcVSgYz5SJ8",1],["BCmz-fgp24E",1],["Ljzj9Msli5o",1]]},"MGMoRu5yrVc":{"wy":"Cited in Electron, Flutter and React Native debates as evidence that large products can share C++ core code with native views.","q":{"x":"Microsoft have a very interesting approach with their Office suite when it comes to cross-platform development.","i":14247185,"y":"2017"},"m":[{"id":14247185,"author":"alimbada","month":"2017-05","story":"Ask HN: Do you think Electron is good for building Desktop Apps?","stance":"rec","why":"Microsoft's approach to cross-platform Office development","quote":"Microsoft have a very interesting approach with their Office suite when it comes to cross-platform development.","text":"Microsoft have a very interesting approach with their Office suite when it comes to cross-platform development. They go through it in some detail in this talk: [VIDEO]"},{"id":12857499,"author":"golf1052","month":"2016-11","story":"Microsoft Teams, the new chat-based workspace in Office 365","stance":"rec","why":"How Office shares code between Windows and Mac","quote":"Pretty interesting talk about cross platform Office.","text":"Word for Windows and Word for Mac are much closer together than you may realize. Pretty interesting talk about cross platform Office. Part 1: [LINK] Part 2: [VIDEO]"},{"id":20981701,"author":"pjmlp","month":"2019-09","story":"Ask HN: React Native or Flutter?","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as verification of how Office does C++ with native views","quote":"they have plenty of talks at CppCon and BUILD about how they do it.","text":"Yes, C++ with native views. How do you think Microsoft does Office on Android and iOS? As for verification, they have plenty of talks at CppCon and BUILD about how they do it. EDIT: to save you some work. CppCon 2014: Zaika Antoun \"Microsoft w/ C++ to Deliver Office Across Different Platforms, Part I\" [LINK] CppCon 2014: Zaika Antoun \"Microsoft w/ C++ to Deliver Office Across Different Platforms, Part II\" [VIDEO] Reacting to Dropbox: another take on cross-platform C++ development [LINK] Ashampoo Systems GmbH [LINK]"},{"id":20614302,"author":"pjmlp","month":"2019-08","story":"Flutter looks good, but is painful","stance":"reference","why":"One of several cited CppCon cross-platform C++ talks","quote":"","text":"I did provide the information for it, Dropbox and Microsoft talks at CppCon, easy with some Google fu. CppCon 2014: Zaika Antoun \"Microsoft w/ C++ to Deliver Office Across Different Platforms, Part I\" [LINK] CppCon 2014: Zaika Antoun \"Microsoft w/ C++ to Deliver Office Across Different Platforms, Part II\" [VIDEO] CppCon 2015: Jacob Potter & Andrew Twyman “Bridging Languages Cross-Platform...\" [LINK] CppCon 2017: Stephen Spann “Cross-Platform Apps with Dropbox’s Djinni...” [LINK] CppCon 2014: T. Grue & S. Kabbes \"A Deep Dive into 2 Cross-Platform Mobile Apps Written in C++\" [LINK] Naturally the native UI has to be used. Are we talking about having a good user experience here, or having the easy lazy path for the developer?"},{"id":17895847,"author":"pjmlp","month":"2018-09","story":"$600 Chromebooks are a dangerous development for Microsoft","stance":"reference","why":"Office team talk on refactoring code for cross-platform use","quote":"The Office team did a couple of session at CppCon about how they refactored the code.","text":"Since 2014 not anymore. The Office team did a couple of session at CppCon about how they refactored the code. \"CppCon 2014: Zaika Antoun \"Microsoft w/ C++ to Deliver Office Across Different Platforms\" [LINK] [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["3HROqnw-nf4",1]]},"NST3u-GjjFw":{"wy":"The go-to reference for ChatOps, recommended for the idea of chat as a shared command line where teammates learn by watching.","q":{"x":"There's a pretty good talk on \"chatops\" by the guys at GitHub[0].","i":10742494,"y":"2015"},"m":[{"id":10742494,"author":"jzelinskie","month":"2015-12","story":"Slack Platform Launch","stance":"rec","why":"ChatOps as shared command line where developers learn by watching","quote":"There's a pretty good talk on \"chatops\" by the guys at GitHub[0].","text":"There's a pretty good talk on \"chatops\" by the guys at GitHub[0]. Basically, it provides a dialog around a \"shared command-line\" where developers from learn from each other by simply watching chat. I think it's more beneficial to distributed companies. However, my question for those using chatops is basically how do you keep the repository for the bot \"tame\"? It needs access to _everything_ if you want it to be useful, which means having lots of secrets exposed to the bot. [0]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":6734665,"author":"holman","month":"2013-11","story":"How GitHub No Longer Works","stance":"rec","why":"How GitHub's ops team runs operations through chat","quote":"if you're interested, check out @jnewland's talk on ChatOps","text":"Absolutely. As I mentioned, we have 150+ rooms that we chill in, and most of our day-to-day work practices are tied into chat. Mention someone's name in a room they're not present in and we send push notifications to their phone and desktop- works great to pull people in as needed, without needing to distract them with the noise of being in the room 24/7. Our ops team is particularly in deep with chat. Instead of siloing everyone's process off individually in SSH sessions, many operations happen in chat, so everyone can learn and help out when diagnosing problems. They've built some really fascinating tooling around the problems they face- if you're interested, check out @jnewland's talk on ChatOps: [VIDEO]"},{"id":25910932,"author":"sciurus","month":"2021-01","story":"Improving how we deploy GitHub","stance":"reference","why":"Early talk from 2013 on GitHub's chatops","quote":"Here's a relevant talk from 2013","text":"Github is well know for doing chatops for everything . In fact, I think they may have invented the term. Here's a relevant talk from 2013: [VIDEO]"},{"id":11056159,"author":"EvanPlaice","month":"2016-02","story":"SSH: Best practices","stance":"reference","why":"GitHub deploys via a bot rather than direct SSH","quote":"AFAIK, that's how GitHub manages deployments via HubBot.","text":"3. Never grand direct SSH to access the the staging/production environment. Automate the build and deployment environment on a system that developers don't have direct access. Instead of pushing changes, the bot pulls a specified release branch, builds, tests, and deploys the code. All without human interaction. If malicious code were somehow introduced from a developer's environment, it would be recorded and reflected in the commit history. AFAIK, that's how GitHub manages deployments via HubBot. See [VIDEO] . ----- To take things a step further, public-facing environments should be made immutable wherever possible. With the entire system being built and released as a whole. Docker alleviates some of the complexity and overhead but I think this space is where Unikernels have a lot of potential to shine. There's a very good talk about how the Wunderlist team used chaos and frequent destruction of their envronments to overcome fear and uncertainty here. [LINK] . Emphasis being placed on the the frequent disposal and recreation of environments rather than building long-running persistent environments. This setup probably won't work for long-lived systems (ex databases). In those cases, access via a transient environment like a USB bootable OS would be ideal to prevent persistent viruses/trojans. Ironically, the best current options are security-focused distros like KaliLinux that put a special emphasis on avoiding persistent state. Maybe one day soon we'll see an admin-focused "},{"id":10892994,"author":"EvanPlaice","month":"2016-01","story":"Ask HN: What software would you pay for right now?","stance":"reference","why":"Chat as a platform connecting data sources","quote":"Check this out","text":"The DevOps approach: Just write a Slack plugin for all those things, if they don't already exist (they totally do tho). /s In all seriousness. Communication IS the baseline requirement for the super-app you speak of. The next requirement is making the platform talk to any data source. Slack does both of these things surprisingly well. Check this out: [VIDEO]"},{"id":10662009,"author":"Rockslide","month":"2015-12","story":"GitHub's Metal Cloud","stance":"reference","why":"Talk on ChatOps","quote":"There was also a talk titled \"Chatops\" about this specific thing if you want to learn more","text":"There was also a talk titled \"Chatops\" about this specific thing if you want to learn more: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["VLHJUfgxEO4",1]]},"OINa46HeWg8":{"wy":"Cited in threads about phone addiction and distraction as a classic, widely seen illustration of the problem.","m":[{"id":6452743,"author":"freshhawk","month":"2013-09","story":"We're creating a culture of distraction","stance":"rec","why":"Short film on phone distraction with a great surreal creepy vibe.","quote":"","text":"Have you seen the short film called \"I Forgot my Phone\" ( [VIDEO] )? It deals with exactly this subject and has a great surreal creepy vibe. Made me think about how many times I've been that blinking person who didn't hear their food order, which I dislike but keep doing."},{"id":6284934,"author":"ohwp","month":"2013-08","story":"Life without a cell phone","stance":"rec","why":"Viral short film about forgetting your phone; commenter says it rings true.","quote":"","text":"I own a cell phone but never use it for the same reasons as you. Never missed it, never will. This month a video went viral about the subject: [VIDEO] (I Forgot My Phone). So true."},{"id":46913309,"author":"jcynix","month":"2026-02","story":"TikTok's 'Addictive Design' Found to Be Illegal in Europe","stance":"reference","why":"Classic video about phone absorption, cited as oldie but goldie.","quote":"","text":"When it's a streaming service, it might be equally worse IMO, but a bit less so if it's music you own. But anyways, I call these folks \"electro autists\" (with apologies to real autists) as they are rarely reachable for social interactions. Saying \"good morning\" in the elevator? No chance. Nor recognizing people left or right. Or in the gym, where they block machines for many minutes, i.e. much more than the one or two minutes of resting in between sets, while paging through social media in between sets. Asking them to unblock a machine in the gym? Some are reachable there if you stand in front of them and wave your hands. And walking the dog, or strolling with kids while on \"social\" media. I often observe them to neither recognize when either dog or kid try to show them things or events. I sometimes wonder (aloud and near them ;-), if they phone with their companions. Oldie but Goldie: Charlene Guzman's video \"I forgot my phone\" from 12 years ago: [VIDEO] I like music and I like videos, but I also learned to concentrate on the task at hand and/or the people besides me. Disclaimer: listening to music while doing chores like washing dishes is OK. But I prefer a dish washer and connect to people while the dish washer is running."},{"id":6288611,"author":"ladino","month":"2013-08","story":"Don’t Be Anti-Social During Meals With Friends.","stance":"reference","why":"Widely-seen video showing a first-world problem of phone distraction.","quote":"","text":"A lot of people got that video (I Forgot My Phone): [VIDEO] which shows a well known first world problem. Maybe there is time for an mobile device (n)etiquette? Let's catch some rules and i will put them together ;)"}],"r":[["CK62I-4cuSY",1],["KZjFVdU8VLI",1],["j-kj2qwJa_E",1]]},"Ot4qdCs54ZE":{"wy":"HN uses it to explain the difference between income and wealth inequality, though some pair it with a rebuttal.","q":{"x":"This video gives a good explanation about their difference","i":25772780,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":34869132,"author":"CalRobert","month":"2023-02","story":"Amsterdam supermarket offers free groceries, life coach to struggling families","stance":"rec","why":"Dutch wealth inequality video, worth watching alongside a rebuttal.","quote":"if you watch that it's well worth watching this Dutch economist's rebuttal","text":"All the same I would much, much, MUCH rather be poor in the Netherlands than the US. Just not having to operate a car would save tons. Also, not sure but this comment may be a reference to: How The Dutch Economy Shows We Can't Reduce Wealth Inequality With Taxes | Economics Explained [VIDEO] and if you watch that it's well worth watching this Dutch economist's rebuttal The Dutch Economy is NOT the Most Unequal [LINK]"},{"id":25772780,"author":"ricc","month":"2021-01","story":"Ask HN: How to increase SWE salaries in Europe?","stance":"rec","why":"Explains the difference between income and wealth inequality.","quote":"This video gives a good explanation about their difference","text":"This is just a PSA for those comparing financial (in)equality between Europe and the USA: income inequality is different from wealth equality. Income inequality might be lower in Europe but apparently wealth inequality is relatively high. This video gives a good explanation about their difference: [VIDEO]"},{"id":30634274,"author":"erichocean","month":"2022-03","story":"Moscow retaliates against Western sanctions with export bans","stance":"reference","why":"Says Dutch Gini skews high because of state-insured housing debt.","quote":"The Netherlands skew falsely high on the GINI coefficient because house loans are insured by the state","text":"From How The Dutch Economy Shows We Can't Reduce Wealth Inequality With Taxes : [VIDEO] > The Netherlands skew falsely high on the GINI coefficient because house loans are insured by the state, causing many families to technically be in large debt, beyond the value of their houses. That makes it hard to trust the wealth numbers, e.g. things like \"the top 10% of the country owns 60% of the wealth\" (according to the OCED). The bottom 60% of the country owns \"almost no\" wealth. It also explains why, if you visit The Netherlands, it doesn't seem like it is struggling with inequality… Source: [LINK]"},{"id":26789180,"author":"abelaer","month":"2021-04","story":"Just Be Rich","stance":"reference","why":"More detail on what the Gini index measures.","quote":"They go into some more detail here","text":"Sure, I'm not saying the housing market is in an especially healthy position, just that the Gini index measures the wrong thing. Its increase alarms people, but it just puts you higher on a list with relatively very equal social democracies at the top. They go into some more detail here: [VIDEO] Note that I'm also not arguing that a large Gini index is good, it just measures something very specific that doesn't really say a lot about inequality."}],"r":[["040ejWnFkj0",1],["YHLU0Uej1WA",1],["bglWCuCMSWc",1],["REni8Oi1QJQ",1],["KPUlgSRn6e0",1],["oHlpmxLTxpw",1],["r-TuGAHR78w",1],["0lvUByM-fZk",1],["CTV-wwszGw8",1]]},"PTUY16CkS-k":{"wy":"HN pointed to it in 2011 as a nice, tongue-in-cheek explanation of quantitative easing.","q":{"x":"QE is Quantitative Easing which is nicely explained in this great xtranormal video","i":2063542,"y":"2011"},"m":[{"id":2317691,"author":"patrickk","month":"2011-03","story":"Why Gas Is So Expensive Today (Hint: It’s Not Libya)","stance":"rec","why":"Tongue-in-cheek explanation of quantitative easing.","quote":"Another interesting video, if you haven't seen it already - Quantitative Easing explained","text":"Thanks for those. Another interesting video, if you haven't seen it already - Quantitative Easing explained: [VIDEO] \" Printing money is the last refuge of failed economic empires and banana republics and the Fed doesn't want to admit this is their only idea. \" Don't worry, it's (partially) tongue-in-cheek. \"In other words, we will see massive inflation, possible even hyper-inflation.\" Do governments ever learn? This is what happened in the Weimar Republic and is happening today in Zimbabwe. Where I live (Ireland), the government is trying to do option 1 and 2, without success so far. Austerity measures are hurting the economy short-term by dampening consumer confidence (fortunately, internal consumption isn't everything, as we are an extremely open economy and will pick up as exports grow.) They'd possibly be trying option 3, except the European Central Bank controls our currency and ECB rules state that a country can't run a deficit over 3% of GDP without incurring major penalties."},{"id":2063542,"author":"tricky","month":"2011-01","story":"Visualization of stock market performance over time, adjusted for inflation","stance":"rec","why":"Nice explanation of quantitative easing.","quote":"QE is Quantitative Easing which is nicely explained in this great xtranormal video","text":"For those of us who don't know - QE is Quantitative Easing which is nicely explained in this great xtranormal video: [VIDEO]"},{"id":2455148,"author":"getsat","month":"2011-04","story":"Online Cash Bitcoin could Challenge Governments, Banks","stance":"reference","why":"Cited regarding quantitative easing and fiat currency.","quote":"You won't have to worry about the quantitative easing when you're not using a fiat currency.","text":"1) You won't have to worry about the quantitative easing when you're not using a fiat currency. [VIDEO] 2) Read \"A Lodging of Wayfaring Men\". Fiction, but an example of the kind of change a system like BTC could usher in. [LINK] 3) You'd only convert as much as you need. > Is transferring BCs easier than writing a check? Yes. Enter address, enter amount, click send, wait a few minutes for the network to confirm the transaction."},{"id":16615784,"author":"pywh","month":"2018-03","story":"Money creation in the modern economy (2014) [pdf]","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"And just for giggles","text":"Grantham on finite growth: [LINK] Grantham on finite resources: [LINK] There was a nifty quote in Green Mars. Think it went something like \"need to shift from sustainable development to sustainable prosperity\". And just for giggles: [VIDEO]"},{"id":2618538,"author":"anonymoushn","month":"2011-06","story":"Groupon is a straight-up Ponzi scheme","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"the more tame treatments of related issues lack depth","text":"While this is nearly true, it acts very slowly, and most people don't know how U.S. dollars work. Since everyone is robbed of some small value rather than a small group being robbed of all value, you can't expect people to notice. Unfortunately most attempts to explain to the general public how U.S. dollars work contain other assertions that are plainly ridiculous (see [LINK] or [LINK] ), and the more tame treatments of related issues lack depth ( [VIDEO] )."}],"r":[["gmn7tjSNyAA",1],["np_ylvc8Zj8",1],["T71ibcZAX3I",1],["RxabLA7UQ9k",1],["Kxryv2XrnqM",1]]},"R13BD8qKeTg":{"wy":"HN points to it as an accessible introduction to Bayesian reasoning and as inspiration for personal projects.","q":{"x":"what is described as the \"Bayesian trap\", explained by the youtuber Veritasium","i":15733174,"y":"2017"},"m":[{"id":15733174,"author":"partycoder","month":"2017-11","story":"Think Bayes: Bayesian Statistics Made Simple (2012)","stance":"rec","why":"Explains the Bayesian trap for those unfamiliar with Bayes theorem.","quote":"what is described as the \"Bayesian trap\", explained by the youtuber Veritasium","text":"Another problem for the less familiar with the Bayes theorem is what is described as the \"Bayesian trap\", explained by the youtuber Veritasium: [VIDEO]"},{"id":14135256,"author":"burntrelish1273","month":"2017-04","story":"Receding glacier causes Canadian river to vanish in four days","stance":"rec","why":"Demonstrates how reality and data can differ significantly.","quote":"Videos demonstrating this with examples","text":"Definitely. Another good idea is to show the difference between reality and data can be overwhelmingly significant. Videos demonstrating this with examples: - \"The Bayesian Trap\" [VIDEO] - \"Is Most Research Wrong?\" [LINK]"},{"id":41132209,"author":"LolBatmanHuntsU","month":"2024-08","story":"Ask HN: What Are You Working On? (August 2024)","stance":"reference","why":"Inspired a Bayesian life-journal project.","quote":"Inspired by this Veritasium video","text":"Inspired by this Veritasium video: [VIDEO] I'm working on a journal that incorporates Bayesian learning on how aspects of my life affect my mental, physical and social well-being. Having used it for nearly 500 days I plan to turn it into a life accounting and auditing tool. [LINK]"},{"id":24390372,"author":"tmamic","month":"2020-09","story":"Ask HN: How to choose which dentist or doctor to believe?","stance":"reference","why":"Cited for statistics of multiple agreeing doctors.","quote":"You can be pretty sure they are right[1] because statistics.","text":"So, in order to do your job of maintaining your health , you need need to do all of these jobs: 1. Learn about the symptoms, diagnosis, and treatment of your health problem 2. Assess the severity of the health problem 3. Learn about the experience of other people 4. Get multiple opinions Bonus: 5. Learn about multiple valid solutions 6. Find doctor's blind spots While doing all that, you pay for healthcare, which includes all that. You pay a doctor to know no1, do the no2, remember no3. You then pay for multiple of them to do the same. But then you still get to the point where you have to decide who to believe. And it would be easy if 4 of them say the same thing. You can be pretty sure they are right[1] because statistics. But what happens when they give multiple differing opinions, like here [2]. [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK]"}],"r":[["LnS0PfNyj4U",1],["42QuXLucH3Q",1],["_tpB-B8BXk0",1],["JEYh5WACqEk",1],["5pdPrQFjo2o",1],["yFIa-Mc2KSk",1]]},"VI_riscmviI":{"wy":"HN mentions it with caveats: despite the author's reputation, commenters say it contains good investing and Excel material.","q":{"x":"has an excellent set of youtube tutorials on reading 10-k and 10-q forms to compile DCF valuation models","i":26974986,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":39551942,"author":"samstave","month":"2024-02","story":"Show HN: We built the fastest spreadsheet","stance":"rec","why":"Fast Excel skills plus due diligence and investing calculations.","quote":"there is good investing info here","text":"This is just a fun FYI ; This is 8 years old! (as of yesterday!!) but its pretty amazing: [VIDEO] Its Martin Skreli (yes that one) -- but see how fast this guy is with excel... this is what I imagine when I think of excel power users... (But also, he does a lesson in DD on investing and doing calcs in Excel - so aside from awesome Excel input, there is good investing info here - recall this was the guy that bought a medical company and immediately raised the price 7,000% or some such (he then bought the Wu Tang Album whilst imprisoned)"},{"id":26974986,"author":"ericjang","month":"2021-04","story":"Facebook Reports First Quarter 2021 Results [pdf]","stance":"rec","why":"Shkreli's tutorials on reading 10-K filings and building DCF models.","quote":"has an excellent set of youtube tutorials on reading 10-k and 10-q forms to compile DCF valuation models","text":"Martin Shkreli (yes, The pharma bro) has an excellent set of youtube tutorials on reading 10-k and 10-q forms to compile DCF valuation models. His video series focuses on household tech names here's the first one in the series: [VIDEO]"},{"id":40682785,"author":"verticalscaler","month":"2024-06","story":"The Microsoft Excel superstars throw down in Vegas","stance":"reference","why":"Excel playlist by Shkreli, known for speed, but dated and finance-focused.","quote":"So there's this weird playlist about Excel by Martin Shkreli of all people","text":"So there's this weird playlist about Excel by Martin Shkreli of all people: [VIDEO] It keeps popping up as he is apparently quick and knows the ins and outs. But I've never bothered to go through it as it is seven years old at this point and focused on finance. Can anybody recommend something similar but up to date with the new goodies so a fella could be competitive at this? I'm talking more keyboard shortcuts and advanced features (many of them overlap). The latest versions of Excel sort of open it up anew. puts on headband and cracks knuckles"},{"id":28971353,"author":"goldenkey","month":"2021-10","story":"Federal judges or their brokers traded stocks of litigants during cases","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as evidence Shkreli is not dumb.","quote":"He's by no means dumb","text":"Indeed, moreso pride and lack of wisdom. Perhaps a bit of martyrdom. Shkreli liked playing around with these people who literally wanted to slay him. I don't think he understood how seriously evil and petty boomers can be, and how most judges are from that generation. He took one for the team in a sense, showing how the justice system serves as the instrument of vengeance for the executive and legislative branches. He's by no means dumb [1,2] but yes, honest to the point of being blunt. Very much in the spirit of Mike Tyson who is also extremely intelligent but often portrayed as a dolt. [3,4,5] [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK] [3] [LINK] [4] [LINK] [5] [LINK]"},{"id":22731148,"author":"DonHopkins","month":"2020-03","story":"Neanderthals Feasted on Seafood, Seabirds, Perhaps Even Dolphins","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"If you eat Martin Shkreli's brain, maybe you'll inherit his skill with spreadsheets! Worth a try. [LINK] [VIDEO] [LINK]"}],"r":[["gQnmP7UD_zk",1],["XI5frPV58tY",1],["lHcTKWiZ8sI",1],["8b7erU_DOfE",1],["rOwMW6agpTI",1],["mPqWstVnRjQ",1]]},"Vrq2akzdFq8":{"wy":"HN recommends it as an in-depth independent review that tempers expectations and adds technical detail beyond Magic Leap's own materials.","q":{"x":"I'd recommend you check out the [Tested review][1]","i":17860764,"y":"2018"},"m":[{"id":21726301,"author":"AndrewKemendo","month":"2019-12","story":"Dented Reality: Magic Leap Sees Slow Sales, Steep Losses","stance":"rec","why":"In-depth independent review of Magic Leap One.","quote":"Here's Tested's in-depth review from last year","text":"There are plenty of independent videos available. Here's Tested's in-depth review from last year: [VIDEO]"},{"id":17829243,"author":"reggieband","month":"2018-08","story":"Magic Leap One Teardown","stance":"rec","why":"Review tempers expectations despite some promotional tone.","quote":"Worth watching Tested's review on youtube","text":"Worth watching Tested's review on youtube [VIDEO] I was expecting typical astroturfing and there is a bit of that. But they are also tempering expectations."},{"id":17860764,"author":"Ajedi32","month":"2018-08","story":"Magic Leap One now available for $2295","stance":"rec","why":"Technical details beyond Magic Leap's own materials.","quote":"I'd recommend you check out the [Tested review][1]","text":"Not many technical details from Magic Leap themselves, unfortunately. I'd recommend you check out the [Tested review][1] and [iFixit teardown][2]. [1]: [VIDEO] [2]: [LINK]"},{"id":20253016,"author":"shasheene","month":"2019-06","story":"Magic Leap Says Ex-Engineer Copied Headset for China","stance":"reference","why":"Reference review of Magic Leap One Creator Edition.","quote":"here's the Tested review for current Magic Leap product","text":"The article reports on the lawsuit, but is light on details about the headset. Tested recently did a 10 minute review on this headset, the 'Nreal Light' [1] [LINK] Regardless of the merits of the lawsuit, I can see why Magic Leap would be scared. The AR glasses looks great. They're certainly stylish enough for the mass-market -- they look like normal glasses at first glance, they're quite reasonably priced (at $500), and run from a regular Android phones via USB-C (for power, as well as sensor and video data). For reference, here's the Tested review for current Magic Leap product, the Magic Leap One Creator Edition: [VIDEO] . It retails for $2295. Though it comes with controllers, eye-tracking for focal accommodation, two waveguides displays that are used to provide two separate physical layers to focus on."}],"r":[["XnRCZK3KjUY",1],["gCWtkvDQ2ZI",1],["HeQX2HjkcNo",1]]},"ZTdUmlGxVo0":{"wy":"HN points to it as a vivid example of the clash between two philosophies of Linux desktop design, often in systemd and Wayland arguments, though many find it disrespectful.","q":{"x":"well worth to watch to see the two different approaches to clash.","i":24027841,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":5665347,"author":"justsee","month":"2013-05","story":"Linux is not about choice (2008)","stance":"rec","why":"Entertaining talk on desktop Linux with interruptions.","quote":"This was an entertaining talk from CCC in 2011 on desktop Linux.","text":"This was an entertaining talk from CCC in 2011 on desktop Linux. 'datenwolf' was the speaker, and he starts talking about sound in Linux here. [1] He's quickly interrupted by the maintainer of PulseAudio, and this interruption continues throughout the talk. My takeaway was that if there was still this amount of confusion and misunderstanding in the community, my frustrating experiences with sound in desktop Ubuntu was not atypical of dekstop Linux users. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":5325533,"author":"ciupicri","month":"2013-03","story":"Crowding out OpenBSD","stance":"rec","why":"Talk on Linux and BSD desktop.","quote":"I strongly recommend watching","text":"If you think that the OpenBSD has it right, why don't you use it as a desktop, too? Why aren't you running a virtual machine of Windows 7 under OpenBSD? Probably because there's no proper virtualization support in OpenBSD. Linux is complex because sometimes it has too be, not just for the sake of it. systemd (which is great in my opinion) wouldn't be possible without cgroups. Should I also mention the better multi-processor support? Regarding the desktop, I strongly recommend watching \"27c3 - Desktop on the Linux... (and BSD, of course) - Wolfgang Draxinger (+ Lennart Poettering)\" [1]. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":26012752,"author":"vetinari","month":"2021-02","story":"Wayland is not ready as a 1:1 compatible Xorg replacement just yet","stance":"reference","why":"Example of clash of approaches in Linux desktop.","quote":"These two approaches still clash","text":"There is no split \"terrible for users, great for some developers\". Casual users do have issue with X; see this thread recently on HN: [LINK] . Would you consider that normal for users to handle? Of course not. Casual users having no issue with X is a lie. The fact is, that some subsytems on Linux are pile of hacks piled upon something built on assumptions that are no longer true. X11 is one of such subsystems. It is a dead end. When you are stuck in the dead end, you can either back up and once you are out, continue in your ride, or keep being stuck in the dead end. Wayland is backing out of the dead end; yes, it is short-term, high intensity pain, when you are not going forward, and even have to go back for a while. Piling new hacks on top of X11 is trying how to continue going forward without backing out of that dead-end. You will be stuck in that place for a long time, and ultimately you will move nowhere. That is long-term, low-intensity pain. Some people do not take the long term view (or do want to avoid high-intensity pain), being stuck in a place works for them (and chronic low-intensity pain is fine to them). These two approaches still clash, it is this [VIDEO] , all over again, 10 years later."},{"id":24027841,"author":"vetinari","month":"2020-08","story":"GTK 3.99","stance":"reference","why":"Clash of two approaches to Linux desktop design.","quote":"well worth to watch to see the two different approaches to clash.","text":"It's not that simple; explaining one reason why something would not work means re-examing many of the underlying assumptions and not many like to go deeper than that one problem at hand in isolation. The \"ok, how would you do it\" forces thinking about underlying issues, because the solution you propose has to account for factors you otherwise wouldn't think of. It is not a new problem either; this video is 9 years old already: [VIDEO] . It is almost one hour long, but well worth to watch to see the two different approaches to clash."},{"id":10524901,"author":"federererer","month":"2015-11","story":"Videos from systemd.conf 2015","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Do any of the videos involve prominent systemd contributors interrupting the presenters, like in the notorious \"Desktop on the Linux\" video at [VIDEO] ?"},{"id":5092432,"author":"EvilLook","month":"2013-01","story":"Unix and the design of modern Linux systems","stance":"reference","why":"Talk on Linux desktop being discussed.","quote":"","text":"The talk that datenwolf is talking about is on YouTube at [VIDEO] I have posted that comment on the linked blog (it's awaiting moderation) without noticing that this post is several months old, so I've posted that same information here in case the author decides there's no reason approving a comment on a blog post that's several months old!"},{"id":31966843,"author":"vetinari","month":"2022-07","story":"Wayland is not ready as a 1:1 compatible Xorg replacement just yet","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Suggestions like that always remind me this","text":"Great. So you managed the easy part - to scale your screen. The elephant in the room that you still ignore after several comments in this thread is, that the display manager cannot know magic variables for misc clients, it can barely tell clients apart (and it certainly doesn't know their image names, linked libraries or environment or how they were launched), and it shouldn't know, the clients should tell what resolution they are using so the display manager can adjusts their display. Wayland clients do that, X11 don't. No, you can't paper over it with some scripts. Suggestions like that always remind me this: [VIDEO]"},{"id":3385422,"author":"hasmoo","month":"2011-12","story":"Anti Leonard Poettering petition: Stop writing useless linux programs.","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Check out this epic battle between Draxinger and Poettering","text":"Haha, \"Wolfgang Draxinger\" signed 28 days ago. Check out this epic battle between Draxinger and Poettering: [VIDEO]"},{"id":38427691,"author":"zlg_codes","month":"2023-11","story":"PipeWire 1.0.0","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"He interrupted throughout the talk and then gets up on stage at the end. Quite disrespectful","text":"[VIDEO] He interrupted throughout the talk and then gets up on stage at the end. Quite disrespectful, and it's not even the talk I was looking for. Side note to flaggers: defend your decision please. I see no real errors in the comment. We're here to discuss."},{"id":36664417,"author":"vetinari","month":"2023-07","story":"Wayland on OpenBSD","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"It is still this, all over again","text":"> I think it is because Linux users tend to be more familiar with their software than users of other OSes. But they really don't. They might have familiarized at one point of time, but that snapshot is not where the world is at now. They cannot really expect that the world around them will stop developing, so their know-how and hacks stay current. It is still this, all over again: [VIDEO] . 12 years later and we still argue in circles."},{"id":11604214,"author":"anonbanker","month":"2016-04","story":"Rethinking PID 1 (2010)","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"","text":"The drama started around the time of Poettering's hijacking of someone else's 27c3 speech, accusing people that don't use logind of \"hating handicapped people\"[0], and continued from there. 0. [VIDEO]"},{"id":8780092,"author":"Jakuv2000","month":"2014-12","story":"Skipping fsck during boot with systemd?","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"Just watch this video of him disrupting a talk","text":"Don't forget Lennart. Just watch this video of him disrupting a talk: [VIDEO] The shenanigans start around 12 minutes in, and continue throughout. The last few minutes are particularly weird. He even gets up on stage at around 53:40. It's unbelievable!"},{"id":8477877,"author":"dnlrn","month":"2014-10","story":"Shall we fork Debian?","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"the presenter just spreads random FUD","text":"[VIDEO] This is the talk you mean right? Have you actually seen the talk? Yes, Lennart interrupts the talk but everything he says is correct, while the presenter just spreads random FUD. The presenter clearly shows that he doesn’t understand most of the stuff he talks about, talks about 3 year old bugs that already got fixed, etc. Yes, it’s not the nicest way to interrupt a talk, but the presenter should have expected some kind of reaction, given the controversial headline. People like Lennart put their heart into their software. If you shit all over it and spread random FUD, you can expect some response. What would you have done? Also he doesn’t “take over the stage”. He comes up after the talk has ended, because people on IRC asked him to."},{"id":5124492,"author":"4ad","month":"2013-01","story":"Systemd: The Biggest Myths","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"","text":"Downvoting this post is unwarranted. Everything that Lennart Poettering has ever done was an unreliable clusterfuck[1] astray from the Unix philosphy. There's nothing that he has ever done that is considered \"good\" by a majority of Unix-derivative users. [1] [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["orMtwOz6Db0",1]]},"aWvHrih-Juk":{"wy":"Recommended as the practical guide for anyone planning to attend a Starship launch in person.","q":{"x":"Everyday Astronaut did a great writeup on seeing a launch in South Texas","i":40919606,"y":"2024"},"m":[{"id":40919606,"author":"gangstead","month":"2024-07","story":"Guide to attending a space launch in person","stance":"rec","why":"Writeup on seeing a launch in South Texas.","quote":"Everyday Astronaut did a great writeup on seeing a launch in South Texas","text":"I've been to the last 3 Starship Launches, it's only a 10 hour drive each way! I hope I'm able to go to everyone and to eventually see a booster catch in person. Everyday Astronaut did a great writeup on seeing a launch in South Texas: [VIDEO] My preferred way is to get a hotel room on the south end of the island (Everyday Astronaut recommends Margaritaville and I agree). I woke up an hour before launch and walked a mile down the beach with my kids to Isla Blanca park. Coming from off island you'll have to leave much earlier because the bridge onto the island becomes a parking lot. Hearing protection is probably wise, but it's a different kind of \"loud\". You feel it in your chest. Looking at the distances you are much closer to a much bigger rocket in South Padre than in Florida. The most important thing is to get down at least a day early and drive over to the rocket. Starbase and the OLM are on a public road so you just pull over and look straight up at the world's biggest rocket. Also plan on being there a couple extra days to account for delays. Second most important thing is to go to Hopper Haus (Starbase themed bar/restaurant in Port Isabel). People always say to me they'd like to join, when's the next launch? Driving down there is a leap of faith since there is only an announcement 4-5 days out and the launch license sometimes isn't issued until the day before. So you have to be ready and have a flexible schedule."},{"id":35592452,"author":"exhilaration","month":"2023-04","story":"SpaceX's First Starship Launch Tomorrow","stance":"rec","why":"Guide to visiting Starbase and seeing a launch.","quote":"lots of good stuff in there about visiting, especially this video","text":"You should read the previous thread [LINK] lots of good stuff in there about visiting, especially this video [VIDEO]"},{"id":38258383,"author":"stevep98","month":"2023-11","story":"Starship will attempt a launch this weekend","stance":"reference","why":"Covers area layout and which parts close during launch.","quote":"Everyday astronaut has a video discussing the layout of the area and which parts are closed during launch.","text":"Everyday astronaut has a video discussing the layout of the area and which parts are closed during launch. [VIDEO]"},{"id":38266760,"author":"tonylemesmer","month":"2023-11","story":"Starship will attempt a launch this weekend","stance":"reference","why":"Explains places to watch launches and travel there.","quote":"explains places to watch launches from and travel to/from those places.","text":"One of the everyday astronaut’s videos explains places to watch launches from and travel to/from those places. [0] [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["ue-1JoJQaEg",1]]},"hJP5GqnTrNo":{"wy":"Cited in AI-and-education threads as the optimistic case and demo for AI tutoring, and as a source on Khan Academy's LLM approach.","q":{"x":"Sal Khan gave a good Ted Talk on his thoughts around AI in Education.","i":40981589,"y":"2024"},"m":[{"id":44526681,"author":"xnx","month":"2025-07","story":"An open letter from educators who refuse the call to adopt GenAI in education","stance":"rec","why":"Case that AI tutors can be patient and affordable","quote":"With the right system prompt, AI can be a patient, understanding, encouraging, non-judgemental tutor that adapts and moves at the student's pace.","text":"> GenAI is a threat to student learning and wellbeing. This blanket dismissal is not going to age well, and reads like a profession lashing out. With the right system prompt, AI can be a patient, understanding, encouraging, non-judgemental tutor that adapts and moves at the student's pace. Most students can not afford that type of human tutor, but an AI one could be free or very affordable. \"How AI Could Save (Not Destroy) Education\" ( [VIDEO] ) from Sal Khan of Khan Academy"},{"id":40981570,"author":"verdverm","month":"2024-07","story":"I am starting an AI+Education company","stance":"rec","why":"Optimistic take and demo of AI tutoring","quote":"Sal Khan provides a more optimistic take and demo","text":"You are just giving them ChatGPT with a bit of prompt engineering, and evaluating them on math problems, which we know LLMs make errors on because they are not calculators. You aren't putting in the effort needed to build a real tutor and learning assistant. I would not extrapolate from these results There are also a lot of things that can come in before you build a full on tutor. One example is being able to tailor word problems (transform the nouns) to subjects interesting to the particular student. They could also be used to help understand where students are struggling. We are still at the early phases of useful AI, optimism is more appreciated, especially as contemporary times have become so pessimistic Sal Khan provides a more optimistic take and demo: [VIDEO]"},{"id":40981589,"author":"verdverm","month":"2024-07","story":"I am starting an AI+Education company","stance":"rec","why":"Good talk with demos of what an AI tutor could be","quote":"Sal Khan gave a good Ted Talk on his thoughts around AI in Education.","text":"Sal Khan gave a good Ted Talk on his thoughts around AI in Education. Some pretty cool demos in there showing what a tutor could be like and other opportunities [VIDEO] How AI Could Save (Not Destroy) Education | Sal Khan --- edit: Khanmigo has its own site now [LINK]"},{"id":47051631,"author":"toomuchtodo","month":"2026-02","story":"Students Are Being Treated Like Guinea Pigs: Inside an AI-Powered Private School","stance":"reference","why":"Khan's TED talk on this AI tutoring model predates the school","quote":"Sal Khan, the founder and CEO of Khan Academy, did a TED talk [3] on this.","text":"They're copying one of Khan Academy's implementation models [1] and rebranding it as AI. It's certainly not new besides the \"help yourself to AI\" part (which, full disclosure, Khan Academy is working on as well with their \"Khanmigo\" assistant [2]). Sal Khan, the founder and CEO of Khan Academy, did a TED talk [3] on this. [1] [LINK] [2] [LINK] [3] How AI Could Save (Not Destroy) Education | Sal Khan | TED - [VIDEO] - May 1st, 2023"},{"id":40385173,"author":"xnx","month":"2024-05","story":"LearnLM models tuned for human learning","stance":"reference","why":"Sal Khan's optimism for AI in education","quote":"I share Sal Khan's (of Khan Academy) optimism for the possibilities for AI in education","text":"I share Sal Khan's (of Khan Academy) optimism for the possibilities for AI in education so I'm glad to see Google working on this too. Like a lot of areas, AI won't be better than the best teachers, but most of us weren't taught by the best teacher. If AI can create a tireless, patient, knowledgeable teacher, that will be a tremendous asset to humanity. How AI Could Save (Not Destroy) Education: [VIDEO]"},{"id":40479737,"author":"EGreg","month":"2024-05","story":"Microsoft, Khan Academy Partner to Make Khanmigo Teaching Tool Free","stance":"reference","why":"Khan on AI solving the two sigma tutoring problem","quote":"A few years ago I saw Salman Khan give a talk about AI solving the Two Sigma problem","text":"Sorry can someone explain to me why the teachers are needed at all in these subjects given the Khan Academy? A few years ago I saw Salman Khan give a talk about AI solving the Two Sigma problem, obviating the need for teachers: [VIDEO]"},{"id":35784008,"author":"jstx1","month":"2023-05","story":"ChatGPT: It has no inner monologue or meta-cognition","stance":"reference","why":"Source for Khan Academy letting the AI think before speaking","quote":"that we can dramatically improve its ability in math and tutoring if we allow the AI to think before it speaks","text":"> We realized (...) that we can dramatically improve its ability in math and tutoring if we allow the AI to think before it speaks. This is from Sal Khan's TED talk on how they're integrating ChatGPT into Khan Academy - [VIDEO]"},{"id":35795798,"author":"dhon_","month":"2023-05","story":"ChatGPT: It has no inner monologue or meta-cognition","stance":"reference","why":"Mentions chatbot with internal monologue, light on details","quote":"It's light on details but might be a starting point.","text":"I just watched a Ted talk by Sal Kahn (founder of Kahn academy) where he talks about giving their chat bot an internal monologue. It's light on details but might be a starting point. [VIDEO]"},{"id":35842736,"author":"unshavedyak","month":"2023-05","story":"Using ChatGPT to generate a GPT project end-to-end","stance":"reference","why":"Blurb about AI prompting itself and judging its own output","quote":"Khan Academy's use of LLMs was interesting","text":"I don't know how true it is vs how much PR it is, but Khan Academy's use of LLMs was interesting in that they apparently craft a prompt from the AI itself. Ie a two step process where the AI generates the steps, and then the AI reasons about the result of the steps to attempt and judge the accuracy of the data. This was just a blurb from the Ted talk[1], but i'd be interested in seeing a slightly more in depth explanation about this strategy. Made me wonder if you could have an recursive cycle of thinking. Where the \"AI\" prompts itself, reasons on the output, and does that repeatedly with guards such that it will stop if it doesn't judge anymore advancement. [1]: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["eGffUODWWSE",1],["vtIzMaLkCaM",1],["WNWYk4DdT_E",1],["zVhQOhxb1Mc",1],["C_r5UJrxcck",1],["5AVO1IyfA9M",1]]},"vI5pbDFDZyI":{"wy":"HN commenters cite it as a concise illustration of how the Netherlands narrowly avoided car-centric planning and then built its bike culture.","q":{"x":"about those plans, the fightback in the 1970s, and the resulting bike culture that Amsterdam has today.","i":30045157,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":32295902,"author":"Tijdreiziger","month":"2022-07","story":"When cities treated cars as dangerous intruders","stance":"rec","why":"Interesting video on the avoided US-style highway plan for Amsterdam.","quote":"Not Just Bikes made an interesting video on the thankfully-avoided plan to build a US-style highway system in Amsterdam","text":"Not Just Bikes made an interesting video on the thankfully-avoided plan to build a US-style highway system in Amsterdam: [VIDEO]"},{"id":30045157,"author":"stevesimmons","month":"2022-01","story":"The Future of E-Bikes","stance":"rec","why":"Short video on Amsterdam's highway plans and the fight that created bike culture.","quote":"about those plans, the fightback in the 1970s, and the resulting bike culture that Amsterdam has today.","text":"I do all those things in Amsterdam. I can get everywhere in Amsterdam much quicker by bike than driving or public transport. Cycling in the rain and snow [2] is no problem, you just need the right gear. What non-Amsterdammers don't realise is the city never used to be a cyclist's paradise. In the 1960s, Amsterdam's car-obsessed urban planners had grand visions to drive motorways through the inner city, razing the historical buildings, and constructing Manhattan-like skyscraper clusters. Here's a 3 minute video \"Highways Almost Destroyed Amsterdam\" [1] about those plans, the fightback in the 1970s, and the resulting bike culture that Amsterdam has today. Enjoy! [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK]"},{"id":39568555,"author":"BadHumans","month":"2024-03","story":"California Approves Waymo Expansion to Los Angeles and SF Peninsula [pdf]","stance":"reference","why":"Amsterdam before it shunned cars.","quote":"Here is a video about Amsterdam before they shunned cars.","text":"Amsterdam and Paris used to be both be very car dependent. Here is a video about Amsterdam before they shunned cars. [VIDEO]"},{"id":32295434,"author":"scrollaway","month":"2022-07","story":"When cities treated cars as dangerous intruders","stance":"reference","why":"How the Netherlands narrowly avoided car-centric highway planning.","quote":"The Netherlands narrowly avoided it","text":"Indeed it wasn’t always this way. And this mindset plagued more countries. The Netherlands narrowly avoided it: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["q3OuCR0EpGo",1],["ZYD9yyMh9Hk",1]]},"vQpUV--2Jao":{"wy":"Offered as a good explanation of the business of dollar stores and how they affect local grocers.","q":{"x":"I think it does a good job explaining the problem","i":37670443,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":39692441,"author":"bonyt","month":"2024-03","story":"Family Dollar to close almost 1,000 stores","stance":"rec","why":"Business of dollar stores.","quote":"A couple of good videos about the business of dollar stores with similar takes","text":"A couple of good videos about the business of dollar stores with similar takes: [LINK] (Last Week Tonight with John Oliver) [VIDEO] (Wendover Productions)"},{"id":37670443,"author":"hnfong","month":"2023-09","story":"Rethinking the Luddites in the Age of A.I","stance":"rec","why":"Explains how dollar stores displace grocers.","quote":"I think it does a good job explaining the problem","text":"> There is nothing inevitable about the dollar store putting the grocer out of business. I recently came across this, and I think it does a good job explaining the problem: [VIDEO]"},{"id":43633489,"author":"natebc","month":"2025-04","story":"Small Town America vs. Big Box Stores","stance":"reference","why":"Dollar General's supplier leverage and custom SKUs.","quote":"they can get better deals from suppliers than Walmart and even get custom SKUs","text":"There's a minute or two in this video(1) about DG from Wendover Productions last year that discusses this. Apparently they're so powerful they can get better deals from suppliers than Walmart and even get custom SKUs ($1 sizes of Tide Detergent, for example) from them to be the product on shelves in DG. Kinda crazy. 1) [VIDEO]"},{"id":39028615,"author":"TinyRick","month":"2024-01","story":"Judge blocks JetBlue from acquiring Spirit Airlines","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"Wendover on YouTube did a video on this recently [0].","text":"> Their cost structure nickel and dimes the consumer so much that by the time you get to the destination you may have paid more for a much less comfortable trip That lends more credence to the comparison against Dollar General and similar discount stores. Dollar stores generally charge a higher price relative to quantity, but they offer smaller quantities than big box stores which allows their customers to spend less (but also get a lot less). Wendover on YouTube did a video on this recently [0]. [0] [VIDEO]"}]},"yaonVYOTSsk":{"wy":"Recommended as a companion to Micro Men, with the real Acorn people commenting on how events actually happened.","q":{"x":"That drama is even more enjoyable the second time around with commentary from three of the Acorn team portrayed","i":25489874,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":30398939,"author":"klelatti","month":"2022-02","story":"ZX Spectrum at 40: a look back","stance":"rec","why":"Participants watch Micro Men and recall their own versions.","quote":"And seeing some of the participants watch it and recall their own versions of the story is good too","text":"It's easy to quibble about some aspects of the Speccy - the rubber keyboard or the limited BASIC for example - but the key thing is that it offered an awful lot for the price - starting at £125 in the UK when the cheapest BBC Micro was £300. This made computing with colour and enough memory to write programs that were substantial and interesting available to a much wider group than before. Sir Clive Sinclair had a unique genius for designing and then marketing products that caught the public imagination. Sadly, he seemed to lose interest in building on his success and quickly moved on to the next project. Famously the C5 electric \"car\" but also wafer scale memory! [1] If anyone hasn't seen Micro Men, on the Sinclair vs Acorn rivalry, its definitely worth a watch: [LINK] And seeing some of the participants watch it and recall their own versions of the story is good too: [VIDEO] [1] [LINK]"},{"id":25489874,"author":"timrichard","month":"2020-12","story":"The Acorn Archimedes and the first ARM processor","stance":"rec","why":"Commentary from Acorn team members portrayed in the drama.","quote":"That drama is even more enjoyable the second time around with commentary from three of the Acorn team portrayed","text":"That drama is even more enjoyable the second time around with commentary from three of the Acorn team portrayed : [VIDEO]"},{"id":43323185,"author":"amiga386","month":"2025-03","story":"A technical history of Acorn Computers","stance":"reference","why":"Chris Curry and Hermann Hauser watch Micro Men and chat.","quote":"It would appear you can watch Chris Curry and Hermann Hauser watch Micro Men","text":"It would appear you can watch Chris Curry and Hermann Hauser watch Micro Men : [VIDEO] -- and then have a post-viewing chat: [LINK] \"I saw the first five minutes and had to run away, because I couldn't bear to see myself portrayed by Martin Freeman\" -- Chris Curry"},{"id":38470059,"author":"tibbydudeza","month":"2023-11","story":"Apple makes up less than 5% of Arm's revenue, and Arm can't do anything about it","stance":"reference","why":"Roundtable with Hermann Hauser on the tenth anniversary.","quote":"Roundtable of the 10th year anniversary","text":"The German guy (Hermann Hauser) is still around doing his thing - think living in the UK rubbed off on him. Roundtable of the 10th year anniversary [VIDEO]"},{"id":35312891,"author":"kencausey","month":"2023-03","story":"Egad 7 key British PCs of the 1980s Americans might have missed","stance":"reference","why":"Anniversary event with Acorn founders.","quote":"","text":"I found these 2: Micro Men - 10th Anniversary - With Chris Curry, Steve Furber and Hermann Hauser [VIDEO] Micro Men - 10th Anniversary - The Chat After the Film [LINK]"}],"r":[["GJCF-Ufapu8",1],["HF1luRD4Qmk",1],["HyznrdDSSGM",1],["XXBxV6-zamM",1],["_6sh097Dk5k",1]]},"3FAmr1la6w0":{"wy":"HN quotes it whenever debating GDP and metrics of success beyond money.","q":{"x":"GDP includes money spent on addictive and harmful drugs, polluting industries, and many other ventures that are actively harmful.","i":40867720,"y":"2024"},"m":[{"id":38954677,"author":"lnxg33k1","month":"2024-01","story":"Meta unlawfully ignores the users' right to easily withdraw consent: complaint","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Kennedy's remarks on measuring success beyond money.","quote":"you had a guy called Kennedy coming up with this perfection","text":"It baffles me that the US has been the nest of some of the greatest things for human beings, and now has completely forgotten itself, it baffles me that you had a guy called Kennedy coming up with this perfection [VIDEO] And now you remember nothing of what he said, it is unbelievable that you're here only measuring the success of a company regardless of the effects it has on the people, you measure the success of the company by money, ok. I consider US tech company as the worst, as the most exploiting, depressing, cheating, dangerous, who serve no purpose other than the one of their shareholders. Do you own the definition of success?"},{"id":40867720,"author":"commodoreboxer","month":"2024-07","story":"I Received an AI Email","stance":"reference","why":"Kennedy noting GDP counts harmful spending such as drugs and pollution.","quote":"GDP includes money spent on addictive and harmful drugs, polluting industries, and many other ventures that are actively harmful.","text":"GDP is a measure. I'm very much not an economist, but I am extremely skeptical that the health of an economy can be reduced to any single number. Goodheart's law and all. I concede that GDP is a good indicator, but I think you can have things that help GDP while simultaneously hurting the economy. Otherwise any scam or con would be considered beneficial, and it would make sense to mandate minimum individual spending to ensure economic activity. A low GDP inherently shows poor economic health, but a high GDP does not guarantee good health. In my mind (noting, again, that I'm no economist), economic health is defined by the effectiveness of allocating resources to things that are beneficial to the members of that economy. Any amount of GDP can be \"waste\", resources flowing to places where they do not benefit the public. As Robert Kennedy famously pointed out, GDP includes money spent on addictive and harmful drugs, polluting industries, and many other ventures that are actively harmful.[0] [0]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":28370769,"author":"lcam84","month":"2021-08","story":"Clinging Desperately to a Metaphor (2011)","stance":"reference","why":"Robert Kennedy's speech critiquing GDP as a measure of progress.","quote":"It measures everything, in short, except that which makes life really worth living.","text":"Gross Domestic Product, is a very gross way to measure progress as the name implies. Robert Kennedy said in respect of GDP “It measures everything, in short, except that which makes life really worth living.” [1] You have alternatives like the genuine progress indicator (GPI) that includes aspects such as pollution, criminality and health to the GDP. Most of all we can have many measures, we should not reduce progress to only a number. [1] [VIDEO]"}]},"RXJKdh1KZ0w":{"wy":"HN uses it as a joke whenever jargon-heavy or meaningless technical pitches appear, and praises it as a masterclass in technobabble.","q":{"x":"the retroencabultaor video [1] is a masterclass on how to do it.","i":24501329,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":24501329,"author":"andreareina","month":"2020-09","story":"When Math Gets Impossibly Hard","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Satirical technobabble video as masterclass of the form","quote":"the retroencabultaor video [1] is a masterclass on how to do it.","text":"If you haven't seen it yet, the retroencabultaor video [1] is a masterclass on how to do it. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":46321625,"author":"n1xis10t","month":"2025-12","story":"Show HN: Stop AI scrapers from hammering your self-hosted blog (using porn)","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"Indeed, complexity (both cyclomatic and post-frontal) must be reduced such that the two spurving bearings make a direct line with the panametric fan. For more details consult this instructional video: [VIDEO]"},{"id":43668903,"author":"paranoidrobot","month":"2025-04","story":"Tunarr: Create and configure live TV channels from media on your servers","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"You could also go down the Turbo Encabulator rabbit hole","text":"There are youtube channels with old TV Ads. Some of those will be non-existant. You could also go down the Turbo Encabulator rabbit hole: [LINK] [VIDEO]"},{"id":39115833,"author":"_fizz_buzz_","month":"2024-01","story":"AI 'hallucinated' fake legal cases filed to B.C. court in Canadian first","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"But that is my point, ChatGPT connected the rockwell retro encabulator[0] in reverse, which will of course make the resonance tank split capacitor bridge oscillate out of sync with the active input bias triodes. This will of course immediately fry the dual-bifet transistor driver stage. Not sure why they call this AI. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":38486619,"author":"kibwen","month":"2023-12","story":"Hyundai Uni Wheel electric drive system could revolutionize EV design","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"the famous Rockwell Retro Encabulator","text":"The video is good, but I couldn't help but draw a parallel to the famous Rockwell Retro Encabulator: [VIDEO]"},{"id":38082494,"author":"pwatsonwailes","month":"2023-10","story":"NASA can't open its asteroid capsule","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"Nah, they need Rockwell Automation's retro encabulator. The inverse reactive current it produces (designed for use in unilateral phase detractors) should also be capable of automatically synchronising cardinal gram meters. My guess would be that the gram meters have become unsynchronised. [VIDEO]"},{"id":37715307,"author":"dleeftink","month":"2023-09","story":"Should Ethereum be okay with enshrining more things in the protocol?","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"I'm okay with jargon, but the ratio can be off sometimes. When the ratio becomes too skewed, there's risk of side-fumbling or even encabuloration [0]. [0]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":36580146,"author":"hyperdimension","month":"2023-07","story":"55 GiB/s FizzBuzz (2021)","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"(my favorite version) It's an old engineering in-joke.","text":"He used the Retro Encabulator to generate this text. [VIDEO] (my favorite version) It's an old engineering in-joke. Edit: oops, thought your comment was a reply to 'mindcrime. I'm keeping it here though for the lucky (very lucky in this case) 10,000."},{"id":35967079,"author":"efitz","month":"2023-05","story":"A guidance language for controlling LLMs","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Reminded me of the Rockwell Encabulator","text":"Hahaha \"The first step of Byzantine Fault Tolerance is tolerance\" omg. That cracked me up. Reminded me of the Rockwell Encabulator: [VIDEO]"},{"id":35385095,"author":"londons_explore","month":"2023-03","story":"How the EU CHIPS Act Could Build “Innovation Capacity” in Europe","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"The obvious way to subvert this is by infiltrating the expert pool. Send in your own guys, who will vote to give all the money to you for inventing the critical retro encabulator[1]. I think the only defence is to make the expert pool large (ie. thousands of people), with a good chunk from academia, and have the expert pool anonymous, with the 'votes' of each expert also anonymous (so they can't go to their boss and show how they voted entirely for their own company). And finally, have some system to throw out the 20% highest and lowest votes before taking an average of the rest. [1]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":29756135,"author":"myrandomcomment","month":"2022-01","story":"Turboencabulator","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Here is the fun take","text":"Here is the fun take - [VIDEO] And the original - [LINK] Also curious as this was on Twitter the other day...how social mixed if this is the OPs reason for sharing."},{"id":27599318,"author":"whalesalad","month":"2021-06","story":"Regularization is all you need: simple neural nets can excel on tabular data","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"> As a result, we propose regularizing plain Multilayer Perceptron (MLP) networks by searching for the optimal combination/cocktail of 13 regularization techniques for each dataset using a joint optimization over the decision on which regularizers to apply and their subsidiary hyperparameters. Pardon? [VIDEO]"},{"id":26809167,"author":"ajcp","month":"2021-04","story":"Bernie Madoff has died","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"> Supposedly he was doing collars (split-strike conversions) to make this money, but it just seemed implausible to me at the time, since I traded options. I figured he just punted futures, nice and easy way to sorta hug the index (I hadn't seen his \"returns\"), maybe Martingale it and get lucky until you're not so lucky. For all I know you might as well be talking about a Rockwell Retro Encabulator[1] 1. [VIDEO]"},{"id":26183562,"author":"aeoleonn","month":"2021-02","story":"Judge: Citibank isn't entitled to $500M it sent to various creditors last August","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"So cringe-inducing! \"best in breed\" \"pre-integrated\" \"...unlocks intelligence ingrained in...\" such presentations remind me of Rockwell's Retro Encabulator: [VIDEO]"},{"id":25543311,"author":"hawktheslayer","month":"2020-12","story":"1937 film explains how a car differential works","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"this classic engineering video","text":"This was excellent to watch, though the whole time it made me think of this classic engineering video: [VIDEO]"},{"id":11023034,"author":"HCIdivision17","month":"2016-02","story":"The Turbo-Encabulator in Industry (1944)","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"Rockwell Automation did some work to modernize this with the ironically named retro-encabulator [0]. It follows the same principles, but uses the off-the-shelf parts we've come to appreciate from RA affiliates. GE was the main supplier of this updated version [1], and reviews show that its performance was excellent in the field. [0] [VIDEO] [1] [LINK] EDIT: There's quite a lot of controversy convering this area of reseach in the following decades. A more technical overview [2] on the turbo-encabulator's operations is discussed in an article covering Chrysler's tulmultuous efforts to apply it to their automotive line. The short of it is that despite being an impressive leap forward in technology, if mass produced it would generate dangerous quantities of DHMO [3]. It just goes to show how a small unexpected side effect can derail even well researched technical solutions. [2] [LINK] [3] [LINK]"},{"id":10596702,"author":"vox_mollis","month":"2015-11","story":"The Doomsday Scam","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"The reported poor performance of both red mercury nuclear devices and the ADE 651 are due to the integrators' failure to power them with the output of a properly-calibrated Turboencabulator. Ref: [LINK] [VIDEO]"},{"id":2015081,"author":"harpastum","month":"2010-12","story":"Git in layman's terms: isomorphic contours in source-code phase space","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"After you've watched the video (don't go first or it will spoil it)","text":"This reminds me a lot of turbo/retroencabulator [1]. After you've watched the video (don't go first or it will spoil it), you can check out the wikipedia article.[2] [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK]"}],"r":[["_8bhtEgB8Mo",1]]},"f61KMw5zVhg":{"wy":"HN returns to it as a reminder to stay humble and as a source of Feynman's view on awards and prestige.","q":{"x":"It's worth watching once or twice a year to remind onself to be humble.","i":1752347,"y":"2010"},"m":[{"id":1752347,"author":"dnautics","month":"2010-10","story":"Ask HN: Good books?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Reminder to be humble","quote":"It's worth watching once or twice a year to remind onself to be humble.","text":"This is a really great video to watch. It's worth watching once or twice a year to remind onself to be humble. And it's particularly apt this week! [VIDEO]"},{"id":42091411,"author":"namuol","month":"2024-11","story":"What Is a Staff Engineer?","stance":"reference","why":"Feynman's repulsion to honors","quote":"Richard Feynman describes his instinct to be repulsed by “honors” and everything like them","text":"Should you care what another engineer’s title is? These kinds of discussions bother me because it always implies some kind of worthiness of the title. Maybe it’s just my work experience but I’ve found very little correlation with e.g. the success of an engineer’s career trajectory and the success of the projects they’re responsible for. Going a little more off topic now but these discussions always leave me feeling a bit like how Richard Feynman describes his instinct to be repulsed by “honors” and everything like them: [VIDEO]"},{"id":29218644,"author":"tauwauwau","month":"2021-11","story":"Apple buying Google ads for high-value subscription apps","stance":"reference","why":"Feynman says the real prize is the pleasure of discovery","quote":"Prize is the pleasure of finding the thing out, kick in the discovery, the observation other people use it.","text":"To me original motivation is solving a problem and innovation. If you come up with an idea to create a service/product, you may be invested in the innovation in the beginning. But, as soon as you start making it using other people's money, it starts being less about the product. [Edit] [VIDEO] Richard Feynman, explaining if \"it\" was worth the Nobel Prize. \"...I already got the prize. Prize is the pleasure of finding the thing out, kick in the discovery, the observation other people use it. Those are the real things...\""},{"id":25455455,"author":"bonoboTP","month":"2020-12","story":"On the cruelty of really teaching computing science (1988) [pdf]","stance":"reference","why":"Feynman's take on bickering among fields","quote":"I like Feynman's take on this kind of bickering among fields","text":"I'm talking about the perception by general people like business managers and HR etc. I think it's silly that there's this purity contest or practicality contest going on. I studied CS but at a more engineering focused university, so the curriculum was closer to electrical engineering than to math and physics. Many engineering profs had a weird distaste for physicists/natural scientists and vice versa. They were both \"elitist\" just from a different view of the world. I like Feynman's take on this kind of bickering among fields: [VIDEO]"},{"id":19754748,"author":"Jach","month":"2019-04","story":"After Pentagon Ends Contract, Top-Secret Scientists Group Vows To Carry On","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"This seems shortsighted... they'll probably find more funding, but what a bunch of nonsense to have to deal with in the interim. This bit of the article makes it seem like it could be as petty as a clash of personalities: > \"The department remains committed to seeking independent technical advice and review,\" Pentagon spokesperson Heather Babb said. But Aftergood sees another reason for the end of the relationship. He says that the Jasons are a blunt bunch. If they think an idea is dumb or won't work, they aren't afraid to say so. > \"They were offering the opposite of cheerleading,\" he says. \"And DOD decided that maybe they didn't want to pay for that any longer.\" An interesting bit of trivia from the wiki page shows they lost funding before in 2002 for something perhaps just as petty, them not relenting on having exclusive control over who they let join: > In 2002, DARPA decided to cut its ties with JASON. DARPA had not only been one of JASON's primary sponsors, it was also the channel through which JASON received funding from other sponsors. DARPA's decision came after JASON's refusal to allow DARPA to select three new JASON members. Since JASON's inception, new members have always been selected by its existing members. After much negotiation and letter-writing—including a letter by Congressman Rush Holt of New Jersey[26]—funding was subsequently secured from an office higher in the defense hierarchy, the office of the Director, Defense Research & Engineering, name changed "},{"id":4430995,"author":"Nevaeh","month":"2012-08","story":"Elon Musk Wins 2012 Popular Mechanics Breakthrough Leadership Award","stance":"reference","why":"Feynman disliked honors","quote":"Feynman himself once said that he didn't like honors","text":"Elon probably shares your sentiments, since he was an avid reader of Feynman. Source: [LINK] Feynman himself once said that he didn't like honors: [VIDEO] So when Elon won the Lady Vivamus sword after accepting the heinlein award for advances in space commercialization, he fools around with it instead of just being another trophy, sometimes to impress the ladies: [LINK]"},{"id":953794,"author":"andreyf","month":"2009-11","story":"Linus Torvalds for Nobel Peace Prize?","stance":"reference","why":"Feynman on his own Nobel prize","quote":"articulated well from another angle by Feynman (commenting on his own Nobel prize)","text":"This observation applies to all prizes, and is articulated well from another angle by Feynman (commenting on his own Nobel prize): [VIDEO]"},{"id":428410,"author":"andreyf","month":"2009-01","story":"Why We Put On The Crunchies","stance":"reference","why":"Longer version of the Feynman quote","quote":"A slightly longer quote","text":"A slightly longer quote: [VIDEO] Brilliant! However, it makes me kind of sad that PG goes to shit like this instead of working on Arc :( 100 year languages don't discover themselves..."}],"r":[["x1v2tX4_dkQ",1],["7Hk9jct2ozY",1],["aP0Y1uAA-2Y",1],["JvBT4XBdoUE",1],["yTkzNHF6rMs",1],["nLApFANtkHs",1],["HyznrdDSSGM",1],["C2RO34b_oPM",1]]},"qB76jxBq_gQ":{"wy":"HN uses it as a short introduction to Glenn Gould and his distinctive playing.","q":{"x":"For anyone unfamiliar with Glenn Gould, check out these two videos","i":4034836,"y":"2012"},"m":[{"id":4034836,"author":"beosrocks","month":"2012-05","story":"The Open Goldberg Variations","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Introduction to the legendary pianist Glenn Gould","quote":"For anyone unfamiliar with Glenn Gould, check out these two videos","text":"Amen! For anyone unfamiliar with Glenn Gould, check out these two videos: [VIDEO] [LINK] He was perhaps the greatest piano player of the 20th century and had a strong hacker/genius/Aspergers aspect. My favorite quote about him comes from Composer Dimitry Tolstoy: \"Gould was an alien on this Earth. People simply cannot play the piano like that!\" [LINK]"},{"id":20187057,"author":"Pimpus","month":"2019-06","story":"Advice on Getting Better from an Accomplished Piano Player (2011)","stance":"reference","why":"Gould practiced fast rather than slow","quote":"Glenn Gould practiced by playing fast","text":"Hah, that is an excellent book. I remember it pissed off one of my friends who claimed that slow practice is the best way to learn. Glad I didn't listen to him, I use these techniques when learning all kinds of stuff. Glenn Gould practiced by playing fast: [VIDEO]"},{"id":16950110,"author":"furgooswft13","month":"2018-04","story":"A Layman’s Intro to Western Classical Music","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"It is vast, you are right. But it is not all a sacred tome. There are a lot of different styles of composition, some which you, depending on tastes, will simply not like, just like any other music. The mainstays of popular classical music, Bach, Mozart, and Beethoven all practically defined the 3 main \"periods\" of the genre; Baroque, Classical, and Romantic respectively (though Beethoven is often considered as somewhat of a bridge between late classical and romantic, details..). Thus each of these periods has a unique character and sound, which is as distinct as different genres of modern music, despite mostly using the same instruments. It is unfortunate that all that variety has been slapped the label of \"classical\", and somewhat ironic, considering the Classical period of classical music was seen at the time as a reaction to the \"overly complex, aristocratic\", style of baroque. It was the pop music of its day, now of course regarded as somewhat elitist. Anyways, I'm somewhat of the Glenn Gould opinion that Mozart is mostly trash (though his keyboard concertos are pure gold). I suppose the wannabe elitist in me gravitates towards baroque, it's intricacy, specifically counterpoint. If the idea of multiple independent melodies playing at the same time, transforming and complementing each other like some kind of musical puzzle fascinates you, as it does me, then I'd suggest binging on some sweet J.S. Bach. You've surely heard BWV 565 at least once every Halloween, and a few of"}],"r":[["BsVydyC8ZGQ",1]]},"5OtKEetGy2Y":{"wy":"Cited in Musk-related threads as a critical perspective on his futurism and his projects, such as hyperloop.","q":{"x":"There was an interesting critique of Musks futurism by Tom Nicholas. It definitely gave me another perspective.","i":32031009,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":32031009,"author":"cycomanic","month":"2022-07","story":"Notice of termination of Twitter merger agreement","stance":"rec","why":"A critique of Musk's futurism offering another perspective.","quote":"There was an interesting critique of Musks futurism by Tom Nicholas. It definitely gave me another perspective.","text":"There was an interesting critique of Musks futurism by Tom Nicholas. It definitely gave me another perspective. [VIDEO]"},{"id":33392313,"author":"HyperSane","month":"2022-10","story":"Tesla engineers were on-site to evaluate the Twitter staff’s code, workers said","stance":"reference","why":"One of several links backing the claim Musk's projects are cons.","quote":"The Fake Futurism of Elon Musk","text":"Hyperloop is very much a con job. \"Full Self Driving is just a year away\" is very much a con job. The cybertruck is a con job. The Tesla Semi is a con job. The tesla robot is a con job. [LINK] [LINK] [LINK] [LINK] [LINK] Mark Spiegel: Elon Musk is ‘a pathological liar’ [LINK] The Fake Futurism of Elon Musk [VIDEO] DEBUNKING ELON MUSK Pt1 [LINK] The HYPERLOOP Will Never Work, And Here's Why [LINK]"},{"id":29354665,"author":"throwaway946513","month":"2021-11","story":"Hyperloop: Elon Musk merely renamed a 200-year-old dream","stance":"reference","why":"Supporting critique of hyperloop versus metro.","quote":"it gives a good similar line with relation to the hyperloop","text":"But one of the biggest issues I've seen regarding the hyperloop is the near vacuum costing and polluting more than a conventional metro, let alone being slower to shuffle total people than a metro, and more overall more expensive than a metro. For reference: [0] [LINK] While this isn't explicitly about the hyperloop, it gives a good similar line with relation to the hyperloop [1] [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["If61baWF4GE",1],["17oCQakzIl8",1],["5pidokakU4I",1]]},"6bG5ps5KdDo":{"wy":"HN cites it, often critically, as Nix openly describing data-driven manipulation and targeting methods.","q":{"x":"Alexander Nix's utterly fascinating OMR presentation","i":21970869,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":21970869,"author":"iamben","month":"2020-01","story":"New Cambridge Analytica Leaks","stance":"rec","why":"Nix explains personality-based targeting.","quote":"Alexander Nix's utterly fascinating OMR presentation","text":"But at that point couldn't you just upload an email list and target by that? I could be miles off the mark, but I assumed a lot of this came from getting people to take a \"personality test\" and then bucketing that person for particular ads. Admittedly I based a bunch of this on Alexander Nix's utterly fascinating OMR presentation ( [VIDEO] ). As per the parent, it always amazes me at how easy it is to influence someone if you know the right levers to pull."},{"id":16605396,"author":"alexdong","month":"2018-03","story":"Suspending Cambridge Analytica and SCL Group from Facebook","stance":"reference","why":"Companion video to review the claimed campaign success.","quote":"","text":"The video above was 7-week before the Trump campaign and this one is after the result and a sort of review of the \"success\": [VIDEO]"},{"id":47108039,"author":"rramadass","month":"2026-02","story":"Palantir's secret weapon isn't AI – it's Ontology. An open-source deep dive","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"Watch these old presentations by CA's then ceo Alexander Nix and extrapolate to today's AI world","text":"Palantir is just \"Cambridge Analytica\" redux but with more money/connections/data/breadth/depth/etc. Their ethical/moral stance i will leave it to you to infer. Watch these old presentations by CA's then ceo Alexander Nix and extrapolate to today's AI world - [LINK] and [VIDEO] (note the Q/A at the end here) Also watch this interview with Christopher Wylie the CA whistleblower and again extrapolate to today's AI world - [LINK] Be afraid, Be very afraid."},{"id":16623111,"author":"fny","month":"2018-03","story":"The Cambridge Analytica scandal isn’t a scandal: this is how Facebook works","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"He also continued to revel in his company's ability to manipulate after the election.[2]","text":"This is completely different. Cambridge Analytica deliberately lied and deceived users about the intention of the application they created to harvest information about users: > Like all app developers, Kogan requested and gained access to information from people after they chose to download his app. His app, “thisisyourdigitallife,” offered a personality prediction, and billed itself on Facebook as “a research app used by psychologists.” [0] Cambridge Analytica is also in the business of using manipulation and psychological techniques to convince users of an outcome. And this isn't something I'm making up, the CEO of Cambridge Analytica peddles this as the potential of his platform: [LINK] The most damning aspect is his naked promotion of misinformation under the newspeak of \"Behavioral Communication\" where he suggests comparing the effectiveness of using \"Private Beach\" vs \"Sharks Sighted\" as a deterrent.[1] He also continued to revel in his company's ability to manipulate after the election.[2] [0]: [LINK] [1]: [LINK] [2]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":16623384,"author":"fny","month":"2018-03","story":"The Cambridge Analytica scandal isn’t a scandal: this is how Facebook works","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"Cambridge Analytica's CEO also espouses complete deceit as a strategy core strategy for using data to manipulate outcomes","text":"This is a scandal, and we should not blur lines here. Facebook engages in surveillance capitalism. Cambridge Analytica engages in Orwellian practices of deliberate and targeted mass manipulation, actively deceiving users from the outset about it's intentions, and using lies as a means to an end. From the Facebook report on the reason CA was suspended: > Like all app developers, Kogan requested and gained access to information from people after they chose to download his app. His app, “thisisyourdigitallife,” offered a personality prediction, and billed itself on Facebook as “a research app used by psychologists.” [0] Cambridge Analytica's CEO also espouses complete deceit as a strategy core strategy for using data to manipulate outcomes and advocates for using \"behaviorally targeted language\" to create an outcome. Like say you have a \"private beach.\" If you really want to keep people out, why not say \"sharks sighted\" instead? His example, not mine: [LINK] , [LINK] , [VIDEO] The scandals for me are two fold. First, we have a data repository (Facebook) that's powerful enough to assist in this type of weaponized manipulation that's entirely unprotected. Even if you think Facebook is some type of benevolent overlord that you trust with your data, it's a scandal that nothing has stopped any number of third-parties from doing what Cambridge Analytica did. Second, we have a sitting president who actively made use of these \"targeted information strategies\" during his election in a bl"}]},"CpfdtgW6_oI":{"wy":"Cited as the video version of his essay on making Pittsburgh a startup hub, and as a talk that persuaded at least one commenter to move there.","q":{"x":"It also happens to be one of the few available on youtube[1] if you would rather watch than read.","i":21181478,"y":"2019"},"m":[{"id":21181478,"author":"paulz_","month":"2019-10","story":"Ask HN: What is necessary for a startup ecosystem to thrive?","stance":"rec","why":"Video version of PG's essay on startup hubs.","quote":"It also happens to be one of the few available on youtube[1] if you would rather watch than read.","text":"PG actually wrote an essay[0] about this a few years back. It also happens to be one of the few available on youtube[1] if you would rather watch than read. [0] [LINK] [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":18166153,"author":"Kuiper","month":"2018-10","story":"Ask HN: Cheap places to live with a good intellectual atmosphere?","stance":"reference","why":"Talk that persuaded commenter to move to Pittsburgh.","quote":"partly persuaded Paul Graham's talk about how Pittsburgh could become a startup hub.","text":"I'm a full-time freelancer/remote worker. In late 2016, I made the decision to move to Pittsburgh, partly persuaded Paul Graham's talk about how Pittsburgh could become a startup hub. (PG grew up in Monroeville, 10 miles east of Pittsburgh.) Transcript: [LINK] Video: [VIDEO] I feel really good about the decision, CMU feels like a peer of Stanford and MIT but living here is substantially cheaper than living in Cambridge or Palo Alto. (I pay ~$750 for a 1-bedroom apartment that's ~1.5 mile from CMU campus.) The legacy of Randy Pausch's Building Virtual Worlds was also a draw for me personally, as I work in game development and was coming of age when Randy Pausch's delivered his famous Last Lecture (if you had asked me in 2008 who my personal heroes were, Randy Pausch would be the first name out of my mouth). Going to gamedev meetups has kind of allowed me to insert myself into the CMU alumni network post hoc. Very green (lots of beautiful neighborhoods), hip enough for my tastes, and the intellectual climate is everything I had hoped it would be. If you want to live in a city where companies like Google, Uber, Amazon, and Apple have offices, Pittsburgh may be your cheapest option. Some discussion of Pittsburgh's (tech) climate on this article from last year: [LINK]"},{"id":11483827,"author":"georgecmu","month":"2016-04","story":"How to Make Pittsburgh a Startup Hub","stance":"reference","why":"Video of Paul Graham's keynote.","quote":"The video of PG's keynote is available here","text":"I was going to attend this event (mainly because of pg's participation), but regrettably had to miss it (family claimed the Saturday). Thanks for the write-up. The video of PG's keynote is available here: [VIDEO]"}]},"NWHfY_lvKIQ":{"wy":"Cited for Joel's account of how Stack Overflow's design and community thinking were figured out from the start.","q":{"x":"Watch Joel's tech talk - they really did figure a ton of it out at the start.","i":2225604,"y":"2011"},"m":[{"id":2225604,"author":"pbiggar","month":"2011-02","story":"Expert Exchange gets narky after snub from Q/A site review","stance":"rec","why":"Joel Spolsky's talk on how Stack Overflow was designed from the start","quote":"Watch Joel's tech talk - they really did figure a ton of it out at the start.","text":"> Just figure out what EE did, and then do the exact opposite. Even if that was the mantra, that's figuring it out from the start. That wasn't the mantra though; EE was only one of the influences. Watch Joel's tech talk [VIDEO] - they really did figure a ton of it out at the start. See Joel's tech talk"},{"id":27373514,"author":"codeulike","month":"2021-06","story":"Kinda a Big Announcement","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Spolsky and Atwood had a podcast when they were putting Stackoverflow together and they talk at length about what they wanted to achieve. Its amazing actually to see they had a very precise idea of what they wanted to do and they absolutely hit their own objective. Podcast 1, April 2008 [LINK] One thing we are noticing is that the book market, the programming book market has just completely vanished. It is falling apart, catastrophically. The programmers I know don't really learn new technologies from a book any more. What they do is they find a tutorial on the web -- maybe -- and they try to do something and then they page fault in knowledge. Basically, they get stuck on something and they either post to a discussion group, or they type their question into Google. And those are the two things we want to serve basically is the posting to the discussion group and typing things into Google. Our longer term goal, if we’re successful, is that you're trying to figure out how to do something in Python like how to merge two arrays in Python and you go to Google and you type \"merge two arrays python\" and submit that, and our goal is to be the number one hit that comes up with a really good edited answer to that question that some individual has contributed and maybe other individuals have edited. ----- So then you go search on the Internet for \"Mac remote desktop connection beta expired\" and you get all kinds of discussions; people discussing what to do and how to work around this pr"},{"id":9063356,"author":"jasode","month":"2015-02","story":"My Love-Hate Relationship with Stack Overflow","stance":"reference","why":"Joel Spolsky's 2009 presentation on anthropology and building good communities","quote":"Joel Spolsky (one of the founders) gave a very interesting presentation[1] in 2009.","text":"To me, I see this strange contradiction in how stackoverflow is evolving. Joel Spolsky (one of the founders) gave a very interesting presentation[1] in 2009. The key takeaway I've always remembered from his comments was the idea of \"anthropology\". The science of trying to really understand human behavior and make the website amplify good communities. What I see 6 years later is a lot of \" why is this question closed as 'not constructive' when it has 500 upvotes?!?! \" There is clearly a disconnect between the way the end users see stackoverflow and the way the moderators see it. These days, it seems like the stackoverflow guys (not necessarily Joel specifically) are inexplicably blind to human behaviors that was was highlighted in 2009. But, I don't have the whole story so maybe someone closer to the situation can explain. I don't know who is right or wrong. The thing I'd like to know is if stackoverflow still \"works\" for the quality contributors who invest their time writing extensive and insightful answers. Or, have a significant percentage abandoned the site because they wade through too many questions from homework, or outsourced sweatshop employees who don't even do modicum of googling, etc. On the other hand, are the majority of complaints distorted because they are mostly voiced by the Eternal September[2] crowd? So, is it experts leaving? Or newcomers overwhelming the site with bad questions? I don't know if one can gather from website analytics which of the 2 situatio"},{"id":7249731,"author":"lukasm","month":"2014-02","story":"Stack Overflow is down","stance":"reference","why":"Source for Joel's figure on number of programmers worldwide","quote":"Joel says that according to MS study there are 9mln people that write code.","text":"I think that was this talk. [VIDEO] Joel says that according to MS study there are 9mln people that write code. [LINK]"},{"id":2224910,"author":"tejaswiy","month":"2011-02","story":"Nobody really gives a damn what you built your site in (why I chose asp.net).","stance":"reference","why":"Joel's view that ASP.NET scales well","quote":"ASP.NET scales pretty well as Joel opines.","text":"This is what I don't get. ASP.NET scales pretty well as Joel opines. Why do people think .NET is bad just because it feels enterprise-y? More: [VIDEO]"},{"id":662733,"author":"w1ntermute","month":"2009-06","story":"Stackoverflow Eating Away at Experts-Exchange","stance":"reference","why":"Joel's talk at Google about StackOverflow","quote":"Joel took quite a few potshots at the \"hyphenated site\", as he put it","text":"Yes, Joel took quite a few potshots at the \"hyphenated site\", as he put it, during his talk at Google about StackOverflow ( [VIDEO] ). Also, Jeff mentions it in his intro to StackOverflow Coding Horror post ( [LINK] ): Stackoverflow is sort of like the anti-experts-exchange (minus the nausea-inducing sleaze and quasi-legal search engine gaming)"},{"id":586614,"author":"asmosoinio","month":"2009-04","story":"Talk about StackOverflow","stance":"reference","why":"Google talk about StackOverflow","quote":"Haven't watched it yet...","text":"The page contains little more than a link to this GoogleTechTalks video: [VIDEO] Haven't watched it yet..."}]},"W5yVGmfivAk":{"wy":"Commenters point to Campbell's channel as a low-tech, content-dense source of pandemic updates and early discussion of vitamin D.","q":{"x":"Anyone that wants to stay up to date with the virus should follow this channel","i":22555298,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":22555298,"author":"gnur","month":"2020-03","story":"Resources For Novel Coronavirus Covid-19","stance":"rec","why":"Dr. Campbell's low-tech, high-content updates and vitamin D advice.","quote":"Anyone that wants to stay up to date with the virus should follow this channel","text":"Anyone that wants to stay up to date with the virus should follow this channel [0] It's from the semi retired Dr. John Campbell, his videos are low tech but high content. I've come to realize it is all our responsibility to make sure the health care professionals can handle this. If we assume this is going to be as big as some predict it will be, we basically cannot escape it, but it is possible to lengthen the time in which most infections well arise, lowering the peak and curve, which will in turn greatly reduce the peak load on healthcare professionals. A 1000 admissions a day might be impossible to handle, but 100 admissions stretched out over 10 days is much more manageable. This is something everyone can help with. Also, take a lot of Vitamin D, it helps with handling the Covid19 and other respiratory tract infections (both viral and bacterial). [1] [0] [LINK] [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":23391054,"author":"gniv","month":"2020-06","story":"Study of the effect of Vitamin D, Magnesium and Vitamin B12 on Covid-19 patients","stance":"reference","why":"Early March video showing the vitamin D link was known.","quote":"See for example this video from John Campbell from March 9","text":"> Was this known early on about COVID? Yes. See for example this video from John Campbell from March 9: [VIDEO]"},{"id":23120795,"author":"fattire","month":"2020-05","story":"Vitamin D looks powerful, underutilized for Covid-19","stance":"reference","why":"One of several Campbell videos on vitamin D deficiency and mortality.","quote":"has put out a few videos from time to time related to vitamin D","text":"Dr. John Campbell, an educator in Northern England who's been doing daily Youtube updates on Covid news since January I believe, has put out a few videos from time to time related to vitamin D: [VIDEO] - A month ago [LINK] - 3 weeks ago [LINK] - 3 days ago He suggests vitamin D deficiency may play a partial role in explaining higher mortality rates among people with darker skin (other important social disparities notwithstanding)."},{"id":23170947,"author":"fattire","month":"2020-05","story":"Role of Vitamin D in the prevention of Coronavirus 2019 infection and mortality","stance":"reference","why":"First of several Campbell videos on vitamin D and mortality.","quote":"has put out a few videos from time to time related to vitamin D","text":"Posted this previously when the subject came up, but John Campbell, an nurse and educator in Northern England who's been doing daily Youtube updates on global Covid news since January I believe, has put out a few videos from time to time related to vitamin D: [VIDEO] - 2 months ago [LINK] - 4 weeks ago [LINK] - 1 week ago [LINK] - 4 days ago [LINK] - today He offers published studies as evidence to suggest vitamin D deficiency may play a partial role in explaining higher mortality rates among people with darker skin (other important social disparities notwithstanding)."},{"id":23024674,"author":"wcarss","month":"2020-04","story":"Vitamin D Insufficiency is Prevalent in Severe Covid-19","stance":"reference","why":"Campbell reviews a meta-analysis on vitamin D and respiratory infections.","quote":"has some good comments on the meaningful statistical power and experiment design","text":"This systematic meta-analysis paper[1] may be more convincing. The popular youtube COVID-commentating Dr. John Campbell did a review of it about a month ago[2] and has some good comments on the meaningful statistical power and experiment design. The overall takeaway is this: in people with Vitamin D deficiency (which is very, very common), continuous daily supplementation can lead to up to a 70% reduction in contracted viral respiratory infections. It has a smaller effect the less deficient you are, and is not associated with any adverse effects. 1 - [LINK] 2 - [VIDEO]"}]},"WkxSyv5R1sg":{"wy":"Cited as evidence that meditation can work as prison reform.","q":{"x":"It documents the use of Vipassana as a reform measure at a harsh prison in India","i":16844744,"y":"2018"},"m":[{"id":16844744,"author":"slipperyp","month":"2018-04","story":"Vipassana for Hackers [pdf]","stance":"rec","why":"Documentary on Vipassana used as reform at a harsh Indian prison","quote":"It documents the use of Vipassana as a reform measure at a harsh prison in India","text":"There is more information on the method from this documentary Doing Time, Doing Vipassana: [VIDEO] It documents the use of Vipassana as a reform measure at a harsh prison in India and traces the impacts it had on some of the inmates there."},{"id":22554318,"author":"vekker","month":"2020-03","story":"What it’s like to spend four and a half years in solitary confinement (2015)","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as support for teaching meditation in prisons","quote":"That's just not being done enough in prisons right now.","text":"Sounds like hell, the way he describes it. However, if you'd take those hellish elements away & ensure silence, I think long solitary confinement might work if you teach people how to meditate. That's just not being done enough in prisons right now. See \"Doing Time, Doing Vipassana\" [VIDEO] EDIT: I know this is probably a controversial opinion to non-meditators, but if you're going to downvote, at least provide an argument for why you don't agree."},{"id":18810994,"author":"abledon","month":"2019-01","story":"California inmate takes unlikely path to freedom: podcasting","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as example of mandatory meditation for prisoners","quote":"mandatory meditation","text":"mandatory meditation: [VIDEO] for someone to just 'sit' and think about their thoughts for 10 days straight without talking, is HELL for a criminal. they are forced to confront reality, or at least their subjective version of it."}]},"d6Pcp944sRI":{"wy":"HN mentions it as a fascinating example of chemistry persistence and as something people learned from, such as discovering purple gold exists.","q":{"x":"There's a fascinating video of a fellow trying to make a ring out of Purple Gold.","i":40916840,"y":"2024"},"m":[{"id":40916840,"author":"whartung","month":"2024-07","story":"Making my own wedding rings","stance":"rec","why":"Fascinating attempt to make a ring out of purple gold.","quote":"There's a fascinating video of a fellow trying to make a ring out of Purple Gold.","text":"There's a fascinating video of a fellow trying to make a ring out of Purple Gold. [VIDEO] No, I hadn't heard of purple gold either."},{"id":39331458,"author":"ajsnigrutin","month":"2024-02","story":"46% of Americans didn't read a book in 2023","stance":"reference","why":"Used as an example of learning something new from a short video.","quote":"Ever heard of purple gold? Neither have I, again, until the video","text":"Sure, but if when are you going to open a book on SNES (super nintendo)? For comparison, here is a very interesting video series for anyone with at least some programming knowledge: [LINK] Sure, you could buy a book about snes (not even sure if any good ones exist) and read about that... but you're not that interested into the topic, because it's something old and not something you'll actually do... but here's a 6 minute video, that you'll probably watch... and if it's interesting, you'll go down a playlist and see \"how thing were done\" \"back then\" and learn something new. Ever heard of Tefifon music player? Neither have I, until i saw the video: [LINK] ... i would have never bought a book on ancient music players, but I learned something new by watching a short video. Ever heard of purple gold? Neither have I, again, until the video: [VIDEO] Compare this to eg. book bestsellers: [LINK] Sometimes all you need is a summary, you're not a chemist, SNES programmer or an old music player collector, but you still learn a lot, and all those things are topics, where you just don't buy a book, because th 10, 20 minute video covers all that you need and want to know."},{"id":38779742,"author":"kragen","month":"2023-12","story":"The transparent chip inside a vintage Hewlett-Packard floppy drive","stance":"reference","why":"NileRed makes a ring from purple gold after enormous effort.","quote":"he successfully makes a beautiful finger ring for himself from purple plague","text":"interestingly, the day after you posted this, popular youtuber nilered published a video in which, after staggering amounts of effort, he successfully makes a beautiful finger ring for himself from purple plague [VIDEO]"}]},"ek0FrCaogcs":{"wy":"It is the standard citation in drone and eVTOL threads for the claim that multirotors can survive a failed rotor with the right control algorithm.","q":{"x":"controlled, safe landings of quadrotors with a single failed motor have been demonstrated, see this ETH Zurich video from 2014","i":20625631,"y":"2019"},"m":[{"id":20621646,"author":"raynr","month":"2019-08","story":"Swiss Post Suspends Drone Delivery Service After Second Crash","stance":"rec","why":"Amazing ETH Zurich work on quadcopter recovery after rotor loss.","quote":"has done amazing work on quadcopter recovery after rotor control loss [1]","text":"As an additional potential recovery mode: ETH Zurich's Institute for Dynamic Systems and Control [0] has done amazing work on quadcopter recovery after rotor control loss [1] (I believe they are down to controlled flight with only one or two rotors left, but I cannot find the link right now). I've been waiting for this to filter into the real world but have yet to see it. [0] [LINK] [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":20625631,"author":"mikepurvis","month":"2019-08","story":"Swiss Post Suspends Drone Delivery Service After Second Crash","stance":"reference","why":"Demonstrates controlled safe landing of a quadrotor with a failed motor.","quote":"controlled, safe landings of quadrotors with a single failed motor have been demonstrated, see this ETH Zurich video from 2014","text":"This isn't quite true. Cheap drones may not have this capability, but controlled, safe landings of quadrotors with a single failed motor have been demonstrated, see this ETH Zurich video from 2014: [VIDEO] Obviously there are still other single points of failure such as the battery or controller, but at this point there's no excuse for crash landing a commercial drone just because a single motor (or rotor) has failed."},{"id":16119115,"author":"SolarNet","month":"2018-01","story":"SureFly –  a personal helicopter designed for safe and easy flight","stance":"reference","why":"Shows a quadcopter staying controllable after losing an axis of control.","quote":"it would still be able to fly in a controllable fashion. See this video","text":"Actually that's a benefit of the quad-copter design. It can sacrifice an axis of control in that situation (a \"minor\" one, like rotation about the gravity vector). In this case it would likely make the passengers sick, but it would still be able to fly in a controllable fashion. See this video: [VIDEO]"},{"id":8047439,"author":"rtkwe","month":"2014-07","story":"Beijing From Above, aka How I was police detained for flying a Quadcopter","stance":"reference","why":"ETH Zurich flight test recovering from propeller loss.","quote":"ETH Zurich has some research out there about recovering to normal(ish) flight after a prop loss.","text":"You can't pull autorotation with fixed pitch blades, at least not in any meaningful manner. Light quads can fall pretty slowly with pinwheeling motors but that's not actually autorotation. The way a helicopter is able to autorotate is to pull negative pitch, which spins the rotors using the fall energy of the copter, then shortly before crashing pulling back to positive pitch and using the still spinning blades to slow down to a controlled landing. For motor failure ETH Zurich has some research out there about recovering to normal(ish) flight after a prop loss. The quad goes into a pretty rapid spin but it's still controllable, link below to an outside the lab test. [VIDEO]"}]},"euiSHuaw6Q4":{"wy":"HN cites it as a famous piece of anti-Apple marketing, with some praising the ad and others mocking the spin.","q":{"x":"Apple has now turned into what they despised.","i":40318335,"y":"2024"},"m":[{"id":40318335,"author":"nojvek","month":"2024-05","story":"Apple apologizes for iPad 'Crush' ad that 'missed the mark'","stance":"rec","why":"Epic's take on Apple's 1984 ad.","quote":"I absolutely loved the Epic ad that was a take of the original Apple ad which was based on 1984.","text":"Take the Apple Ad in Contrast to the Microsoft Ad that aired during Super Bowl. The whole time I thought it was an Apple Ad and then it was Microsoft Copilot ad. The made me dream, made me feel I could do more. The ad was about the viewer. [LINK] See the Apple Siri Ad - it’s about how the device enhances the viewer to do more. [LINK] Apple has now turned into what they despised. I absolutely loved the Epic ad that was a take of the original Apple ad which was based on 1984. Apple - [LINK] Epic - [VIDEO] It’s fair to say if Steve Jobs was still calling shots, this Ad would have never aired. The Ad shows everything wrong with Apple. They’ve become a soulless corporation."},{"id":24148645,"author":"andreasley","month":"2020-08","story":"Apple just kicked Fortnite off the App Store","stance":"reference","why":"Fortnite parody of Apple's 1984 ad.","quote":"published a Fortnite-themed parody of Apple's \"1984","text":"Epic Games has filed a lawsuit [1] and published a Fortnite-themed parody of Apple's \"1984\" [2] to get some publicity for it. [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO]"},{"id":24152312,"author":"sali0","month":"2020-08","story":"Apple just kicked Fortnite off the App Store","stance":"reference","why":"Epic's ad campaign.","quote":"This is the most epic (no pun intended) ad campaign in history.","text":"Epic is releasing a new game mode[1] called Nineteen Eighty-Fortnite. This is the most epic (no pun intended) ad campaign in history. 1: [VIDEO]"},{"id":35171281,"author":"paulmd","month":"2023-03","story":"What Happens When Your Phone Is Spying on You","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"So much for the \"app store is like 1984\" marketing spin that Epic put out lol.","text":"well, thanks to the EU, it's going to apply to Apple real soon too. This is one of the things app store review and fully enforcable permissioning prevents (prevented). [LINK] So much for the \"app store is like 1984\" marketing spin that Epic put out lol. Now you have the freedom to have spyware installed on your phone... which is what I and many others have been saying all along. Not just abusive spouses but Facebook and others will be all over this. [VIDEO] As I said previously... Facebook and others already have this sort of spyware ready to go, because they were already using their developer credentials to get users to sideload it for data mining . Now it won't be optional/\"we'll give you a gift card\", it'll just be mandatory if you want to use Facebook on a ios device. Sorry this browser is not supported, please install the native app. [LINK]"}]},"fCUTX1jurJ4":{"wy":"Cited in privacy and surveillance threads as a thoughtful treatment of being compelled to share data and the nothing-to-hide argument.","q":{"x":"PhilosophyTube recently posted a great video essay (Socratic dialogue?) that explores that exact situation.","i":22259646,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":22259646,"author":"pdkl95","month":"2020-02","story":"Your New Medical Credit Score Could Deny You Care","stance":"rec","why":"Video essay exploring being forced to share data","quote":"PhilosophyTube recently posted a great video essay (Socratic dialogue?) that explores that exact situation.","text":"\"Unless we scan your card, you can't buy alcohol.\" PhilosophyTube recently posted a great video essay (Socratic dialogue?) that explores that exact situation. [VIDEO]"},{"id":38853614,"author":"FrustratedMonky","month":"2024-01","story":"Britain's got some of Europe's toughest surveillance laws. Now it wants more","stance":"reference","why":"Episode addressing the nothing to hide argument","quote":"Philosophy Tube episode on this","text":"It is such a hollow argument. Anybody using \"nothing to hide\" arguments, go ahead and post your passwords. Philosophy Tube episode on this [VIDEO]"},{"id":27921568,"author":"loup-vaillant","month":"2021-07","story":"Technical Introduction to the Use of Trusted Platform Module 2.0 with Linux [pdf]","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"It is not at all clear that we French were actually refusing to get vaccinated. First, it would seem the least vaccinated people were generally the poorest as well. The vaccine is free, but we generally need to get an appointment through some web site, on our own initiative . Our appointed family doctor did not call us to even ask whether we'd like to be vaccinated. This hints towards an accessibility problem. Second, when you look at the charts [LINK] (Click the \"Injections Quotidiennes\" tab on the second figure.), you can see that the daily injection didn't fall. It merely plateaued. Sure, first injections did fall, but they did so at the same time second injections were soaring, so they compensated each other. This plateau suggests our vaccination centres were simply functioning at (or close to) full capacity. Third, on July the 12th, at the time our President made his speech about restricting everyone's freedom (including the vaccinated, since you'd have to present your digital pass basically everywhere to basically anyone), they were justifying our \"Reluctance\" by quoting vaccination rate across the whole population, which was around 52%. What they didn't say is that this stats includes children under 12, for which vaccination is simply not available, and teenagers, for which our CCNE (Comité Consultatif National d'Éthique) said on June the 9th that it was not clear teenage vaccination was safe enough [LINK] It's only reasonable under these circumstances to exclude all u"}],"r":[["46ehrFk-gLk",1],["RqQhWitJ1As",1],["spUNpyF58BY",1],["nT-TGvYOBpI",1],["Bkm2Vfj42FY",1],["FfDa8tR25dk",1],["2gFsOoKAHZg",1],["7CgXvIuZR40",1],["5f9R9MtkpqM",1],["QEJ9HrZq7Ro",1],["Ylomy1Aw9Hk",1],["4loZGYqaZ7I",1],["7tScAyNaRdQ",1],["eXR1olg_I0w",1],["CaPgDQkmqqM",1],["14wqBA5Q1yc",1],["tcdVC4e6EV4",1],["PGLYEDpNu60",1],["MBnnXbOM5S4",1],["BYRTvoZ3Rho",1]]},"i-fvwg9zy08":{"wy":"HN mentions it to identify the Secret Life of Machines theme music.","m":[{"id":37020833,"author":"throw0101a","month":"2023-08","story":"Tim Hunkin (Secret Lives of Machines)","stance":"rec","why":"Reggae remix of Take Five, the show's intro theme.","quote":"Worth watching just for the intro theme song","text":"Worth watching just for the intro theme song, \"The Russians Are Coming\", a reggae remix of Dave Brubeck's \"Take Five\": * [VIDEO] * [LINK]"},{"id":39117009,"author":"throw0101d","month":"2024-01","story":"The Secret Life of Machines (1988)","stance":"reference","why":"Reggae cover of Take Five used as theme song.","quote":"The theme song is Bennett's \"The Russians are Coming\" (a reggae cover of \"Take Five\")","text":"See also playlist from Tim Hunkin's channel: * [LINK] * [LINK] * [LINK] The theme song is Bennett's \"The Russians are Coming\" (a reggae cover of \"Take Five\"): * [VIDEO]"},{"id":35345436,"author":"throw0101b","month":"2023-03","story":"Apple Music Classical","stance":"reference","why":"Theme tune variant of Take Five used in Secret Life of Machines.","quote":"This is the \"Take Five\" variant by Val Bennett, \"The Russians Are Coming","text":"This is the \"Take Five\" variant by Val Bennett, \"The Russians Are Coming\", used in the show Secret Life of Machines (awesome series, now freely/legally posted on YT): * [LINK] * [VIDEO] * [LINK]"}],"r":[["K3lP3BhvnSo",1],["ORzNZUeUHAM",1],["ipVxxxqwBQw",1],["WVDQEoe6ZWY",1],["BCmz-fgp24E",1],["rkfAcWpOYAA",1],["jN7mSXMruEo",1],["Uwl3-jBNEd4",1],["ASXJgvy3mEg",1],["1FXuMs6YRCY",1],["wdgULBpRoXk",1],["_rBO8neWw04",1],["-ZDZtBRTyeI",1],["8wlld3Z9wRc",1],["lBVvnDfW2Xo",1],["7zrx-b2sLUs",1],["Ll6-eGDpimU",1],["4j_UjIshzMc",1],["NSaRb-iFwPA",1],["YtfzjehDg74",1]]},"lKhAd0Tyny0":{"wy":"HN cites it in discussions of pay fairness and inequality as a short, funny demonstration of the fairness instinct.","q":{"x":"it's a short video and pretty hilarious, worth a watch","i":7237416,"y":"2014"},"m":[{"id":7237416,"author":"thaumaturgy","month":"2014-02","story":"The Star Trek Economy","stance":"rec","why":"Monkeys reacting to unequal reward.","quote":"it's a short video and pretty hilarious, worth a watch","text":"So, economic models are more or less based on, or at least constrained by, human psychology, right? Communism and socialism don't work because peoples' brains aren't wired to do lots of work for little or no or unequal reward. This isn't restricted to just people, either, it's been observed in monkeys ( [LINK] , [VIDEO] -- it's a short video and pretty hilarious, worth a watch). At the same time, capitalism has serious flaws, also exploited by human psychology. Capitalism rewards not just work and the investment of capital but also greed and cheating. So, when looking for a new economic model, why not go back to psychology? Is it really true that most people don't want to work? There are some people who are content to receive a small stipend and spend their lives jut getting by without doing very much, but does that describe enough people to cause the collapse of a strong welfare society? I think we probably are headed towards a post-scarcity (or low-scarcity) economy. Food costs as percentage of income in the U.S. have fallen quite a bit just in the last thirty years ( [LINK] ). People are already going to find that it takes less real effort to survive ( [LINK] , posted earlier to HN: [LINK] ). A different economic model seems inevitable. Likewise, there will probably always be jobs that people don't want to do. Take oil rig work for example: hard, dangerous work, and it pays well, starting at the U.S. median wage for unskilled, inexperienced labor ( [LINK] ) and going up fr"},{"id":19781577,"author":"pacala","month":"2019-04","story":"Google Staffers Share Stories of ‘Systemic’ Retaliation","stance":"reference","why":"Fairness instinct observed in monkeys.","quote":"The fairness instinct has been observed even in monkeys","text":"Most people have a fairness instinct. The fairness instinct has been observed even in monkeys [0]. Given that most people also live within a social fabric, it is not too hard to imagine that most people have an instinct for a fair society. Alas, this is simply too vague of a definition, as it encompasses most religious/philosophical/political perspectives out there, on the left as well as on the right [gasp!]. The modern \"social justice\" movement has a number of very specific insistences, which align with a much smaller subset of perspectives than the umbrella \"fair society\" ideal would imply. I simply want to have someone sympathetic to the modern \"social justice\" movement to state some specifics on what they mean by \"social justice\". Looks like you're not the right person, no worries. I'll keep asking. One day someone will answer. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":9498584,"author":"whybroke","month":"2015-05","story":"“Information asymmetry” cuts both ways","stance":"reference","why":"Short video of fairness instinct in primates.","quote":"or tldr;","text":"That is very true. Indeed we know fairness is an innate value for us and all primates [1] Likewise \"meritocracy\" is a very imprecise word depending a lot on what you want to maximize and so, if not focusing on net benefit to humanity, could produce horrendous outcomes. Blindly maximizing wealth creation could be such a mistake but given how much good prosperity usually does for people, it looks like a good starting point. But I believe the article and I are referring to fairness among employees rather than employee vs employer (which is of course also interesting). Specifically, employees comparing wages would create quite appropriate outrage between employees when some individuals receive more for less work. And this regardless of how much or little of the company's produce goes to wages (a separate issue) [1] [LINK] or tldr; [VIDEO]"},{"id":7029637,"author":"Intermernet","month":"2014-01","story":"Light Table is now open source","stance":"reference","why":"Fairness experiment with capuchin monkeys.","quote":"Frans De Waal demonstrates an experiment with Capuchin Monkeys showing this effect in action.","text":"My feeling is that the fairness effect comes into play strongly with open source distribution models. People are willing to play fairly (i.e. not re-distribute source code that's probably only a google search away anyway) when the author does the same. Frans De Waal demonstrates an experiment with Capuchin Monkeys showing this effect in action. [LINK] [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["Gj8IA6xOpSk",1],["kRlhlCWplqk",1],["X8aWBcPVPMo",1],["jVkLVRt6c1U",1],["kya_LXa_y1E",1],["j2Mu8qfVb5I",1]]},"pWI5aVKvkCg":{"wy":"Offered as calm piano-led music for programming and shared as a personal favorite and memorial to Sakamoto.","m":[{"id":32845982,"author":"Dracophoenix","month":"2022-09","story":"Ask HN: How do you discover new music?","stance":"rec","why":"Sakamoto piano piece inspired by Ravel, a personal favorite.","quote":"One of my favorites is \"Blu","text":"Debussy is a common inspiration for many Japanese classical compositions (especially in anime). Maurice Ravel (of \"Bolero\" and \"Pavane\" fame) is another common one. Since you mentioned Yellow Magic Orchestra, you likely know of Ryuichi Sakomoto. In case you were unaware, he has composed several movie and concert pieces for piano many of which were notably inspired by Ravel. One of my favorites is \"Blu\" ( [VIDEO] )."},{"id":35413124,"author":"pacomerh","month":"2023-04","story":"Ryuichi Sakamoto Has Died","stance":"reference","why":"Memorial link to Sakamoto's music upon his death.","quote":"Truly admire Sakamoto's work, a big influence in my life.","text":"Truly admire Sakamoto's work, a big influence in my life. From Yellow Magic Orchestra to all his solo career. RIP [VIDEO]"},{"id":26680706,"author":"kkoncevicius","month":"2021-04","story":"Ask HN: Does anything beat Slipknot's Unsainted for programming productivity?","stance":"reference","why":"Piano piece offered as calm classical background for programming.","quote":"","text":"I seriously cannot believe music like this helps you with programming, but if it does for you then try \"The Algorithm - Access Denied\"[1]. For me no music is best. Followed by some white noise. Followed by classical, i.e. \"Dvorak - Noon Witch\"[2], \"Ryuichi Sakamoto - Blu\"[3]. [1]: [LINK] [2]: [LINK] [3]: [VIDEO]"}]},"ridS396W2BY":{"wy":"Cited as a real-world example of automatic emergency braking already working on heavy trucks.","q":{"x":"Here's a really impressive video of it in action on a semi-truck","i":10303729,"y":"2015"},"m":[{"id":10303729,"author":"irl_zebra","month":"2015-09","story":"Meet Model X","stance":"rec","why":"Impressive demonstration of emergency braking on a semi-truck","quote":"Here's a really impressive video of it in action on a semi-truck","text":"My Volvo does this. Not sure if it's the same thing, but stops you if you're about to collide with something. I've never experienced it (as I've never yet needed the feature), but have heard good things. Here's a really impressive video of it in action on a semi-truck: [VIDEO] Counterpoint (with the model I have, unfortunately): [LINK]"},{"id":9844268,"author":"gambiting","month":"2015-07","story":"Google Self-Driving Car Project Monthly Report [pdf]","stance":"reference","why":"Truck automatic emergency braking preventing collisions","quote":"Isn't that solved by the automatic collision detection with emergency braking, as implemented by Volvo?[1]","text":"\"There seem to be hundreds of accidents like this [1] annually where large trucks smash into cars stopped at red lights or that have slowed down during traffic jams.\" Isn't that solved by the automatic collision detection with emergency braking, as implemented by Volvo?[1] [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":6637589,"author":"drbawb","month":"2013-10","story":"Toyota's firmware: Bad design and its consequences","stance":"reference","why":"Example of collision avoidance made possible by drive-by-wire","quote":"","text":"The throttle control on an early 90s, or early 2000 FORD is also a lever and rod [or braided steel cable] attached to a couple springs... Unless you had cruise control [remember when that was an _option_?]... then there's a servo or equivalent motor in-line with the cable and springs. --- Even with a digital system: there's no benefit. It's the same mechanical piece, it has to be, that's how combustion engines work: you open a throttle plate, the vacuum sucks in more air, the computer compensates w/ more fuel, compress that, spark it, you have more power. You will always have a throttle plate and some sort of lever; and since you want it to fail closed you best have a return spring somewhere. All you gain by adding a digital control system is [perhaps] clever software control and [certainly] additional complexity. Is it worth it? Perhaps... there are some _very_ cool systems developed as a result of drive-by-wire. Lane following assist, parking assist, assisted hill starts, manual transmissions with automated clutches, collision avoidance, etc.[0] I would definitely argue for more _transparency_ in this technology, but I don't know that I could argue that the technology itself is a bad thing. [0]: [VIDEO] \"Volvo Trucks - Emergency braking at its best!\""}]},"sXwDrcd1t-E":{"wy":"Recommended as the companion explanation to the Espargos project, showing how phase and time differences localize devices.","q":{"x":"There's an excellent companion video here","i":43079024,"y":"2025"},"m":[{"id":43079024,"author":"leoedin","month":"2025-02","story":"Espargos: ESP32-based WiFi sensing array","stance":"rec","why":"Excellent companion explanation of the ESP32 antenna array project.","quote":"There's an excellent companion video here","text":"This project uses an array of ESP32s and some clever calibration techniques to do pretty accurate localisation of WiFi signals. There's an excellent companion video here: [VIDEO]"},{"id":47360868,"author":"alter_igel","month":"2026-03","story":"Full Spectrum and Infrared Photography","stance":"reference","why":"Author cites own video as example of DIY microwave imaging.","quote":"with microwaves via phased arrays [2]","text":"Author here. I agree with you, \"full spectrum\" is a generous marketing phrase for what might more accurately be called _extended_ spectrum. People way smarter than me have been able to achieve DIY spatial imaging with x-rays via compressed sensing [1] and with microwaves via phased arrays [2]. Optical wavelengths seem to be at a sweet spot of good angular resolution, varied natural sources, and harmless to humans. [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO]"},{"id":46978525,"author":"rubatuga","month":"2026-02","story":"WiFi Could Become an Invisible Mass Surveillance System","stance":"reference","why":"Shows an ESP32 array localizing WiFi devices by phase and time.","quote":"it uses both time and phase differences to localize based on MAC addresses","text":"I was really impressed that a ESP32 Antenna Array Can essentially make a WiFi camera - it uses both time and phase differences to localize based on MAC addresses (which are sent plaintext) [VIDEO]"},{"id":43086511,"author":"rollulus","month":"2025-02","story":"Espargos: ESP32-based WiFi sensing array","stance":"reference","why":"Summarizes how ESP phase and amplitude data enable localisation.","quote":"All ESPs are calibrated relatively to each other.","text":"As shared elsewhere in this thread: [VIDEO] TL;DW: the ESP’s WiFi driver provides per incoming packet the carrier’s phase and amplitude. All ESPs are calibrated relatively to each other."}]},"thnXzUFJnfQ":{"wy":"Cited as a classic of Amiga music, and commenters use it as a reference or comparison track.","q":{"x":"you can't mention Amiga music without also mentioning Space Debris","i":30920253,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":30920253,"author":"Sohcahtoa82","month":"2022-04","story":"The cult of Amiga and SGI, or why workstations matter","stance":"rec","why":"Classic Amiga music track","quote":"you can't mention Amiga music without also mentioning Space Debris","text":"My late dad was a huge Amiga fan back in the day. I was just a little kid at the time and didn't see what the big deal was. Looking back at what it was capable of though...they were doing 256 colors and sampled audio at a time when x86 was still pushing 16 colors and could only produce generated tones through the speaker built into the case. There was some really good music on the Amiga, too. Some of my favorites: Hybris theme: [LINK] Pioneer Plague theme: [LINK] Treasure Trap theme: [LINK] And of course, you can't mention Amiga music without also mentioning Space Debris: [VIDEO]"},{"id":29621511,"author":"exogeny","month":"2021-12","story":"Space Debris is an Amiga mod I composed back in 1991","stance":"reference","why":"Starting point of a commenter's Amiga music playlist","quote":"","text":"I went ahead and created it: [VIDEO] I stayed within the Amiga scene, which means a lot of the picks are demo soundtracks that have that Italo/synthpop kind of style. But there's lots of classics in there, from Jester/Sanity (Germany) to Firefox & Mantronix/Phenomena (Sweden) to Dr. Awesome/Crusaders (Norway) and on. Captain, is of course, Finnish. Let the Eurovision-ish arguments commence ;)"},{"id":29621892,"author":"nyanpasu64","month":"2021-12","story":"Space Debris is an Amiga mod I composed back in 1991","stance":"reference","why":"Version with aliasing used to compare against the remaster","quote":"Compared to a video with aliasing","text":"As someone who got into trackers after the \"golden age\", I wanted to comment with my perspective (focused on DSP, workflow, and chiptune): I noticed the video in the page is a remaster. To my ears, it sounds like it's being played with non-Amiga interpolation with less spectral imaging/replication (often mistakenly called aliasing), altered panning (the Amiga has an infamously rigid hard-panning setup), and possibly eq/reverb mastering on top of that. Compared to a video with aliasing ( [VIDEO] , no clue if the Amiga low-pass filtering is accurate), I like how the remaster has less high-pitched whine, but I feel it's missing out on the spectral replicas which influence the original's sound. > I could try “hiding” the loop point by adding fades in the beginning and end of the sample, and then overlapping the fade areas. I've heard people saying that crossfading is effective at looping, but in my experience it's difficult to pick good crossfade regions which don't result in altered timbre or audible discontinuities. Good crossfades are still slightly visible in a spectrogram, and bad crossfades are visibly and audibly discontinuous. I'm probably 30 years too late, but I implemented a more sophisticated (but more situational) algorithm which analyzes a sample as a spectrum, then resynthesizes it using a variation of the existing padsynth algorithm. This produces a perfectly looped sound with no discontinuities at the loop point (unlike crossfading), with built-in chorusing (not "},{"id":17165583,"author":"themodelplumber","month":"2018-05","story":"Building a YouTube MP3 Downloader with Exodus, FFmpeg, and AWS Lambda","stance":"reference","why":"Used as example input to a personal youtube-dl script","quote":"","text":"youtube-dl is awesome. I built a bash script around this use case, with some additional bits just for file management. I run \"yety [VIDEO] and poof, the mp3 is dropboxed and available on all my devices."}],"r":[["6RhtiPefVzM",1],["uWUHkCgslNE",1]]},"vV7YgnPUxcU":{"wy":"Commenters cite it as the primary source for Thiel's Antichrist argument whenever his views come up.","q":{"x":"worth watching the interview with the NYT's Ross Douthat","i":47420545,"y":"2026"},"m":[{"id":47420545,"author":"andsoitis","month":"2026-03","story":"Peter Thiel Fears the Antichrist Is Coming","stance":"rec","why":"Thiel explains his Antichrist framing, tied to civilizational stagnation.","quote":"worth watching the interview with the NYT's Ross Douthat","text":"Better to hear from him directly than filtered, so worth watching the interview with the NYT's Ross Douthat, in which Thiel frames what he means by the Antichrist. [VIDEO] Without spoiling too much, it is related to civilizational stagnation, and that our lack of progress risks catastrophic outcomes."},{"id":44760072,"author":"onepremise","month":"2025-08","story":"Corporation for Public Broadcasting ceasing operations","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Again, it's all part of the plan, which is referred to as the butterfly revolution, by Curtis Yarvin. Leaders that have literally invested in this platform are buying into this nonsense. These guys have polarized the two parties to a point all weaknesses are surfacing. It isn't about democrats vs republicans. It's just working class vs the billionaires. You know the ppl sitting behind Trump at his inauguration. Literally, they want to break apart the US and discredit the constitution. Unless we come together and carve a new narrative that works. These guys may succeed and you can kiss your life, liberty, and pursuit of happiness goodbye, as well bill of rights. Peter Theil, JD Vance, Marc Andreesen, Garry Tan, Srinivasan, and many others, wanting to overthrow democracy and dissolve nation states. This effort is to establish Network States with those that worship them, sycophants and cults. They want to transform the US into an Autocracy. The polarization of the media and political parties is on purpose. They want America to fall. It's not a secret, not a conspiracy theory. It's definitely being rolled out by billionaires. It would be wise for others here to really do your research and understand why we are being polarized to hate each other. Enter the butterfly revolution: 1. Reboot (“full-power start”) Suspend or bypass existing constitutional limits; concentrate absolute sovereignty in one new organization—analogous to Allied occupation powers in post-1945 Japan/Germany. El"},{"id":44797378,"author":"estearum","month":"2025-08","story":"Palantir is extending its reach even further into government","stance":"reference","why":"Thiel's own words on the Antichrist and Greta Thunberg.","quote":"The way the Antichrist would take over the world is you talk about Armageddon nonstop.","text":"My claim: \"he believes Greta Thunberg is very possibly the actual antichrist\" Thiel's words: > Thiel: ... The way the Antichrist would take over the world is you talk about Armageddon nonstop. You talk about existential risk nonstop, and this is what you need to regulate.... > in the 17th century, I can imagine a Dr. Strangelove, Edward Teller-type person taking over the world. > In our world, it’s far more likely to be Greta Thunberg. [VIDEO]"},{"id":44704204,"author":"onepremise","month":"2025-07","story":"4k NASA employees opt to leave agency through deferred resignation program","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"It's all part of the plan, which is referred to as the butterfly revolution, by Curtis Yarvin. Leaders that have literally invested in this platform are buying into this nonsense. Peter Theil, JD Vance, Marc Andreesen, Garry Tan, Srinivasan, and many others, wanting to overthrow democracy and dissolve nation states. This effort is to establish Network States with those that worship them, sycophants and cults. They want to transform the US into an Autocracy. The polarization of the media and political parties is on purpose. They want America to fall. It's not a secret, not a conspiracy theory. It's definitely being rolled out by billionaires. It would be wise for others here to really do your research and understand why we are being polarized to hate each other. Enter butterfly revolution: 1. Reboot (“full-power start”) Suspend or bypass existing constitutional limits; concentrate absolute sovereignty in one new organization—analogous to Allied occupation powers in post-1945 Japan/Germany. Eliminate checks and balances that block rapid change. 2. CEO-Monarch model A single executive (chosen like a corporate CEO) rules; the former president becomes a figurehead “chairman of the board.” Treat the state as a firm run for efficiency, not democratic representation. 3. RAGE strategy “Retire All Government Employees” by mass-firing the civil service and replacing it with loyal appointees. Remove institutional resistance (“the Cathedral”) and ensure obedience. 4. Parallel regime Build"}]},"zGcKURD_osM":{"wy":"Used as background viewing in threads on Purdue's bankruptcy and opioid settlements.","m":[{"id":26029427,"author":"Geminidog","month":"2021-02","story":"McKinsey to pay $573M to settle claims over opioid crisis role: source","stance":"rec","why":"Shows the scale of harm from the opioid crisis.","quote":"Watch these videos","text":"It's very possible for everyone to be guilty. We don't live in a world where if something isn't \"useful\" it isn't true. Your statement ends up reading sort of like \"Is every Nazi guilty of the holocaust?\" Technically maybe not, doesn't change the fact that of the matter that overall all Nazis are guilty. You can't run away from this with some garbage statement of \"Not a very useful perspective.\" This incident literally killed an amount of people that is equivalent to a genocide. Imagine if you were a Nazi and you said that. If you were just a mere guard at one of these concentration camps could you say what you just said to me to a victim who lived through the atrocity? Think about what you should say to the parents of a man/woman who died from an opioid overdose. Literally, I think you're unaware of the magnitude of the crime that was committed here. Watch these videos: [VIDEO] [LINK]"},{"id":28408342,"author":"ryneandal","month":"2021-09","story":"You’ve Never Seen the Opioid Crisis Like This Before: A Review of Empire of Pain","stance":"reference","why":"Documents the Sacklers' role in opioid deaths.","quote":"Prison time is well-deserved considering the outcome of their lies and fraud","text":"Considering the scope and breadth of their involvement in the deaths of hundreds of thousands, I don't think losing their money and \"empire\" is adequate. Prison time is well-deserved considering the outcome of their lies and fraud: [VIDEO]"},{"id":20981322,"author":"formatkaka","month":"2019-09","story":"Purdue Pharma files for bankruptcy with $10B plan to settle claims","stance":"reference","why":"Background on the Sackler family and Purdue Pharma.","quote":"If anyone doesn't know what's going on here exactly.","text":"If anyone doesn't know what's going on here exactly. The Sackler Family – A Secretive Billion Dollar Opioid Empire - [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["3WMuzhQXJoY",1]]},"22wlLy7hKP4":{"wy":"HN mostly cites it as an example of a misleading or faked product demo, with a few pointing to its teachable-mode segment.","m":[{"id":38933905,"author":"cwrichardkim","month":"2024-01","story":"Rabbit: LLM-First Mobile Phone","stance":"reference","why":"Rabbit r1 keynote showing teachable mode for agents","quote":"I think the interesting bit here is actually the teachable mode (19:30 in the vid)","text":"I think the interesting bit here is actually the teachable mode (19:30 in the vid): [VIDEO] there are tools that exist that teach agents what to do, but the ability to have them on command and return a nice little summary in a neat UI is actually kind of compelling. It also feeds the company routine information to fine tune agents and maybe results in a dev ecosystem of some kind? need to learn more about how the system prevents some of the concerns hear like taking undesirable actions on your behalf"},{"id":45457292,"author":"godelski","month":"2025-10","story":"Pre-record your demos","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"This whole thing was faked yet it was presented as a live demonstration, not an illustration.","text":"If you pre-record your live demo then it isn't a live demo... What I dislike is presenting something as if it is live when it isn't. Demos are fantastic things and it is even perfectly fine to have imaginative aspects to them. But there's a division line between demoing and lying. Take Rabbit R1 as an example[0]. This whole thing was faked yet it was presented as a live demonstration, not an illustration. In other words, fraud. The difference is if you're trying to show your customers (or potential customers) what you're imagining the device works like vs what the device actually works like. The difference is how you're communicating the difference to them. I'm not sure why this is a contentious opinion, but just don't lie to people? [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":40988039,"author":"mewse-hn","month":"2024-07","story":"Jailbreaking RabbitOS","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"You really have to watch the Steve Jobs-esque announcement video to understand what they were promising with this device","text":"You really have to watch the Steve Jobs-esque announcement video to understand what they were promising with this device, and understand how utterly it failed to deliver on those promises. [VIDEO]"},{"id":40590288,"author":"mtgr18977","month":"2024-06","story":"Rabbit R1 It's a Scam","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"my view (and I understand how plugins, LLM, AI and other related things work) ist thre are no LAM in the rabbit r1.","text":"Maybe you should watch their announcement video again: [VIDEO] Or the interview of the CEO, Jesse Lyu aboutn the LAM: [LINK] If you're still sure that the LAM is present in their product, that's fine, but my view (and I understand how plugins, LLM, AI and other related things work) ist thre are no LAM in the rabbit r1."},{"id":40135833,"author":"SheinhardtWigCo","month":"2024-04","story":"Rabbit R1 source code [part 1]","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"It's the most blatantly fake product demo I have ever seen.","text":"Their \"keynote\" just screams shenanigans. It's the most blatantly fake product demo I have ever seen. To take one example, at 14m30s, the CEO is shown using the device to book a trip to London. This is presented as a live demo, but it's clearly simulated. [VIDEO]"},{"id":40144302,"author":"maxsilver","month":"2024-04","story":"Rabbit R1 source code [part 1]","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"But the spreadsheet it sent, that they then show on screen, has not filtered any candidates to LA-only.","text":"Fun tidbit: There's a point in the tech demo where he asks the rabbit device to \"filter job candidates to ones that are LA-based\" When the r1 address replies to him, it attaches a new spreadsheet, and he opens it on-stage. But the spreadsheet it sent, that they then show on screen, has not filtered any candidates to LA-only. (The column next to their names shows labels on screen like \"NYC\", \"Austin\", \"Miami\", \"Seattle\", etc) [VIDEO] (exact timestamp)"}]},"5GpOfwbFRcs":{"wy":"Used as a joke reference whenever the cool-kids-use-X trope comes up in technology threads.","m":[{"id":43661350,"author":"ticoombs","month":"2025-04","story":"You might not need WebSockets","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"they would be a tech architect who only uses","text":"I always try and push back on those beliefs, about reasonings why they believe it will be faster or more efficient than some other solution. I've found , if you could type cast those people, they would be a tech architect who only uses \"web scale\" items. (Relevant link: [VIDEO] )"},{"id":7928307,"author":"glibgil","month":"2014-06","story":"Why The Cool Kids Don't Use Erlang","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"He is making it ridiculous.","text":"gar1t is the guy that blew up \"cool kids\". He is doing better than banning the phrase. He is making it ridiculous. [VIDEO]"},{"id":2896717,"author":"mtogo","month":"2011-08","story":"What Does an Architect Do in an Agile Shop? An Agile Architect Explains ","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"All the cool kids use ruby","text":"Haha, hey guys, us Agile Ruby guys are so much cooler than those stupid people that don't use Apple MacBook Pros and chill at Starbucks. Sure, they write code and get things done while we talk about how awesome our Ruby code is, but hey, All the cool kids use ruby[1]. -- Send from my iPad [1] [VIDEO]"}]},"7tAYXQPWdC0":{"wy":"HN commenters mostly cite it skeptically in climate threads, as an example of past predictions of cooling.","m":[{"id":29379627,"author":"aww_dang","month":"2021-11","story":"Dam the Bering Strait, use nuclear-powered propellers to melt poles (2020)","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"In 1978, doomsayers predicted a new ice age.","text":"Is there any irony in observing the fickle preferences of man in regards to geoengineering? Perhaps our understanding is more limited than some are willing to admit. 63 years ago, there was a push to warm the arctic. In 1978, doomsayers predicted a new ice age. For all of the predictions of apocalypse, none have materialized. However, throughout history fear has been a powerful tool for motivating and manipulating the public. \"Ice Age 1978 Leonard Nimoy\" - [VIDEO] \"List of dates predicted for apocalyptic events\" - [LINK]"},{"id":28173526,"author":"dotcommand","month":"2021-08","story":"It’s official: July was Earth’s hottest month on record","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"","text":"> Who still believes global warming does not exist? Nobody is denying that the weather has been warming. It's all the political hysterics and nonsense that a lot of people have issues with. And of course the \"world-is-ending\" chicken little craziness. > I am currently listening to audiobook of Tom Clancy's Rainbow Six (1998). It has a paragraph on global warming and trying to convince POTUS it is real. And POTUS not wanting to act on it because it would be too large economic drag on US. And if you watched Day After Tomorrow, are you going to use that as an example of the globe cooling? With every shift in weather, the media and the mindless always scream the world is ending. Our parents' generation had to worry about global cooling. \"As they review the bizarre and unpredictable weather pattern of the past several years, a growing number of scientists are beginning to suspect that many seemingly contradictory meteorological fluctuations are actually part of a global climatic upheaval. However widely the weather varies from place to place and time to time, when meteorologists take an average of temperatures around the globe they find that the atmosphere has been growing gradually cooler for the past three decades. The trend shows no indication of reversing. Climatological Cassandras are becoming increasingly apprehensive, for the weather aberrations they are studying may be the harbinger of another ice age.\" [LINK] Here's a documentary featuring leonard nemoy about \"climate cha"},{"id":27979655,"author":"mythrwy","month":"2021-07","story":"Japan's cherry blossom: as indisputable as climate change evidence gets","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"here's Leonard Nimoy talking about it","text":"\"It's happening\" and the effects attributable to humans burning fossil fuels is a scale though. There's very little doubt C02 does increase the holding of heat. There's also very little doubt the earth has gotten slightly warmer over the past century. Where I become skeptical is in the modeling and predictions. More heat means more evaporation means more cloud cover means less heat but clouds hold heat held in. The earth tilts slightly on it's axis. Energy output from the sun varies. There are so many interacting factors. \"Butterfly effect\" and all. Then you throw politics and redistribution-ism and media sensationalism on top of it all and wow what a stinky mess. Suffice it to say I personally don't think global catastrophe is imminent from C02. Not convinced at all. I lived through the ice age scare (here's Leonard Nimoy talking about it: [VIDEO] ) and the start of the global warming alarm in the late 80's (which just didn't pan out as many expected 30+ years later). So I don't trust the ability to model long term climate changes and present it in an objective and non political fashion. But I do believe humans are affecting the climate and from caution we should reduce C02 as we reasonably can without starving millions to death. It's cleaner anyway and fossil fuels are just nasty in other respects and they are limited. Am I an illiterate unscientific \"denier\"? An oil company shill? Nope. Just a guy who has seen nonsense hype cycles which this certainly resembles come and go"}]},"9kKp0oguzr8":{"wy":"HN points to it as the official showcase that Godot works for non-game application development.","q":{"x":"I've been interested in using Godot for application development since I saw their official","i":41647324,"y":"2024"},"m":[{"id":41647324,"author":"rychco","month":"2024-09","story":"Godot for App Development","stance":"reference","why":"Official showreel of applications and tools made with Godot.","quote":"I've been interested in using Godot for application development since I saw their official","text":"I'm happy to see that this usage of Godot has continued. I've been interested in using Godot for application development since I saw their official \"Apps & Tools - 2022 Showreel\"[1] video. The only concern I see commonly mentioned is in terms of accessibility (lack of screen reader support, etc.) - which could be a major drawback for certain types of software. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":39910523,"author":"mentos","month":"2024-04","story":"3D + 2D: Testing out my cross-platform WASM graphics engine","stance":"reference","why":"Showcase of apps and tools built with Godot beyond games.","quote":"I've seen some cool applications made in it too","text":"I agree Unreal and Unity are not appropriate but I do wonder about Godot. Its early enough where it doesn't have the strong connotations of being a game engine yet. I've seen some cool applications made in it too ( [VIDEO] ). So I wonder if you could apply your energy to making it more cross platform using WASM (if that's even necessary) and extend it with your own UI language instead of rolling your own?"},{"id":37776009,"author":"mdtrooper","month":"2023-10","story":"","stance":"reference","why":"Showreel of serious applications built in Godot.","quote":"It is a good time for send the showreel of serious apps in Godot","text":"It is a good time for send the showreel of serious apps in Godot: [VIDEO] I know a free software monitoring tool made with Godot: [LINK] [LINK]"},{"id":35212564,"author":"follower","month":"2023-03","story":"Something Pretty Right: The History and Legacy of Visual Basic","stance":"reference","why":"Godot adopted for non-game applications.","quote":"Godot's modularity, ease of development & distribution has led to it being adopted for non-game applications too","text":"One of the leading contenders for being a modern equivalent IMO would be the Godot game engine. The Godot editor is less than 100MB to download for many platforms--the recent Godot 4 release also supports running the editor on the web[0] and on Android. Its packaging/distribution story for GUI-based applications is easier & stronger than any other modern programming environment of which I'm aware--in a large part due to the (IMO inspired ) architecture of using per-platform pre-built application templates/shells paired with per-project \"data\" files that contain assets & script byte code. Godot's modularity, ease of development & distribution has led to it being adopted for non-game applications too: \"Apps & Tools - 2022 Showreel\" < [VIDEO] > I do think the initial \"complexity of the programming\" for an absolute beginner is probably still at least a little bit higher than drag-and-drop GUI environments like classic VB, REALbasic, VBA or Hypercard. (Primarily from a UI/UX point of view because Godot doesn't directly have the \"drag button from toolbar\" workflow which I think was key in earlier systems. But it is extremely modular by way of configuration & extension--one intended use case being e.g. specialized feature-subsetted tools for artists.) In my experience it also works really well for iterative development where you can just hack around (from visual & code perspectives) to get something working & then refine it (and using Godot's \"Scenes\" paradigm for encapsulation). An"}]},"EIyixC9NsLI":{"wy":"HN invokes it purely as a joke or nostalgic meme reference.","m":[{"id":48023989,"author":"sspiff","month":"2026-05","story":"The best is over: The fun has been optimized out of the Internet","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"OP, the Badger Badger link is leading to a wiki page about the dead Internet theory. Here's the correct link for all to enjoy: [VIDEO] Used to be just a Flash animation on Weebl's stuff, but preserved on YouTube by the OG :)"},{"id":38498210,"author":"Kye","month":"2023-12","story":"Salad Fingers and the dawn of ‘weird YouTube’","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"You sure about that? [VIDEO] This is the era terms like \"lolrandom\" were invented for. [LINK] See also: ROFLcopter, AEIOUAEIOUAEIOUAEIOUAEIOU, making funny noises with text to speech."},{"id":29877161,"author":"petecooper","month":"2022-01","story":"_Application.Run(Object, Object, Object, Object, Object, Object, Object, Object","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":">Object, Object, Object, Object, Object, Object, Object, Object Cmd+F, badger (Not found). Cmd+F, mushroom (Not found). [VIDEO] (video sfw, comments may be the usual YouTube battleground)"},{"id":11670768,"author":"walrus01","month":"2016-05","story":"Ask HN: How do I deal with the first sexual harassment complaint at my startup?","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"you know the flash animation video that goes \"badger badger badger badger badger\"? [VIDEO] well, think like that, except; lawyer lawyer lawyer lawyer lawyer lawyer lawyer lawyer lawyer lawyer lawyer lawyer lawyer lawyer lawyer lawyer lawyer mushroom, mushroom"}]},"JtUAAXe_0VI":{"wy":"HN cites it when debating Kurzgesagt's credibility and its corrections and removals of earlier content.","q":{"x":"They also vet their videos with experts and are transparent in their methodology","i":24542351,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":31449926,"author":"Dextro","month":"2022-05","story":"PBS SpaceTime","stance":"reference","why":"Kurzgesagt addresses presenting uncertain things as fact.","quote":"They also removed a few of their older videos at the time","text":"I believe they did it on this video called \"Can You Trust Kurzgesagt?\". They also removed a few of their older videos at the time because they had this exact issue of presenting as fact some things that were not at all certain. [VIDEO]"},{"id":30584000,"author":"irjustin","month":"2022-03","story":"My lizard brain is no match for infinite scroll","stance":"reference","why":"Kurzgesagt's correction video softening its earlier stance on addiction.","quote":"the basically walked it back from such a hard line stance in a correction/update video[1]","text":"> physical vs psychological dependence is kind of outdated I wouldn't say outdated, but I do agree it is 100% up for debate. Kurzgesagt did a really similar video[0] on addiction, but the basically walked it back from such a hard line stance in a correction/update video[1]. It's not so easy to tease apart the social vs the physical vs the pure mental. > Just the same, you can be addicted to coffee. Caffeine is a drug. Right, I think you're agreeing with me. That they have similar aspects, but to treat them the same - like putting caffine on Class 1 - would be insane. [0] [LINK] [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":24542351,"author":"CaptArmchair","month":"2020-09","story":"The Social Dilemma","stance":"reference","why":"Kurzgesagt's transparent vetting methodology cited to defend its credibility.","quote":"They also vet their videos with experts and are transparent in their methodology","text":"> I think this explanation, as well as that from the video, is layman psychology at best Well, kurzgesagt back their statements with references to academic research: [LINK] They also vet their videos with experts and are transparent in their methodology: [VIDEO] > Yes, there are factors or mechanisms that drive your desire for belonging, but it is a pretty unconvincing observation. It doesn't have to be tribalism to prefer being around people you trust. Why would you assume that I didn't consider other explanations? > FOMO is more connected to the fear of the unknown and fear of loss in my opinion. In what way wouldn't \"the fear of the unkown\" or \"the fear of loss\" be less connected with the fear of likely missing crucial parts of the conversations your social network is having? e.g. you might miss out hearing about a party, where someone makes an personal announcement (e.g. getting married, moving to another country,...). So, now your friends have a shared experience of having heard the news first hand that you aren't part of. > A \"tribe\" would shield you of course, but it is mostly a sign of other needs not being met. Advertisers use it to their advantage for decades. Some appeal to their audiences to be the source of other peoples FOMOs. \"think different\" instead of \"stay connected\". What \"other needs\" are these? > There are less suicides than in the 90s. How is the number of suicides 30 years ago relevant to a dynamic observed over the course of the past 15 years? > That "}]},"Mr0rWJhv9jU":{"wy":"Mostly cited as the source of Hotz's AMD driver rant, which some commenters found validating and others found unflattering.","q":{"x":"Now I know it wasn't just me.","i":36550525,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":39889198,"author":"roenxi","month":"2024-03","story":"Documentation for the AMD 7900XTX","stance":"reference","why":"Context on Hotz's rant giving up on AMD drivers.","quote":"he had a moment in the spotlight recently for a long rant [1] where he \"gave up on AMD","text":"So for reference, this geohot is George Hotz who has a company Tiny Corp [0] in the space. Among a long list of things, he had a moment in the spotlight recently for a long rant [1] where he \"gave up on AMD\" because their drivers sucked more than he expected. The situation is reasonably complex - AMD have some programmers working on ROCm who seemed to be operating at standard fare when that is really not what AMD needs right now (I personally, suspect there is/was a PM in a key position who didn't \"get it\", although I am not sure what it is either). As far as I know it got the attention of Lisa Su. I'm cheering him on, even though his complaints were a little melodramatic. My experience is the driver technically supports everything I could possibly want. The problem is if I spend an evening trying to do anything with OpenCL or ROCm the kernel hard-locks and I go to bed early. If the problem inside is what it looks like from the outside (repeating myself, a key manager somewhere just doesn't get the space) they really need some pressure from grumpy customers like George to realign their software development process. That context might be related to this particular case. I see a suspicious folder called \"crash\" in this repo. [0] [LINK] [1] [VIDEO] - as I recall he ran the demo suite and it crashed."},{"id":37095091,"author":"XTXinverseXTY","month":"2023-08","story":"Ask HN: Cheapest hardware to run Llama 2 70B","stance":"reference","why":"Hotz recommends 3090s over 4090s for running big models.","quote":"3090s over 4090s. 3090s have NVLink","text":"> according to George Hotz In this video from June, George Hotz says to go with \"3090s over 4090s. 3090s have NVLink... 4090s are $1600 and 3090s are 750. RAM bandwidth is about the same.\" Has he changed his recommendations since then? [VIDEO]"},{"id":36550525,"author":"roenxi","month":"2023-07","story":"VUDA: A Vulkan Implementation of CUDA","stance":"reference","why":"Hotz's rant validated the commenter's own AMD driver problems.","quote":"Now I know it wasn't just me.","text":"Hmm. I found [VIDEO] and [LINK] I feel a lot better about my journey with AMD now; there seemed to be some major issues with their GPU drivers. Now I know it wasn't just me."},{"id":36197927,"author":"alecco","month":"2023-06","story":"Lisa Su Saved AMD. Now She Wants Nvidia's AI Crown","stance":"reference","why":"Hotz giving up on AMD over bad drivers.","quote":"he \"gave up on AMD\", \"this driver will never, ever be good\".","text":"It seems he reached out to AMD and Su, but got back a horrible patch and wasted a lot of time and he \"gave up on AMD\", \"this driver will never, ever be good\". [VIDEO]"},{"id":36274014,"author":"icpmacdo","month":"2023-06","story":"Ted Kaczynski, ‘Unabomber’ Who Attacked Modern Life, Dies at 81","stance":"reference","why":"Hotz stream clip on powerlessness in industrial society.","quote":"","text":"Here is a timestamped clip of George Hotz Twitch stream from 6 days ago making the same point about rationalizing his own emotional state of powerlessness in Industrial Society and its Future [VIDEO]"},{"id":36307033,"author":"born-jre","month":"2023-06","story":"","stance":"reference","why":"Hotz rant on AMD driver quality.","quote":"you can watch george hotz rant about how amd driver sucks","text":"short answer is they have somewhat competent hardware but software sucks or you can watch george hotz rant about how amd driver sucks [VIDEO]"},{"id":36190389,"author":"ftxbro","month":"2023-06","story":"Geohot/Tinycorp gives up on AMD","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"> \"... aww shit they have alignment, ok well, we don't want any alignment. our AIs are unaligned\" lmao [VIDEO]"},{"id":36247572,"author":"ftxbro","month":"2023-06","story":"CIL: Common MLIR abstraction for C / C++/ Fortran (2020) [pdf]","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"it's hilarious especially if you never heard 'remove dead loop' before these two times","text":"When I got to slide 5 I LMAO when they wrote \"LLVM doesn’t remove the dead loop\" in bright red it reminded me so much of the geohot drama on his batshit twitch archive on youtube where some AMD GPU driver author says something about 'remove the dead loop' in their patch to fix the driver which was causing hotz's linux to actually kernel panic. Please read slide 5 here and then watch [VIDEO] it's hilarious especially if you never heard 'remove dead loop' before these two times. To be honest, I still don't know what 'remove dead loop' actually means."},{"id":36960514,"author":"ImprobableTruth","month":"2023-08","story":"Nvidia H100 GPUs: Supply and Demand","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"this definitely doesn't scream stability to me","text":"They're talking about the meltdown he had on stream [1] (in front of the mentioned pirate flag), that ended with him saying he'd stop using AMD hardware [2]. He recanted this two weeks after talking with AMD [3]. Maybe he'll succeed, but this definitely doesn't scream stability to me. I'd be wary of investing money into his ventures (but then I'm not a VC, so what do I know). [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK] [3] [LINK]"},{"id":36638529,"author":"paulmd","month":"2023-07","story":"Why Nvidia Keeps Winning: The Rise of an AI Giant","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"that youtube doesn't make him come off looking that good","text":"He flamed out, then is back after Lisa Su called him (lmao) [LINK] [VIDEO] [LINK] [LINK] On a personal level that youtube doesn't make him come off looking that good... like people are trying to get patches to him and generally soothe him/damage control and he's just being a bit of a manchild. And it sounds like that's the general course of events around a lot of his \"efforts\". On the other hand he's not wrong either, having this private build inside AMD and not even validating official, supported configurations for the officially supported non-private builds they show to the world isn't a good look, and that's just the very start of the problems around ROCm. AMD's OpenCL runtime was never stable or good either and every experience I've heard with it was \"we spent so much time fighting AMD-specific runtime bugs and specs jank that what we ended up with was essentially vendor-proprietary anyway\". On the other other hand, it sounds like AMD know this is a mess and has some big stability/maturity improvements in the pipeline. It seems clear from some of the smoke coming out of the building that they're cooking on more general ROCm support for RDNA cards, and generally working to patch the maturity and stability issues he's talking about. I hate the \"wait for drivers/new software release bro it's gonna fix everything\" that surrounds AMD products but in this case I'm at least hopeful they seem to understand the problem, even if it's completely absurdly late. Some of what he was vi"},{"id":36372022,"author":"brucethemoose2","month":"2023-06","story":"AMD's Lisa Su and Tiny Corp's George Hotz Repair Business Relationship","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"has made me more skeptical of tinygrad","text":"Even with this very notable reconciliation, the freewheeling YT video: [VIDEO] And a harder look at the ML space outside of PyTorch eager mode has made me more skeptical of tinygrad."}]},"OWwOJlOI1nU":{"wy":"Used purely as a joke reference when someone should have heard an internal warning voice.","m":[{"id":41719452,"author":"palisade","month":"2024-10","story":"COBOL has been “dead” for so long, my grandpa wrote about it","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"Oh, btw, COBOL has the 2038 problem and it is right around the corner. We're going to need A LOT of new COBOL engineers to fix it. It runs so much of our world. We managed to save the world from Y2K in the nick of time. But, I'm not sure if we're going to have the minds necessary to solve 2038 by then as the can has just been kicked down the road without consideration. If anyone is worried there won't be jobs, there WILL be jobs. Not to be too macabre, but we need to transfer the knowledge while the people who have it are still alive, can remember and can teach others to pick up the torch. And, let us call it was it is, of those remain and still have the desire to make the effort to transfer that knowledge. It is easy to look back on y2k and think well that wasn't a big deal, but the only reason it wasn't is because people tirelessly worked to stop it. It is a testament to their success. Regarding y2k Robert Bemer tried to warn people in 1971, with 29 years left to go. And, Peter de Jager published his attention-getting article \"Doomsday 2000,\" in 1993 (in Computerworld), with a mere 7 years left which finally put the fire under everyone's ass. Keep in mind, there were still many original COBOL programmers and mainframe experts left to talk to at that time. And, there was a lot less code to change back then than there is now. Voting tabulation, insurance, utilities, administrative systems, banking, ATMs, travel, healthcare, social security, point of sale, IRS, pension funds, "},{"id":14107801,"author":"musesum","month":"2017-04","story":"United removes first-class passenger to make room for 'higher-priority' traveler","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Who designs these things? A Lost in Space fan?","text":"User surly UX. Was going to book an impromptu multi-legged trip between San Francisco, Iowa and Arizona. American Airlines quoted a price, but the only available seats cost (around) $33 - $58 extra for each flight -- total was $200 extra. Bait and switch? So, I switched to Delta. But Delta's antique web design wouldn't allow a change. So, I attempted to talk to a Human. Voice menu was a bit schizoid: transitioning from voice to a touch-only menu. Love the response: \"your selection was invalid\" Who designs these things? A Lost in Space fan? [VIDEO] Then I get to the part where it says: \"You may choose to have us call you back ... or stay on hold ... wait time is over 3 hours\". So, I cancelled."},{"id":13730631,"author":"rebootthesystem","month":"2017-02","story":"Amazon guts affiliate program, cuts fees for electronics in half","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Some people just don't seem to have that internal voice. You know, this one","text":"You have to take into consideration that the seminar peddlers are really, really good. Some people just don't seem to have that internal voice. You know, this one: [VIDEO]"}]},"PxuUkYiaUc8":{"wy":"Used as a joke or as an example of why workplace rules and dating dynamics are funny and relatable.","m":[{"id":33680689,"author":"unsupp0rted","month":"2022-11","story":"‘Why am I talking to 10 guys?’ The rise and fall of dating apps","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"The reason that skit is funny and relatable","text":"Another commenter posted the same concept in the form of an SNL skit. [VIDEO] The reason that skit is funny and relatable is because it doesn’t take an incel to recognize this nuance of women’s reactions to being approached by two different classes of men."},{"id":32074480,"author":"astura","month":"2022-07","story":"Heathrow tells airlines to stop selling additional flights until September","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"By \"relatively little money\" you mean triple the cost of the ticket... So just like the Tom Brady method of avoiding sexual harassment[1] [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":14679457,"author":"coldtea","month":"2017-07","story":"McClure steps back at 500Startups after internal sexual misconduct investigation","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"Yeah, it's not fine. I meant it in a different way (and was being somewhat sarcastic): that in a lot of cases, office flirting (even among equal peers) can seem OK if the woman likes the guy, and creepy if they don't (e.g. he is awkward, nerdy, out of shape, etc). In essence, a comment on how \"jocks\" get away with more of that kind of stuff. [VIDEO]"}]},"Q4zd7X98eOs":{"wy":"HN cites it critically as an example of corporate apology marketing that rang hollow, and as a TV ad using gray placeholder rectangles.","m":[{"id":18285906,"author":"ascagnel_","month":"2018-10","story":"Show HN: Websites in 2018","stance":"reference","why":"Facebook TV ad using gray placeholder rectangles.","quote":"Facebook used them in TV ads.","text":"The \"gray rectangles showing me where words and letters could be\" is so prevalent that Facebook used them in TV ads. [VIDEO]"},{"id":42226285,"author":"xyst","month":"2024-11","story":"Supreme Court allows multibillion-dollar class action to proceed against Meta","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"remember those bs “sorry” videos and newspaper ads?","text":"FB will settle out of court. Probably a third of what shareholders are asking. Lawyers get paid handsomely on both sides. Parent company has washed away the stink of Cambridge Analytica fallout (remember those bs “sorry” videos and newspaper ads? [1,2]). Shareholders get a pittance. I’m a bit over this hyper-capitalistic market. When are C-level executives going to pay for their actions? Where is the jail time for these people? This country is a joke. [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK]"},{"id":17126806,"author":"aaron-lebo","month":"2018-05","story":"Facebook management moves around but people don’t leave","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"It seems fake/manipulative and too convenient.","text":"It's only powerful if you actually change. Otherwise you're just using one of the classic tricks of manipulators. \"I'm sorry, it won't happen this time\" is what abusive relationships are made of. [VIDEO] Do people find this sincere? It seems fake/manipulative and too convenient. Facebook has been doing this for a decade+."},{"id":17134977,"author":"joering2","month":"2018-05","story":"Facebook wants your naked photos to stop revenge porn","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"","text":"Upload your own porn to Facebook, hilarious! Zuckerberg really hasn't changed and he still believes his users are dumb fucks. Knowing real Facebook, I would imagine at minimum they will be extracting tattoos from these photos and sharing it with FBI/LEs of all sorts. AS it turns out many criminal cases are being solved because someone flashed their tattoo. So 10 years from now Zuck will be on the road apologizing that they shared your naked photos/tattoos with LEs but you know thats for the common good so its allright. But definitely we will do better lol :) Here Together: [VIDEO] Edit: its beyond hilarious that the upload above has comments section disabled LOL! Social network not so much LOL"}]},"RuVS7MsQk4Y":{"wy":"HN points to it as an example of hobbyist, at-home integrated circuit fabrication.","q":{"x":"Here is BreakingTaps fabricating home made chips, he has a fair bit of equipment though","i":42757124,"y":"2025"},"m":[{"id":42757124,"author":"aeonik","month":"2025-01","story":"Reverse-engineering a carry-lookahead adder in the Pentium","stance":"reference","why":"Home-made chip fabrication showing DIY processors are accessible","quote":"Here is BreakingTaps fabricating home made chips, he has a fair bit of equipment though","text":"While it's not \"easy\", I was surprised to learn recently that DIY microprocessors are far more accessible than I thought. Here is BreakingTaps fabricating home made chips, he has a fair bit of equipment though [VIDEO] There a a few different techniques that can be used. Most of the lithography techniques use Hydrofluoric Acid, but if you are okay with slower build times you can etch the circuits directly with Ion beams or Electron Beams. But the short story is you need an overhead projector, a microscope, and a few gnarly chemicals. Otherwise fairly straightforward."},{"id":42055118,"author":"pragma_x","month":"2024-11","story":"Hacker Fab","stance":"reference","why":"Example of hobbyist at-home IC fabrication","quote":"there _is_ work being done on the hobby front for IC fabrication \"at home\".","text":"FWIW, there _is_ work being done on the hobby front for IC fabrication \"at home\". We're still far from buying a miniature chip-fab-in-a-box product, but current technology makes yesterday's tech far more affordable. We're on our way. Example: [VIDEO]"},{"id":41591049,"author":"zeckalpha","month":"2024-09","story":"Have you ever seen soldering this close? [video]","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Yes, and I've seen closer even on YouTube recently. [VIDEO] The isometric view camera is neat though!"}]},"V6CLumsir34":{"wy":"Cited as the direct link to the infamous exploding whale footage, and used for jokes about explosions and whales.","m":[{"id":25071682,"author":"ceph_","month":"2020-11","story":"The Exploding Whale remastered: 50th anniversary of legendary Oregon event","stance":"reference","why":"News segment on the Oregon exploding whale","quote":"Direct link to the video without the shitty local news site","text":"Direct link to the video without the shitty local news site: [VIDEO] Side rant: Their ad-tracking cookie opt-out is the epitome of a dark design pattern. It took over 2 minutes to run. Including saying \"done\" in a pop out and the only leaving a cancel button for another 15 seconds. There's absolutely zero reason it should take even 1/10th of that time, other than purposefully bad UX."},{"id":38573852,"author":"al2o3cr","month":"2023-12","story":"Whale carcass washed up at Tramore pier in Waterford currently unmoveable","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"The Oregon State Highway Division can tell the folks in Waterford one whale-moving solution that DOESN'T work: [VIDEO]"},{"id":29051569,"author":"dredmorbius","month":"2021-10","story":"The untold story of the world’s biggest nuclear bomb","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"I've somewhat deliberately left the pulverising mechanism unspecified. But the point remains: under the influence of the cloud's own gravity a cloud of merely separated particles will re-coalesce. You can look up in the night sky much of the time to see an example of this yourself, the Earth's moon. And again, depending on the mechanism , a fair fraction of particles, quite possibly / probably the majority, will approach the original centroid of the mass. In practice, pulverising planets and/or planetoids is likely a challenging proposition. Early experimentation on animal models tends to support this hypothesis: [VIDEO]"}]},"W_tqQYmgMQg":{"wy":"Used as a historical analogy for public resistance to new safety rules, and as an amusing vox pop.","q":{"x":"Here's one vox pop with the introduction of breathalizers in UK (1967)","i":46222960,"y":"2025"},"m":[{"id":46222960,"author":"Sevrene","month":"2025-12","story":"Australia begins enforcing world-first teen social media ban","stance":"reference","why":"Vox pop on 1967 UK breathalyzer introduction","quote":"Here's one vox pop with the introduction of breathalizers in UK (1967)","text":">I feel like you haven't really been listening to the arguments against these types of age-verification-for-websites laws. Or maybe I just have a different conclusion to you? Because I do care, I do try to listen to the arguments. I'm no stranger to advocacy for civil liberties, they are important to me. I think all else being equal, freedom should be valued more over harm prevention. So if I'm for these laws, consider that a sign of how bad these sites have become, not how uninformed I am. > I mean, there's some similarities, of course. But I think there are some very stark differences. Yep of course it's not a 1:1, I agree. I don't mean to imply that people saying the same arguments today are wrong simply because people in the past were, but it does make me think more about it when I spot the same rhetoric. Often both sides have very reasonable concerns, as an example, the question isn't \"should we have all or no freedom\" Either extreme creates issues, yet both sides have valid arguments worth our time considering. We settle somewhere in the middle. Here's one vox pop with the introduction of breathalizers in UK (1967): [VIDEO]"},{"id":35234750,"author":"Levitz","month":"2023-03","story":"OpenAI CEO warns that GPT-4 could be misused for nefarious purposes","stance":"reference","why":"Amusing reporter interview before drunk driving became illegal","quote":"As an amusing tidbit: a reporter asking people about making drunk driving illegal","text":"The rise of the automobile did cause a whole lot of legal and social ramifications that ought to be a good warning. Drunk driving was legal for years, I think it's a good thing to be wary of how things can change and to be ready, as a society, to deal with unintended consequences of this new technology, even if we assume it to be a worthwhile net good. As an amusing tidbit: a reporter asking people about making drunk driving illegal, a little before that came to be a reality: [VIDEO]"},{"id":34289292,"author":"richbell","month":"2023-01","story":"Study Finds That Buttons in Cars Are Safer and Quicker to Use Than Touchscreens","stance":"reference","why":"Historic pushback against drunk driving laws as analogy","quote":"When drunk driving was made illegal, people were furious and insisted they were fine to drive drunk","text":"> The point is your opinion is concordant with every other poster in this thread, aka hivemind By this logic isn't everyone who is pro-touchscreens also a hivemind? > Nobody is asking whether some people are fine with touchscreens in cars and why that might be the case. Many people are demonstrably bad drivers who are over-confident in their ability and down-play the risk of distractions. When drunk driving was made illegal, people were furious and insisted they were fine to drive drunk and that it was perfectly safe: [VIDEO] Touchscreens, while convenient and extensible, are demonstrably inferior to static physical controls for saftey-critical contexts."}]},"X8XZriAdB1g":{"wy":"Cited to show that empty slot-keeping flights predate COVID and Lufthansa's recent practice.","m":[{"id":29886745,"author":"lathiat","month":"2022-01","story":"Lufthansa confirmed that 18k flights had been flown empty to keep slots","stance":"reference","why":"Pre-COVID explanation of empty flights to Heathrow to keep slots.","quote":"This existed pre-COVID but is more of a thing now.","text":"This existed pre-COVID but is more of a thing now. See Half as Interesting - Why Heathrow Airport Had Empty Flights to Nowhere (3 years ago, pre-covid) [VIDEO] RealLifeLore2 also covered the same topic as the original submission: [LINK]"},{"id":22511649,"author":"scrollaway","month":"2020-03","story":"Airlines flying empty planes to keep flight slots during coronavirus outbreak","stance":"reference","why":"Empty flights to keep slots predate COVID.","quote":"it's not a new practice it's just been exacerbated due to COVID-19.","text":"Yep. Featured on HAI a year ago, it's not a new practice it's just been exacerbated due to COVID-19. [VIDEO]"},{"id":22149939,"author":"gh02t","month":"2020-01","story":"If Everyone Hates Spirit Airlines, How Is It Making So Much Money?","stance":"reference","why":"Airline flew empty planes to hold Heathrow slots.","quote":"where an airline was flying empty planes to hold their slot at Heathrow","text":"Probably filling seats on unpopular routes that they would have to fly anyway. Perhaps in a similar situation to what's described in [VIDEO] , where an airline was flying empty planes to hold their slot at Heathrow."}]},"YM5zUwiCTzw":{"wy":"HN commenters mostly cite it as evidence in arguments about BLM's ideology rather than recommending it.","m":[{"id":35755846,"author":"zmgsabst","month":"2023-04","story":"How to Subvert a Democracy, Stage 2: Condition the Masses","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as the BLM founder explaining her ideology.","quote":"BLM founder explaining","text":"The Frankfurt school is a set of communist revolutionaries who promoted “critical theories” as their methodology of fomenting civil unrest and social breakdown to bring about Marxist revolution. When the Germans chased them out by preferring literal Nazis, they settled in the US and performed the same schtick — most recently with “critical race theory” and “critical queer theory”, both of which have increased division in US society and undermined social progress, such as civil rights. Eg, “critical race theory” promotes continued organized racism against Asians while promoting grievance myths which encourage terrorism. As seen in the burning of dozens of cities and dozens of murders and institutions such as Harvard arguing before the Supreme Court they’re allowed to discriminate against Asians. [LINK] [LINK] [LINK] BLM founder explaining: [VIDEO] Harvard case: [LINK] Harvard filing showing nearly double chance of admission if you’re black or Hispanic versus white or Asian, in exhibit 26: [LINK]"},{"id":32432564,"author":"zmgsabst","month":"2022-08","story":"Is this the end of social networking?","stance":"reference","why":"Cited clip of BLM founder calling herself a trained Marxist.","quote":"She is literally answering a question about the ideology behind BLM.","text":"@dang has been explicit that they deem certain topics “inherent flame wars” and ban discussion because it upsets people. Certain ideas aren’t allowed here — even when calmly stated and cited with evidence. For example, citing the clip of the BLM founder saying she’s a “trained Marxist” would get you banned: HN was in flat out denial, even though it was her own words on film [1]. She is literally answering a question about the ideology behind BLM. Unfortunately, that kind of censorship enabled BLM to commit the fraud they did [2]. [1] - [VIDEO] [2] - [LINK]"},{"id":26153417,"author":"AnHonestComment","month":"2021-02","story":"“Silicon Valley’s Safe Space” has misinformed readers","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as the BLM founder describing BLM as Marxist.","quote":"It’s her own words","text":"Duh? The NYT writes hit pieces for the establishment; social media (including HN) uses shadowbans to enforce narrative conformity. Manipulation is the norm. Case in point: I was shadowbanned on HN for linking to a video where the BLM founder described BLM as a Marxist organization. It’s her own words: [VIDEO] The reason the NYT sees a radical conspiracy behind every bush is that’s how communists act — and the NYT staff are projecting."},{"id":23636873,"author":"virmundi","month":"2020-06","story":"On Liberty (1859) [pdf]","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as evidence that BLM leaders are trained Marxists.","quote":"BLM has leaders that are trained Marxists[1].","text":"Allowed in the sense that those in cancel culture and the like will cancel the book. Remember that BLM has leaders that are trained Marxists[1]. Marxists are not the most sympathetic to free speech. 1 - [VIDEO]"},{"id":26154017,"author":"dahdum","month":"2021-02","story":"“Silicon Valley’s Safe Space” has misinformed readers","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"BLM the organization has diverged pretty drastically from BLM the movement.","text":"> Case in point: I was shadowbanned on HN for linking to a video where the BLM founder described BLM as a Marxist organization. It’s her own words: [VIDEO] BLM the organization has diverged pretty drastically from BLM the movement. Linking them together without acknowledging that is a tad manipulative. It's much like the slogan \"defund the police\" has been watered down from the original meaning. You can't hold those using the term today to the original intent."}]},"Zgk8UdV7GQ0":{"wy":"HN mostly uses it jokingly as a reference for anti-science or trust-your-own-research arguments rather than recommending it.","m":[{"id":19746874,"author":"swebs","month":"2019-04","story":"The End of Being a Duke Professor and What It Means for the Future of Higher Ed","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"blindly trusting authority rather than doing your own research.","text":"He was an ethics professor. I bet a good amount of money that the point he was making pertained to blindly trusting authority rather than doing your own research. Or in other words: [VIDEO]"},{"id":18827238,"author":"byproxy","month":"2019-01","story":"Sobriety startups shaking up the 12-step model","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"I feel like it's a less-crude version of Mac's argument","text":"I've just read one ( [LINK] ) and it was less than convincing. In fact, I feel like it's a less-crude version of Mac's argument ( [VIDEO] )."},{"id":15982330,"author":"pfisch","month":"2017-12","story":"The Hunt for Aliens Is a Grassroots Movement Funded by Billionaires","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Pretty much all scientists have been proven wrong so far in significant ways.","text":"\"Our understanding of physics is very wrong\" - this seems very likely to me. Pretty much all scientists have been proven wrong so far in significant ways. The entire structure of the universe makes no sense without tons of dark matter and dark energy we can't observe, which sounds a lot like the way we used to balance the equations of orbits so the earth was the center of the galaxy. [VIDEO] 2. I think this alleges a pretty large conspiracy theory on the order of 9/11 being faked. There is a wide body of evidence from a lot of reputable witnesses of unexplained phenomena. The idea that the military fabricated that video and the pilots who are the witnesses is more ridiculous to me than \"Our understanding of physics is very wrong\""},{"id":10146401,"author":"smcl","month":"2015-08","story":"Dinosaur Feathers Found in Amber Reinforce Evolution Theories (2011)","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"is a plot point in an episode of It's Always Sunny in Philadelphia","text":"Interestingly the first sentence of your second paragraph is a plot point in an episode of It's Always Sunny in Philadelphia (\"Science is a liar ... sometimes\") : [VIDEO]"},{"id":25665892,"author":"smcl","month":"2021-01","story":"London will be overwhelmed by Covid in a fortnight says leaked NHS briefing","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"many of the arguments seem to boil down to conspiracy theories","text":"Indeed there would. The naive estimate of deaths is bad enough, the knock on impact is a bit harder to wrap our heads around in terms of magnitude, but would still be severe. Basically - this is not nothing, it’s being treated like a Big Deal because it legitimately is. There’s a lot of talk from the “treatment is worse than the disease” crowd, scant few of whom are experts. And like climate change and evolution deniers many of the arguments seem to boil down to conspiracy theories or the It’s Always Sunny In Philadelphia argument - “science is a liar ... sometimes”: [VIDEO]"}]},"bx_LGilOuE4":{"wy":"HN links it as the canonical talk on Jujutsu's design, especially conflict handling.","q":{"x":"I think this was the talk","i":40913324,"y":"2024"},"m":[{"id":40913324,"author":"snthpy","month":"2024-07","story":"Jujutsu: A Next Generation Replacement for Git","stance":"reference","why":"Talk on Jujutsu and first-class conflict handling.","quote":"I think this was the talk","text":"I think this was the talk: [VIDEO] I would like to know more about the first class conflict handling. That could potentially be useful for an application I have in mind but I need to understand the algorithms better. How does it compare to CRDTs or other merge strategies?"},{"id":38371983,"author":"aseipp","month":"2023-11","story":"I (kind of) killed Mercurial at Mozilla","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"I completely rewrote the README recently to be more \"user friendly\"; does it address some of your question? I'm not trying to be snarky, I'm genuinely interested in if the README is now tantalizing enough to make you interested: [LINK] But in short, it has a better UX than Git by a mile while remaining interoperable at the storage level, so you can use GitHub; it has many of the niceties of Mercurial's UX like revsets, no staging area, and a templating language for log output. Conflict resolution and rebasing is clean, easy, and nearly automatic. It has features neither support, like a real undo command and \"operation log\"[1]. The UX is smaller and conceptually cleaner; fewer \"verbs\" that operate on less \"nouns\", so many things are more regular. It is available as an API (Rust crates) so you can extend it naturally to handle your own workflow, without monkey-patching things. You could even use Mercurial itself as a storage backend, or a custom, centralized server like Google does. Most importantly though, its internal \"automatic snapshot model\" design is quite elegant and makes the design and internals very clear. And storage system is completely independent and abstracted from this, and many of the other algorithmic details (Git sort of intertwines the data model, the algorithms, and the UX in various ways.) Features like hg changeset evolution, git rerere, and rebase --update-refs are all obsoleted and naturally handled by this design, among other things. Martin, the lead d"},{"id":36953263,"author":"teleforce","month":"2023-08","story":"Jujutsu: A Git-compatible DVCS that is both simple and powerful","stance":"reference","why":"Jujutsu's Git Merge 2022 presentation.","quote":"It's presented at Git Merge 2022","text":"Jujutsu started as author's personal project and now author's full-time project at Google. It's presented at Git Merge 2022: Jujutsu: A Git-Compatible VCS - Git Merge 2022: Video: [VIDEO] Slides: [LINK]"},{"id":33616608,"author":"martinvonz","month":"2022-11","story":"Sapling: A new source control system with Git-compatible client","stance":"reference","why":"Jujutsu talk about integrating with Google's monorepo.","quote":"We're going to copy some of Sapling's solutions to Jujutsu soon","text":"I think those two things that arxanas mentioned are the biggest differences (i.e. working-copy-as-commit and conflicts in commits). I haven't used Sapling, but I suspect Jujutsu has better support for moving commits (and parts of commits) around without touching the working copy. Sapling, on the other hand, has much better support very large repositories, since they've spent a lot of time on that over the years. We're going to copy some of Sapling's solutions to Jujutsu soon, since we're working on integrating it with Google's monorepo (slides: [LINK] , recording: [VIDEO] )."}]},"fqYyxvM85zU":{"wy":"It is used as a joke or shorthand for the prevention paradox, where successful safety work looks unnecessary because nothing bad happens.","m":[{"id":40271683,"author":"orthoxerox","month":"2024-05","story":"Bollards: Why and What","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Yes, we've all heard [0] and probably agreed with the person Mitchell plays, but the cost of bollards is actually really low. I can buy concrete hemispherical bollards for less that $20 a piece. Let's make the total installation cost $50 per bollard. How many bollards does a 7/11 parking need? [0] - [VIDEO]"},{"id":47241511,"author":"rhet0rica","month":"2026-03","story":"Helsinki just went a full year without a single traffic death","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"I am reminded of a certain Mitchell and Webb skit that suggests the absence of deaths by drowning in a county indicates perhaps too much public funding has gone into preventing them: [VIDEO]"},{"id":10163856,"author":"mryan","month":"2015-09","story":"Safe from what?","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"This reminds me of a sketch by Mitchell and Webb (a pair of British comedy actors). It is a satirical news broadcast, in which Mitchell points out that the fact that there was not a single death from drowning in the local region proves that too much money is being spent on anti-drowning measures. [VIDEO]"}]},"hIXhnWUmMvw":{"wy":"Linked as a reference in privacy and big tech threads for framing the business model of data extraction.","q":{"x":"spells out the history and The Truth in a very 21st Century way","i":29525663,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":30230880,"author":"boraoztunc","month":"2022-02","story":"Ask HN: Does targeted advertising work at all?","stance":"reference","why":"Zuboff on how online advertising differs from traditional advertising","quote":"","text":"Advertising and online advertising are different things. Advertising was a thing, long before technology. Great ad people (like David Ogilvy) was created effective, targeted advertising and achieved success. How to Create Advertising That Sells Ad by David Ogilvy [1972] [LINK] What I meant was, there is nothing wrong with advertising, or targeting right people to deliver value, products, services to them with creative approaches. Online advertising though, especially programmatic, is totally a different scheme. I suggest reading more about; \"Surveillance Capitalism\" Professor Shoshana Zuboff, author of “[The Age of Surveillance Capitalism]( [LINK] , who coined the term surveillance capitalism in 2014, defines it as; \" …the unilateral claiming of private human experience as free raw material for translation into behavioral data. These data are then computed and packaged as prediction products and sold into behavioral futures markets — business customers with a commercial interest in knowing what we will do now, soon, and later.\" In an interview with The Gurdian, Zuboff explains how surveillance capitalism is a human creation; >It lives in history, not in technological inevitability. It was pioneered and elaborated through trial and error at Google in much the same way that the Ford Motor Company discovered the new economics of mass production or General Motors discovered the logic of managerial capitalism. >Surveillance capitalism was invented around 2001 as the solution to fi"},{"id":29468748,"author":"chiefalchemist","month":"2021-12","story":"The next great game: Politicians vs. tech companies","stance":"reference","why":"Framing of big tech's power to track and influence","quote":"does a thorough job of framing the massive depth and breadth of tech's power","text":"> For skeptics, Bremmer poses this question: Who will do more to influence the outcome of next year’s US midterm congressional elections: The President of the United States or the CEO of Meta? According to Bremmer, since the vote will be influenced by both real-world rules changes and the online flow of information, the answer isn’t obvious. The question is flawed. Better would be: - From the perspective of the status quo power (i.e., US government) what is the bigger threat to that power persisting or not, China or Big Tech? (Hint: It's not China). - Put a slightly different way, on a day to day basis who is better positioned to influence the thoughts and decisions of individuals, some elected official or Big Tech? --- While she doesn't get heavily into gov v tech per se, \"The Age of Surveillance Capitalism\" does a thorough job of framing the massive depth and breadth of tech's power, especially when it comes to tracking and influence. --- [VIDEO]"},{"id":29525663,"author":"chiefalchemist","month":"2021-12","story":"Nobel Peace laureates blast tech giants and warn against rising authoritarianism","stance":"reference","why":"History and mechanics of surveillance capitalism","quote":"spells out the history and The Truth in a very 21st Century way","text":"Has Nobel bar been lowered? I can't tell if this is a misunderstanding of the current state of the world, hypocrisy, or a combination of both. The State (as well as traditional Media), regardless of country, is losing its power and influence. Big Tech knows more and thus is able to influence more. The USA and other \"democracies\" are not immune to this shift. If nothing else, Covid has swung the pendulum back a bit, or has at least delayed Big Tech's complete undermining of the long standing status quo. That status quo isn't going to surrender. So do not expect 21st Century totalitarianism to look like the 20th Century variety. No one saw it coming then, just as too few see it coming now. I mention this book often on HN, \"The Age of Surveillance Capitalism\" spells out the history and The Truth in a very 21st Century way. [VIDEO] Calling out Russia isn't bold or new. In fact, it's a dangerous distraction."},{"id":29279744,"author":"chiefalchemist","month":"2021-11","story":"The price of individualism has proved to be the loss of privacy","stance":"reference","why":"Surveillance as a feedback loop of influence and control","quote":"It's not about surveillance. It's about control.","text":"The article has a minor flaw that should be highlighted. Per \"The Age of Surveillance Capitalism\" the issue isn't the surveillance per se. It's how that awareness is used against us. That is, the Surveillance Capitalist isn't simply watching, there's a feedback loop that allows them to influence. Influence where to go. Influence what to buy. Ultimately, influence what to think. It's not about surveillance. It's about control. [VIDEO]"},{"id":28729828,"author":"chiefalchemist","month":"2021-10","story":"As a whistleblower prepares to speak out, what can be done to rein in Facebook?","stance":"reference","why":"Zuboff on surveillance capitalism","quote":"Surveillance Capitalism is real.","text":"FB is primarily a data collection and behavior directing organization. Surveillance Capitalism is real. [VIDEO]"},{"id":24134634,"author":"wombatmobile","month":"2020-08","story":"Show HN: NeuralCam Live – Using ML to Turn iPhones into Smart Webcams","stance":"reference","why":"Explains why big tech offers such software free","quote":"for big tech the business model for this kind of software would be surveillance capitalism","text":"Not speaking for NeuralCam, but for big tech the business model for this kind of software would be surveillance capitalism, which is why they will offer it \"free\". [VIDEO] For that reason, I'd expect NeuralCam to end up as an acquisition, depending on their motivation, resources, and all the rest."},{"id":24183182,"author":"wombatmobile","month":"2020-08","story":"Google: Open Letter to Australians","stance":"reference","why":"Zuboff documentary on surveillance capitalism","quote":"No such thing as a free lunch.","text":"Pot. Kettle. No such thing as a free lunch. Shoshana Zuboff on surveillance capitalism | VPRO Documentary [VIDEO]"},{"id":24183248,"author":"wombatmobile","month":"2020-08","story":"Why does Google wants to be your bank?","stance":"reference","why":"Explains how Google predicts clicks for advertisers","quote":"Google is able to do that by predicting what you will click on before you click","text":"Google is the world's biggest advertising company. It makes its money by offering advertisers the highest return possible on their advertising dollars. Google is able to do that by predicting what you will click on before you click, and charging advertisers a premium to make sure the link that's underneath your mouse or your finger when you click is going to their product site. How does Google know what you are likely to click on? Shoshana Zuboff on surveillance capitalism | VPRO Documentary [VIDEO]"},{"id":23992573,"author":"wombatmobile","month":"2020-07","story":"Del.icio.us","stance":"reference","why":"Zuboff on surveillance happening beyond user access","quote":"This is being conducted at a layer that is not accessible to us.","text":"\"This is being conducted at a layer that is not accessible to us.\" -- Professor Shoshana Zubuff Shoshana Zuboff on surveillance capitalism | VPRO Documentary [VIDEO]"},{"id":23312731,"author":"stiray","month":"2020-05","story":"Facebook Novi: Wallet for Libra Digital Currencies","stance":"reference","why":"Background on surveillance capitalism and distrust of big tech finance","quote":"One is goverment, something completely different is survailance capitalism","text":"Facebook and Google. Money. No way. Those two companies are having absolutely no trust from my side, they have abused the trust so often, that they no loger know what the term means. I dont care what they do, I dont care how usefull their \"gifts\" are. Just NO. They have seriously flawed internet and human society as it was (is) and there is just no way I will let them into my finance. One is goverment, something completely different is survailance capitalism ( [VIDEO] and there is a book that I would put in schools for mandatory reading: [LINK] ) \"Timeo Danaos et dona ferentes.\" (\"Beware of Danaans bringing the gift\", reference to Trojan horse, [LINK] ) p.s.: people downvoting, you just dont understand what you are against with. Educate yourself, please, this is no longer the time of cheering for your favorite coorporation, the things have gone over the edge."},{"id":21976395,"author":"duelingjello","month":"2020-01","story":"Chinese Spyware Pre-Installed on All Samsung Phones (& Tablets)","stance":"reference","why":"Video on surveillance capitalism","quote":"See also a video on surveillance capitalism","text":"Can we see some executable hashes, file names and network activity to prove it? Also, there is digital surveillance being monetized in iOS and Android phones and tablets innately. Why else would FAANG basically give them away? Calling out one minor company is a drop in the overall bucket. See also a video on surveillance capitalism: [VIDEO]"}]},"jdM3ID4m38U":{"wy":"Cited by commenters as a source of examples of Napoleon's cruelty and poor decisions.","m":[{"id":28478510,"author":"eru","month":"2021-09","story":"Why don’t the French celebrate Lafayette?","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"all lindy really does is point out how absolutely horrible Napoleon actually was.","text":"It's not just the extreme left that doesn't like Napoleon. [VIDEO] > relevant part starts at about 16 minutes, all lindy really does is point out how absolutely horrible Napoleon actually was."},{"id":27068822,"author":"JoeyBananas","month":"2021-05","story":"France Battles over Whether to Cancel or Celebrate Napoleon","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"This video is full of good reasons why Napoleon was not a good person","text":"[VIDEO] This video is full of good reasons why Napoleon was not a good person, see 24:30 for my personal favorite."},{"id":16536640,"author":"jerf","month":"2018-03","story":"Xi Jinping decides to abolish presidential term limits","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"Which contains numerous examples of Napoleon doing stupid and evil things that cost thousands of lives","text":"Are you demanding easy answers? There aren't any. The goal is deterrence, but deterrence doesn't work if you don't have a credible threat, which means that you have to have the credible threat, and a sufficiently insane opponent may call you on it. It's the reason why China going to a dictatorship is scary... the details of the dictatorship are irrelevant, the mere fact that one man will be in charge of the military of China is intrinsically scary. This is one place where the general bias towards inaction and other problems of a committee are advantageous; history shows that an individual is much likelier to do something really stupid for really stupid reasons than any government that is run by many people, even if there is a clear leader. A committee may fail to exploit many opportunities and may in various ways run their country into the ground, or they may even engage in misguided military adventures, but they never really rise to the level of outright evil and stupidity that an individual can muster up. For instance, if you've got a spare hour to listen, you could do worse than this: [VIDEO] Which contains numerous examples of Napoleon doing stupid and evil things that cost thousands of lives and burned massive piles of reputation just to benefit himself. If you do watch that video, can you imagine a world in which that Napoleon has nukes ? Or, can you imagine a world in which he has them, but doesn't use them? I can't. So, alas, no, I can't promise that some of the reall"}]},"kw-DlWwlXHo":{"wy":"Cited as an early, primary-source look at Carmack's VR prototype, which preceded his move to Oculus.","q":{"x":"here is a video of him demoing a unit at E3 2012","i":6173148,"y":"2013"},"m":[{"id":14000521,"author":"sorenjan","month":"2017-03","story":"Palmer Luckey Leaves Facebook","stance":"reference","why":"Early Oculus proof of concept.","quote":"","text":"What did Luckey contribute? The first seed of the idea? A proof of concept [0]? I haven't followed it very closely, but it seems to me that Carmack is the one that not only had the technical skills at the early stage and did a lot of evangelizing for the young technology even before he joined Oculus, his name was also a major factor in bringing in other talent and investment. I don't think Oculus would have been close to where they are today without Carmack, I've yet to read about any crucial contributions by Luckey. Right place at the right time does sound accurate. And of course companies need more than engineers, Steve jobs wasn't and engineer and his importance for Apple can hardly be overstated. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":6899283,"author":"amvp","month":"2013-12","story":"Oculus VR raises $75M","stance":"reference","why":"Carmack talking about VR work over a year earlier.","quote":"Carmack has been talking about working on it for for well over a year now","text":"Carmack has been talking about working on it for for well over a year now - [VIDEO] . When I saw that last year, I thought it was already his company."},{"id":6173148,"author":"LandoCalrissian","month":"2013-08","story":"John Carmack joins Oculus as CTO","stance":"reference","why":"Carmack demoing an early Oculus prototype at E3 2012.","quote":"here is a video of him demoing a unit at E3 2012","text":"John Carmack believes in the tech, and I believe in John Carmack, so I will be getting one. He has basically been with them since the beginning in some capacity, here is a video of him demoing a unit at E3 2012: [VIDEO] You can see that one is still a very early prototype held together with duct tape. It's not specifically stated, but it sounds like he will be leaving id for this. I'm fine with that since there is only so much you can do in the game engine world these days. Seeing him try to advance gaming and immersion in a different way seems like it will fit him very well. Congrats to everyone at Oculus."}]},"vydgkCCXbTA":{"wy":"HN uses it as an example of how severe long COVID and ME/CFS can become.","m":[{"id":38149583,"author":"moffkalast","month":"2023-11","story":"SARS-CoV-2 Infection Affects Energy Stores in the Body, Causing Organ Failure","stance":"reference","why":"Practical example to counter dismissal of long covid","quote":"Well maybe a practical example [0] would be more convincing.","text":"Well maybe a practical example [0] would be more convincing. It's really infuriating that most people still seem to discount even the existence of long covid. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":37360602,"author":"PaulKeeble","month":"2023-09","story":"What Happened to Dianna Cowern – Physics Girl? Dianna Cowern Health Update [video]","stance":"reference","why":"Her own video when she first became extremely ill","quote":"She did her own video when she first become extremely ill with Long Covid","text":"She did her own video when she first become extremely ill with Long Covid: [VIDEO] There has also been a few updates on shorts showing the extent of how severely unwell she is. [LINK] [LINK] [LINK] [LINK] [LINK] She also gives some updates on twitter/X as well. She has severe to very severe ME/CFS brought on by Long Covid. This disease is brutal, its one of the most deblitating diseases with the worst quality of life known and it has almost no research funding for decades. She is one of many millions now missing from life stuck in bed suffering with no one offering any help."},{"id":37130458,"author":"quenched","month":"2023-08","story":"Brandon Gilles Dies from Long-Covid, Electrical Engineer and Spatial AI Pioneer","stance":"reference","why":"Example of someone with severe ME/CFS","quote":"An example of someone becoming severe is PhysicsGirl - Dianna Cowern.","text":"While long Covid is an encompassing term, there is symptom overlap between long COVID and ME/CFS. Around 50% of people with long COVID meet the criteria for a diagnosis of ME/CFS. [LINK] [LINK] [LINK] Mild ME/CFS is usually associated with around 50% reduction in daily functioning. Moderate will usually lead to people being housebound and intermittently bed-bound. Severe and very severe are associated with a level of suffering that is hard to imagine possible. An example of someone becoming severe is PhysicsGirl - Dianna Cowern. [VIDEO] The associated severe dysautonomia means that people are unable to sit or stand and often they can not eat or digest. They may require intravenous nutrition. Regrettably there are cases where the disease has not been treated appropriately and young people have died as a result. A recent example is Maeve Boothby-O'Neill and her father Sean O'Neill wrote in The Times with an interview with Maeve's Mother by Dr David Tuller. [LINK] [LINK]"},{"id":37145728,"author":"aestetix","month":"2023-08","story":"WASF3 disrupts mitochondria and may mediate exercise intolerance in CFS","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as example of severe post-COVID illness","quote":"","text":"The two easy areas I can cite are the lackluster results of the RECOVER initiative ( [LINK] ), and the complete failure of any government to distribute vaccines well. Another: 19 September 2022, US President Biden declares COVID to be \"over\" [LINK] Meanwhile, it is easy to get Pfizer and Moderna vaccines (mRNA based), and hard to get Novavax (protein based). By hard I mean impossible. mRNA are not well understood, have nasty side effects for some people (including me), and lose their efficacy after a few months, thus the need for boosters. You can verify this with blood tests that show antibody levels. With Novavax, which is based on technology that's been around for 20 years, it appears (based on studies) that it keeps its efficacy for at least a year after multiple doses, and it has minimal side effects (including my own shot). That said, many governments have relationships with Pfizer and Moderna, and so these vaccines are easily attainable, while no such relationship exists with Novavax, and it is impossible to get it. I say impossible after asking about 50 doctors, contacting pharmacies, and finally contacting Novavax themselves, who told me that it is not possible to get until the new mix comes out. So why would a vaccine that seems to actually work be unavailable, while ones that do not work are easy to get? I realize that this is more a commentary on COVID than ME/CFS, but I'm arguing that they are very similar. Just consider Physics Girl, who contracted COVID a year "},{"id":37149820,"author":"ugh123","month":"2023-08","story":"WASF3 disrupts mitochondria and may mediate exercise intolerance in CFS","stance":"reference","why":"Example of extreme long covid cases","quote":"For those not aware of the extreme cases of long covid, see \"Physics Girl\" Dianna Cowern and her illness.","text":"For those not aware of the extreme cases of long covid, see \"Physics Girl\" Dianna Cowern and her illness. [VIDEO]"},{"id":36903913,"author":"audunw","month":"2023-07","story":"Fatigue Is So Much More Than Being Tired","stance":"reference","why":"Shows how devastating long covid fatigue can be","quote":"Really shows you how devastating it can be","text":"I’m reminded of Physics Girl who got a very serious case of long covid fatigue. Really shows you how devastating it can be [VIDEO]"},{"id":35072817,"author":"jcims","month":"2023-03","story":"I broke a wine glass with my voice (2018) [video]","stance":"reference","why":"Update on Physics Girl's severe ME/CFS","quote":"Dianna (Physics Girl) is currently experiencing severe complications from ME/CFS/long COVID.","text":"Dianna (Physics Girl) is currently experiencing severe complications from ME/CFS/long COVID. Per the last update on her twitter: The last weeks were scary. ER visits were hard. Dianna still can’t walk, or even sit up in bed. But we see signs of progress. Sleeping more. Eating more. More treatments coming. Update here - [VIDEO] [LINK]"}]},"zP0sqRMzkwo":{"wy":"HN references it as a joke about silly or absurd hiring practices.","m":[{"id":8033979,"author":"potatolicious","month":"2014-07","story":"Postmates NYC treats their deliverymen like trash","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"shitty mind games no better than the classic Monty Python sketch","text":"> \"That depends on whether they're constrained by supply (couriers) or demand (customers).\" Treating someone decently and with respect is not a supply/demand equation. Your sentence is why everyone hates us. Are you rude to your waiter because he's on the short end of the supply/demand stick? > \"and choosing which couriers to accept based on how early they arrive for their job interview seems like an excellent metric\" No, not it doesn't. This is cargo cult hiring, no different than hiring programmers based on how well they can reason why manhole covers are round, or why one lightbulb is warmer than the next. You can determine who is an effective courier and who isn't by having them deliver things. Just like you can determine who is an effective programmer and who isn't by having them write code. This sort of \"hiring by proxy signal even though the primary signal is perfectly testable\" is endemic in our industry, and apparently isn't limited to hiring devs. Tell people what you expect of them, and expect that of them. To do otherwise is shitty mind games no better than the classic Monty Python sketch: [VIDEO] > \"an excellent metric to use to attempt to differentiate between applicants you otherwise know very little about.\" ... and we arrive at the core of tech industry idiocy. \"We don't have enough information to make a good decision\" is answered by \"Let's concoct logical-sounding but completely unverified proxy signals to make the decision instead of collecting more informati"},{"id":5070678,"author":"ChuckMcM","month":"2013-01","story":"What If Cars Were Rented Like We Hire Programmers?","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Sort of like the Monty Python sketch","text":"Ah yes, another person describing their Google interview experience. They do leave a lot of solid candidates out but in their defense, when I was there, being able to survive the non-intuitive interview process was a good indicator you could survive the non-intuitive business dynamics. If didn't really crush people when they didn't get called back it would make it funny. Sort of like the Monty Python sketch [VIDEO]"},{"id":408364,"author":"jodrellblank","month":"2008-12","story":"The Dumbest Interview Question","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"I think it would end up like this Monty Python Silly Job Interview Sketch","text":"I think it would end up like this Monty Python Silly Job Interview Sketch: [VIDEO] Not something I want to see when it's me/my life on the line, really. Take it seriously and talk sense, please."}]}});
